PSR_ADD("Money & Budgeting", [
{t:"Needs vs. Wants",
goal:"Today we will learn the difference between what we need and what we want, so our money goes to the most important things first.",
warm:["If someone handed you twenty dollars right now, what is the first thing you would buy?","What is something you bought once and later wished you had not?","Who taught you about money when you were growing up?"],
terms:[
["Need","something you must have to live and stay safe and healthy.","Food, a place to live, medicine, clothes for the weather, and soap are needs. Without them, we get sick or unsafe.","What are three needs you have every single month?"],
["Want","something that would be nice to have, but you can live without it.","Name-brand sneakers, a new phone case, snacks from the store, and cable TV are wants. They make life fun, but we will be OK without them.","What is one want you spend money on that you really enjoy?"],
["Priority","the thing that comes first because it matters most.","If you have ten dollars and you need toothpaste and also want a soda, the toothpaste is the priority. You buy it first.","What is your number one money priority right now?"],
["Impulse buy","buying something quickly without planning, just because you saw it and felt like it.","Candy bars and lottery tickets by the register are placed there on purpose so people grab them without thinking. That is an impulse buy.","Where are you most tempted to make an impulse buy?"],
["Trade-off","giving up one thing to get another thing.","If you spend $15 on cigarettes, that is $15 you cannot spend on a new shirt. Every time we spend, we give up something else.","What is a trade-off you made this month with your money?"]],
story:"Marcus gets $60 spending money this month. On the first day he walks past a store and buys a $25 pair of headphones and a $10 pack of snacks. By the second week, he runs out of deodorant and his phone bill is due. He only has $25 left, and the phone bill is $30. Marcus feels upset and asks staff to borrow money.",
sq:["What did Marcus buy that was a want?","What were Marcus's needs that month?","How could Marcus have planned his $60 differently?","Has something like this ever happened to you or someone you know?"],
act:{n:"Sort It Out",m:"Paper, pen, or index cards with items written on them",s:["Before group, write these items on cards or a sheet of paper: rent, soda, toothpaste, phone bill, new shoes, medicine copay, movie ticket, winter coat, candy, bus pass, video game, laundry soap.","Hold up one card at a time and read it out loud.","Ask the group: Is this a NEED or a WANT? Let them talk it over.","Put the cards in two piles, NEED and WANT. Some items, like a phone, may cause a debate. That is good. Let people explain their thinking.","Count each pile together at the end."]},
d:["Which item was the hardest to decide about, and why?","Did anyone change their mind after hearing someone else?","Why do you think stores put wants right at the front of the store?"],
disc:[["Why is it important to pay for needs before wants?","Needs keep us safe and healthy; if we spend on wants first we may run out."],["Can something be a need for one person and a want for another? Give an example.","A phone may be a need for someone who needs to call doctors; for someone else it is a want."],["What feelings make you want to spend money?","Boredom, stress, sadness, celebrating, peer pressure."],["What is one trick that can help you stop an impulse buy?","Wait 24 hours, leave the card at home, make a list, ask: do I need this?"],["How does it feel when you run out of money before the month is over?","Stress, worry, embarrassment. Validate their feelings."]],
take:"Needs come first, wants come second. Before you buy, stop and ask yourself: Do I need this, or do I want this?",
tryit:"The next time you are about to buy something, say to yourself, Need or want? and wait one minute before you decide."},

{t:"Making a Simple Monthly Budget",
goal:"Today we will learn how to make a simple budget, which is a plan that tells your money where to go each month.",
warm:["Do you have a plan for your money each month, or do you spend as things come up?","What is one bill or expense you have to pay every month?","How do you feel at the end of the month when it comes to money?"],
terms:[
["Budget","a plan for how you will spend and save your money.","If you get $100 a month, a budget might say: $30 for phone, $20 for toiletries, $20 for snacks, $10 for savings, and $20 for fun. Now every dollar has a job.","Have you ever written down a budget? What happened?"],
["Income","the money that comes in to you.","A paycheck from a job, a Social Security check, or money a family member gives you is income.","Without saying the amount, where does your income come from?"],
["Expense","money that goes out, money you spend.","A phone bill, buying shampoo, bus fare, and eating out are all expenses.","What is your biggest expense each month?"],
["Fixed expense","a cost that is the same amount every month.","A phone plan that is $30 every month is a fixed expense. You know it is coming.","What fixed expenses do you have?"],
["Variable expense","a cost that changes from month to month.","Snacks, clothes, and eating out are variable. One month you may spend $10 and another month $40.","Which of your expenses changes the most from month to month?"]],
story:"Tasha gets $120 each month. She used to spend it until it was gone. One day she sat with her case manager and wrote it down: $35 phone, $20 toiletries, $15 laundry, $10 savings, $40 for fun and snacks. She put the fun money in a separate envelope. When the fun envelope was empty, she stopped buying snacks. At the end of the month, she still had her phone on and $10 saved for the first time.",
sq:["What did Tasha do differently this month?","Why do you think the envelope helped her?","What were Tasha's fixed expenses?","How do you think Tasha felt at the end of the month?"],
act:{n:"Build a Budget Together",m:"Large paper or board, markers, paper and pens for residents",s:["Write at the top: Pretend Monthly Income = $150.","Ask the group to name things they spend money on each month. Write each one on the board.","Go down the list and have the group decide how much money each item should get. Write the amount next to it.","Add up the total with the group. If it is over $150, ask: What can we cut or lower?","Ask each resident to try writing their own simple budget on paper using the same steps. They do not have to share real amounts."]},
d:["Was it easy or hard to make the money fit?","What did the group decide to cut, and why?","Did anyone include savings in their budget?"],
disc:[["Why do you think many people do not use a budget?","It seems hard, they do not like looking at money, they never learned how."],["What could make sticking to a budget easier?","Envelopes, writing it down, checking it weekly, a buddy or case manager."],["What should you do if something unexpected costs money, like a broken phone?","Use savings, cut a want for that month, ask for help early."],["Who can help you make a budget here?","Staff, case manager, social worker, family."],["How could a budget change your stress level?","Less worry because you know where your money goes."]],
take:"A budget is not a punishment. It is a plan that puts YOU in charge of your money instead of your money controlling you.",
tryit:"This week, write down every single thing you spend money on, even small things like a soda. Bring it to our next money group if you want."},

{t:"Understanding Credit",
goal:"Today we will learn what credit is, how it works, and how it can help us or hurt us.",
warm:["What do you think of when you hear the word credit?","Have you ever had a credit card, store card, or loan? You do not have to share details.","Why do you think stores and banks want to give people credit?"],
terms:[
["Credit","borrowing money now and promising to pay it back later.","When you buy a $300 TV with a credit card, the bank pays the store for you. You now owe the bank $300. That is using credit.","Can you think of a time someone used credit to buy something?"],
["Lender","the bank, company, or store that lends you the money.","Capital One, a car dealership, or a furniture store that says buy now, pay later are lenders.","What lenders have you seen advertised on TV?"],
["Credit limit","the most money a lender will let you borrow on a card.","If your credit card limit is $500, you cannot charge more than $500. If you try, the card may be declined.","Why do you think lenders give new people a low credit limit?"],
["Minimum payment","the smallest amount you must pay each month to stay in good standing.","If you owe $500, the bill might say minimum payment $25. If you only pay $25 each month, it can take years to pay off because of interest.","What happens if someone only pays the minimum every month?"],
["Credit report","a record that shows how you have handled borrowed money, like a report card for money.","If you paid your phone bill late three times, that can show up on your credit report for years. Landlords and car dealers can look at it.","Who might look at your credit report?"]],
story:"Darnell got a store credit card with a $400 limit to buy a new bed. The sign said no payments for six months. Darnell forgot about it. After six months, he got a bill for $400 plus $90 in interest. He could only pay $20. The late fees kept adding up, and a year later he owed over $600 for a $400 bed. When Darnell tried to rent an apartment, the landlord said no because of his credit report.",
sq:["What did the store promise Darnell?","What happened when Darnell did not pay on time?","How did the credit card affect Darnell's housing?","What could Darnell have done differently?"],
act:{n:"The Real Cost",m:"Board or paper, markers",s:["Write on the board: $300 TV on a credit card.","Explain: If you only pay $15 a month with interest, it takes about two years to pay off and the TV ends up costing about $380.","Ask the group to guess first, then reveal the real cost.","Now write: Save $25 a month for 12 months and pay cash. Cost: $300.","Ask the group to compare the two ways side by side."]},
d:["Which way would you choose, and why?","Were you surprised by the real cost?","When might using credit actually make sense?"],
disc:[["Is credit good or bad?","It is a tool. It helps if you pay on time; it hurts if you cannot pay."],["What are the signs that someone is in trouble with credit?","Only paying minimums, late fees, calls from collectors, using one card to pay another."],["Why do lenders offer things like no payments for six months?","To get you to buy; they make money from interest and fees."],["What would you tell a young person getting their first credit card?","Only charge what you can pay off each month, pay on time."],["Where can a person go for free help with credit?","Case manager, nonprofit credit counseling, the bank, annualcreditreport.com for a free report."]],
take:"Credit is borrowed money. Borrow only what you can pay back, pay on time every month, and always read the fine print.",
tryit:"Next time you see an ad that says buy now, pay later, point it out and ask yourself: What will this really cost me?"},

{t:"Credit Scores Made Simple",
goal:"Today we will learn what a credit score is, what makes it go up or down, and why it matters for housing, jobs, and loans.",
warm:["Have you ever heard someone say, I have bad credit? What did they mean?","What kinds of things do you think a landlord checks before renting to someone?","If you could fix one money mistake from your past, what would it be? You can keep it general."],
terms:[
["Credit score","a three-digit number from about 300 to 850 that shows how trustworthy you are with borrowed money.","A score of 750 is very good. A score of 520 is low. A person with a higher score usually gets approved more easily and pays less interest.","Why do you think a number is used to judge people with money?"],
["Payment history","the record of whether you paid your bills on time. It is the biggest part of your score.","Paying your phone bill on time for a year raises your score. Missing three payments lowers it a lot.","What bill would be easiest for you to always pay on time?"],
["Collections","when an unpaid bill is sent to a company that tries to collect the money from you.","If you leave a $200 hospital bill unpaid, it may be sent to collections. Then a collector calls you, and it hurts your score.","How would you handle a call from a collector?"],
["Credit utilization","how much of your credit limit you are using.","If your limit is $1,000 and you owe $900, you are using 90 percent. That lowers your score. Owing only $100 is much better.","Why do you think using all your credit lowers your score?"],
["Rebuilding credit","slowly fixing your credit score by making good choices over time.","A secured card, where you put down $200 and can only spend $200, and paying it off each month, can raise a score in about a year.","What is one step someone could take to rebuild their credit?"]],
story:"Keisha's credit score was 480 because of old medical bills and an unpaid cell phone. She wanted her own apartment someday. Her case manager helped her get a free credit report. They found one bill that was not even hers and had it removed. Keisha set up a payment plan for the phone bill and paid $15 every month. After 18 months, her score was 610. It was not perfect, but a landlord approved her.",
sq:["What hurt Keisha's credit score?","What was the first thing Keisha did to fix it?","How long did it take to see a change?","What does Keisha's story teach us about patience?"],
act:{n:"Score Goes Up, Score Goes Down",m:"None, or two signs that say UP and DOWN",s:["Explain: I will read an action. If it makes a credit score go UP, raise your hand high. If it makes it go DOWN, put your hand down low.","Read each action: Paying the phone bill on time. Missing a rent payment. Paying off a credit card. Ignoring a hospital bill. Using a secured card and paying it every month. Maxing out a card. Opening five new cards in one month. Keeping an old card open and paid.","After each one, ask a resident to explain why.","Correct gently if needed, and praise good thinking."]},
d:["Which answer surprised you?","What is the number one thing that helps a credit score?","How could knowing this help you in the future?"],
disc:[["Why might bad credit follow someone even after they change their life?","Late payments can stay on a report for about seven years."],["How can a credit score affect where someone lives?","Landlords check credit and may deny or ask for a bigger deposit."],["What should you never do if a bill collector calls?","Never give your bank or card numbers on the phone without checking first; ask for it in writing."],["Is it ever too late to fix your credit?","No; it can always improve with time and on-time payments."],["Who can you ask for help checking your credit report for free?","Case manager, social worker, annualcreditreport.com."]],
take:"Your credit score is not who you are. It is just a record, and every on-time payment helps rebuild it.",
tryit:"Ask your case manager or social worker if they can help you get your free credit report."},

{t:"Debt and Interest",
goal:"Today we will learn what debt is, how interest makes things cost more, and ways to get out of debt.",
warm:["What does the word debt make you feel?","Have you ever loaned money to a friend? Did you get it back?","Why do you think so many people are in debt?"],
terms:[
["Debt","money you owe to someone else.","If you borrowed $50 from your cousin, you have a $50 debt to your cousin until you pay it back.","What is the difference between owing a friend and owing a company?"],
["Interest","the extra money you pay for borrowing.","If you borrow $100 and the interest is 20 percent for the year, you pay back about $120. That extra $20 is interest.","Why do lenders charge interest?"],
["Interest rate (APR)","the percent of interest charged each year.","A credit card might have an APR of 25 percent. A payday loan can be over 300 percent. The higher the number, the more it costs you.","Which would you choose: a 10 percent loan or a 30 percent loan? Why?"],
["Late fee","a charge added when you pay a bill after the due date.","If your bill is due on the 5th and you pay on the 10th, the company may add a $35 late fee.","Have you ever paid a late fee? How did it feel?"],
["Payment plan","an agreement to pay back what you owe in smaller amounts over time.","A hospital bill of $600 might be paid as $25 a month. Many companies will agree to this if you call and ask.","Who could you call to ask for a payment plan?"]],
story:"Ray owed money on three things: a credit card, a cell phone, and a loan for a used car. He felt so overwhelmed that he stopped opening his mail. Late fees piled up. One day a counselor helped him write all three debts on one page. They called each company. The phone company gave him a payment plan. Ray paid the smallest debt first, then the next. It took two years, but he became debt free.",
sq:["Why did Ray stop opening his mail?","What happened when he ignored his bills?","What was the first step that helped Ray?","Why do you think paying the smallest debt first helped him keep going?"],
act:{n:"Debt Snowball",m:"Board or paper",s:["Write three pretend debts on the board: Phone $90, Store card $250, Hospital $600.","Explain the snowball method: pay the minimum on all, and put any extra money on the smallest debt first.","Pretend there is $40 extra each month. Ask the group: Which debt do we attack first?","Walk through it: about 2 months to pay off the phone, then roll that payment to the store card, and so on.","Show how the payment grows like a snowball rolling downhill."]},
d:["Why do you think it is called a snowball?","How would it feel to pay off that first debt?","What could someone cut from their budget to find extra money?"],
disc:[["What emotions come with being in debt?","Shame, stress, fear, avoiding mail and calls."],["Why is ignoring debt a bad idea?","Fees grow, it goes to collections, it can lead to court."],["Is all debt bad?","Some debt, like a small loan you can afford, can help; high-interest debt hurts."],["How can someone avoid going into debt in the first place?","Budget, save for emergencies, avoid payday loans, buy only what you can afford."],["What is one kind thing you could say to someone who is ashamed of their debt?","Many people have debt; it can be fixed one step at a time."]],
take:"Debt grows when we ignore it and shrinks when we face it. One small payment at a time can get you free.",
tryit:"If you owe money to anyone, write it down on one sheet of paper this week. Just seeing it is the first step."},

{t:"Checking and Savings Accounts",
goal:"Today we will learn how bank accounts work, the difference between checking and savings, and how to keep our money safe.",
warm:["Where do you keep your money now: a bank, a wallet, a card, or somewhere else?","What do you think is the safest place to keep money?","Have you ever lost money or had money stolen?"],
terms:[
["Bank account","a safe place at a bank or credit union where your money is kept for you.","Instead of keeping $300 in a sock drawer where it could be lost or stolen, you keep it in an account. The bank protects it.","Why might someone not want to use a bank?"],
["Checking account","an account for everyday spending, paying bills, and using a debit card.","Your check goes into checking, and you use your debit card at the store. The money comes out of checking.","What would you use a checking account for?"],
["Savings account","an account for money you are not planning to spend right away.","You put $10 a month into savings for emergencies or a goal, like new shoes or a deposit on an apartment.","What would you like to save for?"],
["Deposit","putting money into your account.","When your check is direct deposited, or you hand cash to the bank teller, that is a deposit.","What is direct deposit, and why is it helpful?"],
["Overdraft","when you spend more money than you have in your account.","If you have $20 and swipe your card for $30, the bank may pay it but charge you a $35 overdraft fee. Now you owe $45.","How can someone avoid an overdraft?"]],
story:"Janelle kept all her money in cash in her purse. One day her purse was stolen at the bus stop, and she lost $200, all the money she had for the month. Her social worker helped her open a checking account at a credit union with direct deposit. Now her money goes straight into the bank. She uses a debit card and checks her balance on her phone. She even has a small savings account with $40 in it.",
sq:["What happened to Janelle's money?","How did opening an account protect her?","What is one thing Janelle does now to keep track of her money?","How do you think Janelle felt after her purse was stolen?"],
act:{n:"Checkbook Practice",m:"A simple register printed or drawn on paper for each resident, pens",s:["Draw a simple register: Date, What For, Money In, Money Out, Balance.","Start everyone with a balance of $100.","Read out events one at a time: Bought toothpaste $4. Phone bill $30. Got $20 from family. Snacks $6. Laundry $8.","Have residents add or subtract after each event and write the new balance.","Check the final answer together: $72."]},
d:["Was it easy or hard to keep the balance?","Why is it important to know your balance before you spend?","What could happen if you never check your balance?"],
disc:[["What is the difference between a bank and a credit union?","Credit unions are member-owned and often have lower fees."],["Why is direct deposit safer than cash?","It cannot be lost or stolen on the way, and it arrives on time."],["What should you do if your debit card is lost?","Call the bank right away to lock it."],["How often should you check your balance?","At least once a week, or before big purchases."],["Who can help you open an account?","Social worker, case manager, payee, bank staff."]],
take:"A bank account keeps your money safe. Checking is for spending, savings is for later, and always know your balance before you swipe.",
tryit:"If you have an account, check your balance this week. If you do not, ask staff what you would need to open one."},

{t:"Debit Cards vs. Credit Cards",
goal:"Today we will learn the difference between debit cards, credit cards, and prepaid cards so we can use each one safely.",
warm:["What kind of cards do you see people use at the store?","Have you ever used a card to pay for something? How did it work?","Do you prefer paying with cash or a card? Why?"],
terms:[
["Debit card","a card that takes money straight out of your bank account when you use it.","You buy a $5 lunch with your debit card. Right away, $5 leaves your checking account. You are spending your own money.","What happens if there is no money in the account?"],
["Credit card","a card that lets you borrow money from a lender that you must pay back later, often with interest.","You buy a $5 lunch with a credit card. The bank pays, and you owe the bank $5 when the bill comes.","What is the biggest danger of a credit card?"],
["Prepaid card","a card you load with money ahead of time, like a gift card you can reload.","Some benefits come on a prepaid card. You can only spend what is loaded on it, so you cannot overspend.","Why might a prepaid card be helpful for someone learning to budget?"],
["PIN","a secret four-digit number that protects your card.","When you use your debit card at a store, you type your PIN. If someone knows your PIN and has your card, they can take your money.","Who should know your PIN?"],
["Fraud","when someone steals or uses your money or card information without permission.","Someone copies your card at a gas pump and buys $200 of things online. That is fraud. Call your bank right away.","What should you do if you see a charge you did not make?"]],
story:"Tony let his friend borrow his debit card to pick up cigarettes for him and told him the PIN. A week later, Tony's account was empty. His friend had used the card several times. Tony was embarrassed, but he called his bank. The bank shut off the card and sent a new one. Tony learned that even friends should not have his PIN.",
sq:["What mistake did Tony make?","How did Tony fix the problem?","Why do you think Tony trusted his friend?","What would you say if a friend asked for your card and PIN?"],
act:{n:"Which Card Would You Use?",m:"None",s:["Read a situation and have the group vote: debit, credit, prepaid, or cash.","Buying groceries with money you have. (Debit or cash.)","An emergency car repair when you have no money. (Maybe credit, but only with a plan to pay.)","Giving a teen spending money. (Prepaid.)","Buying from a website you have never heard of. (Be careful. A credit card has more fraud protection, or do not buy.)","Buying a soda at a gas station. (Cash or debit.)","Ask each resident to explain their vote."]},
d:["Which situation caused the most disagreement?","What did you learn from someone else's answer?","Which card feels the safest for you right now?"],
disc:[["Why do many people overspend with credit cards but not cash?","Cash feels real; a card feels like it is not your money."],["What are good ways to protect your card?","Never share your PIN, cover the keypad, keep the card in a safe place."],["What should you do if your card is stolen?","Call the bank right away and tell staff."],["Is it ever OK to let someone use your card?","Generally no; ask staff or your payee for help instead."],["How can you check that nobody is using your card?","Check your balance and statements often."]],
take:"Debit spends your own money, credit borrows money, and prepaid only spends what is loaded. Never share your PIN, not even with friends.",
tryit:"Make sure your PIN is not written on your card or kept in your wallet next to it."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Money & Budgeting", [
{t:"Spotting and Stopping Scams",
goal:"Today we will learn how scammers try to trick people out of their money and how to protect ourselves.",
warm:["Have you ever gotten a strange phone call or text asking for money or information?","Why do you think scammers often target older adults and people living on fixed incomes?","What would you do if someone called and said you won a prize?"],
terms:[
["Scam","a trick used to steal your money or personal information.","A caller says, This is the IRS, you owe $500, pay now with gift cards or you will be arrested. That is a scam. The real IRS never asks for gift cards.","What scams have you heard about on the news or from friends?"],
["Personal information","private facts about you that someone could use to steal from you.","Your Social Security number, bank card number, PIN, date of birth, and Medicare number are personal information. Never give them to a caller you did not call.","Which piece of your personal information do you think is most important to protect?"],
["Phishing","a fake email, text, or message made to look real so you will click a link or share information.","A text says, Your bank account is locked, click here. The link goes to a fake website that steals your login.","How can you tell if a text message might be fake?"],
["Pressure tactics","when a scammer rushes or scares you so you do not have time to think.","Act now or you will lose your benefits! Do not tell anyone! These words are a big red flag.","Why do scammers want you to keep it a secret?"],
["Verify","to check that something is true before you act.","If someone says they are from Social Security, hang up and call the real number on your card or letter, or ask staff to help you check.","Who here could help you verify a call or letter?"]],
story:"Ms. Bernice got a call from someone who said, Grandma, it's me, I'm in jail and need $800 for bail. Please don't tell Mom. He sounded upset. Ms. Bernice almost went to the store to buy gift cards. Then she remembered a group like this one. She hung up and called her grandson's real phone number. He answered. He was at work and was fine. It was a scam.",
sq:["What did the scammer pretend to be?","What pressure tactics did the scammer use?","What smart thing did Ms. Bernice do?","How do you think she felt after she found out it was a scam?"],
act:{n:"Scam or Safe?",m:"None",s:["Explain: I will read a message. Say SCAM or SAFE, then tell us why.","A text says you won a $1,000 Walmart gift card, click this link. (Scam.)","Your doctor's office calls to remind you of an appointment and does not ask for money. (Safe.)","A caller says your Social Security will stop unless you pay with gift cards. (Scam.)","A Facebook friend messages, I'm stuck overseas, send money. (Scam. Call them on their real number.)","A letter from your pharmacy with your real name about a refill. (Likely safe, but check if unsure.)","Someone at the door says they are from the power company and need cash now. (Scam.)"]},
d:["Which one was hardest to decide?","What words or phrases kept showing up in the scams?","What is your plan if you get one of these calls?"],
disc:[["What are the biggest red flags of a scam?","Asking for gift cards, wire transfers, secrecy, urgency, prizes you did not enter."],["Why should you never pay anyone with gift cards?","Real companies and government offices do not take gift cards; once sent the money is gone."],["What should you do if you already gave information to a scammer?","Tell staff right away, call the bank, it is not your fault."],["Why might someone be embarrassed to report a scam?","Shame; remind them scammers fool smart people every day."],["What is a simple sentence you can say to end a scam call?","I don't do business over the phone. Goodbye. Then hang up."]],
take:"If it sounds too good to be true, or someone rushes you to pay, it is probably a scam. Hang up, do not click, and ask someone you trust.",
tryit:"Practice saying: I don't give information over the phone. Goodbye. Use it the next time an unknown caller asks for anything."},

{t:"Saving Money and Emergency Funds",
goal:"Today we will learn why saving money matters, how to start saving even a little, and what an emergency fund is.",
warm:["What is something you saved up for in your life?","Is it hard or easy for you to save money? Why?","If you had $100 saved right now, what would you do with it?"],
terms:[
["Saving","putting money aside instead of spending it, so you have it later.","If you put $5 in a jar every week, in one year you will have $260.","What is one place you could keep saved money safe?"],
["Emergency fund","money saved only for surprises, like a lost phone, broken glasses, or a sudden bill.","Kim had $75 in her emergency fund. When her glasses broke, she paid for new ones without borrowing.","What kinds of emergencies cost money?"],
["Savings goal","a specific thing you are saving for, with an amount and a date.","I want to save $60 for new work shoes by March. That is a savings goal.","What is a savings goal you could set?"],
["Pay yourself first","putting money into savings as soon as you get money, before spending on anything else.","When your check comes, take $10 out for savings right away, before shopping.","Why do you think paying yourself first works better than saving what is left over?"],
["Small change adds up","the idea that little amounts saved often become big amounts over time.","Skipping one $2 soda a day saves about $60 a month.","What small daily purchase could you cut to save money?"]],
story:"Luis never thought he could save because he only had a little money each month. His staff member gave him a jar and said, Just put in your change. Luis started dropping in quarters and a few dollar bills. After six months he counted $93. When his phone charger broke, he used the jar instead of asking anyone. He felt proud and in control.",
sq:["Why did Luis think he could not save?","How did he start?","What did Luis use his savings for?","How do you think saving changed the way Luis saw himself?"],
act:{n:"The Savings Challenge",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Ask each resident to pick a small amount they could save each week: $1, $2, or $5.","Help them multiply it: weekly amount times 52 weeks equals the total for one year. ($1 = $52, $2 = $104, $5 = $260.)","Ask each person to name one thing they would do with that total.","Ask each resident to write their savings goal at the top of a paper: I will save ___ each week for ___.","Invite volunteers to read their goal out loud."]},
d:["Were you surprised how much small amounts add up?","What will make it hard to stick to your goal?","Who could help you stay on track?"],
disc:[["Why do many people not have any savings?","Low income, emergencies, never learned how, spending on wants."],["Where is the safest place to keep savings?","A savings account or with a trusted payee; not under a mattress."],["How do you avoid spending your savings on wants?","Keep it separate, give it a name, make it harder to reach."],["How does having savings affect stress?","Less worry, more peace, more choices."],["What is one reason worth breaking into your emergency fund?","A real emergency like medicine, glasses, or a needed bill; not a sale."]],
take:"Saving is not about how much you have. It is about the habit. Even $1 a week is a start.",
tryit:"Find a container or envelope this week, label it SAVINGS, and put in your first dollar or some change."},

{t:"Smart Shopping and Comparing Prices",
goal:"Today we will learn how to get the most for our money when we shop by comparing prices, sizes, and brands.",
warm:["Where do you like to shop?","Have you ever found a really good deal? What was it?","Do you usually buy name brands or store brands?"],
terms:[
["Store brand","a product made with the store's own name, usually cheaper than the famous brand.","Walmart's Great Value cereal is often about half the price of the name-brand cereal and tastes very close.","What store brand products have you tried?"],
["Unit price","the price for each ounce, pound, or item, shown on the shelf tag.","A small shampoo costs $3 for 10 ounces, which is 30 cents an ounce. A big one costs $5 for 25 ounces, which is 20 cents an ounce. The big one is the better deal.","Have you ever looked at the small numbers on a shelf tag?"],
["Comparison shopping","checking prices at more than one place before you buy.","Deodorant may cost $5 at the gas station and $2.50 at the dollar store.","Where would you go to compare prices?"],
["Shopping list","a written list of what you need before you go to the store.","People with a list spend less because they buy what they planned instead of grabbing extras.","What would be on your shopping list this week?"],
["Bulk buying","buying a larger amount at once to pay less per item.","Buying a 12-pack of toilet paper costs less per roll than buying one roll at a time. But only buy bulk if you will use it and have space.","When might buying in bulk be a bad idea?"]],
story:"Two friends, Andre and Kim, needed the same things: shampoo, toothpaste, chips, and socks. Andre went to the corner gas station and grabbed everything there. He spent $27. Kim made a list, went to the dollar store and bought store brands. She spent $12. They both had what they needed, but Kim had $15 left over.",
sq:["Why did Andre spend more money?","What three things did Kim do to save?","What could Kim do with the $15 she saved?","Which friend are you more like when you shop?"],
act:{n:"Which Is the Better Deal?",m:"Two or three real products or pictures with prices, or write examples on a board",s:["Write these pairs on a board: Cereal A: $4.00 for 12 oz. Cereal B: $5.00 for 20 oz.","Help the group find the price per ounce: A is about 33 cents per ounce, B is 25 cents per ounce. B is the better deal.","Do another: Soap 1 bar for $1.25, or 4 bars for $3.50. (4-pack is about 88 cents a bar.)","Do another: Name-brand toothpaste $4.50, store brand $1.75.","Ask residents to think of things they buy often and where they could find them cheaper."]},
d:["Was the bigger package always the better deal?","What surprised you?","Will you look at unit prices next time you shop?"],
disc:[["Why do convenience stores charge more?","You pay for being close and open late."],["How does shopping hungry or upset change what you buy?","You buy more, especially snacks and wants."],["Are cheaper things always better?","No; sometimes quality matters, like shoes or a coat that lasts."],["How can staff or friends help you shop smart?","Go together, help make a list, point out deals."],["What is one item you buy often that you could get cheaper?","Any honest answer, help them think of a cheaper place."]],
take:"Make a list, compare prices, and try store brands. Smart shopping means more money stays in your pocket.",
tryit:"Next time you go to the store, compare the store brand and the name brand of one item you buy and see the difference."},

{t:"Using Coupons, Sales, and Discounts",
goal:"Today we will learn how coupons, sales, and discounts work, and how to use them without being tricked into spending more.",
warm:["Have you ever used a coupon? What for?","What is the best sale you remember?","Do you think sales always save you money? Why or why not?"],
terms:[
["Coupon","a paper or digital ticket that takes money off the price of an item.","A coupon that says $1 off Tide means if the detergent costs $9, you pay $8.","Where can people find coupons?"],
["Sale price","a lower price the store offers for a short time.","Was $10, now $6. You save $4 if you needed that item anyway.","What makes a sale a good deal?"],
["Discount","an amount taken off the regular price, often shown as a percent.","25 percent off a $20 shirt means you save $5 and pay $15.","Have you ever gotten a senior, student, or veteran discount?"],
["BOGO","buy one, get one free, or buy one, get one half off.","If toothpaste is BOGO, you get two for the price of one, which is great if you will use both.","When is BOGO not a good deal?"],
["Store app or loyalty card","a free card or phone app from a store that gives you extra deals.","A Dollar General or Kroger app may give $5 off a $25 purchase or free items.","What stores do you shop at that might have a free rewards card?"]],
story:"Danielle saw a sign that said Spend $50, save $10. She only needed $20 worth of things, but she wanted the $10 off. She grabbed more stuff to reach $50. At home she realized she spent $40 total, twice what she planned, on things she did not need. The sale saved her $10 but cost her $20 more than she meant to spend.",
sq:["Did the sale really save Danielle money?","What trick did the store use?","What should Danielle have asked herself?","Has a sale ever tricked you into buying more?"],
act:{n:"Do the Math",m:"Board, paper and pens",s:["Write: Shirt $20, 50% off. Ask: How much do you pay? ($10)","Write: Soap $3, coupon $0.75 off. Ask: How much? ($2.25)","Write: Chips $4, BOGO free. Ask: How much for two bags? ($4, $2 each)","Write: Spend $30 get $5 off, but you only need $18 worth. Ask: Is it worth it? (No, you would spend $25 instead of $18.)","Let residents take turns solving. Help with math as needed and celebrate correct answers."]},
d:["Which problem was the trickiest?","What did you learn about sales?","How can you tell if a deal is real?"],
disc:[["Why do stores have sales?","To get you in the store and buy more."],["What question should you ask before buying something on sale?","Would I buy this if it was not on sale?"],["How can coupons help someone on a tight budget?","Lower costs on needs like soap and food."],["What are the downsides of chasing deals?","Buying things you do not need, wasting time and money."],["Who here knows a good place for deals in Chattanooga?","Let residents share; dollar stores, discount grocery, thrift stores."]],
take:"A sale only saves you money if you were going to buy that item anyway. Ask: Would I buy this at full price?",
tryit:"Before you shop this week, check if the store has a free app or rewards card, or ask staff to help you find coupons."},

{t:"Paying Bills on Time",
goal:"Today we will learn how to keep track of bills, why paying on time matters, and what to do if we cannot pay.",
warm:["What bills do people usually have each month?","Have you ever forgotten to pay something? What happened?","How do you remember important dates?"],
terms:[
["Bill","a paper or message telling you how much you owe and when.","Your phone company sends a bill that says Amount due: $35, Due date: the 15th.","What information is on a bill?"],
["Due date","the last day to pay a bill without getting a late fee.","If the due date is May 10 and you pay May 12, you are late.","Why should you pay a few days before the due date?"],
["Autopay","setting a bill to be paid automatically from your bank account each month.","Your phone bill comes out of your checking account on the 5th every month without you doing anything.","What is good about autopay? What could go wrong?"],
["Shut-off notice","a warning that a service will be turned off if you do not pay.","A letter says, Your phone will be disconnected on the 20th unless you pay $70. Do not ignore it. Get help that day.","Who could you show a shut-off notice to here?"],
["Bill calendar","a simple calendar where you write when each bill is due.","On the 1st: phone. On the 10th: streaming service. On the 15th: storage unit.","Where could you keep a bill calendar so you see it?"]],
story:"Kevin had three monthly bills, but he kept the papers in a pile and never knew when anything was due. His phone got shut off twice. A staff member helped him make a bill calendar and taped it to his wall. He circled due dates in red. Now he checks it every Sunday. He has not had a late fee in four months.",
sq:["What was Kevin's problem?","What tool helped him?","Why does checking every Sunday help?","What other tool could Kevin use?"],
act:{n:"Make a Bill Calendar",m:"Blank one-month calendar printouts or paper, pens",s:["Hand out a blank calendar or have residents draw boxes for days 1 to 31.","Ask each resident to write one or two real or pretend bills and their due dates.","Show them how to write a reminder 3 days before each due date.","Ask: Where will you keep this so you see it every day?","Encourage them to take it to their room."]},
d:["How many bills did people come up with?","Why write a reminder 3 days early?","What would you do if two bills are due the same week?"],
disc:[["What happens when bills are paid late?","Late fees, shut-offs, bad credit, stress."],["What should you do if you know you cannot pay a bill?","Call the company before the due date and ask for a payment plan or extension; ask staff."],["Why do people avoid looking at bills?","Fear, stress, shame."],["What are the pros and cons of autopay?","Pro: never late. Con: could overdraft if the money is not there."],["Who helps you with your bills now?","Payee, family, staff; encourage asking for help."]],
take:"Know your due dates, pay a little early, and if you cannot pay, call before the due date, not after.",
tryit:"Write down every bill you have and its due date this week. Ask staff to help if you are not sure."},

{t:"Understanding Your Benefits",
goal:"Today we will learn common words about disability and income benefits so we can understand our letters and ask good questions.",
warm:["Have you ever gotten a letter from Social Security or another office that was confusing?","Who helps you understand official mail?","What questions do you have about benefits in general?"],
terms:[
["Benefits","money or help a person gets from a government program.","SSI, SSDI, SNAP (food stamps), and Medicaid (TennCare) are benefits that help people pay for basic needs and health care.","What benefits have you heard of?"],
["SSI and SSDI","SSI is Supplemental Security Income, based on need. SSDI is Social Security Disability Insurance, based on past work.","Someone who worked for years then became disabled may get SSDI. Someone with little work history and low income may get SSI.","Why do you think there are two different programs?"],
["Report changes","telling the benefits office when something in your life changes.","If you get a job, move, or have more money in the bank, you may need to report it. Not reporting can cause an overpayment you have to pay back.","Who could help you report a change?"],
["Overpayment","when an office paid you more than it should have and asks for money back.","A letter says you were overpaid $400 because a job was not reported. You can ask for a payment plan or appeal.","What would you do if you got an overpayment letter?"],
["Appeal","asking an office to look at a decision again because you think it is wrong.","If your benefits are cut and you disagree, you usually have a short time, often 60 days, to appeal. Some deadlines are only 10 days to keep benefits going.","Why is it important to read letters right away?"]],
story:"Mr. Carl got a letter from Social Security that said his check would be lowered. He put it in a drawer because it scared him. Two months later his check was smaller and the time to appeal was almost gone. When he finally showed the letter to the social worker, she helped him call. It turned out there was a mistake, and it was fixed, but he lost two months of money while he waited.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Carl put the letter away?","What happened because he waited?","Who helped him fix it?","What will you do the next time you get an official letter?"],
act:{n:"Read the Letter",m:"A sample benefits letter written on paper or the board (make up one with no real names)",s:["Write a short pretend letter: Dear Member, Your monthly payment will change from $943 to $850 starting next month because of income reported. If you disagree, you have 60 days to appeal. Call 1-800-772-1213.","Read it slowly with the group.","Ask: What is changing? Why? What can you do? How long do you have? Who do you call?","Circle the important parts together: amount, reason, deadline, phone number.","Explain: These four things are what to look for in any official letter."]},
d:["What were the four important parts of the letter?","Was the letter harder or easier to understand when we broke it down?","Who will you show letters to in the future?"],
disc:[["Why do official letters feel so stressful?","Hard words, fear of losing money."],["What should you never do with an official letter?","Ignore it or throw it away."],["What information should you keep in a safe place?","Award letters, ID, benefit cards, important papers."],["Who at Rose of Sharon can help with benefits questions?","The social worker or staff; we do not give legal advice in group."],["How can you protect your benefits from scammers?","Never give your Social Security or benefit card number to callers."]],
take:"Read official letters right away, look for the amount, reason, deadline, and phone number, and ask the social worker for help. Never wait.",
tryit:"If you have any official letters you do not understand, bring them to the social worker this week."},

{t:"Reading Bills and Receipts",
goal:"Today we will practice reading bills and receipts so we know what we paid for and can catch mistakes.",
warm:["Do you usually keep your receipts or throw them away?","Have you ever been charged the wrong price at a store?","What does the word total mean to you?"],
terms:[
["Receipt","a paper or email that shows what you bought, what it cost, and how you paid.","After buying snacks, your receipt shows chips $1.50, soda $1.25, tax $0.20, total $2.95.","Why should you keep receipts?"],
["Subtotal","the price of everything before tax.","Your items add up to $10.00. That is the subtotal.","What comes after the subtotal on a receipt?"],
["Sales tax","extra money added to most purchases that goes to the state and city.","In Tennessee, sales tax is a little over 9 percent in most places, so a $10 item is about $10.93.","Why do you think the price on the shelf is different from what you pay?"],
["Total","the final amount you pay, including tax.","Subtotal $10.00 plus tax $0.93 equals total $10.93.","What should you do if the total looks too high?"],
["Refund or return","getting your money back when you bring an item back to the store.","Most stores need the receipt and give you 30 days. No receipt may mean store credit only or no return.","Have you ever returned something?"]],
story:"Shay bought three things at the store and the cashier said $24. Shay thought that was too much. She looked at the receipt and saw she was charged twice for the same $8 bottle of lotion. She went back to the counter, showed the receipt, and got $8 back. If she had thrown the receipt away, she would have lost that money.",
sq:["How did Shay know something was wrong?","What did the receipt show?","What would have happened without the receipt?","How would you feel speaking up at the counter?"],
act:{n:"Find the Mistake",m:"A pretend receipt written on the board",s:["Write a pretend receipt: Bread $2.50, Milk $3.25, Milk $3.25, Eggs $2.75, Subtotal $11.75, Tax $1.08, Total $12.83.","Say: This person bought bread, ONE milk, and eggs. Can you find the mistake?","Let the group find it: milk was charged twice.","Ask: What should the subtotal be? ($8.50)","Practice what to say at the counter: Excuse me, I think I was charged twice for milk. Here is my receipt."]},
d:["How long did it take to find the mistake?","How would you feel going back to the counter?","What would you say?"],
disc:[["Why do mistakes happen on receipts?","Scanner errors, prices not updated, double scans."],["Where can you keep receipts so they do not get lost?","An envelope, a folder, a jar by the door."],["How long should you keep receipts?","At least until you know you will not return it, about a month; longer for big items."],["What is the difference between the shelf price and what you pay?","Tax, and sometimes the shelf price is wrong."],["Why is it OK to speak up about a mistake?","It is your money; stores fix mistakes every day."]],
take:"Always look at your receipt before you leave. Check the items and the total, and keep it in case you need a return.",
tryit:"The next time you buy something, read your receipt line by line before you leave the store."},

{t:"Counting Money and Making Change",
goal:"Today we will practice counting money and checking our change so we do not get shorted.",
warm:["Do you prefer paying with cash or a card?","Have you ever been given the wrong change?","What coins can you name?"],
terms:[
["Bills and coins","paper money and metal money.","Bills are $1, $5, $10, $20. Coins are penny 1 cent, nickel 5 cents, dime 10 cents, quarter 25 cents.","Which coin is worth more, a nickel or a dime?"],
["Making change","the money you get back when you pay more than the price.","If something costs $3.50 and you pay with a $5 bill, your change is $1.50.","How much change would you get from $10 for a $6 item?"],
["Counting up","a trick to check change by counting from the price up to what you paid.","Price $3.50, you paid $5. Count: $3.50 plus two quarters is $4.00, plus a dollar is $5.00. Change is $1.50.","Why might counting up be easier than subtracting?"],
["Short-changed","when someone gives you less change than you should get.","You pay $20 for a $12 item and only get $3 back instead of $8.","What would you say if you were short-changed?"],
["Rounding","making a number simpler to estimate quickly.","$4.89 is about $5. $2.10 is about $2. Rounding helps you guess the total before you get to the register.","How could rounding help you at the store?"]],
story:"Mr. James paid for his lunch, which was $7.25, with a $20 bill. The cashier was busy and gave him back $2.75. Mr. James counted it right away and said, Excuse me, I gave you a twenty. The cashier checked and said, You are right, I'm sorry, and gave him $10 more. Mr. James almost walked away without counting.",
sq:["How much change should Mr. James have gotten?","What did he do right?","Why do you think the cashier made a mistake?","Would you feel comfortable speaking up like Mr. James?"],
act:{n:"Store Counter Practice",m:"Play money or real coins and bills, or draw them on paper",s:["Set up a pretend store. One resident is the cashier, one is the shopper.","Use these prices: soda $1.75, chips $2.25, lotion $4.50, socks $6.00.","Shopper picks one item and pays with a $5, $10, or $20.","Cashier counts up the change out loud. The group checks the answer.","Switch roles so several people get a turn."]},
d:["Was it easier to be the cashier or the shopper?","Did counting up help?","What will you do now when you get change?"],
disc:[["Why should you count your change before walking away?","Once you leave, it is hard to prove a mistake."],["How can you protect your cash when out?","Keep it in a front pocket, do not flash it, only bring what you need."],["What could help someone who struggles with math at the store?","A calculator on a phone, rounding, asking a trusted staff member."],["Is it rude to count your change in front of a cashier?","No, it is smart."],["Why might some people avoid using cash?","Easy to lose, hard to track; others prefer it to avoid overspending."]],
take:"Always count your change before you walk away. Count up from the price to what you paid.",
tryit:"The next time you pay with cash, count your change at the counter."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Money & Budgeting", [
{t:"Payday Loans and Rent-to-Own Traps",
goal:"Today we will learn how payday loans, title loans, and rent-to-own stores work, and why they can cost much more than they seem.",
warm:["Have you seen signs that say Cash Today or No Credit Check?","Why do you think there are so many of those stores in some neighborhoods?","Have you or someone you know ever needed money fast?"],
terms:[
["Payday loan","a small, short loan you pay back on your next payday, with very high fees.","You borrow $300 and must pay back $345 in two weeks. If you cannot, you pay another $45 just to wait two more weeks, and you still owe the $300.","What happens if someone keeps rolling over a payday loan?"],
["Title loan","a loan where you give the lender the title to your car. If you do not pay, they take your car.","Borrow $800 using your car. Miss payments, and you lose a car worth $4,000.","Why is a title loan risky?"],
["Rent-to-own","renting furniture or electronics with weekly payments until you own it.","A $500 TV at $25 a week for 72 weeks costs $1,800. Miss a payment and they can take it back.","How much more than the real price does that TV cost?"],
["Debt trap","when a loan is set up so it is very hard to ever pay off.","Many payday loan customers take out loan after loan for months, paying more in fees than they borrowed.","Why do you think these businesses make so much money?"],
["Better options","safer ways to get help with money in an emergency.","Asking a credit union for a small loan, a payment plan with the company, help from a church or charity, or using an emergency fund.","What local places could help in an emergency?"]],
story:"Tamika needed $250 for a car repair to get to work. She went to a payday lender. In two weeks she could not pay the $295, so she paid $45 to extend it. This happened six times. She paid $270 in fees and still owed the $250. Finally a coworker told her about a credit union that gave her a small loan at low interest. She paid off the payday loan and promised herself never again.",
sq:["How much did Tamika pay in fees?","Why could she not get out?","What finally helped her?","What would you tell a friend who is thinking about a payday loan?"],
act:{n:"Add Up the Real Cost",m:"Board, markers",s:["Write: Payday loan $200, fee $30 every two weeks.","Ask: If you roll it over 5 times, how much in fees? ($150) Plus the $200 still owed = $350 total.","Write: Rent-to-own couch, $30 a week for 60 weeks. Ask: Total? ($1,800) A similar couch at a store might be $500.","Write: Save $20 a week for 25 weeks = $500. Ask: Which way is better?","Talk about how saving first, even slowly, costs far less."]},
d:["Which number shocked you most?","Why do people still use these stores?","What is one better choice you learned today?"],
disc:[["Who do payday lenders target?","People with low income, no savings, bad credit."],["What questions should you ask before signing any loan?","Total cost, interest rate, fees, what happens if I miss a payment."],["Is it ever OK to use one of these?","Rarely; only after trying every other option; best to ask for help first."],["How can an emergency fund protect you from these traps?","You have your own money, so you do not need to borrow."],["Who at Rose of Sharon could help you think it through first?","Social worker, case manager, payee."]],
take:"Fast cash costs the most. Before you borrow, ask for help and add up what it will really cost you in the end.",
tryit:"If you see a Cash Today sign this week, figure out in your head how much it might really cost."},

{t:"Protecting Your Identity",
goal:"Today we will learn what identity theft is and simple ways to protect our personal information.",
warm:["What important cards do you carry or keep in your room?","Who do you think should be allowed to see your Social Security number?","Have you heard of someone having their identity stolen?"],
terms:[
["Identity theft","when someone uses your name or personal information to steal money or open accounts.","A thief uses your Social Security number to open a phone plan. You get a bill for $600 you never agreed to.","How do you think thieves get people's information?"],
["Social Security number","the nine-digit number the government gives you. It is one of the most important numbers to protect.","Keep your Social Security card in a safe place, not your wallet. Only give the number to places that truly need it, like a bank or doctor.","Where is a safe place to keep your Social Security card?"],
["Password","a secret word or code that protects your phone, email, or accounts.","Using 1234 or your birthday is easy to guess. Something like BlueRose!22 is much harder.","What makes a password strong?"],
["Shredding","tearing or cutting papers into small pieces so no one can read them.","Old bank statements, medical bills, and pre-approved credit card letters should be shredded, not just thrown away.","What papers should be shredded?"],
["Report it","telling the right people right away when you think your information was stolen.","Tell staff, call your bank, and you can report it at IdentityTheft.gov.","Why is it important to report quickly?"]],
story:"Robert left his wallet on a table in the day room with his Social Security card inside. Later it was gone. Weeks later, he got letters about credit cards he never opened. The social worker helped him call the bank, report it, and freeze his credit. It took months to clean up. Now he keeps his card locked up and only carries his ID.",
sq:["What mistake made it easy to steal Robert's identity?","What signs showed his identity was stolen?","What steps did he take to fix it?","What does Robert do differently now?"],
act:{n:"Wallet Check",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Draw a big wallet on the board.","Ask the group to name what people often keep in a wallet: ID, bank card, Social Security card, Medicare card, cash, PIN written on paper, photos, receipts.","For each item, ask: Should this be in your wallet every day?","Circle the items that are safe to carry (ID, one card, small cash). Cross out items that should stay locked up (Social Security card, PIN, extra cards).","Ask residents to think about what is in their own wallet or purse right now."]},
d:["Is there anything you carry that you should keep at home?","Where could you lock up important papers?","What surprised you in this activity?"],
disc:[["Who should you never give your information to?","Callers you did not call, people on social media, strangers, texts asking for it."],["How can a phone or social media put your identity at risk?","Posting your birthday, address, or full name publicly; clicking links."],["What should you do with mail that has your information on it?","Shred or tear it before throwing it away."],["Why might someone close to you be a risk?","Sadly, many identity thefts are by people we know; keep information private."],["What is the first thing you would do if your card was stolen?","Tell staff and call the bank right away."]],
take:"Keep your Social Security card and PIN locked up, never share your information with strangers, and report anything strange right away.",
tryit:"Check your wallet or purse tonight. Take out anything that should be kept in a safe place."},

{t:"Money and Emotions",
goal:"Today we will talk about how our feelings affect the way we spend, and how to make calm money choices.",
warm:["When you feel stressed, what do you like to do or buy?","Do you remember a time you spent money because you felt happy or sad?","Growing up, was money a calm or stressful topic in your home?"],
terms:[
["Emotional spending","buying things to change how we feel instead of because we need them.","After a hard phone call, Lisa went straight to the store and bought $30 of snacks and nail polish to feel better.","What feelings lead you to spend?"],
["Trigger","something that sets off a feeling or urge.","Getting paid, a fight with family, boredom, or seeing an ad can be triggers to spend.","What are your money triggers?"],
["Pause","stopping to think before acting.","Before buying, wait 10 minutes, walk around, or ask: How will I feel about this tomorrow?","What could you do during a pause?"],
["Free comfort","ways to feel better that do not cost money.","A walk, calling a friend, music, prayer, a shower, drawing, or talking to staff.","What is your favorite free way to feel better?"],
["Money shame","feeling bad about yourself because of money mistakes or not having enough.","Feeling embarrassed about debt can make people hide bills instead of getting help.","How can we be kinder to ourselves about money?"]],
story:"Every time Brian talked to his brother on the phone, they argued. Afterward, Brian would walk to the store and spend money on lottery tickets and energy drinks. By the end of the month, he was broke and more upset. A staff member noticed the pattern and asked, What if you took a walk around the building first? Brian tried it. Some days he still went to the store, but he spent half as much.",
sq:["What was Brian's trigger?","What was he trying to feel when he spent money?","What did the staff member suggest?","Did Brian have to be perfect to make progress?"],
act:{n:"Feelings and Spending Chart",m:"Board or paper",s:["Draw three columns: Feeling, What I Might Buy, Free Thing I Could Do Instead.","Ask the group for feelings: bored, sad, angry, lonely, happy, stressed.","For each feeling, ask what people might buy, then brainstorm a free option.","Example: Bored, buy snacks, play cards with someone.","Have residents pick one feeling that is true for them and write their own free option."]},
d:["Which feeling leads to the most spending in this group?","Which free idea do you like best?","Will you try it this week?"],
disc:[["Why does buying something feel good for a moment?","Quick reward; the feeling fades fast."],["How do you feel after emotional spending?","Guilt, regret, still have the original feeling."],["How can you tell if you are spending because of a feeling?","Ask: Am I hungry, bored, angry, lonely, or tired?"],["How can friends or staff help?","Notice patterns kindly, offer company, go for a walk together."],["What is one kind thing you can say to yourself after a money mistake?","I can learn from this and do better next time."]],
take:"Our feelings are real, but they do not have to control our money. Pause, name the feeling, and try a free comfort first.",
tryit:"The next time you want to buy something, ask yourself: What am I feeling right now?"},

{t:"Gifts and Holidays on a Budget",
goal:"Today we will learn how to enjoy holidays and show love to people without spending more than we have.",
warm:["What is your favorite holiday and why?","What is the best gift you ever got that did not cost much?","Do holidays make you feel happy, stressed, or both?"],
terms:[
["Holiday budget","a set amount you plan to spend on gifts and celebrations.","Maria has $40 for Christmas. She plans $10 for each of her 3 grandkids and $10 for treats.","Why make a plan before the holidays?"],
["Homemade gift","a gift you make yourself.","A card with a handwritten note, a drawing, a poem, a bracelet, or cookies from the facility kitchen with permission.","What homemade gift could you make?"],
["Gift of time","giving your attention or help instead of something bought.","Calling your sister every Sunday, playing a game with a friend, or writing a letter.","Who would love a gift of your time?"],
["Pressure to spend","feeling like you have to buy expensive things to show love.","Ads say the perfect gift costs $100. But people remember how you made them feel more than the price.","Where does holiday pressure come from?"],
["Drawing names","a group plan where each person buys for only one person.","A family of 8 draws names, so each person buys one $15 gift instead of seven.","Could your family or friends try this?"]],
story:"Ms. Gloria felt sad because she could not afford gifts for her grandchildren. Her caregiver helped her write each grandchild a letter telling a story about when she was their age, and she drew a picture on each one. Years later, her oldest grandson told her he still had the letter in his drawer and read it when he missed her.",
sq:["Why was Ms. Gloria sad at first?","What gift did she give?","Why do you think the grandson kept the letter?","What does this story say about what makes a gift special?"],
act:{n:"Make a Card",m:"Paper or card stock, markers, crayons, envelopes if available",s:["Hand out paper and markers.","Ask each resident to think of one person they care about.","Have them fold the paper into a card and decorate the front.","Inside, ask them to write a memory or something they like about that person. Help with spelling if needed.","Offer to help them address and mail it, or keep it for a visit."]},
d:["Who did you make your card for?","How did it feel to make it?","Would you rather get a card like this or a store-bought gift?"],
disc:[["Why do holidays make some people feel lonely or stressed?","Missing family, money worries, memories."],["What are free ways to celebrate here at the facility?","Music, decorating, games, sharing stories, special meals."],["How can you say no to spending more than you have?","Be honest: This year I'm keeping it simple."],["What holiday traditions do you remember?","Let residents share; this builds community."],["How can we support each other during holidays?","Check on each other, include people who have no visitors."]],
take:"The best gifts are love, time, and kind words. Set a holiday budget and remember that you are enough.",
tryit:"Write or call one person this week just to tell them you are thinking of them."},

{t:"Planning for a Big Purchase",
goal:"Today we will learn how to plan and save for something that costs more than our usual budget.",
warm:["What is something big you have always wanted to buy?","Have you ever saved up for something big? What was it?","What usually gets in the way of saving for big things?"],
terms:[
["Big purchase","something that costs more than you can pay with one month of extra money.","A new phone, a mattress, a TV, glasses, or a bus pass for the year.","What big purchase is most important to you right now?"],
["Research","finding out about something before you buy it.","Looking up phone prices at three stores and reading reviews before choosing.","Who could help you research a purchase?"],
["Timeline","how long it will take to reach your goal.","If a phone costs $150 and you save $25 a month, your timeline is 6 months.","How long would it take you to save $100 if you saved $10 a month?"],
["Used or refurbished","something that was owned before but still works, often much cheaper.","A refurbished phone may cost $90 instead of $250 new, with a warranty.","What things are smart to buy used?"],
["Warranty","a promise from the seller to fix or replace something if it breaks.","A 1-year warranty means if your phone stops working in 8 months, they fix it free.","Why does a warranty matter on a big purchase?"]],
story:"DeShawn wanted a $200 tablet. His friend said he could get one at a rent-to-own store for $15 a week. DeShawn did the math: that was over $700! Instead, he looked online with staff and found a refurbished tablet for $110. He saved $20 a month and bought it in six months. He paid less than one-sixth of the rent-to-own price.",
sq:["What did DeShawn want?","Why did he say no to rent-to-own?","How did research help him?","How long did it take him to save?"],
act:{n:"My Big Purchase Plan",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Have each resident write at the top: I want to buy ____.","Under it, write: It costs about $____. (Help them estimate.)","Write: I can save $____ each month.","Divide the cost by the monthly amount together to find how many months.","Write one way to make it cheaper: used, store brand, sale, or waiting."]},
d:["What are people saving for?","Whose timeline is shortest? Longest?","What could make your timeline shorter?"],
disc:[["Why is it better to save first than to buy on credit?","No interest, no debt, less stress."],["What can keep you motivated while you save?","A picture of the item, a chart, telling a friend."],["How do you decide if a big purchase is worth it?","Need vs. want, how long you will use it, what you give up."],["What would you do if an emergency happened while saving?","Use the money for the emergency if needed and start again."],["What big purchase do you think would improve your daily life the most?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Big things are possible with a plan: know the price, research, save a set amount each month, and be patient.",
tryit:"Draw a simple chart with boxes for each month of your savings goal and color one in each time you save."},

{t:"Paychecks and Working Income",
goal:"Today we will learn how a paycheck works, why it is less than we expect, and how working can affect benefits.",
warm:["What jobs have you had in your life?","What did you buy with your very first paycheck?","What kind of work would you enjoy now, even part-time?"],
terms:[
["Gross pay","the total money you earn before anything is taken out.","If you work 20 hours at $10 an hour, your gross pay is $200.","How would you figure out your gross pay?"],
["Net pay","the money you actually get after taxes and other things are taken out. Some call it take-home pay.","Gross pay $200, minus taxes $25, equals net pay $175.","Why is net pay less than gross pay?"],
["Deductions","money taken out of your paycheck.","Federal tax, Social Security, Medicare, and sometimes insurance or uniforms are deductions.","What deductions have you seen on a pay stub?"],
["Pay stub","the paper or online page that shows your hours, pay, and deductions.","Your pay stub shows: Hours 20, Rate $10, Gross $200, Taxes $25, Net $175.","Why should you keep your pay stubs?"],
["Work incentives","rules that let people on disability benefits work some and still keep part of their benefits.","Social Security has programs like Ticket to Work. Always report work income and ask the social worker how a job affects your benefits.","Who can you ask about working and benefits?"]],
story:"Angela started a part-time job at a grocery store making $11 an hour. She worked 15 hours and expected $165. Her check was $148. She was upset and thought she was cheated. Her manager showed her the pay stub: $17 went to taxes. Angela also reported her job to Social Security with her social worker's help, so she did not get an overpayment later.",
sq:["Why was Angela's check smaller than she expected?","What did the pay stub show?","Why did Angela report her job?","How would you have felt in Angela's shoes?"],
act:{n:"Figure the Paycheck",m:"Board, paper, calculators if available",s:["Write: Job pays $12 an hour. You work 10 hours. What is the gross pay? ($120)","Write: About 10 percent goes to taxes. What is the net pay? ($108)","Write: You work 20 hours. Gross? ($240) Net? (about $216)","Ask: If you need $150 a month for your goals, how many hours would you need to work?","Let residents work in pairs and share answers."]},
d:["Was the math easy or hard?","What surprised you about taxes?","Would working part-time help your goals?"],
disc:[["Besides money, what do people get from working?","Purpose, routine, friends, pride, skills."],["What are good jobs or volunteer roles for people getting back into work?","Let residents share ideas; volunteering is a good start."],["Why is reporting work income important?","To avoid overpayments and losing benefits unexpectedly."],["What would you do if your paycheck looked wrong?","Look at the pay stub and ask your manager politely."],["What skills do you already have that could help at a job?","Encourage strengths: kindness, cooking, cleaning, being on time."]],
take:"Your take-home pay is less than your hourly pay times hours because of deductions. Read your pay stub and always report work income.",
tryit:"Think of one kind of work or volunteering you might enjoy, and tell a staff member about it."},

{t:"Borrowing and Lending with Friends",
goal:"Today we will talk about the risks of borrowing and lending money with friends, family, and other residents, and how to set healthy money boundaries.",
warm:["Has anyone ever borrowed money from you and not paid it back?","How does it feel to ask someone for money?","Do you think friends should lend each other money?"],
terms:[
["Lending","giving someone money that they promise to pay back.","You lend your friend $10 for cigarettes until Friday.","What makes lending to a friend risky?"],
["Boundary","a limit you set to protect yourself.","My boundary is that I do not lend money, but I am happy to share a snack.","What is one money boundary you could set?"],
["Peer pressure","feeling pushed by people around you to do something.","Come on, everybody else is putting in $5 for pizza. If you do not have it to spare, you can say no.","How can you say no to peer pressure?"],
["Saying no kindly","refusing in a clear but respectful way.","I care about you, but I can't lend money. Is there another way I can help?","How would you say no to a friend who asks to borrow money?"],
["Gift vs. loan","a gift is given freely; a loan must be paid back.","If you cannot afford to lose the money, do not lend it. Only give what you are OK never getting back.","Why is it smart to think of a loan as a gift?"]],
story:"Two residents, Pam and Tina, were good friends. Tina asked Pam for $20 and promised to pay her back on the first of the month. The first came and went. Pam felt angry but did not want to start a fight. The friendship got tense. Finally Pam told Tina calmly, I need my $20 back, can you pay $10 now and $10 next month? Tina agreed. Pam decided next time she would not lend more than she could lose.",
sq:["How did the loan affect the friendship?","How did Pam handle it?","What would you have done?","What did Pam decide for the future?"],
act:{n:"Practice Saying No",m:"None",s:["Pair residents up or have volunteers come up.","One person asks: Hey, can I borrow $10 until next week?","The other person practices saying no kindly. Use the script: I care about you, but I don't lend money. Is there another way I can help?","Switch roles.","Try harder versions: the person begs, says they will be in trouble, or says, I thought we were friends."]},
d:["How did it feel to say no?","Which version was the hardest to handle?","What words worked best?"],
disc:[["Why can money hurt friendships?","Broken promises, resentment, feeling used."],["What should you do if someone keeps asking you for money?","Set a boundary, tell staff if you feel pressured or unsafe."],["Is it OK to ask staff for help with money pressure?","Yes; that is what they are there for."],["What if you need to borrow money?","Ask staff, payee, or family first; make a clear plan to pay back."],["What other ways can friends help each other besides money?","Rides, company, sharing skills, listening."]],
take:"Only lend what you are willing to lose, set clear money boundaries, and remember: saying no kindly is OK.",
tryit:"Decide on your own money boundary this week and practice saying it out loud once."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Health & Medication", [
{t:"Taking Medications as Prescribed",
goal:"Today we will learn why it matters to take our medicine the way the doctor ordered, and what to do when we have questions or concerns.",
warm:["What helps you remember to do things every day?","Why do you think some people stop taking their medicine?","How do you feel when it is time to take your medicine?"],
terms:[
["Prescription","an order from a doctor or nurse practitioner for a specific medicine, amount, and time.","Your prescription might say: Take 1 tablet by mouth twice a day with food.","Who writes prescriptions?"],
["Dose","the amount of medicine you take at one time.","One pill is one dose. Taking two when the label says one is a double dose and can be dangerous.","What could happen if someone takes too much?"],
["As prescribed","taking medicine exactly the way the doctor said: the right amount, at the right time, the right way.","If it says take at bedtime, taking it in the morning may make you sleepy all day.","Why do you think timing matters?"],
["Missed dose","when you forget or skip a dose.","If you miss a dose, do not double up next time. Tell the nurse or staff, and they will tell you what to do.","Who should you tell if you miss a dose?"],
["Stopping suddenly","quitting a medicine all at once without talking to the doctor.","Stopping some mental health or blood pressure medicines suddenly can make you very sick or bring symptoms back fast. Always talk to the doctor first.","Why might someone want to stop their medicine?"]],
story:"Jerome felt much better after a few months on his medicine. He thought, I'm fine now, I don't need these pills. He quietly stopped taking them. A few weeks later he could not sleep, felt on edge, and started hearing things again. He ended up in the hospital. His doctor explained that he felt better BECAUSE of the medicine. Now when Jerome has questions, he talks to the nurse instead of stopping on his own.",
sq:["Why did Jerome stop his medicine?","What happened after he stopped?","What did the doctor explain?","What does Jerome do now when he has questions?"],
act:{n:"Medicine Myths: True or False",m:"None",s:["Read each statement and have the group say TRUE or FALSE.","If I feel better, I can stop my medicine. (False. Talk to the doctor first.)","It is OK to take a friend's pill if we have the same problem. (False.)","If I miss a dose, I should take two next time. (False. Ask the nurse.)","I can ask my doctor questions about side effects. (True.)","Drinking alcohol with some medicines can be dangerous. (True.)","Natural pills or supplements are always safe with my medicine. (False. Ask first.)","After each, ask a volunteer why."]},
d:["Which myth have you heard before?","Which answer surprised you?","What is one thing you will remember about your medicine?"],
disc:[["What makes it hard to take medicine every day?","Side effects, forgetting, not wanting to be sick, cost, feeling better."],["What are good ways to remember?","Same time each day, linking it to a meal, staff reminders, a pill box."],["If a medicine makes you feel bad, what should you do?","Tell the nurse or doctor; do not stop on your own."],["How can taking medicine as prescribed help you reach your goals?","Stable health, fewer hospital stays, more independence."],["What questions would you like to ask the nurse about your medicine?","Encourage them to write questions down; the facilitator does not answer medical questions."]],
take:"Take your medicine the right way, at the right time, every time. If you have a question or want to make a change, talk to the nurse or doctor first.",
tryit:"Write down one question about your medicine and ask the nurse this week."},

{t:"Never Share Medications",
goal:"Today we will learn why medicine should never be shared, traded, or sold, and how to keep our medicine safe.",
warm:["Has anyone ever offered you a pill and said it would help?","Why might someone want to share their medicine?","Where do you think medicine should be kept?"],
terms:[
["Personal medication","medicine prescribed only for you, based on your body, weight, health, and other medicines.","Your doctor picked your medicine and dose just for you, like a key cut for one lock.","Why does the same medicine work differently for different people?"],
["Drug interaction","when two medicines, or a medicine and alcohol or food, mix in a harmful way.","Taking a friend's sleeping pill on top of your own medicine could slow your breathing to a dangerous level.","Why can mixing medicines be dangerous?"],
["Allergic reaction","when the body reacts badly to a medicine, causing rash, swelling, or trouble breathing.","Someone takes a friend's antibiotic, not knowing they are allergic, and their throat starts to swell. That is an emergency.","What should you do if someone has trouble breathing after a pill?"],
["Diversion","giving, trading, or selling your prescribed medicine to someone else. It is against the law.","Trading your pain pills for cigarettes is diversion and can get you in legal trouble and hurt someone.","Why is selling or trading medicine against the law?"],
["Medication storage","keeping medicine in a safe place where others cannot take it.","At Rose of Sharon, medicine is kept locked and handed out by staff to keep everyone safe.","Why do you think the facility keeps medicine locked?"]],
story:"Two roommates, Leon and Dave, were friends. Dave had trouble sleeping one night, and Leon gave him one of his own pills. Dave was already taking a medicine that made him drowsy. In the morning, staff could not wake Dave up easily, and he had to go to the hospital. He was OK, but the doctor said it could have been much worse. Leon felt terrible. He only wanted to help.",
sq:["Why did Leon share his pill?","What went wrong?","Was Leon a bad person?","What could Leon have done instead to help?"],
act:{n:"What Would You Do?",m:"None",s:["Read each situation and ask the group what they would do.","A friend says, I have a headache, can I have one of your pills? (Tell them to ask staff or the nurse.)","Someone offers you a pill that will help you relax. (Say no, tell staff.)","Someone asks to buy your medicine. (Say no, tell staff.)","You find a pill on the floor. (Do not take it, give it to staff.)","Have residents practice the words: No thanks, I only take my own medicine."]},
d:["Which situation would be hardest to say no to?","Why is telling staff helpful, not snitching?","How did it feel to practice saying no?"],
disc:[["Why might someone feel pressure to share medicine?","Wanting to help, wanting to be liked, being asked by a friend."],["What could happen to the person who shares?","Guilt, legal trouble, losing housing, running out of their own medicine."],["What could happen to the person who takes it?","Overdose, allergic reaction, interaction, hospital, death."],["Who should you go to if you feel sick and want medicine?","The nurse or staff on duty."],["How can we look out for each other about medicine?","Encourage friends to see the nurse, report unsafe situations."]],
take:"Your medicine is only for you. Never share, trade, borrow, or sell medicine. Send friends to the nurse instead.",
tryit:"Practice saying: No thanks, I only take my own medicine. Then go get staff."},

{t:"Side Effects: What to Know and Tell",
goal:"Today we will learn what side effects are, common ones to watch for, and how to tell staff or the doctor about them.",
warm:["Have you ever had a medicine make you feel strange?","What did you do about it?","Why might someone keep side effects to themselves?"],
terms:[
["Side effect","an unwanted change in your body from a medicine.","A medicine for mood might also cause a dry mouth, weight gain, or feeling sleepy.","What side effects have you heard of?"],
["Common side effect","a side effect many people get that is usually mild.","Upset stomach, sleepiness, dry mouth, or headache. These often get better after a few weeks.","What could help with a dry mouth?"],
["Serious side effect","a side effect that needs help right away.","Trouble breathing, swelling of the face, chest pain, fainting, a very high fever, or stiff muscles with confusion. Tell staff immediately.","What should you do if you have a serious side effect?"],
["Symptom log","a simple record of how you feel each day.","Monday: dizzy after morning pill. Tuesday: dizzy again, sat down. This helps the doctor understand.","How could writing it down help?"],
["Speaking up","telling staff, the nurse, or the doctor how you really feel.","Say: Since I started this new pill, I feel shaky every afternoon. The doctor may change the time or dose.","What makes it hard to speak up to a doctor?"]],
story:"Whenever Ms. Rita stood up, she felt dizzy, and once she almost fell. She thought it was just getting older. A caregiver asked how she was feeling, and Ms. Rita mentioned the dizziness. The caregiver told the nurse, who called the doctor. The doctor changed when Ms. Rita took her blood pressure pill. The dizziness got much better.",
sq:["What side effect did Ms. Rita have?","Why did she not say anything at first?","Who helped her?","What could have happened if she never spoke up?"],
act:{n:"Common or Call Now?",m:"None",s:["Explain: I will read a side effect. Say COMMON if it is usually mild, or CALL NOW if staff should know right away.","Dry mouth. (Common, but still mention it.)","Face or lips swelling. (Call now.)","Feeling a little sleepy. (Common.)","Chest pain. (Call now.)","Upset stomach. (Common.)","Fainting. (Call now.)","Rash all over the body. (Call now.)","Remind everyone: when in doubt, tell staff."]},
d:["Which ones were surprising?","Who would you tell first?","What is the rule when you are not sure?"],
disc:[["Why do medicines have side effects?","They affect the whole body, not just one part."],["Should you stop a medicine because of side effects?","No; tell the nurse or doctor, they may change it."],["How do side effects affect daily life?","Tiredness, weight, mood, energy; all worth telling the doctor."],["What are some ways to describe a side effect clearly?","What it feels like, when it started, how often, what makes it better or worse."],["What would you want your doctor to know about how you feel?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Side effects are common, and many can be fixed. Never suffer in silence. Tell staff, and when in doubt, speak up.",
tryit:"If you notice anything new in how you feel this week, tell a staff member that day."},

{t:"Talking to Your Doctor",
goal:"Today we will practice how to talk to doctors and nurses so we get the care we need and understand what they tell us.",
warm:["How do you usually feel at doctor appointments?","Have you ever left an appointment and forgotten what the doctor said?","What makes a good doctor in your opinion?"],
terms:[
["Health care provider","anyone who gives you medical care, like a doctor, nurse practitioner, nurse, or psychiatrist.","Your psychiatrist handles mental health medicine. Your primary care doctor handles things like blood pressure.","Who are your health care providers?"],
["Symptom","something you feel or notice that might mean something is wrong.","Coughing for two weeks, trouble sleeping, or pain in your knee are symptoms.","How would you describe a symptom clearly?"],
["Ask questions","it is your right to ask anything you do not understand.","What is this medicine for? What side effects should I watch for? When should I come back?","What is one question you wish you had asked a doctor?"],
["Teach-back","repeating back what the doctor said in your own words to be sure you understood.","So you want me to take this pill every morning with breakfast, and come back in 3 months?","Why would teach-back help?"],
["Bring a helper","having a staff member or family member come with you or help you prepare.","A caregiver can help write down your questions and take notes during the visit.","Who could you bring to an appointment?"]],
story:"Mr. Paul always nodded at the doctor and said, OK, even when he did not understand. At home, he did not know when to take his new pills. Next time, a staff member helped him write three questions. At the visit, Mr. Paul read his questions and repeated back the answers. He left knowing exactly what to do and felt proud.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Paul just nod?","What did he do differently the second time?","How did teach-back help?","How do you think he felt after?"],
act:{n:"Doctor Visit Role-Play",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Have each resident write 2 questions they might ask a doctor.","Pick a volunteer to be the doctor and one to be the patient.","Patient explains a pretend symptom: I have had a headache every afternoon for a week.","The doctor gives a simple answer. The patient asks their questions and practices teach-back.","Switch volunteers a few times."]},
d:["How did it feel to ask questions?","Was teach-back helpful?","What would you do differently at your next real appointment?"],
disc:[["Why do people feel nervous at the doctor?","Fear of bad news, feeling rushed, not understanding words."],["What should you bring to an appointment?","Medicine list, ID, insurance card, questions, a helper."],["What if a doctor uses words you do not understand?","Ask: Can you say that in simpler words?"],["What if you feel the doctor is not listening?","Repeat yourself calmly, bring a helper, ask the social worker for help."],["How does being honest with your doctor help you?","They can give better care with the truth."]],
take:"You are part of your care team. Write your questions down, ask until you understand, and repeat back what you heard.",
tryit:"Before your next appointment, write down two questions and ask staff to help you remember them."},

{t:"Understanding Blood Pressure",
goal:"Today we will learn what blood pressure is, why it matters, and everyday things that help keep it healthy.",
warm:["Has anyone ever checked your blood pressure? What did it feel like?","Does anyone in your family have high blood pressure?","What do you think raises blood pressure?"],
terms:[
["Blood pressure","how hard your blood pushes against the walls of your blood vessels as your heart pumps.","Think of a garden hose. If the water pressure is too high for too long, the hose wears out. Too much pressure for years can harm your heart, brain, and kidneys.","Why do you think high pressure is hard on the body?"],
["Reading","the two numbers you get when blood pressure is checked, like 120 over 80.","The top number is the pressure when your heart beats. The bottom number is when it rests between beats. Your nurse will tell you what is healthy for you.","Do you know what your last reading was?"],
["High blood pressure (hypertension)","blood pressure that stays too high over time.","It is called the silent killer because most people feel fine and do not know they have it until something serious happens, like a stroke.","Why is it important to check even if you feel fine?"],
["Salt (sodium)","a mineral in food that can raise blood pressure when we eat too much.","Chips, canned soup, lunch meat, fast food, and ramen noodles have a lot of salt.","What salty foods do you eat often?"],
["Healthy habits","daily choices that help keep blood pressure down.","Walking, eating more fruits and vegetables, less salt, not smoking, less stress, and taking blood pressure medicine as prescribed.","Which healthy habit could you start this week?"]],
story:"Ms. Denise felt fine, so she skipped her blood pressure checks and ate ramen noodles almost every night. One day she got a terrible headache and her arm felt weak. She was having a small stroke. She recovered, but her doctor told her high blood pressure caused it. Now she lets staff check it, takes her pill, and makes her noodles with only half the seasoning packet.",
sq:["Why did Ms. Denise skip her checks?","What happened to her?","What changes did she make?","What small change could help someone in this room?"],
act:{n:"Salt Detective",m:"A few food labels or packages (chips, canned soup, ramen) or write the numbers on the board",s:["Explain: Most adults should aim for less than about 2,300 mg of sodium a day. Your doctor may say less.","Show or write: Ramen noodles about 1,500 to 1,800 mg. Canned soup about 800 to 1,800 mg per can. Small bag of chips about 170 mg. An apple, 0 mg.","Ask: How much of a day's salt is in one pack of ramen? (Most of it.)","Have residents guess which foods are highest before you reveal.","Brainstorm lower-salt swaps together: half the packet, fresh fruit, unsalted nuts."]},
d:["Which food surprised you the most?","What easy swap could you make?","How can staff help you choose lower-salt foods?"],
disc:[["Why is high blood pressure called the silent killer?","Often no symptoms until damage is done."],["How does stress affect blood pressure?","Stress can raise it; calming activities help."],["What are warning signs of a stroke?","Face drooping, arm weakness, speech trouble: tell staff right away and call 911."],["Why is it important to take blood pressure medicine every day?","Keeps pressure steady; stopping can cause it to jump."],["What is one healthy habit the group could do together?","A daily walk, a water challenge, fruit snacks."]],
take:"High blood pressure is often silent. Get it checked, take your medicine, cut back on salt, and move your body.",
tryit:"Ask the nurse what your blood pressure goal is, and try one lower-salt swap this week."},

{t:"Diabetes Basics",
goal:"Today we will learn what diabetes is, how blood sugar works, and healthy habits that help everyone, whether or not they have diabetes.",
warm:["What do you know about diabetes?","Does anyone in your family have diabetes?","What is your favorite sweet treat?"],
terms:[
["Blood sugar (glucose)","the sugar in your blood that gives your body energy.","When you eat bread, rice, or sweets, your body turns it into sugar for energy. Insulin moves that sugar into your cells.","Where does blood sugar come from?"],
["Diabetes","a condition where the body cannot control blood sugar well, so it stays too high.","Over years, high blood sugar can harm the eyes, kidneys, nerves in the feet, and heart.","Why do you think diabetes affects so many body parts?"],
["Low blood sugar","when blood sugar drops too low, which can happen with diabetes medicine.","Signs are shaking, sweating, confusion, or feeling very hungry or grumpy. Tell staff right away; they may give juice or glucose.","What would you do if a friend seemed shaky and confused?"],
["Carbohydrates (carbs)","foods that turn into sugar in the body.","Bread, rice, pasta, potatoes, cereal, fruit, sweets, and soda are carbs. People with diabetes often watch how much they eat.","Which carbs do you eat most?"],
["Foot care","checking your feet every day, because diabetes can cause numbness and slow healing.","A small blister that goes unnoticed can become a serious infection. Wear shoes and tell staff about any sores.","Why might someone with diabetes not notice a cut on their foot?"]],
story:"Mr. Earl drank four regular sodas a day. He was always thirsty, tired, and his vision was blurry. At a checkup, the doctor found his blood sugar was very high. Mr. Earl was diagnosed with diabetes. He switched to water and diet drinks, started walking after lunch, and took his medicine. After six months his numbers improved, and he said he had more energy than he had in years.",
sq:["What signs did Mr. Earl have?","What changes did he make?","How did he feel after?","Which change do you think was the hardest for him?"],
act:{n:"Sugar in a Drink",m:"A bag of sugar and a teaspoon, a clear cup, or draw it on the board",s:["Explain: About 4 grams of sugar equals 1 teaspoon.","A 20-ounce regular soda has about 65 grams of sugar. Ask: How many teaspoons is that? (About 16.)","If you have sugar, scoop 16 teaspoons into a clear cup for everyone to see.","Do the same for sweet tea (often 10 to 15 teaspoons) and a 16-oz energy drink (about 13).","Ask residents to name lower-sugar drinks: water, unsweet tea, diet drinks, water with lemon."]},
d:["What did you think when you saw all that sugar?","Which drink do you drink the most?","What swap could you try?"],
disc:[["What are signs that blood sugar might be high?","Thirst, peeing a lot, tiredness, blurry vision."],["Can people with diabetes still enjoy treats?","Often in small amounts, as planned with their doctor or nurse."],["How does walking help blood sugar?","Muscles use sugar for energy."],["Why is it important to tell staff if you feel shaky or confused?","It could be low blood sugar, which needs quick help."],["What healthy habit from today helps everyone, with or without diabetes?","Less sugar, more water, more movement."]],
take:"Less sugar, more water, more movement, and regular checkups help everyone. If you feel shaky or confused, tell staff right away.",
tryit:"Swap one sugary drink for water each day this week."},

{t:"Sleep and Your Health",
goal:"Today we will learn why sleep matters for our body and mind, and simple habits that help us sleep better.",
warm:["How many hours do you usually sleep?","What keeps you up at night?","How do you feel the day after a bad night's sleep?"],
terms:[
["Sleep routine","doing the same calming things at the same time every night before bed.","Brush teeth, put on pajamas, turn off the TV, read or pray, lights out at 10 p.m.","What is part of your bedtime routine now?"],
["Insomnia","trouble falling asleep or staying asleep.","Lying awake for two hours with racing thoughts, or waking at 3 a.m. and not getting back to sleep.","What do you do when you can't sleep?"],
["Caffeine","a substance in coffee, tea, soda, and energy drinks that keeps you awake.","A soda at 8 p.m. can keep you up past midnight. Caffeine can stay in your body 6 hours or more.","What time do you have your last caffeine?"],
["Screen time","time looking at phones, tablets, or TV.","The bright light from a phone tells your brain it is daytime. Putting the phone away 30 minutes before bed helps.","How could you cut back on screens before bed?"],
["Napping","sleeping during the day.","A short 20-minute nap can help. A long nap late in the afternoon can make it hard to sleep at night.","Do naps help or hurt your night sleep?"]],
story:"Tasha drank sodas all afternoon, napped from 4 to 6, and watched videos on her phone until 2 a.m. She felt tired and grumpy every day. With help from staff, she made a plan: no soda after 2 p.m., no naps after 3, and phone away at 10. The first few nights were hard. By the second week, she was falling asleep by 11 and felt calmer during the day.",
sq:["What habits were hurting Tasha's sleep?","What was in her plan?","Why were the first few nights hard?","Which part of her plan would be hardest for you?"],
act:{n:"My Sleep Plan",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Ask everyone to write MY SLEEP PLAN at the top.","Write: My bedtime goal is ____.","Write: My last caffeine will be at ____.","Write: One calming thing I will do before bed: ____ (prayer, music, reading, warm shower, deep breathing).","Write: I will put away screens at ____.","Invite volunteers to share their plans."]},
d:["What calming activities did people choose?","What might get in the way of your plan?","How could staff or roommates help?"],
disc:[["How does poor sleep affect mood?","Irritable, sad, anxious, harder to cope."],["How does sleep affect physical health?","Weakens immune system, raises blood pressure, causes weight gain."],["What can you do if racing thoughts keep you awake?","Write them down, deep breathing, prayer, talk to staff."],["When should someone tell the nurse about sleep problems?","When it lasts more than a couple of weeks or affects daily life; medicine changes can affect sleep."],["What makes a bedroom good for sleep?","Dark, cool, quiet, comfortable."]],
take:"Good sleep is medicine for the body and mind. Keep a routine, cut caffeine early, and put screens away before bed.",
tryit:"Try one part of your sleep plan every night this week and notice how you feel."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Health & Medication", [
{t:"Drinking Enough Water",
goal:"Today we will learn why our bodies need water, signs of dehydration, and easy ways to drink more.",
warm:["What do you drink most during the day?","How do you know when you are thirsty?","Do you like water? Why or why not?"],
terms:[
["Hydration","having enough water in your body to work well.","Your body is more than half water. It uses water to keep you cool, move food through your gut, and help your brain think.","Why do you think the brain needs water?"],
["Dehydration","when your body does not have enough water.","Signs include dark yellow pee, headache, dizziness, dry mouth, feeling tired, and constipation.","Have you ever felt dehydrated?"],
["Urine color check","looking at the color of your pee to see if you are drinking enough.","Pale yellow like lemonade is good. Dark yellow like apple juice means drink more water.","Why is this an easy check?"],
["Hot weather risk","heat makes us lose water faster through sweat.","In a Tennessee summer, sitting outside for an hour can dry you out. Some medicines also make it easier to overheat.","What should you do on hot days?"],
["Water habits","simple routines that help you drink more.","Drink a glass when you wake up, with every meal, and with your medicine. Keep a bottle with you.","Which habit could you start?"]],
story:"Mr. Leroy only drank coffee and soda. He often had headaches and felt dizzy in the afternoons. One summer day he got so lightheaded he had to sit down on the porch. The nurse said he was dehydrated. Leroy started keeping a water bottle by his chair and refilling it after each meal. His headaches came less often.",
sq:["What did Mr. Leroy drink?","What signs of dehydration did he have?","What did he change?","How could you remind yourself to drink water?"],
act:{n:"Water Tracker",m:"Paper, pens, or printed cups chart",s:["Have each resident draw 8 small cups on a sheet of paper.","Explain: Many adults need around 6 to 8 cups of fluid a day, but some people have a fluid limit from their doctor. Always follow your doctor's limit.","Have residents color one cup for each glass they have had today so far.","Brainstorm ways to make water tastier: lemon, cucumber, ice, a fun cup.","Encourage them to keep the chart and color cups the rest of the day."]},
d:["How many cups have people had so far today?","What will help you reach your goal?","What did you learn about your own habits?"],
disc:[["Do coffee and soda count as water?","They have water but caffeine and sugar; water is best."],["Why do older adults feel less thirsty?","Thirst signals get weaker with age, so drink on a schedule."],["How can dehydration affect mood and thinking?","Confusion, crankiness, tiredness."],["Why might some people have a fluid limit?","Heart or kidney conditions; follow the doctor."],["How can we remind each other to drink water?","Water breaks in group, offering a cup to a friend."]],
take:"Your body runs on water. Drink a glass with every meal and medicine, and check the color of your pee.",
tryit:"Drink one glass of water as soon as you wake up every morning this week."},

{t:"Preventing Colds, Flu, and Germs",
goal:"Today we will learn how germs spread and simple ways to protect ourselves and each other from getting sick.",
warm:["When was the last time you had a cold or the flu?","What did you do to feel better?","How do you think germs spread in a house with many people?"],
terms:[
["Germs","tiny living things, like viruses and bacteria, that can make us sick. They are too small to see.","When someone sneezes, germs can fly up to 6 feet and land on tables, doorknobs, and remotes.","Where do you think the most germs are in our home?"],
["Handwashing","scrubbing your hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds.","That is about as long as singing Happy Birthday twice. Wash before eating, after the bathroom, and after coughing.","When is the most important time to wash your hands?"],
["Cover your cough","coughing or sneezing into a tissue or your elbow, not your hand.","If you sneeze into your hand and then touch the TV remote, the next person picks up your germs.","Why the elbow instead of the hand?"],
["Vaccine","a shot that teaches your body to fight a certain germ.","The yearly flu shot helps protect you and the people around you. Ask the nurse if it is right for you.","Have you had a flu shot before?"],
["Stay apart when sick","keeping some distance from others when you are ill.","If you have a fever, stay in your room as the nurse instructs, and do not share cups or food.","Why is that important in a group home?"]],
story:"One winter, a resident came to dinner with a bad cough and did not cover it. Within a week, six people were sick, including two who had to go to the hospital. The next winter, everyone made a plan: wash hands before meals, cover coughs, get flu shots if the doctor agreed, and tell staff early when feeling sick. Far fewer people got sick that year.",
sq:["How did the sickness spread?","What did the house do differently the next year?","Why do you think it worked?","What part of the plan can you help with?"],
act:{n:"Glitter Germs",m:"A little glitter or flour, and a sink nearby (or a pretend version)",s:["Put a small pinch of glitter or flour on one volunteer's hands. Explain that this is pretend germs.","Have that person shake hands with two people and touch a doorknob or a table.","Show everyone how far the germs spread.","Have the volunteers wash with water only, then with soap for 20 seconds, and compare.","If no glitter is available, demonstrate the 20-second handwash while the group sings Happy Birthday twice."]},
d:["How far did the germs travel?","What worked better, water alone or soap?","What surprised you?"],
disc:[["What are signs of the flu?","Fever, body aches, cough, chills, feeling very tired."],["Why should you tell staff early if you feel sick?","They can help you rest, call the nurse, and protect others."],["What things should never be shared?","Cups, utensils, toothbrushes, razors, cigarettes, vapes."],["Why do germs spread easily in shared homes?","Shared spaces, surfaces, meals, close contact."],["How can we be kind to someone who is sick?","Bring them water, give them space, check on them through staff."]],
take:"Wash your hands, cover your cough, do not share cups, and tell staff early if you feel sick. We protect each other.",
tryit:"Wash your hands for a full 20 seconds before every meal this week."},

{t:"Heart Health",
goal:"Today we will learn how the heart works and everyday habits that keep it strong.",
warm:["What do you think of when you hear the word heart?","What makes your heart beat faster?","Do you know anyone who has had heart trouble?"],
terms:[
["Heart","a muscle about the size of your fist that pumps blood through your whole body.","Your heart beats about 100,000 times a day, every day of your life.","Why does a muscle need exercise?"],
["Cholesterol","a waxy fat in the blood. Too much of the bad kind can clog blood vessels.","Fried foods, fatty meats, and lots of butter can raise it. Oatmeal, beans, and fruits can help lower it.","What foods do you eat that might raise cholesterol?"],
["Heart attack","when blood cannot reach part of the heart, often because a blood vessel is blocked.","Signs: chest pain or pressure, pain in the arm, jaw, or back, shortness of breath, sweating, nausea. Women may just feel very tired or sick to their stomach. Tell staff and call 911 right away.","What would you do if someone had chest pain?"],
["Physical activity","moving your body in a way that gets your heart working a little harder.","A 10-minute walk after each meal, dancing to music, or chair exercises.","What activity do you enjoy?"],
["Smoking and the heart","smoking damages blood vessels and makes the heart work harder.","One year after quitting, the risk of heart disease drops a lot.","How does smoking affect the heart?"]],
story:"Mr. Tony felt tightness in his chest while walking to the store but thought it was indigestion. He did not tell anyone for two days. When he finally did, the nurse sent him to the hospital, and he learned he had a blocked artery. The doctors fixed it, and he was told that waiting longer could have killed him. Now he takes walks with staff and has cut back on cigarettes.",
sq:["What sign did Mr. Tony ignore?","Why do you think he waited?","What did the doctor tell him?","What has he changed?"],
act:{n:"Feel Your Heartbeat",m:"A clock or watch with seconds",s:["Show residents how to find their pulse on the inside of the wrist or side of the neck with two fingers.","Count beats for 15 seconds and multiply by 4. Help with the math. A resting heart rate is often between 60 and 100.","Do one minute of gentle movement in chairs: march feet, swing arms, or clap.","Check the pulse again and compare.","Explain: The heart speeds up to bring more blood when we move. That is how it gets stronger."]},
d:["Did your heartbeat change after moving?","How did it feel to find your pulse?","What movement could you do every day?"],
disc:[["What everyday habits help the heart?","Moving, eating more fruits and vegetables, less salt and fried food, not smoking, managing stress."],["What should you do if you think someone is having a heart attack?","Get staff, call 911, do not wait."],["How do stress and anger affect the heart?","Raise blood pressure and heart rate."],["Why do people ignore warning signs?","Fear, not wanting to bother anyone, thinking it is minor."],["What is one heart-healthy food you like?","Let residents share: oatmeal, fruit, beans, fish, nuts."]],
take:"Your heart works for you all day. Move every day, eat well, do not smoke, and never ignore chest pain.",
tryit:"Take a 10-minute walk after one meal every day this week."},

{t:"Managing Pain Safely",
goal:"Today we will learn safe ways to manage aches and pains, and how to talk with staff about pain.",
warm:["What kind of aches or pains do people commonly have?","What do you usually do when something hurts?","How does pain affect your mood?"],
terms:[
["Acute pain","pain that comes on suddenly and usually goes away as you heal.","A sprained ankle or a toothache is acute pain.","What acute pain have you had?"],
["Chronic pain","pain that lasts for months or longer.","Back pain or arthritis in the knees that hurts most days is chronic.","How does living with chronic pain affect a person?"],
["Pain scale","a way to describe how bad pain is, from 0 (no pain) to 10 (worst pain ever).","Saying, My back is a 6 today, it was a 3 yesterday, helps the nurse understand.","Why is a number helpful?"],
["Over-the-counter medicine","medicine you can buy without a prescription, like Tylenol or ibuprofen.","Even these can be harmful if you take too much or mix them with your prescriptions. Always ask the nurse before taking anything.","Why ask the nurse before taking a store-bought pain pill?"],
["Non-medicine relief","ways to ease pain without pills.","Heat or ice packs, gentle stretching, a warm shower, rest, distraction, deep breathing, and changing position.","Which of these has helped you?"]],
story:"Ms. Carla's knee hurt every day. She started buying pain pills at the store and taking more than the label said, without telling anyone. She began having stomach pain. When the nurse found out, she explained that too much ibuprofen can hurt the stomach. Together they made a safer plan with the doctor: a set dose, an ice pack after walking, and gentle exercises. Her pain went down to a 3 most days.",
sq:["What did Ms. Carla do that was unsafe?","What happened because of it?","What was in her new plan?","Why is telling the nurse important?"],
act:{n:"Pain Scale Practice",m:"Draw a 0 to 10 scale on the board with faces if possible",s:["Draw a line from 0 to 10. Write 0 = no pain, 5 = medium, 10 = worst ever.","Read examples and have the group guess a number: stubbed toe, mild headache, broken arm, sore muscles after exercise.","Ask residents how they would describe their pain using words: sharp, dull, aching, burning, throbbing.","Practice a full sentence: My lower back is a 5, it is aching, and it gets worse when I sit too long.","Let volunteers practice their own sentence."]},
d:["Was it easy to pick a number?","Which words describe pain best?","Who will you tell the next time you are in pain?"],
disc:[["Why might people hide their pain?","Not wanting to complain, fear of medicine changes, feeling tough."],["What is dangerous about taking extra pain medicine?","Overdose, liver or stomach damage, addiction with some medicines."],["How does pain affect sleep and mood?","Harder to sleep, irritable, sad."],["What non-medicine relief could the group try together?","Stretching, music, walks."],["How can staff help with pain?","Tell the nurse, help with heat or ice, adjust activities."]],
take:"Never suffer in silence and never take extra medicine on your own. Describe your pain with a number and words, and let the nurse help.",
tryit:"The next time something hurts, tell staff using a number from 0 to 10."},

{t:"Preparing for a Doctor's Appointment",
goal:"Today we will learn how to get ready for a doctor's appointment so we get the most out of every visit.",
warm:["When is your next appointment, if you know?","What is the hardest part about going to the doctor?","What do you usually bring with you?"],
terms:[
["Appointment","a scheduled time to see a doctor or provider.","Tuesday at 10:30 with Dr. Smith. Arrive 15 minutes early to check in.","Why arrive early?"],
["Medication list","a list of every medicine you take, with the dose and time.","Staff can print this for you. Doctors need it to keep you safe.","Why does the doctor need your full medicine list?"],
["Insurance card","the card that shows which health plan pays for your care, like TennCare or Medicare.","The front desk will ask for it at every visit. Keep it in a safe place.","Where do you keep your insurance card?"],
["Question list","questions you write down before the visit so you do not forget.","Why am I so tired? Can I take this pill at night instead? Do I need any tests?","What questions would you write?"],
["Follow-up","the next steps after a visit, like a new appointment, lab test, or new prescription.","The doctor says come back in 3 months and get blood work next week. Make sure staff knows.","Who should you tell about your follow-up?"]],
story:"Mr. Greg went to his appointment without his medication list or insurance card. The front desk could not check him in right away. When the doctor asked what medicines he took, he could not remember. He forgot to mention his trouble sleeping. Next time, staff helped him pack a folder with everything. The visit went smoothly and he got help for his sleep.",
sq:["What did Mr. Greg forget?","What problems did that cause?","What was in his folder the next time?","What would you put in your appointment folder?"],
act:{n:"Pack Your Appointment Bag",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Ask the group: What should we bring to the doctor? Write answers on the board.","Make sure the list includes: ID, insurance card, medication list, question list, pen and paper, glasses, snack and water, a sweater.","Have each resident write their own checklist.","Have each resident write 2 questions for their next visit.","Remind them staff can help prepare and keep a copy."]},
d:["What item do people forget most often?","What questions did people write?","How could this checklist help you?"],
disc:[["Why do people miss appointments?","Forgetting, transportation, fear, not feeling sick."],["What should you do if you cannot make an appointment?","Tell staff so they can call and reschedule; do not just skip."],["How can you remember what the doctor said?","Write it down, bring a helper, ask for a printed summary."],["What can you do if you feel anxious before a visit?","Deep breathing, bring someone, write your worries down."],["Why is it important to tell the doctor everything, even embarrassing things?","They cannot help with what they do not know."]],
take:"Bring your ID, insurance card, medicine list, and questions. Write down what the doctor says, and share follow-up steps with staff.",
tryit:"Write down two questions for your next doctor visit and give a copy to staff."},

{t:"Knowing When to Get Help",
goal:"Today we will learn the difference between minor health problems, problems to tell staff about, and true emergencies.",
warm:["Have you ever had to call 911 or go to the emergency room?","What do you think counts as an emergency?","Who would you tell first if you felt sick here?"],
terms:[
["Emergency","a serious problem that needs help right now to protect life.","Chest pain, trouble breathing, fainting, a seizure, heavy bleeding, signs of stroke, or someone not waking up. Call staff and 911.","What is an example of an emergency?"],
["Urgent problem","a problem that needs care soon, usually the same day, but is not life-threatening.","A fever, a painful earache, a cut that may need stitches, or vomiting that will not stop. Tell staff right away.","What is the difference between urgent and emergency?"],
["Minor problem","a small issue you can usually handle with rest or simple care, but still tell staff.","A small scrape, mild cold, or a little headache.","What minor problems have you had this month?"],
["F.A.S.T.","a way to remember stroke signs: Face drooping, Arm weakness, Speech trouble, Time to call 911.","If a friend's smile looks uneven and their words are slurred, it is time to call for help.","What does each letter in F.A.S.T. stand for?"],
["Mental health crisis","when someone is in danger because of their mental health, such as wanting to hurt themselves or others or losing touch with what is real.","Tell staff right away. In the U.S., you can also call or text 988 for the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.","Who would you tell if a friend said they wanted to hurt themselves?"]],
story:"During dinner, Mr. Ben's face suddenly drooped on one side and he could not lift his fork. His tablemate, Ms. Ann, remembered F.A.S.T. from a group. She waved down a caregiver immediately. Staff called 911, and Mr. Ben got to the hospital quickly. The doctor said acting fast saved his ability to walk and talk.",
sq:["What signs did Mr. Ben have?","What did Ms. Ann do?","Why does time matter with a stroke?","How do you think Ms. Ann felt afterward?"],
act:{n:"Sort the Situations",m:"Three signs or headings on a board: Emergency, Tell Staff Today, Minor",s:["Read each situation and let the group decide which column it goes in.","Someone cannot breathe. (Emergency.)","You have a fever of 101. (Tell staff today.)","A paper cut. (Minor, still clean it.)","Your friend's speech is slurred and their arm is weak. (Emergency.)","You threw up twice and feel weak. (Tell staff today.)","A friend says they do not want to live anymore. (Tell staff right now.)","A mild cold. (Minor.)","Talk about why each one goes where it does."]},
d:["Which one did the group argue about most?","What is the safest choice when you are not sure?","Who is your first person to tell here?"],
disc:[["Why do people wait too long to get help?","Not wanting to bother anyone, fear of the hospital, thinking it will pass."],["What should you do if you find someone who will not wake up?","Yell for staff right away; do not leave them."],["Why is it never wrong to tell staff about a health worry?","They would rather check and have it be nothing."],["How can you help keep calm in an emergency?","Get help, stay with the person, follow staff directions."],["What number can someone call or text in a mental health crisis?","988, and tell staff."]],
take:"When in doubt, tell staff. Use F.A.S.T. for strokes, and for chest pain, trouble breathing, or someone not waking up, get help immediately.",
tryit:"Teach someone else the letters F.A.S.T. this week."},

{t:"Smoking and Your Health",
goal:"Today we will talk honestly about how smoking and vaping affect the body, and the help that exists for people who want to cut back or quit.",
warm:["What do you enjoy about smoking, or what do you think others enjoy about it?","How much do cigarettes cost these days?","Have you or someone you know tried to quit?"],
terms:[
["Nicotine","the drug in cigarettes and vapes that makes them addictive.","Nicotine reaches the brain in seconds and gives a quick feeling of calm, but when it wears off you crave more.","Why is nicotine so hard to quit?"],
["Withdrawal","uncomfortable feelings when the body does not get a substance it is used to.","Being cranky, anxious, restless, or hungry for a few days or weeks after cutting back.","What helps people get through withdrawal?"],
["Lung health","how well your lungs bring in oxygen.","Smoking damages the lungs and can cause coughing, shortness of breath, COPD, and lung cancer.","How does smoking affect breathing?"],
["Cutting back","smoking fewer cigarettes than before, a step toward quitting.","Going from 20 cigarettes a day to 10, or not smoking until after breakfast.","What is one way someone could cut back?"],
["Quit help","free tools and support for quitting.","Nicotine patches or gum (ask the nurse), the Tennessee Tobacco QuitLine at 1-800-QUIT-NOW, and support from friends and staff.","What kind of help would you want if you tried to quit?"]],
story:"Ms. Lorraine smoked a pack a day for 30 years. She coughed every morning and could not walk up stairs without stopping. She also spent over $200 a month on cigarettes. She did not want to quit all at once, so she started by cutting down to half a pack and using a patch the nurse helped her get. A year later she was smoke-free and used the money she saved to buy a new coat and visit her sister.",
sq:["What made Ms. Lorraine want to quit?","How did she start?","What help did she use?","What did she do with the money she saved?"],
act:{n:"The Cost of Smoking",m:"Board, markers",s:["Ask the group what a pack of cigarettes costs now. Use their answer, about $8 for example.","Multiply: 1 pack a day times 30 days equals about $240 a month.","Multiply by 12 for the year: about $2,880.","Ask: What could you do with $2,880? Write their ideas.","Do it again for half a pack a day to show what cutting back saves."]},
d:["Were you surprised by the cost?","What would you buy with that money?","Does knowing the cost change how you feel about smoking?"],
disc:[["Why do people start smoking?","Stress, friends, family habits, boredom, to fit in."],["Why is quitting so hard?","Addiction, routine, it is tied to coffee or stress or socializing."],["What triggers the urge to smoke?","After meals, with coffee, stress, being around smokers, boredom."],["What could someone do instead when a craving hits?","Drink water, chew gum, take a walk, breathe deeply, wait 10 minutes; cravings pass."],["How can we support someone who is trying to quit without judging them?","Encourage, do not offer cigarettes, celebrate small wins."]],
take:"Smoking harms the heart, lungs, and wallet. You do not have to quit all at once. Every cigarette you skip is a win, and free help is available.",
tryit:"If you smoke, try waiting 10 minutes the next time a craving hits, and notice whether it passes."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Health & Medication", [
{t:"Understanding Mental Health",
goal:"Today we will talk about what mental health is, why it matters just like physical health, and how to break the shame around it.",
warm:["What does the phrase mental health mean to you?","How do people in your family or community talk about mental illness?","What is something that helps your mind feel good?"],
terms:[
["Mental health","how we think, feel, and act, and how we handle stress and relationships.","Just like everyone has a body, everyone has mental health. Some days it is strong; some days it struggles.","Why do you think everyone has mental health, not only people with a diagnosis?"],
["Mental illness","a health condition that changes how a person thinks, feels, or acts, like depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety, or schizophrenia.","Like diabetes affects the body, depression affects the brain. Neither is anyone's fault, and both can be treated.","Why do you think people blame themselves for mental illness?"],
["Stigma","negative beliefs or shame that society puts on people with certain conditions.","Calling someone crazy or acting afraid of a person because they take mental health medicine is stigma.","Have you ever felt judged because of mental health?"],
["Recovery","living a full, meaningful life even while managing a mental health condition.","Recovery does not mean never having symptoms. It means having hope, goals, support, and tools.","What does recovery look like for you?"],
["Support system","the people and places that help you stay well.","Staff, the nurse, a counselor, family, friends, church, and this group.","Who is in your support system?"]],
story:"For years, Marcus hid that he had depression. His family said, Just pray harder or snap out of it. He felt weak and alone. When he came to a group like this one, he heard another man share the same struggle. For the first time, Marcus felt understood. He started talking with his counselor more openly and joined a weekly walking group. He still has hard days, but he says, I'm not broken. I'm healing.",
sq:["Why did Marcus hide his depression?","How did his family's words affect him?","What changed when he came to group?","What does Marcus mean by I'm not broken, I'm healing?"],
act:{n:"Words That Hurt, Words That Help",m:"Board divided into two columns",s:["Label one column Words That Hurt and the other Words That Help.","Ask the group to name hurtful words or phrases they have heard about mental illness. Write them in the first column (keep it respectful).","For each one, ask: What could we say instead? Write it in the second column. Example: Crazy becomes Living with a mental illness. Snap out of it becomes I'm here for you.","Read the Words That Help column out loud together.","Ask residents to choose one helpful phrase they want to use."]},
d:["How did it feel to see the hurtful words written down?","Which helpful phrase do you like most?","How can we use helpful words here at home?"],
disc:[["Why is mental health just as important as physical health?","It affects everything: sleep, relationships, body health, goals."],["What are ways to take care of your mental health daily?","Medicine, sleep, routine, exercise, talking, faith, hobbies."],["How can we fight stigma?","Use kind words, share stories, treat people with respect."],["What do you wish people understood about mental illness?","Let residents share; listen without fixing."],["What gives you hope?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Mental illness is a health condition, not a weakness or a choice. Recovery is possible, and nobody has to do it alone.",
tryit:"Use one helpful phrase from today when you talk about mental health this week."},

{t:"Knowing Your Early Warning Signs",
goal:"Today we will learn how to spot the early warning signs that our mental health is slipping, so we can get help before things get worse.",
warm:["How can you tell when a storm is coming?","How do you feel when you are at your best?","What is the first thing you notice when you start to feel off?"],
terms:[
["Early warning sign","a small change that shows trouble might be starting.","Sleeping much more or less, skipping meals, pulling away from people, getting irritated easily, or racing thoughts.","What is one early warning sign for you?"],
["Relapse","when symptoms come back or get worse after a period of feeling better.","After months of feeling stable, someone stops sleeping, feels paranoid, and needs to go to the hospital.","Why do you think relapses happen?"],
["Trigger","a situation or stress that can set off symptoms.","Anniversaries of a loss, conflict with family, holidays, missing medicine, using drugs or alcohol, or not sleeping.","What are some of your triggers?"],
["Wellness plan","a written plan of what keeps you well and what to do if warning signs show up.","My signs: not sleeping, staying in my room. My plan: tell staff, call my counselor, take my medicine, go for a walk.","What would be in your wellness plan?"],
["Reaching out early","asking for help when you first notice signs, not waiting for a crisis.","Saying to a caregiver, I haven't slept in two nights and my thoughts are racing. Can you let the nurse know?","Who would you reach out to?"]],
story:"Ms. Kendra noticed she had stopped going to dinner, was staying up all night, and felt like people were talking about her. In the past, she would have kept it to herself until she ended up in the hospital. This time, she remembered her wellness plan. She told a caregiver. The nurse called her doctor, who adjusted her care, and staff checked on her more often. Within two weeks she was back to her routine, without a hospital stay.",
sq:["What early warning signs did Ms. Kendra notice?","What did she do differently this time?","Who helped her?","How did reaching out early change things?"],
act:{n:"My Warning Light",m:"Paper, pens, or a drawn traffic light (green, yellow, red)",s:["Draw a traffic light. Explain: Green means I'm doing well. Yellow means warning signs. Red means crisis.","Ask each resident to write what they are like in GREEN: sleeping, eating, enjoying things.","In YELLOW: their early warning signs.","In RED: signs they need help right away.","Next to YELLOW, write one person to tell and one thing to do. Next to RED: tell staff immediately, call 988 if needed."]},
d:["Was it easy or hard to think of your yellow signs?","Who did you list as your person to tell?","Where will you keep your traffic light?"],
disc:[["Why do people ignore early warning signs?","Denial, fear of the hospital, not noticing, feeling ashamed."],["How can friends tell when someone is in the yellow?","Changes in sleep, mood, hygiene, staying in their room."],["What could you say to a friend who seems to be slipping?","I've noticed you seem down lately. Want to talk or go to staff together?"],["How does a routine help prevent relapse?","Structure, stable sleep, medicine on time."],["Why is asking for help a sign of strength?","It takes courage and shows you are taking care of yourself."]],
take:"Know your green, yellow, and red. When you notice yellow, reach out right away. Early help can prevent a crisis.",
tryit:"Share your warning light with one staff member you trust this week."},

{t:"Preventing Falls",
goal:"Today we will learn why falls happen and simple ways to keep ourselves safe at home and outside.",
warm:["Have you ever fallen or almost fallen? What happened?","What places in a house are easy to slip in?","How do you feel about using a cane or walker?"],
terms:[
["Fall risk","the chance that a person might fall.","Dizziness, weak legs, poor eyesight, some medicines, clutter, and poor lighting can all raise fall risk.","What raises fall risk the most, do you think?"],
["Trip hazard","something on the floor that can make you trip.","Shoes left in a walkway, loose rugs, cords, spills, and clothes on the floor.","What trip hazards have you seen?"],
["Proper footwear","shoes that fit well, have non-slip soles, and stay on your feet.","Sturdy sneakers are safer than flip-flops, slippers that slide off, or walking in socks on a smooth floor.","What shoes do you usually wear inside?"],
["Get up slowly","sitting for a moment on the edge of the bed before standing.","Some medicines and low blood pressure can make you dizzy if you stand quickly. Sit, count to 10, then stand.","Have you ever felt dizzy when standing up?"],
["Assistive device","a tool that helps you move safely, like a cane, walker, grab bar, or shower chair.","Using the walker the doctor ordered every time, even for short trips to the bathroom.","Why do some people not use their walker?"]],
story:"Mr. Walter got up quickly in the middle of the night to use the bathroom. It was dark, he was wearing socks, and he did not use his walker because it was only a few steps. He slipped and hurt his hip. After his recovery, he started using a nightlight, kept his walker next to the bed, sat up slowly, and wore non-slip socks. He has not fallen since.",
sq:["What things led to Mr. Walter's fall?","Which change do you think helped the most?","Why did he skip the walker?","What could you change in your own room?"],
act:{n:"Room Safety Hunt",m:"Paper and pens, or walk around the common room if safe",s:["Ask residents to think about their own room or look around the group room.","List possible trip hazards: rugs, cords, shoes, clutter, poor lighting, wet floors.","Have each person write one hazard they could fix in their own room.","Practice the safe way to stand up: scoot to edge, feet flat, hands on chair or bed, lean forward, stand, pause.","Have volunteers demonstrate."]},
d:["What hazards did people find?","How did it feel to practice standing up slowly?","What will you fix first?"],
disc:[["Why are falls dangerous as we get older?","Broken bones, head injuries, losing independence."],["How can medicines affect falls?","Some cause dizziness or sleepiness; tell the nurse."],["What should you do if you fall?","Do not rush to get up, call for help, tell staff even if you feel fine."],["How can exercise help prevent falls?","Stronger legs and better balance."],["How can we help each other stay safe?","Pick up clutter, wipe spills, report hazards to staff."]],
take:"Clear the path, wear good shoes, use your walker or cane, turn on a light, and stand up slowly.",
tryit:"Check your room tonight and move one thing that could make you trip."},

{t:"Reading Your Medication Label",
goal:"Today we will learn how to read a medicine label so we understand what we take and why.",
warm:["Have you ever looked closely at a medicine bottle?","What information do you think is on a medicine label?","Why do you think labels are written in such small print?"],
terms:[
["Medication name","the name of the medicine, often with a brand name and a generic name.","Tylenol is a brand name. Acetaminophen is the generic name. It is the same medicine.","Why might there be two names for one medicine?"],
["Strength","how much medicine is in each pill, usually in milligrams (mg).","Metformin 500 mg means each tablet has 500 milligrams.","Why does strength matter?"],
["Directions","how and when to take the medicine.","Take 1 tablet by mouth twice daily with food. Twice daily usually means morning and evening.","What does with food mean?"],
["Warning sticker","colored stickers on the bottle that give important safety information.","May cause drowsiness. Do not drink alcohol. Avoid sunlight. Take with water.","Why are warning stickers important?"],
["Refills and expiration","how many more times the prescription can be filled, and the date after which medicine should not be used.","Refills: 2. Use before: 06/2027. Expired medicine may not work well.","What should you do with expired medicine?"]],
story:"Mr. Dennis saw a sticker on his new bottle that said Do not drink alcohol, but he did not read it. One evening he had two beers with his medicine and felt so dizzy he could barely walk. A caregiver helped him and called the nurse. The nurse showed him the warning sticker. Mr. Dennis now asks the nurse to go over every new medicine with him.",
sq:["What did Mr. Dennis miss on the label?","What happened?","What does he do now?","Why do you think people skip reading labels?"],
act:{n:"Label Detective",m:"A pretend label written large on the board or paper",s:["Write a pretend label: Pharmacy name. Patient: Jane Doe. Sertraline 50 mg tablet. Take 1 tablet by mouth every morning. Qty: 30. Refills: 3. Dr. Lee. Warning: May cause drowsiness. Do not drink alcohol.","Ask: What is the medicine name? What is the strength? When do you take it? How many pills are in the bottle? How many refills? What are the warnings?","Let residents take turns finding each answer.","Ask: If you take 1 a day, how long will the bottle last? (30 days.)","Remind them: At Rose of Sharon, staff manage medicine, but it is still your right to know what you take."]},
d:["Which part of the label was easiest to find?","Which was hardest?","Do you know the names of your own medicines?"],
disc:[["Why is it your right to know what medicines you take?","It is your body and your health."],["How could knowing your medicines help in an emergency?","You can tell paramedics or doctors."],["What should you do if a pill looks different than usual?","Ask the nurse before taking it; it may be a different maker or a mistake."],["Why should you never mix alcohol with medicines that warn against it?","Dangerous side effects, overdose risk."],["Who can explain your medicine to you?","The nurse, pharmacist, or doctor."]],
take:"Your medicine label tells you the name, strength, directions, and warnings. Ask the nurse or pharmacist if anything is unclear.",
tryit:"Ask the nurse to tell you the name and purpose of one of your medicines this week."},

{t:"Stress and Your Body",
goal:"Today we will learn how stress affects the body and simple ways to calm our body down.",
warm:["What stresses you out the most?","Where in your body do you feel stress?","What is one thing that helps you relax?"],
terms:[
["Stress","the body and mind's reaction to pressure or change.","Getting bad news, an argument, a deadline, or moving can cause stress.","What is something small that stresses you?"],
["Fight-or-flight","the body's automatic alarm system when it senses danger.","Your heart pounds, breathing speeds up, muscles tense, and your stomach knots. This helped people run from danger long ago.","Have you felt fight-or-flight before?"],
["Chronic stress","stress that lasts a long time.","Worrying about money every day for months can lead to headaches, poor sleep, high blood pressure, and getting sick more often.","How has long-term stress affected you?"],
["Deep breathing","slow breaths that tell your body it is safe.","Breathe in through your nose for 4 seconds, hold for 4, and breathe out through your mouth for 6.","How do you feel after a few deep breaths?"],
["Muscle relaxation","tightening and then relaxing muscles to release tension.","Squeeze your fists tight for 5 seconds, then let go and notice the difference.","Where do you hold the most tension?"]],
story:"Ms. Janice had headaches and stomachaches every afternoon. The doctor found nothing wrong and asked about her stress. She realized her son called every afternoon to argue about money. With her counselor, she set a boundary to talk to him only on weekends, and she started doing deep breathing after hard calls. Her headaches became much less frequent.",
sq:["What symptoms did Ms. Janice have?","What was causing them?","What changes did she make?","How can stress show up in the body?"],
act:{n:"Calm Body Practice",m:"Chairs, calm music if available",s:["Ask everyone to sit comfortably with feet flat on the floor.","Lead 4-4-6 breathing: in through the nose for 4, hold for 4, out through the mouth for 6. Repeat 5 times slowly.","Lead muscle relaxation: squeeze fists 5 seconds, release. Shrug shoulders to ears 5 seconds, release. Press feet into the floor 5 seconds, release.","Ask everyone to notice how their body feels now.","Offer a moment of quiet for 30 seconds before talking."]},
d:["How did your body feel before and after?","Which part did you like best?","When could you use this during your day?"],
disc:[["What are signs your body is stressed?","Headaches, tight shoulders, stomachaches, fast heartbeat, trouble sleeping."],["What unhealthy ways do people cope with stress?","Smoking, drinking, overeating, isolating, yelling."],["What healthy ways help?","Breathing, walking, music, prayer, talking, journaling."],["How can we lower stress here at home?","Quiet spaces, kindness, routines, talking things out."],["Who can you talk to when stress is too much?","Staff, counselor, nurse, friends."]],
take:"Stress lives in the body, and so does calm. A few slow breaths can tell your body you are safe.",
tryit:"Do 5 slow 4-4-6 breaths before bed every night this week."},

{t:"Staying Active and Healthy Aging",
goal:"Today we will learn why staying active matters at every age and easy ways to move more each day.",
warm:["What activities did you enjoy when you were younger?","What kind of movement do you do now?","What is one thing you want to be able to keep doing as you get older?"],
terms:[
["Physical activity","any movement that uses your muscles and energy.","Walking, cleaning, dancing, gardening, stretching, and chair exercises all count.","What activity do you do without even thinking of it as exercise?"],
["Strength","how strong your muscles are.","Standing up from a chair 10 times builds leg strength, which helps you walk, climb stairs, and avoid falls.","Why does leg strength matter?"],
["Balance","being able to stay steady on your feet.","Standing with feet together while holding a counter for 30 seconds helps train balance.","When do you notice your balance most?"],
["Flexibility","how easily your joints and muscles can move.","Gentle stretches make it easier to reach a high shelf or tie your shoes.","What stretch feels good for you?"],
["Use it or lose it","the idea that the body gets weaker if it is not used.","After a week in bed, muscles weaken. Daily movement keeps us able to do things for ourselves.","What do you want to keep being able to do?"]],
story:"Ms. Hattie, age 72, spent most of the day in her recliner. She started having trouble getting up and walking to the dining room. A caregiver invited her to a 10-minute chair exercise group each morning. At first she only watched. Then she joined. After two months, she could stand up from her chair without pushing off as hard, and she walked to dinner on her own.",
sq:["What problem was Ms. Hattie having?","How did she start?","What changed after two months?","Why do you think she only watched at first?"],
act:{n:"Chair Exercise Session",m:"Sturdy chairs without wheels",s:["Remind everyone to go at their own pace and stop if anything hurts. Anyone with a doctor's limit should follow it.","March in place while seated for 1 minute.","Arm circles forward 10 times, backward 10 times.","Sit-to-stand: stand up from the chair and sit back down slowly, 5 times (use hands as needed).","Ankle circles 10 each way. Shoulder rolls 10 times.","End with 3 slow deep breaths."]},
d:["How do you feel after moving?","Which exercise was easiest? Hardest?","Could you do a few of these every day?"],
disc:[["What gets in the way of staying active?","Pain, tiredness, weather, low mood, no one to go with."],["How does movement help mood?","Releases feel-good chemicals, lowers stress."],["What activities could we do together here?","Walks, dancing, chair exercise, games."],["How can you stay active even with pain?","Gentle movement, ask the nurse for safe exercises."],["What would you like to be able to do six months from now?","Let residents share goals."]],
take:"It is never too late to get stronger. A few minutes of movement every day keeps you independent longer.",
tryit:"Do 5 sit-to-stands from your chair every day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Hygiene & Self-Care", [
{t:"Building a Daily Hygiene Routine",
goal:"Today we will learn what a daily hygiene routine is and build one that fits our own life.",
warm:["What is the first thing you do when you wake up?","How do you feel after a shower and clean clothes?","What makes it hard to take care of yourself on a bad day?"],
terms:[
["Hygiene","the things we do to keep our bodies clean and healthy.","Bathing, brushing teeth, washing hands, wearing deodorant, and changing clothes are all hygiene.","What hygiene task is easiest for you?"],
["Routine","doing the same steps in the same order at the same time each day.","Wake up, use the bathroom, wash face, brush teeth, get dressed, comb hair. After a few weeks, it happens almost automatically.","What routine do you already have?"],
["Checklist","a list of steps you check off as you finish them.","A sheet taped to the mirror: brush teeth, wash face, deodorant, clean clothes, comb hair.","Where could you put a checklist so you see it?"],
["Self-care","taking care of your body and mind because you are worth it.","Taking a warm shower, putting on lotion, and wearing clothes you like are ways to show yourself respect.","How does taking care of yourself show self-respect?"],
["Small steps","breaking a task into tiny pieces when you feel low.","On a hard day, just brushing teeth and changing your shirt is a win.","What is the smallest step you could take on a hard day?"]],
story:"When Darnell felt depressed, he stopped showering and wore the same clothes for days. Other residents started avoiding him, and he felt even more alone. A caregiver helped him make a simple morning checklist with only five steps and tape it to his mirror. Some days he only did two steps, and that was OK. Over time, he did all five most days, and he said he felt more like himself.",
sq:["What happened when Darnell stopped taking care of his hygiene?","What tool helped him?","Why was it OK to only do two steps some days?","How did his routine affect his mood?"],
act:{n:"Make Your Morning Checklist",m:"Paper, markers, tape",s:["Give each resident a sheet of paper and markers.","Have them write MY MORNING at the top.","Ask them to list 4 to 6 steps in order (for example: bathroom, wash face, brush teeth, deodorant, clothes, hair).","Add small boxes next to each step to check off.","Encourage them to decorate it and tape it to their mirror or door."]},
d:["What steps did people include?","Is there a step you often skip?","Where will you hang your checklist?"],
disc:[["How does hygiene affect how others treat us?","People may avoid us, or feel more comfortable around us."],["How does hygiene affect how we feel about ourselves?","More confident, fresh, ready for the day."],["Why might depression make hygiene hard?","Low energy, low motivation, feeling it does not matter."],["How can staff or friends help without embarrassing someone?","Private, kind reminders and offering supplies."],["What hygiene supplies do you need right now?","Let them share; staff can follow up."]],
take:"A simple routine makes self-care easier. On hard days, small steps still count.",
tryit:"Use your checklist every morning this week and check off what you do."},

{t:"Showering and Bathing",
goal:"Today we will learn how often and how to bathe well, and how to make shower time easier and safer.",
warm:["Do you prefer a shower or a bath? Why?","Do you like to shower in the morning or at night?","What makes shower time hard sometimes?"],
terms:[
["Body odor","the smell that happens when sweat mixes with bacteria on the skin.","After a hot day or a few days without a shower, body odor builds up, especially under the arms and feet.","Why can't we always smell our own body odor?"],
["Washcloth or scrubber","a cloth used to scrub skin and remove dirt and dead skin.","Using soap on a washcloth cleans better than splashing water. Wash the cloth often.","How often should a washcloth be washed?"],
["Key areas","parts of the body that need extra attention.","Underarms, private areas, feet, between toes, behind ears, under skin folds.","Which areas do people often forget?"],
["Shower safety","ways to avoid slipping or burns.","Use the grab bar, a non-slip mat, a shower chair if needed, and test the water temperature with your hand first.","What shower safety tools do you use?"],
["Drying off","drying your skin fully, especially between toes and skin folds.","Leaving skin damp can cause rashes or fungus like athlete's foot.","Why does drying matter?"]],
story:"Ms. Bea did not like showering because the bathroom was cold and she was scared of slipping. She went a week at a time without bathing. Staff helped by warming the bathroom first, adding a shower chair, and keeping her towel and robe close. Ms. Bea started showering every other day and said she sleeps better after a warm shower.",
sq:["Why did Ms. Bea avoid showering?","What changes did staff make?","How did it help her?","What would make showering easier for you?"],
act:{n:"Step-by-Step Shower",m:"Paper or board",s:["Ask the group to list every step of a good shower, in order, from gathering supplies to getting dressed.","Write them on the board: get towel, clothes, soap, shampoo; check water temperature; wet body; wash hair; soap washcloth; wash face, underarms, private areas, legs, feet; rinse; dry fully; lotion; deodorant; clean clothes.","Ask: Which step do people rush or skip?","Talk about how long a good shower takes (about 10 minutes).","Have residents share one tip for making showers better."]},
d:["Were there steps you had not thought about?","Which step do you want to do better?","What tip from someone else will you try?"],
disc:[["How often should most people shower?","Most people do well with daily or every other day; more when sweaty."],["What can you do if you run out of soap or shampoo?","Ask staff, the donation closet, or add it to your shopping list."],["How do you politely tell a friend they might need a shower?","Privately and kindly, or ask staff to help."],["How can a shower help your mood?","Refreshing, relaxing, a fresh start."],["What makes the shared bathroom pleasant for everyone?","Clean up, do not take too long, wipe the floor."]],
take:"A good shower means soap, a washcloth, key areas, rinsing well, and drying fully. Safety first with grab bars and warm, not hot, water.",
tryit:"Set a regular shower time this week and stick to it."},

{t:"Caring for Your Teeth",
goal:"Today we will learn how to brush and floss well and why dental health affects the whole body.",
warm:["When was the last time you went to the dentist?","How do you feel about the dentist?","How many times a day do you brush your teeth?"],
terms:[
["Plaque","a sticky film of germs that forms on teeth every day.","If you do not brush, plaque hardens and causes cavities and gum disease.","How does it feel when your teeth have not been brushed?"],
["Cavity","a hole in a tooth caused by decay.","Sweets and soda feed the germs that make cavities. Cavities can hurt and need filling.","What foods and drinks cause cavities?"],
["Gum disease","infection of the gums that causes bleeding, swelling, bad breath, and loose teeth.","If your gums bleed when you brush, tell staff or a dentist. It can be treated.","What are signs of gum disease?"],
["Brushing the right way","brushing two times a day for two minutes with fluoride toothpaste, on all sides of each tooth.","Small circles on the front, back, and chewing surfaces, then brush your tongue.","Do you brush your tongue?"],
["Dentures","removable false teeth.","Dentures should be brushed daily, soaked overnight, and the gums cleaned too.","How should dentures be cared for?"]],
story:"Mr. Calvin had a toothache for weeks and chewed only on one side. He was scared of the dentist, so he ignored it. Eventually his face swelled up from an infection, and he had to go to urgent care. The tooth had to be pulled. Now Mr. Calvin brushes twice a day and goes for cleanings, and he says the dentist was not as scary as the pain.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Calvin avoid the dentist?","What happened because he waited?","What does he do now?","What might help someone who is scared of the dentist?"],
act:{n:"Two-Minute Brush Practice",m:"A timer or clock; toothbrush to demonstrate (no brushing needed)",s:["Demonstrate with a toothbrush in the air: small circles on the front of teeth, back of teeth, chewing surfaces, then the tongue.","Explain 30 seconds for each quarter of the mouth: upper right, upper left, lower right, lower left.","Set a two-minute timer and have everyone sit quietly to feel how long two minutes is.","Ask: Was that longer than you usually brush?","Talk about using a song or timer in the bathroom."]},
d:["Did two minutes feel long or short?","What part of brushing do you usually skip?","How will you time yourself?"],
disc:[["How do teeth affect overall health?","Mouth infections can affect the heart and other organs; pain affects eating."],["How do teeth affect confidence?","Smiling, talking, job interviews."],["What should you do if a tooth hurts?","Tell staff so a dental visit can be set up."],["How often should you replace a toothbrush?","About every 3 months or when bristles are worn."],["What makes going to the dentist hard?","Fear, cost, transportation; staff can help with each."]],
take:"Brush two times a day for two minutes, brush your tongue, and tell staff about any tooth pain right away.",
tryit:"Brush for a full two minutes, morning and night, every day this week."},

{t:"Hair Care and Grooming",
goal:"Today we will talk about caring for our hair and grooming so we look and feel our best.",
warm:["What hairstyle did you love most in your life?","How often do you wash or style your hair?","How does a fresh haircut or style make you feel?"],
terms:[
["Hair type","the natural texture of your hair: straight, wavy, curly, or coily.","Coily hair often needs more moisture and fewer washes. Straight hair may get oily faster and need more washing.","How would you describe your hair type?"],
["Moisture","keeping hair from drying out and breaking.","Using leave-in conditioner, oil, or a satin bonnet or pillowcase at night helps protect hair.","What products do you use for your hair?"],
["Scalp care","keeping the skin on your head clean and healthy.","Flakes or itching can mean dry scalp or dandruff. Washing regularly and certain shampoos can help.","Have you had trouble with your scalp?"],
["Grooming","keeping hair, beards, eyebrows, and nails neat.","Combing hair, trimming a beard, and cleaning up nails before going out.","What part of grooming do you enjoy?"],
["Haircut schedule","planning regular trims.","Short hair often needs a cut every 3 to 4 weeks. Longer hair may need a trim every 2 to 3 months.","When was your last haircut?"]],
story:"Ms. Renee used to love doing her hair, but after a hard year she stopped caring and kept it under a hat. A volunteer came to do hair one Saturday. Ms. Renee got her hair washed, conditioned, and styled. She looked in the mirror and smiled for the first time in weeks. She asked staff to help her get a satin bonnet and a wide-tooth comb so she could keep it up.",
sq:["Why did Ms. Renee stop caring for her hair?","How did she feel after it was done?","What did she ask for afterward?","How can our appearance affect our mood?"],
act:{n:"Grooming Kit Plan",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Ask everyone to list what is in a good grooming kit for them.","Ideas: comb or brush, shampoo, conditioner, oil or moisturizer, bonnet or durag, clippers or razor, nail clippers, hair ties, mirror.","Have each person circle what they already have and star what they need.","Discuss low-cost places to get supplies, and that staff can help request items.","Invite volunteers to share a favorite hair tip."]},
d:["What tips did you hear that you might try?","What supplies do you need?","How can we help each other with grooming?"],
disc:[["How does grooming affect how people see us?","First impressions, respect, confidence."],["What hair care traditions did you grow up with?","Let residents share cultural traditions with respect."],["What can you do if you cannot afford a barber or stylist?","Volunteers, beauty schools, help from friends, staff."],["Why is sharing combs or clippers risky?","Lice, scalp infections, cuts."],["How do you feel when you look in the mirror after grooming?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Caring for your hair is caring for yourself. Find the routine and products that work for your hair type.",
tryit:"Take 10 minutes this week to give your hair extra care."},

{t:"Deodorant and Fresh Body Care",
goal:"Today we will learn how to stay fresh throughout the day and handle body odor with confidence.",
warm:["What scents do you like to wear?","How can you tell when you need to freshen up?","Why do you think body odor can be an awkward subject?"],
terms:[
["Deodorant","a product that covers or reduces body odor.","Deodorant fights the smell but does not stop sweat.","When do you put on deodorant?"],
["Antiperspirant","a product that reduces sweating.","Many products are both deodorant and antiperspirant. Read the label.","What is the difference between the two?"],
["Clean skin first","putting deodorant on clean, dry skin works best.","Putting deodorant on top of sweat without washing just mixes the smells.","Why does clean skin matter?"],
["Breathable fabrics","clothes that let air through and reduce sweat smell.","Cotton breathes better than polyester. Clean socks every day help foot odor.","What fabric do you wear most?"],
["Freshen up","quick ways to stay clean between showers.","Washing underarms with a washcloth, changing your shirt, or using a body wipe after exercise.","When might you need to freshen up during the day?"]],
story:"Jamal liked to play basketball every afternoon, then sit in the day room in the same sweaty shirt. A friend kindly told him, Hey man, you might want to change after you play. Jamal felt embarrassed at first but then grateful. He started keeping a clean shirt and deodorant in a bag. He said he felt more comfortable around others, especially at dinner.",
sq:["What was Jamal's habit?","How did his friend handle it?","What did Jamal change?","How would you want someone to tell you?"],
act:{n:"Kind Ways to Say It",m:"None",s:["Explain that it can be hard to tell someone they smell, but kindness helps.","Ask volunteers to practice: one person is the friend with body odor, the other tells them kindly and privately.","Sample script: I care about you, and I wanted to let you know privately, you might want to freshen up.","Practice receiving it well: Thanks for telling me.","Discuss when it is better to ask staff to help."]},
d:["How did it feel to say it?","How did it feel to hear it?","What words worked best?"],
disc:[["Why can't we always smell our own body odor?","The nose gets used to our own smell."],["What causes extra odor?","Sweat, unwashed clothes, some foods, some medicines, stress."],["What should you do if you cannot afford deodorant?","Ask staff or the donation closet."],["How can foot odor be prevented?","Clean socks daily, dry feet well, air out shoes."],["Why is it important to be kind about this subject?","Shame makes people withdraw; kindness helps them change."]],
take:"Wash, dry, apply deodorant to clean skin, and wear clean clothes daily. When someone tells you kindly, thank them.",
tryit:"Keep deodorant somewhere you see it every morning and use it every day."},

{t:"Skin Care Basics",
goal:"Today we will learn simple ways to keep our skin healthy and comfortable.",
warm:["Does your skin feel dry, oily, or just right?","What lotion or skin products do you like?","How does weather affect your skin?"],
terms:[
["Moisturizer","lotion or cream that keeps skin soft and prevents dryness.","Putting lotion on right after a shower, while skin is still a little damp, holds in moisture.","When do you usually use lotion?"],
["Dry skin","skin that is rough, flaky, itchy, or cracked.","In winter, heaters dry the air and our skin can crack, especially hands and heels.","What helps your dry skin?"],
["Sunscreen","lotion that protects skin from sunburn and damage.","Use SPF 30 or higher on face, ears, neck, and arms if you will be outside more than 15 minutes, even on cloudy days.","Do people with darker skin need sunscreen?"],
["Skin check","looking at your skin for changes.","A new mole, a sore that will not heal, a rash, or a red, warm spot should be shown to the nurse.","Why is it important to tell the nurse about a sore that won't heal?"],
["Pressure sores","sores that form when someone sits or lies in one position too long.","Changing position every couple of hours and keeping skin clean and dry help prevent them.","Who is at risk for pressure sores?"]],
story:"Ms. Opal's hands got so dry in the winter that they cracked and bled. She was embarrassed and hid them in her pockets. The nurse suggested putting on a thick cream after every hand wash and at bedtime, with cotton gloves on overnight. Within a week her hands felt soft again and she stopped hiding them.",
sq:["What problem did Ms. Opal have?","Why did she hide her hands?","What did the nurse suggest?","What skin problems do you deal with?"],
act:{n:"Hand Care Moment",m:"Lotion if available, paper towels",s:["Offer each person a small amount of lotion (check for allergies first).","Lead a slow hand massage: rub lotion on the backs of hands, between fingers, around each fingertip, and the palms.","Encourage everyone to breathe slowly while they do it.","Ask how their hands feel now.","Discuss times in the day they could do this."]},
d:["How did that feel?","Was it relaxing?","When could you do this on your own?"],
disc:[["How does water temperature affect skin?","Very hot water dries skin; warm is better."],["What foods and drinks help skin?","Water, fruits, vegetables."],["What should you do if you get a rash?","Tell the nurse; do not scratch."],["How can smoking affect skin?","Dryness, wrinkles, slow healing."],["What skin care tradition do you have?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Moisturize after washing, protect your skin from the sun, and show the nurse any skin changes.",
tryit:"Put on lotion right after your shower every day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Hygiene & Self-Care", [
{t:"Nail and Foot Care",
goal:"Today we will learn how to care for our nails and feet to prevent pain, infection, and falls.",
warm:["When did you last trim your toenails?","Do your feet ever hurt? When?","What kind of shoes are most comfortable for you?"],
terms:[
["Trimming nails","cutting nails short and straight across.","Cut toenails straight across, not rounded at the corners, to prevent ingrown toenails.","Why cut straight across?"],
["Ingrown toenail","when the corner of a nail grows into the skin, causing pain and redness.","A red, swollen, painful big toe could be ingrown. Tell the nurse; do not dig at it.","What causes ingrown toenails?"],
["Athlete's foot","a fungus that causes itchy, peeling skin between the toes.","It grows in warm, damp places like sweaty shoes and shower floors. Dry feet well and wear shower shoes.","How can you prevent athlete's foot?"],
["Daily foot check","looking at your feet every day for cuts, blisters, or color changes.","Especially important for people with diabetes, who may not feel a small cut.","Who needs to check their feet most carefully?"],
["Clean hands, clean nails","keeping fingernails short and clean to avoid spreading germs.","Germs hide under long or dirty nails and get on food.","How often do you clean under your nails?"]],
story:"Mr. Odell's toenails had grown so long they curled and pressed against his shoes. He started walking strangely to avoid the pain and almost fell. He was embarrassed to ask for help because he could not reach his feet anymore. When he finally told staff, they set him up with a foot care visit. He walked better right away and said he wished he had asked sooner.",
sq:["What problem did Mr. Odell have?","How did it affect his walking?","Why did he wait to ask for help?","Who can you ask for help with foot care?"],
act:{n:"Foot Care Checklist",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Together, write a weekly foot care checklist on the board.","Daily: look at feet, wash and dry between toes, clean socks.","Weekly: check nail length, lotion on heels (not between toes).","As needed: trim nails straight across, tell staff about any pain or sores.","Have residents copy the checklist and circle what they need help with."]},
d:["What part of foot care is hardest for you?","Who will you ask for help?","Why is checking your feet daily important?"],
disc:[["How do foot problems affect daily life?","Pain, trouble walking, falls, staying in the room."],["Why is it OK to ask for help with nails?","Reaching feet is hard for many people; it is about safety."],["What kinds of shoes protect feet?","Closed-toe, good fit, non-slip, supportive."],["Why should you never share nail clippers?","Fungus and infections can spread."],["What should you do if a cut on your foot is not healing?","Tell the nurse right away."]],
take:"Check your feet daily, keep them clean and dry, trim nails straight across, and ask for help when you need it.",
tryit:"Look at your feet tonight, and tell staff if you see anything that worries you."},

{t:"Shaving and Facial Grooming",
goal:"Today we will learn safe shaving and facial grooming habits to avoid cuts, bumps, and irritation.",
warm:["Do you shave, trim, or let it grow?","Have you ever gotten razor bumps or cuts?","What does a neat appearance mean to you?"],
terms:[
["Razor","a tool with blades used to remove hair.","Disposable razors should be replaced when dull, about every 5 to 7 shaves.","What happens when a razor is dull?"],
["Shaving cream or gel","a product that softens hair and protects skin while shaving.","Shaving dry skin causes more cuts and burns. Warm water and shaving cream help.","What do you use when you shave?"],
["Razor bumps","small, painful bumps from hairs curling back into the skin.","Common with curly hair. Shaving with the grain, using a sharp single-blade razor, or trimming instead of close shaving can help.","Have you ever had razor bumps?"],
["With the grain","shaving in the same direction the hair grows.","Shaving against the grain gives a closer shave but more irritation.","How can you tell which way hair grows?"],
["Electric clippers or trimmers","tools that cut hair short without a blade touching the skin.","A good option for people who get bumps or who have shaky hands.","Why might trimmers be safer for some people?"]],
story:"Tyrone shaved fast with an old dull razor and no shaving cream. His neck was always covered in red bumps that hurt and made him self-conscious. A staff member suggested he wash with warm water, use shaving gel, use a fresh razor, and shave in the direction his hair grew. Within a few weeks, the bumps were much better.",
sq:["What was causing Tyrone's bumps?","What changes did he make?","How did the bumps affect how he felt?","What tip from the story could help you?"],
act:{n:"Shaving Steps in Order",m:"Steps written on separate slips of paper, mixed up",s:["Before group, write these steps on separate slips: wash face with warm water; apply shaving cream; check razor is sharp; shave with the grain using short strokes; rinse blade often; rinse face with cool water; pat dry; apply unscented lotion.","Mix them up and hand them out to residents.","Ask the group to put themselves or the slips in the right order.","Talk about why each step matters.","Discuss safe storage of razors and never sharing them."]},
d:["Which step do people usually skip?","Why is rinsing the blade important?","Why should razors never be shared?"],
disc:[["How does grooming affect first impressions?","Interviews, appointments, meeting people."],["Why should razors be kept safe and not shared?","Blood-borne infections, safety."],["What can you do if your hands are shaky?","Use electric trimmers, ask for help."],["How should used razors be thrown away?","Cap them and put them in a safe container; ask staff."],["How do you feel after grooming?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Warm water, shaving cream, a sharp razor, and shaving with the grain make for a safer, smoother shave. Never share razors.",
tryit:"Next time you shave, try every step in the right order."},

{t:"Clean Clothes and Getting Dressed",
goal:"Today we will learn how to choose clean, appropriate clothes each day and keep track of what needs washing.",
warm:["What is your favorite outfit?","How do you decide what to wear each day?","How do clean clothes make you feel?"],
terms:[
["Clean vs. dirty","clean clothes have been washed and smell fresh. Dirty clothes have stains, smell, or have been worn.","Underwear and socks should be changed every day. Jeans can sometimes be worn a few times if they are not dirty.","Which clothes should be changed every day?"],
["Hamper","a basket for dirty clothes.","Putting dirty clothes in a hamper keeps your room neat and makes laundry day easier.","Where do you put dirty clothes now?"],
["Laying out clothes","choosing your clothes the night before.","Setting out tomorrow's outfit on a chair makes mornings faster and easier.","Would laying out clothes help you?"],
["Appropriate clothing","wearing clothes that fit the situation.","Pajamas are for bedtime. For a doctor visit or outing, wear clean daytime clothes and shoes.","What would you wear to church, a job interview, or a picnic?"],
["Fit","how well clothes match your size.","Clothes that are too tight can be uncomfortable; too-loose pants can be a trip hazard.","Do your clothes fit well right now?"]],
story:"Ms. Lena wore the same sweatshirt for over a week because it was comfortable. She did not notice the stains and smell, but others did. When staff gently mentioned it, she said she only had two shirts that fit. Staff helped her get a few more from the donation closet. Now she lays out a clean outfit every night and puts the worn one in her hamper.",
sq:["Why did Ms. Lena wear the same shirt?","What was the real problem?","How did staff help?","What habit does she have now?"],
act:{n:"Dress for the Day",m:"Pictures or a list of situations",s:["Read different situations: a doctor appointment, a rainy day walk, a job interview, a summer cookout, bedtime, a church service, a cold winter outing.","For each, ask the group what clothes and shoes would fit best.","Talk about layers, weather, and comfort.","Ask each resident to describe what they will wear tomorrow.","Encourage them to lay it out tonight."]},
d:["Which situation had the most different answers?","What did you learn about choosing clothes?","Will you lay out your clothes tonight?"],
disc:[["How do clothes affect how others treat us?","First impressions, respect."],["How do clothes affect our mood?","Feeling put-together can boost confidence."],["What can you do if you do not have enough clothes?","Ask staff, donation closets, thrift stores."],["Why is it important to change underwear and socks daily?","Odor, skin infections, health."],["What is your personal style?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Clean clothes every day, especially underwear and socks. Lay out tomorrow's outfit tonight, and ask for help if you need more clothes.",
tryit:"Lay out your clothes for tomorrow before bed every night this week."},

{t:"Dressing for the Weather",
goal:"Today we will learn how to dress safely for heat, cold, and rain to protect our health.",
warm:["What is your favorite season?","What is the worst weather you have ever been out in?","How do you check the weather each day?"],
terms:[
["Forecast","a prediction of the weather.","Checking the forecast on TV, a phone, or asking staff before going out: High 38, rain in the afternoon.","How do you find out the forecast?"],
["Layering","wearing several thin layers instead of one thick one.","A T-shirt, sweater, and jacket. If you get warm, take off a layer.","Why is layering helpful?"],
["Heat exhaustion","when the body gets too hot, causing heavy sweating, dizziness, nausea, and weakness.","On a 95-degree day, sitting in the sun without water can lead to heat exhaustion. Move to shade or AC, drink water, tell staff.","What are signs of getting too hot?"],
["Hypothermia","when the body gets too cold.","Shivering, confusion, slurred speech, and sleepiness in the cold are warning signs. Get indoors and tell staff.","Who is most at risk in the cold?"],
["Proper outerwear","coats, hats, gloves, raincoats, and boots that fit the weather.","Much body heat escapes from the head and hands, so a hat and gloves help a lot in winter.","What outerwear do you have?"]],
story:"On a cold January morning, Mr. Lamar walked to the store in a T-shirt and thin jacket because it looked sunny. By the time he got back, his hands were numb and he was shivering hard. Staff helped him warm up slowly with blankets and warm drinks. The next week, staff helped him get a warm coat, hat, and gloves from donations. He started checking the weather before going out.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Lamar dress lightly?","What happened to him?","How did staff help?","What does he do now before going out?"],
act:{n:"Pack for the Weather",m:"Board",s:["Write four weather types on the board: Hot and sunny, Cold and windy, Rainy, Mild and cool.","For each, ask the group to list what to wear and bring.","Hot: light clothes, hat, sunscreen, water. Cold: layers, coat, hat, gloves, warm socks. Rainy: raincoat, umbrella, shoes that stay dry. Mild: light jacket, layers.","Ask: What items do you not have right now?","Let staff know who needs weather gear."]},
d:["What items were mentioned most?","What do you need for winter?","How will you check the weather?"],
disc:[["Why can some medicines make heat or cold more dangerous?","Some affect sweating or temperature control; ask the nurse."],["What should you do if you see someone too hot or too cold?","Get them indoors or to shade and tell staff."],["How do you stay comfortable indoors when it is too warm or cold?","Layers, fans, blankets, tell staff."],["Why do people sometimes refuse a coat?","Do not feel cold, do not like how it looks, do not own one."],["Where can people get free coats in our community?","Coat drives, churches, donation closets."]],
take:"Check the weather, dress in layers, and protect your head, hands, and feet. In heat, drink water and find shade.",
tryit:"Check the forecast every morning this week before you get dressed."},

{t:"Keeping Your Bedroom Clean",
goal:"Today we will learn simple daily and weekly habits for a clean, safe, peaceful bedroom.",
warm:["What does your ideal bedroom look like?","How does a messy room make you feel?","What is the hardest chore in your room?"],
terms:[
["Clutter","too many things out of place.","Clothes on the floor, piles of papers, cups and wrappers on the dresser.","How does clutter affect how you feel?"],
["Daily tidy","five minutes of picking up each day.","Make the bed, put dirty clothes in the hamper, throw away trash, put cups in the kitchen.","Which of these could you do every day?"],
["Weekly clean","bigger cleaning once a week.","Change sheets, dust, wipe surfaces, sweep or vacuum, empty the trash.","What day could be your cleaning day?"],
["Food in the room","snacks and drinks left in a bedroom can attract bugs and mice.","Keep food in sealed containers and throw wrappers away right away.","Why is food in the room a problem in shared homes?"],
["A place for everything","giving each item a home so it is easy to put away.","Shoes under the bed, papers in a folder, toiletries in a basket.","What item never seems to have a home?"]],
story:"Shanice's room was so cluttered she could not find her ID for an appointment and missed it. She also had ants from old snacks. A staff member helped her pick one corner at a time. They got a basket for toiletries and a folder for papers. Now she does a five-minute tidy every night before bed. She says her room feels calm, and she sleeps better.",
sq:["What problems did the clutter cause?","How did they start cleaning?","What daily habit does Shanice have now?","How did a clean room change how she felt?"],
act:{n:"Five-Minute Tidy Plan",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Ask everyone to imagine their room right now.","Have them write 3 things they could do in 5 minutes to make it better.","Then pick one day a week for a bigger clean and write it down.","Ask: What supplies would you need? (trash bags, wipes, basket, hamper.)","Encourage residents to ask staff for any supplies they lack."]},
d:["What day did people choose for their weekly clean?","What is your first 5-minute task?","What supplies do you need?"],
disc:[["How does a clean room help mental health?","Less stress, easier to relax, easier to find things."],["How does a clean room help physical health?","Fewer germs, fewer bugs, fewer falls."],["What can you do when you feel too overwhelmed to clean?","Start with one small area, set a timer, ask for help."],["How can roommates share responsibility?","Agree on rules, split chores, talk respectfully."],["What makes your room feel like home?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Five minutes a day keeps clutter away. Give everything a home and clean one corner at a time.",
tryit:"Do a five-minute tidy in your room every night this week."},

{t:"Sharing Bathrooms and Common Spaces",
goal:"Today we will learn respectful habits for sharing bathrooms, the kitchen, and common rooms in a group home.",
warm:["Have you ever shared a home with lots of people? What was it like?","What bothers you most in a shared bathroom?","What makes a shared space feel welcoming?"],
terms:[
["Common space","rooms everyone shares, like the bathroom, kitchen, day room, and porch.","The TV room belongs to everyone, so the remote and the volume are shared too.","What common spaces do we have here?"],
["Clean as you go","cleaning up after yourself right away.","Wipe the sink after brushing, throw away your paper towel, rinse your dish.","Why is it easier to clean right away?"],
["Time limits","being mindful of how long you use a shared space.","If others are waiting, keep your shower to about 10 to 15 minutes.","How long is fair for a shower?"],
["Personal items","things that belong only to you and should not be left in shared spaces.","Take your toothbrush, razor, and towels back to your room.","Why should personal items not be left out?"],
["Speaking up respectfully","talking about a shared-space problem calmly.","Hey, could you please wipe the sink when you're done? Thanks. Or tell staff if it keeps happening.","How would you bring up a problem with a housemate?"]],
story:"In one house, the bathroom was always messy, and residents argued about it every day. During a house meeting, they made a simple bathroom checklist taped to the door: flush, wipe sink, hang towel, take your things, tell staff when supplies run low. Within two weeks, the arguments mostly stopped, and people said the house felt more peaceful.",
sq:["What was causing the arguments?","What did the house decide to do?","Why do you think the checklist worked?","What would you add to the checklist?"],
act:{n:"Our House Agreement",m:"Large paper, markers",s:["Ask the group: What rules would make our shared spaces better for everyone?","Write each idea on the large paper.","Make sure ideas are positive: Please wipe the sink instead of Don't be gross.","Vote on the top 5 ideas.","Offer to share the list with staff to post if the group agrees."]},
d:["Which rule got the most votes?","How does it feel to help make the rules?","How will we remind each other kindly?"],
disc:[["Why do shared spaces cause conflict?","Different habits, different standards, feeling disrespected."],["What should you do if someone keeps leaving a mess?","Talk kindly, then tell staff if it continues."],["How can we be patient when someone is struggling?","Remember everyone has bad days; offer help."],["What is your responsibility in a shared home?","Clean up after yourself, respect others' things and time."],["What makes you feel respected in a shared home?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Clean as you go, take your things with you, respect time limits, and speak up kindly. A peaceful home is everyone's job.",
tryit:"Every time you use the bathroom this week, leave it a little cleaner than you found it."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Hygiene & Self-Care", [
{t:"First Impressions and Looking Your Best",
goal:"Today we will learn how grooming and clothing help us make a good first impression at appointments, interviews, and community outings.",
warm:["What do you notice first when you meet someone new?","Have you ever dressed up for something special? What was it?","How do you feel when you know you look good?"],
terms:[
["First impression","the opinion people form in the first few seconds of meeting you.","A clean outfit, a smile, and a handshake can make a landlord or employer feel good about you right away.","What gives you a good first impression of someone?"],
["Neat and clean","clothes are clean and wrinkle-free, hair is combed, and nails are clean.","You do not need expensive clothes. Clean jeans and a pressed shirt look neat.","What does neat mean to you?"],
["Body language","what your body says without words.","Standing up straight, eye contact, and a smile say I'm confident and friendly. Slouching and looking at the floor say I don't want to be here.","What does your body language usually say?"],
["Dress for the occasion","choosing clothes that fit where you are going.","Job interview: clean slacks and a collared shirt or blouse. Court: neat and conservative. Park: casual is fine.","What would you wear to a job interview?"],
["Preparing ahead","getting your clothes and grooming ready the day before.","Iron your shirt, shine your shoes, and lay out your ID the night before an important day.","What could you get ready the night before?"]],
story:"Mr. Andre had an interview for a dishwasher job. The night before, he asked staff to help iron his shirt. He got a haircut, showered, and practiced a handshake. At the interview, the manager said, You look ready to work. Andre got the job. He said looking sharp made him feel more confident to answer the questions.",
sq:["How did Mr. Andre prepare?","What did the manager notice?","How did preparing affect his confidence?","What important event could you prepare for this way?"],
act:{n:"Handshake and Hello",m:"None",s:["Demonstrate a good greeting: stand up, smile, make eye contact, firm (not crushing) handshake, and say: Hello, my name is ____. Nice to meet you.","Pair residents up to practice.","Have each person practice three times, switching partners.","Practice an alternative for people who prefer not to shake hands: a nod and a smile.","Give lots of praise."]},
d:["How did it feel to practice?","What part felt most natural?","What part was hardest?"],
disc:[["Is it fair that people judge by appearance?","Not always fair, but it happens, and we can use it to our advantage."],["What can you do if you do not have nice clothes?","Donation closets, Goodwill, ask staff for help."],["Besides looks, what makes a great impression?","Kindness, being on time, honesty, a smile."],["How do you want people to see you?","Let residents share."],["What upcoming event do you want to look your best for?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Clean, neat, and a smile go a long way. Prepare the night before and walk in with confidence.",
tryit:"Practice your greeting in the mirror this week: smile, eye contact, and your name."},

{t:"Caring for Your Eyes and Glasses",
goal:"Today we will learn how to take care of our eyes and glasses, and when to get our vision checked.",
warm:["Do you wear glasses or contacts?","How is your eyesight these days?","When was your last eye exam?"],
terms:[
["Eye exam","a checkup that tests your vision and the health of your eyes.","Adults should have an eye exam every 1 to 2 years, or more often with diabetes or high blood pressure.","Why do people skip eye exams?"],
["Prescription glasses","glasses made just for your eyes.","Reading glasses from the store can help with small print, but they do not fix all vision problems.","What is the difference between store readers and prescription glasses?"],
["Cleaning glasses","washing lenses gently so they stay clear and do not scratch.","Use warm water and a drop of dish soap, then dry with a soft cotton cloth. Shirts and paper towels can scratch.","How do you usually clean your glasses?"],
["Eye strain","tired, sore eyes from focusing too long.","Staring at a phone for hours can cause headaches. Try the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds.","When do your eyes feel tired?"],
["Warning signs","eye changes that need quick attention.","Sudden blurry vision, seeing flashes or a curtain over your vision, eye pain, or a red eye with discharge. Tell staff right away.","Who would you tell if your vision changed suddenly?"]],
story:"Ms. Patrice kept tripping and could not read her mail. She thought her eyes were just getting old. When staff arranged an eye exam, she learned she needed new glasses and had early cataracts. With the right glasses, she could read again and stopped bumping into things. She told the group, I didn't know what I was missing.",
sq:["What signs showed Ms. Patrice needed help?","What did she think was happening?","What did the exam show?","How did new glasses change her life?"],
act:{n:"Glasses Care Demo and Vision Check-In",m:"A pair of glasses, mild soap, soft cloth (optional)",s:["Demonstrate cleaning glasses: rinse with warm water, a tiny drop of dish soap, rub gently, rinse, dry with a soft cloth.","Show safe storage: in a case, lenses up, never face down on a table.","Ask each resident: Can you read the print on today's handout clearly? Can you see faces across the room?","Encourage anyone with trouble to tell staff so an eye exam can be scheduled.","Try the 20-20-20 rule together: look at something far away for 20 seconds."]},
d:["Did you learn a new way to clean glasses?","Does anyone need an eye exam?","How did the 20-20-20 rule feel?"],
disc:[["How can poor vision affect safety?","Falls, medicine mistakes, missing signs."],["How can poor vision affect mood?","Frustration, isolation, not enjoying TV or reading."],["Why should you not share glasses?","Different prescriptions can cause headaches and poor vision."],["Where can people get help paying for glasses?","Insurance, Lions Club programs, ask the social worker."],["What do you love to look at?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Get your eyes checked regularly, keep glasses clean and in a case, and tell staff right away about sudden vision changes.",
tryit:"Clean your glasses the right way and keep them in a case this week. If you need an eye exam, tell staff."},

{t:"Washing Sheets and Towels",
goal:"Today we will learn how often to wash bedding and towels, and why it matters for our health and sleep.",
warm:["How often do you change your sheets?","Is there anything better than fresh sheets?","Where do you hang your towel after a shower?"],
terms:[
["Bedding","sheets, pillowcases, and blankets.","Sheets should be washed about once a week. Pillowcases more often if you have oily skin or acne.","When were your sheets last washed?"],
["Dust mites","tiny bugs that live in bedding and feed on dead skin.","They are too small to see but can cause sneezing and itchy eyes. Weekly washing in warm water helps.","How do dust mites make people feel?"],
["Towel care","drying towels fully between uses and washing them often.","Hang your towel spread out to dry. Wash bath towels after about three uses. A damp towel in a pile grows mildew and smells.","Why should towels dry fully?"],
["Stripping the bed","taking off all sheets and pillowcases to wash them.","Pick one day, like Sunday, as sheet day.","What day could be your sheet day?"],
["Accidents happen","handling a spill or accident in the bed without shame.","If the bed gets wet or soiled, tell staff quietly. They will help change it. It happens to many people.","How can staff help make this less embarrassing?"]],
story:"Mr. Keith had acne on his face and itchy skin and could not figure out why. Staff noticed his pillowcase had not been changed in a month. They helped him set Sunday as sheet day. After a few weeks, his skin cleared up a lot and he said the fresh sheets helped him sleep better too.",
sq:["What problems did Mr. Keith have?","What did staff notice?","What changed after he set sheet day?","What day could you choose?"],
act:{n:"Make the Bed Relay",m:"A sheet and pillowcase if available, or demonstration",s:["If a bed or table is available, demonstrate putting on a fitted sheet, top sheet, and pillowcase.","Show a simple trick: corners first, then tuck the sides.","Invite volunteers to try putting a pillowcase on.","Talk about where clean sheets are kept and how to ask for them.","Have each resident say out loud which day will be their sheet day."]},
d:["What day did you choose?","What part of making a bed is hardest?","Who can help you if you need it?"],
disc:[["How does clean bedding affect sleep?","Feels fresh, fewer allergies, more comfortable."],["What should you do if your bed has bugs?","Tell staff right away so they can treat it."],["Why should towels never be shared?","Germs, skin infections."],["How can you make your bed feel cozy?","A favorite blanket, clean pillows, a made bed."],["How can we talk about bed accidents with kindness?","No teasing, privacy, quick help."]],
take:"Wash sheets weekly, dry towels fully, and tell staff about any bed problems without shame.",
tryit:"Choose your sheet day and change your sheets on that day this week."},

{t:"Self-Care on Hard Days",
goal:"Today we will talk about how low mood affects self-care and find small, doable steps to care for ourselves even on hard days.",
warm:["What does a hard day look like for you?","What is the first thing that slips when you are feeling low?","What helps you get out of bed on a tough morning?"],
terms:[
["Low energy","feeling tired, heavy, or unmotivated.","Even brushing your teeth can feel like a mountain on a depressed day.","Have you ever felt this way?"],
["Minimum routine","the smallest set of self-care steps you can do on your worst days.","Get up, take medicine, brush teeth, drink water, change your shirt.","What would your minimum routine be?"],
["Self-compassion","being kind to yourself the way you would be kind to a friend.","Instead of I'm lazy, say I'm having a hard day, and I'm doing what I can.","What would you say to a friend having a hard day?"],
["Asking for help","letting someone know you are struggling.","Telling a caregiver, I'm having a tough day. Can you check on me later?","Who do you feel safe asking?"],
["Small wins","celebrating little accomplishments.","I showered today! counts as a win on a hard day.","What small win did you have this week?"]],
story:"Ms. Lynn had days when she could not make herself get out of bed. She would skip showers for days and then feel ashamed. Her counselor helped her make a minimum routine: take medicine, brush teeth, drink a glass of water, and sit by the window for five minutes. On hard days, she only did those four things and told herself it was enough. Over time, hard days did not pull her down as far.",
sq:["What was hard for Ms. Lynn?","What was in her minimum routine?","How did the routine change how she felt about herself?","What would you put in your minimum routine?"],
act:{n:"My Hard Day Plan",m:"Index cards or paper, pens",s:["Give each resident a card.","On one side, write MY HARD DAY PLAN and list 3 to 5 tiny self-care steps.","On the other side, write one kind sentence to tell yourself, like I am doing my best.","Add the name of one person they can ask for help.","Suggest keeping the card by the bed."]},
d:["What steps did people choose?","What kind sentence did you write?","Where will you keep your card?"],
disc:[["Why do we judge ourselves harshly on hard days?","Old messages, shame, comparing to others."],["How can staff tell when someone is struggling?","Missing meals, staying in the room, poor hygiene."],["What could a friend do to help without nagging?","Invite them, sit with them, bring water."],["When should a hard day be reported to the nurse or counselor?","When it lasts many days, or there are thoughts of self-harm."],["What gives you hope on hard days?","Let residents share."]],
take:"On hard days, do the minimum and call it a win. Be as kind to yourself as you would be to a friend, and ask for help.",
tryit:"Keep your Hard Day Plan card by your bed and use it when you need it."},

{t:"Personal Care Supplies on a Budget",
goal:"Today we will learn how to keep a steady supply of hygiene items without overspending.",
warm:["What hygiene item do you use the most?","Have you ever run out of something like soap or toothpaste? What did you do?","Where do you usually buy personal care items?"],
terms:[
["Toiletries","personal care items like soap, shampoo, toothpaste, deodorant, and lotion.","A basic monthly toiletry list might be: soap, shampoo, toothpaste, deodorant, lotion, razors.","What toiletries do you need each month?"],
["Running low","noticing an item is almost empty before it runs out.","When your toothpaste is getting flat, add it to your shopping list right away.","How can you tell when you are running low?"],
["Dollar store and store brands","cheaper places and products that work just as well.","Store-brand shampoo might be $1.25 compared to $6 for a name brand.","What store brand toiletries do you like?"],
["Making it last","using the right amount so products last longer.","A pea-sized amount of toothpaste and a quarter-sized amount of shampoo are enough.","What products do people often use too much of?"],
["Donation closet","a place where donated items are given to people who need them.","Ask staff if there are donated toiletries available when money is tight.","Who could you ask about donated items?"]],
story:"Every month, Marcus spent $40 on toiletries at the corner store and then ran out of money. A staff member took him to a dollar store with a list. He bought the same items, mostly store brands, for $14. He also learned to use less shampoo. His supplies lasted the whole month, and he had more money for other things.",
sq:["Where did Marcus shop at first?","How much did he save?","What two changes helped him?","What could you do with the money you save?"],
act:{n:"Build a Toiletry List",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident make a list of the toiletries they need each month.","Next to each item, write how long it usually lasts.","Have them circle items they could switch to a store brand.","Estimate the monthly cost together using dollar-store prices.","Talk about keeping a backup of the most important items."]},
d:["How much might your list cost?","What item will you switch to store brand?","What backup item is most important to you?"],
disc:[["Why is it stressful to run out of hygiene items?","Embarrassment, feeling dirty, missing activities."],["What can you do if you run out before the end of the month?","Tell staff, check donations, borrow only with permission."],["Is it OK to share toiletries?","Some are OK with permission, like lotion; never toothbrushes or razors."],["How can you remember what you need?","A list on the mirror, check every Sunday."],["Which products are worth paying more for?","Let residents share their opinions."]],
take:"Make a list, watch for running low, choose store brands, and use just the right amount.",
tryit:"Check your toiletries this week and write down anything that is running low."},

{t:"Hygiene When You Are Sick",
goal:"Today we will learn how to take care of ourselves and protect others when we are sick.",
warm:["What do you like to have nearby when you are sick?","Is it hard to take care of yourself when you feel bad?","How do you let others know you are not feeling well?"],
terms:[
["Contagious","able to spread an illness to others.","With the flu, people are usually most contagious in the first few days. Staying apart helps protect housemates.","What illnesses are contagious?"],
["Tissue habits","using a tissue once and throwing it away, then washing hands.","Stuffing used tissues in your pocket or leaving them on tables spreads germs.","Where should used tissues go?"],
["Rest and fluids","sleeping and drinking water, broth, or tea to help your body heal.","When you have a fever, your body loses water, so drinking fluids is very important.","What fluids do you like when you are sick?"],
["Washing after illness","cleaning sheets, towels, and surfaces after being sick.","After a stomach bug, staff should help clean the bathroom and wash bedding in hot water.","Why wash bedding after being sick?"],
["Telling staff","letting staff know you are sick so they can help and call the nurse.","Staff can bring meals, check your temperature, and make sure you get care.","Why might someone hide that they are sick?"]],
story:"Mr. Ricky had a stomach bug but did not tell anyone. He kept using the shared bathroom without cleaning and went to dinner. Within days, three other residents were sick. The next time Ricky felt sick, he told staff right away. They helped him rest in his room, brought fluids, and cleaned the bathroom. No one else got sick.",
sq:["What did Mr. Ricky do the first time?","What happened to the other residents?","What did he do differently the second time?","How did that protect others?"],
act:{n:"Sick Day Kit",m:"Board",s:["Ask: What should be in a sick day kit? Write answers on the board.","Make sure to include: tissues, a trash bag, hand sanitizer, water bottle, a thermometer (staff), crackers, a list of who to tell.","Discuss: What does staff do for you when you are sick?","Practice what to say: I'm not feeling well. I think I have a fever. Can you tell the nurse?","Remind everyone it is never a bother to report being sick."]},
d:["What items were most important?","What will you say when you feel sick?","How can we help a sick housemate?"],
disc:[["Why is it hard to keep up hygiene when sick?","No energy, feeling bad."],["How can staff help with hygiene when you are sick?","Help with bathing, clean sheets, bring supplies."],["What should you avoid sharing when sick?","Cups, food, utensils, towels."],["When should a sick person see the nurse or doctor?","High fever, trouble breathing, cannot keep fluids down, getting worse."],["How can we be kind to someone who is sick?","Check on them through staff, give them space, bring fluids."]],
take:"When you are sick, tell staff, rest, drink fluids, throw away tissues, wash your hands, and stay apart to protect others.",
tryit:"Remember the words: I'm not feeling well, can you tell the nurse? Use them the next time you feel sick."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Independent Living Skills", [
{t:"Doing Your Own Laundry",
goal:"Today we will learn the steps to wash, dry, and fold laundry so our clothes stay clean and last longer.",
warm:["Who taught you how to do laundry?","What is the worst laundry mistake you ever made?","What is your least favorite part of doing laundry?"],
terms:[
["Sorting","separating clothes into piles before washing.","Whites in one pile, darks in another, and towels in a third. A red sock in a white load can turn everything pink.","Why do we sort laundry?"],
["Care label","the small tag inside clothing that tells how to wash it.","It might say Machine wash cold, tumble dry low, or Hand wash only.","Have you ever looked at a care label?"],
["Detergent","the soap made for washing clothes.","Use the amount the cap shows for your load size. Too much leaves clothes stiff and soapy.","What happens if you use too much detergent?"],
["Water temperature","hot, warm, or cold water settings.","Cold is good for most clothes and saves money. Hot is good for towels, sheets, and very dirty items.","What would you wash in hot water?"],
["Lint trap","the screen in the dryer that catches fuzz.","Clean it before every load. A full lint trap is a fire risk and makes clothes take longer to dry.","Why is a full lint trap dangerous?"]],
story:"The first time Jasmine did laundry on her own, she put everything in one load with lots of detergent on hot. Her new black jeans faded, a sweater shrank, and her white shirts turned gray. A staff member walked her through sorting and reading care labels. The next week her load came out clean and bright, and she was proud to fold it herself.",
sq:["What mistakes did Jasmine make?","What happened to her clothes?","What did staff teach her?","How did she feel the second time?"],
act:{n:"Laundry Step Sort",m:"Steps written on slips of paper",s:["Before group, write steps on slips: gather dirty clothes, check pockets, sort by color, read care labels, add detergent, choose water temperature, start washer, move clothes to dryer, clean lint trap, start dryer, fold and put away.","Mix them up and hand them out.","Have the group put them in order together.","Talk about checking pockets (pens, tissues, money).","Ask who wants help doing a load with staff this week."]},
d:["Which step do people forget most?","What happens if you skip checking pockets?","Who feels ready to do a load on their own?"],
disc:[["How often should you do laundry?","Usually once a week, or when you run low on clean underwear and socks."],["Why does doing your own laundry build independence?","It is a skill you need to live on your own."],["How do you handle laundry in a shared home politely?","Move your clothes promptly, do not leave them in the machine."],["What should you do with clothes that need special care?","Read the label; hand wash or ask staff."],["How can you save money on laundry?","Full loads, cold water, right amount of detergent."]],
take:"Sort, check pockets, read labels, use the right amount of detergent, and clean the lint trap every time.",
tryit:"Do one load of laundry this week, with staff help if needed, from start to folded."},

{t:"Kitchen Safety",
goal:"Today we will learn how to stay safe in the kitchen and prevent burns, cuts, fires, and food sickness.",
warm:["What is your favorite thing to cook or eat?","Have you ever burned yourself cooking?","What kitchen rules did you grow up with?"],
terms:[
["Stay with the stove","never leaving food cooking on the stove unattended.","Leaving a pan of grease to answer the phone is how many kitchen fires start.","Why is it dangerous to walk away from the stove?"],
["Grease fire","a fire in a pan with oil or grease.","Never pour water on a grease fire. Put a lid on it, turn off the burner, and get staff. Call 911 if it spreads.","What should you NEVER do with a grease fire?"],
["Knife safety","handling knives carefully.","Cut away from your body, use a cutting board, keep fingers curled under, and never try to catch a falling knife.","What is the safest way to cut?"],
["Handle in","turning pot handles toward the back of the stove.","A handle sticking out can be bumped, spilling hot food on you.","Why should handles face in?"],
["Hot surfaces","using oven mitts and assuming things are hot.","Pans, microwave bowls, and oven racks stay hot for minutes. Use a mitt, not a wet towel.","Why not use a wet towel?"]],
story:"Mr. Hank was frying chicken when he stepped out of the kitchen to watch the end of a ballgame. The oil caught fire. He panicked and threw a cup of water on it, and the flames shot up. Staff used the fire extinguisher and no one was hurt, but the kitchen was damaged. Hank later said, I didn't know water made it worse. Now he stays with the stove and knows to use a lid.",
sq:["What two mistakes did Mr. Hank make?","Why did water make the fire worse?","What should he have done?","What would you do if you saw a grease fire?"],
act:{n:"Safe or Unsafe Kitchen",m:"None",s:["Read each situation and have residents say SAFE or UNSAFE and why.","Pot handle sticking out over the edge. (Unsafe.)","Using a cutting board and curling fingers under. (Safe.)","Wearing loose sleeves over the burner. (Unsafe.)","Putting a lid on a small pan fire and turning off the heat. (Safe.)","Putting metal or foil in the microwave. (Unsafe.)","Wiping up a spill right away. (Safe.)","Leaving a dish towel next to a burner. (Unsafe.)"]},
d:["Which one surprised you?","What kitchen habit will you change?","Do you know where the fire extinguisher is here?"],
disc:[["What are the most common kitchen injuries?","Burns, cuts, slips, fires."],["What should you do if you get a small burn?","Cool running water for 10 minutes, tell staff; no butter or ice."],["What should you do if you cut yourself?","Press with a clean cloth, tell staff."],["Why do kitchen rules matter in a shared home?","Everyone's safety depends on them."],["What kitchen skill would you like to learn?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Stay with the stove, turn handles in, cut away from yourself, and never put water on a grease fire.",
tryit:"Find where the fire extinguisher is in this building and point it out to a staff member."},

{t:"Grocery Shopping with a List",
goal:"Today we will learn how to plan a grocery trip, make a list, and stay on budget.",
warm:["What store do you like to shop at?","What do you always end up buying even when it is not on your list?","Do you like grocery shopping? Why or why not?"],
terms:[
["Grocery list","a written list of foods and items you need.","Bread, peanut butter, bananas, milk, eggs, frozen vegetables.","How does a list save money?"],
["Store layout","how a store is arranged.","Fresh foods like fruit, vegetables, meat, and milk are usually around the outside walls. Snacks and boxes are in the middle aisles.","Why are milk and eggs often at the back of the store?"],
["Perishable","food that spoils quickly.","Milk, bread, meat, and fresh fruit go bad in days. Buy only what you will eat soon.","What perishable foods do you buy?"],
["Shelf-stable","food that lasts a long time without a fridge.","Canned beans, rice, pasta, peanut butter, and oatmeal.","Why is it good to keep some shelf-stable food?"],
["Expiration date","the date printed on food that tells you when it is best or safe to eat by.","Check milk and meat dates. Grab from the back of the shelf, where newer items often are.","Where do you find the expiration date?"]],
story:"Kenya went grocery shopping hungry and without a list. She came home with chips, cookies, soda, and frozen pizza, and spent $45. By Wednesday, she had nothing healthy to eat. The next week, she ate lunch first, wrote a list, and stuck to it. She spent $32 and had food for the whole week.",
sq:["What two things made Kenya overspend?","What did she do differently?","How much did she save?","What tip will you use?"],
act:{n:"Plan a $30 Grocery Trip",m:"Board, paper, pens",s:["Tell the group: You have $30 for a week of breakfasts and lunches.","Ask them to call out foods. Write each with an estimated price (for example: bread $2, peanut butter $3, bananas $2, eggs $3, oatmeal $3, milk $3, canned soup $1.50 each).","Add up the list as you go.","If it goes over $30, ask the group what to swap or remove.","Have residents copy a list they could actually use."]},
d:["Was it hard to stay under $30?","What foods gave the most meals for the money?","What did the group decide to remove?"],
disc:[["Why should you not shop hungry?","You buy more snacks and impulse items."],["What foods give the most nutrition for the money?","Eggs, beans, rice, oats, bananas, frozen vegetables, peanut butter."],["How can you avoid food going to waste?","Buy only what you will eat, check dates, store food right."],["What programs help with groceries?","SNAP/EBT, food banks, church pantries; ask the social worker."],["What is one healthy food you would like to buy more of?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Eat first, make a list, stick to it, and check the dates. Your money and your body will thank you.",
tryit:"Before your next trip to the store, write a list and only buy what is on it."},

{t:"Simple Meals Without a Stove",
goal:"Today we will learn easy, healthy meals you can make with a microwave or no cooking at all.",
warm:["What is the easiest meal you know how to make?","Do you feel comfortable using a microwave?","What is your favorite sandwich?"],
terms:[
["No-cook meal","a meal you can put together without heating anything.","A peanut butter and banana sandwich, tuna salad on crackers, or yogurt with fruit and granola.","What no-cook meal do you like?"],
["Microwave-safe","dishes that can safely go in the microwave.","Glass and microwave-safe plastic are OK. Metal, foil, and some takeout containers are not and can spark.","How do you know if a dish is microwave-safe?"],
["Balanced plate","a meal with protein, a grain or starch, and a fruit or vegetable.","A turkey sandwich (protein and grain) with baby carrots and an apple.","What could you add to a sandwich to balance the plate?"],
["Steam vent","leaving a gap so steam can escape when microwaving.","Cover the bowl loosely or leave one corner of the lid open. Stir halfway through so food heats evenly.","Why stir halfway?"],
["Food prep","getting food ready ahead of time.","Washing and cutting fruit on Sunday so it is ready to grab all week.","What could you prep ahead?"]],
story:"When Mr. Lewis moved into his first apartment, he did not have a working stove for two weeks. He thought he would have to eat fast food every day. A friend showed him how to make microwave oatmeal, scrambled eggs in a mug, baked potatoes, and frozen vegetables in the microwave. He ate well, spent less, and learned he could cook more than he thought.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Lewis think he would eat fast food?","What meals did he learn?","How did this help his budget?","Which of those meals would you try?"],
act:{n:"Build-a-Meal",m:"Board divided into Protein, Grain, Fruit or Vegetable",s:["Draw three columns: Protein, Grain/Starch, Fruit/Vegetable.","Have the group brainstorm no-cook or microwave items for each column.","Protein: eggs, tuna, peanut butter, beans, cheese, yogurt, chicken from a can. Grain: bread, crackers, oatmeal, tortillas, rice cups. Fruit/Vegetable: banana, apple, baby carrots, frozen veggies, canned fruit.","Ask each resident to pick one from each column to build a meal.","Share a few combinations out loud."]},
d:["What was the most creative meal?","Which meal would you actually make?","Was it easier than you expected?"],
disc:[["Why do people rely on fast food?","Convenient, do not know how to cook, tired."],["How much can you save by making simple meals?","A $10 fast-food meal versus a $2 homemade meal."],["What microwave safety rules matter most?","No metal, vent steam, use a mitt, stir food."],["How can simple meals still be healthy?","Add fruits and vegetables, choose protein."],["What simple meal did your family make growing up?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You do not need a stove to eat well. With a few basics and a microwave, you can build a balanced, cheap meal.",
tryit:"Try making one simple meal or snack this week with staff support."},

{t:"Storing Food and Leftovers Safely",
goal:"Today we will learn how to store food so it stays fresh and does not make us sick.",
warm:["Have you ever gotten sick from food?","How long do you think leftovers are good for?","What is the strangest thing you ever found in a fridge?"],
terms:[
["Food poisoning","sickness from eating food with harmful germs.","Stomach cramps, vomiting, and diarrhea a few hours after eating old chicken salad.","What foods do you think cause food poisoning most often?"],
["Two-hour rule","perishable food should not sit out more than 2 hours (1 hour if it is very hot outside).","Leftover pizza left on the counter overnight should be thrown away.","Why do germs grow when food sits out?"],
["Refrigerator temperature","the fridge should be 40 degrees or colder.","A fridge thermometer helps check. Do not overpack the fridge; air needs to move.","Why does cold slow germs?"],
["Leftover timeline","most cooked leftovers are safe in the fridge for 3 to 4 days.","Label the container with the date: Spaghetti, Mon 11/16.","How can labeling help?"],
["When in doubt, throw it out","if food smells bad, looks strange, or you are not sure how old it is, do not eat it.","Mold on bread, slimy lunch meat, or sour milk should go in the trash.","Why is this rule important?"]],
story:"Tasha found a container of chicken and rice in the back of the fridge. She was not sure how old it was, but it smelled OK, so she heated it and ate it. That night she was up sick for hours. The nurse said it was likely food poisoning. Tasha started labeling her leftovers with the date and cleaning out her fridge shelf every Friday.",
sq:["What mistake did Tasha make?","Why is smell not always a good test?","What did she change?","What habit could you use?"],
act:{n:"Fridge or Trash?",m:"None",s:["Read each item and ask: keep it or throw it out?","Leftover pasta from 2 days ago, in a sealed container. (Keep.)","Chicken left on the counter overnight. (Trash.)","Bread with a few spots of mold. (Trash.)","Lunch meat opened 10 days ago. (Trash; most last 3 to 5 days once opened.)","Apples on the counter for a week, still firm. (Keep.)","Milk past the date that smells sour. (Trash.)","Discuss each answer."]},
d:["Which answer surprised you?","Do you label your leftovers?","What will you check in your fridge space this week?"],
disc:[["Who is most at risk from food poisoning?","Older adults, people with weak immune systems, pregnant people."],["How can you prevent food waste?","Buy less, eat leftovers first, freeze extras."],["How should you thaw frozen meat safely?","In the fridge, not on the counter."],["What should you do if you think you have food poisoning?","Tell staff, drink fluids, see the nurse if it is severe."],["How do we share a fridge respectfully?","Label food, keep your space clean, do not take others' food."]],
take:"Refrigerate within 2 hours, label leftovers with the date, eat them within 3 to 4 days, and when in doubt, throw it out.",
tryit:"Label any food you put in the fridge this week with your name and the date."},

{t:"Riding the Bus and Getting Around",
goal:"Today we will learn how to use public transportation and other ways to get around the community safely.",
warm:["How do you usually get where you need to go?","Have you ever ridden a city bus? How was it?","Where would you go if you could get there easily?"],
terms:[
["Public transportation","buses and other rides open to everyone for a fare.","In Chattanooga, CARTA runs city buses and the free electric shuttle downtown.","Have you ridden CARTA?"],
["Route and schedule","the path a bus takes and the times it stops.","Route 4 might stop at the corner every 30 minutes. Check the schedule or app so you are not waiting long.","Where can you find a bus schedule?"],
["Fare","the cost to ride.","Have exact change or a bus pass ready. Ask about reduced fares for seniors and people with disabilities.","Who might qualify for a reduced fare?"],
["Paratransit","a door-to-door ride service for people who cannot use regular buses because of a disability.","CARTA's Care-A-Van picks people up at home if they qualify and schedule ahead.","How could paratransit help someone here?"],
["Travel plan","knowing where you are going, how to get there and back, and who to call if something goes wrong.","Write down the bus number, the stop, the return time, and the facility phone number.","What should be in a travel plan?"]],
story:"Ms. Yolanda wanted to visit the library but was nervous about the bus. A staff member rode with her the first time, showed her how to pay, and pointed out landmarks near her stop. The second time, Yolanda went alone with a written plan and the facility phone number in her pocket. Now she goes every Tuesday and says it gave her a new piece of freedom.",
sq:["Why was Ms. Yolanda nervous?","How did staff help her?","What did she carry on her own?","What place would you like to go to by bus?"],
act:{n:"Plan a Trip",m:"Paper, pens; a printed bus map or app on a phone if available",s:["Choose a destination together: the library, a grocery store, the park, or a clinic.","Have the group fill in a travel plan: Where am I going? What bus or ride? Where is the stop? What time does it leave? What does it cost? What time will I return? Who do I call if I get lost?","If a phone is available, show how to look up the route on Google Maps or the CARTA app.","Discuss what to do if you miss your stop.","Have residents copy the plan for their own future trip."]},
d:["What part of planning was new to you?","What would you do if you missed your stop?","What destination did people pick?"],
disc:[["What makes using the bus stressful?","Crowds, getting lost, safety, not knowing how to pay."],["How can you stay safe on the bus?","Sit near the driver, keep belongings close, stay alert, do not share personal info."],["What should you always check before leaving the facility?","Sign out, tell staff your plan, take your phone and the facility number."],["What are other ways to get around?","Rides from staff, family, Medicaid transportation for appointments, walking, paratransit."],["How does getting around on your own build independence?","Freedom, confidence, access to community."]],
take:"Plan your trip, carry your return plan and the facility number, and tell staff where you are going.",
tryit:"Write a travel plan for one place you would like to go, and talk it over with staff."},

{t:"Keeping Track of Appointments",
goal:"Today we will learn how to use a calendar and reminders so we never miss important appointments.",
warm:["How do you remember important dates?","Have you ever missed an appointment? What happened?","Do you prefer paper calendars or phone reminders?"],
terms:[
["Calendar","a chart of days and months where you write events.","On the 14th, write: Dr. Jones 10 a.m., bring med list.","Where could you keep a calendar so you see it every day?"],
["Reminder","something that helps you remember, like an alarm or a note.","A phone alarm the day before and the morning of, or a sticky note on your mirror.","What reminders work best for you?"],
["Confirm","checking that an appointment is still on.","Many offices call or text to confirm. Reply or call back so they keep your spot.","Why do offices ask you to confirm?"],
["Reschedule","changing an appointment to a new time.","If you are sick, call as early as you can and say, I need to reschedule.","Why is it better to reschedule than to just not show up?"],
["No-show","missing an appointment without calling.","Some offices charge a fee or stop seeing people who no-show several times.","What could happen after several no-shows?"]],
story:"Mr. Glenn missed three psychiatrist appointments in a row because he forgot. The office said one more miss and he would have to find a new doctor. Staff helped him get a wall calendar and set two phone alarms for each appointment. He has not missed one since, and his care has been steadier.",
sq:["What happened because Mr. Glenn kept missing appointments?","What tools helped him?","Why did he need two alarms?","How did his care improve?"],
act:{n:"Fill in the Calendar",m:"Blank month calendar printouts, pens",s:["Hand out blank calendars.","Read pretend appointments and have residents write them in: Dentist on the 5th at 9 a.m. Group outing on the 12th at 1 p.m. Doctor on the 20th at 2:30 p.m. Birthday of a family member on the 25th.","Show how to write a reminder the day before each appointment.","Talk about writing what to bring.","Encourage residents to write their real appointments on a calendar with staff."]},
d:["Was it easy to use the calendar?","Where will you keep yours?","What will you use for reminders?"],
disc:[["Why do people miss appointments?","Forgetting, transportation, anxiety, feeling well."],["What should you do the day before an appointment?","Check the time, plan your ride, gather your papers."],["How can staff help you keep track?","Shared calendar, reminders, rides."],["How does keeping appointments help your health?","Steady care, medicine refills, problems caught early."],["What could you say when calling to reschedule?","Hi, this is ___. I have an appointment on ___ and need to reschedule."]],
take:"Write it down, set reminders, confirm, and call to reschedule if you cannot go. Showing up keeps your care strong.",
tryit:"Write your next appointment on a calendar and set a reminder for the day before."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Independent Living Skills", [
{t:"Cleaning the Kitchen",
goal:"Today we will learn how to clean a kitchen step by step to prevent germs, bugs, and bad smells.",
warm:["What do you think is the dirtiest place in a kitchen?","Do you like to clean as you cook or after?","What cleaning products do you know how to use?"],
terms:[
["Sanitize","killing germs on a surface, not just wiping off crumbs.","After cutting raw chicken, wash the board with hot soapy water, then use a sanitizing spray or wipe.","What is the difference between cleaning and sanitizing?"],
["Dish routine","washing dishes soon after eating.","Scrape food into the trash, rinse, wash with hot soapy water, rinse, and dry. Do not let them pile up.","Why do piled-up dishes cause problems?"],
["Sponge care","keeping sponges and dishcloths from growing germs.","Replace sponges every week or two and let them dry out. Wash dishcloths often.","How often do you change your sponge?"],
["Counters and stovetop","wiping down surfaces after each use.","Wipe crumbs and spills right away so they do not get sticky or attract ants.","Why do crumbs attract bugs?"],
["Trash routine","emptying the trash before it overflows or smells.","Take it out when it is full or at least every couple of days, and wipe the can if it gets dirty.","How often should kitchen trash go out?"]],
story:"In one house, dishes piled up in the sink for days. Soon there were fruit flies and a bad smell, and residents argued about whose dishes they were. The house decided everyone washes their own dishes within 30 minutes of eating, and one person each night wipes counters. The flies disappeared and so did most of the arguments.",
sq:["What problems did the dirty dishes cause?","What rule did the house make?","Why did it help with arguments?","Would that rule work here?"],
act:{n:"Kitchen Cleaning Order",m:"Board",s:["Ask the group to list every step to clean a kitchen after dinner.","Write them in order: clear the table, scrape plates, wash dishes, dry and put away, wipe table, wipe counters, wipe stovetop, sweep floor, take out trash if full, rinse sink.","Ask: Which steps take the longest?","Talk about cleaning top to bottom (counters before floors).","Invite volunteers to sign up with staff to help clean the kitchen after one meal this week."]},
d:["Why clean from top to bottom?","Which step do you think gets skipped most?","Who volunteered to help?"],
disc:[["How does a clean kitchen protect health?","Fewer germs, less food poisoning, fewer bugs."],["How can a dirty kitchen affect mood in a shared home?","Frustration, arguments, feeling disrespected."],["What is a fair way to share kitchen chores?","Chore chart, take turns, clean your own dishes."],["What cleaning products should never be mixed?","Bleach and ammonia; bleach and vinegar; they make toxic gas."],["What kitchen cleaning skill would help you live on your own?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Wash dishes soon, wipe counters right away, sanitize after raw meat, and never mix bleach with other cleaners.",
tryit:"Wash your own dishes within 30 minutes of eating every day this week."},

{t:"Using the Phone and Leaving Messages",
goal:"Today we will practice making phone calls, leaving clear voicemails, and handling calls with confidence.",
warm:["Do you like talking on the phone? Why or why not?","Who do you call most often?","What kind of phone calls make you nervous?"],
terms:[
["Voicemail","a recorded message you leave when someone does not answer.","Hi, this is Mary Smith. My number is 423-555-1234. I'm calling about my appointment. Please call me back. Thank you.","What should be in a good voicemail?"],
["Phone script","notes you write before a call so you know what to say.","Who I am, why I'm calling, what I need, my phone number.","How could a script help you feel calmer?"],
["Hold","when you are asked to wait on the line.","Please hold. Stay on the line and do not hang up; it may take a few minutes.","How do you handle being on hold?"],
["Phone menu","the automatic options when you call a business.","Press 1 for appointments, press 2 for billing. Listen carefully, or press 0 to try to reach a person.","Have you ever gotten stuck in a phone menu?"],
["Phone manners","being polite and clear on the phone.","Say hello, say your name, speak slowly, say please and thank you, and write down important information.","Why are manners important on the phone?"]],
story:"Mr. Isaiah needed to call his pharmacy about a refill, but he got so nervous he kept hanging up. A staff member helped him write a script on an index card. He practiced it once, then called. When the pharmacy did not answer, he read his script into voicemail. They called back within an hour. Isaiah said the card made all the difference.",
sq:["Why was Mr. Isaiah nervous?","What helped him?","What was on his card?","What call could you use a script for?"],
act:{n:"Phone Practice",m:"Two phones or pretend phones, index cards",s:["Have each resident write a script on an index card for a pretend call: scheduling a haircut, calling a pharmacy about a refill, or calling a family member.","Pair residents up. One calls, one answers.","Practice leaving a voicemail too: name, phone number twice, reason, thank you.","Switch roles.","Celebrate everyone who tries."]},
d:["Did the script help?","What part of calling feels hardest?","What call will you make this week?"],
disc:[["Why do phone calls make people anxious?","Cannot see faces, fear of saying the wrong thing, being rushed."],["Why say your phone number twice in a voicemail?","So they can write it down correctly."],["What should you never share on a phone call you did not make?","Social Security number, bank info, passwords."],["How can you end a call politely?","Thank you for your help. Have a good day. Goodbye."],["What phone skill would help you most?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Write a short script, say who you are and why you are calling, say your number twice in voicemails, and write down what you hear.",
tryit:"Use a written script for your next important phone call."},

{t:"Managing Your Time",
goal:"Today we will learn simple ways to plan our day, so we have time for what matters most.",
warm:["What does a typical day look like for you?","Do you ever feel like the day goes by and you got nothing done?","What time of day do you feel most energetic?"],
terms:[
["Schedule","a plan of what you will do and when.","8 a.m. breakfast and medicine, 10 a.m. shower, 1 p.m. PSR group, 3 p.m. walk, 6 p.m. dinner.","Do you follow a daily schedule?"],
["Priority","the most important task to get done.","Calling the pharmacy before they close is a higher priority than watching a show.","How do you decide what is most important?"],
["Procrastination","putting things off until later.","I'll do my laundry tomorrow, said for a whole week, until there are no clean socks.","What do you tend to put off?"],
["Time blocks","setting aside a chunk of time for one task.","From 10 to 10:30, I will clean my room. Set a timer and focus.","What task could you put in a time block?"],
["Balance","making time for work, rest, fun, and people.","A good day might include a chore, a group, some quiet time, a walk, and time with a friend.","What is missing from your days?"]],
story:"Ms. Carmen felt bored all day but also stressed because chores piled up. She spent most of the day watching TV. With staff, she wrote a simple daily schedule with chores in the morning, group after lunch, and TV after dinner. In two weeks her room was clean, she went to more groups, and she said she actually enjoyed TV more because she had earned it.",
sq:["How was Ms. Carmen spending her day?","What was in her new schedule?","What changed after two weeks?","Why do you think TV felt better afterward?"],
act:{n:"Plan Tomorrow",m:"Paper with times down the side (8 a.m. to 9 p.m.)",s:["Hand out a sheet with hours listed down the left side.","Ask residents to fill in the things that are already set: meals, medicine, PSR groups.","Then add one chore, one healthy activity, one fun activity, and one way to connect with someone.","Circle the top priority for the day.","Encourage them to try following it tomorrow."]},
d:["What did you choose as your top priority?","Was there more free time than you expected?","What will be hardest to stick to?"],
disc:[["Why does having a schedule help mental health?","Routine, purpose, less boredom, better sleep."],["What gets in the way of following a plan?","Low energy, interruptions, mood, distractions."],["How can you beat procrastination?","Start for just 5 minutes, use a timer, reward yourself."],["Is it OK to change your plan?","Yes; be flexible, but come back to priorities."],["What would your perfect day look like?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A simple plan for your day helps you get things done, feel less stressed, and enjoy your free time more.",
tryit:"Write a plan for tomorrow tonight, and try to follow it."},

{t:"Mail and Important Papers",
goal:"Today we will learn how to handle mail and keep important papers organized and safe.",
warm:["What kind of mail do you usually get?","Where do you keep important papers?","Have you ever lost an important document?"],
terms:[
["Important papers","documents you need for benefits, health, and identification.","Birth certificate, Social Security card, ID, insurance cards, award letters, leases, and medical records.","Which of these do you have?"],
["Sorting mail","going through mail right away and putting it into groups.","Three piles: Action needed (bills, letters to answer), Keep (important records), and Trash or shred (junk mail).","Why sort mail right away?"],
["Junk mail","advertisements and offers you did not ask for.","Pre-approved credit card offers should be shredded because they have your name and address.","How can you tell junk mail from important mail?"],
["Folder system","keeping papers in labeled folders.","Folders labeled: Health, Benefits, ID copies, Bills, Personal.","What labels would you use?"],
["Copies","extra copies of important documents in case the original is lost.","Make a copy of your ID and insurance cards and keep it in a different place, or ask staff to keep a copy on file.","Why is a copy helpful?"]],
story:"Mr. Barry kept all his mail in a big pile in a shopping bag. When his benefits office asked for a letter to prove his income, he could not find it, and his benefits were delayed for a month. Staff helped him set up a simple folder with five labels. Now when mail comes, he opens it right away and files it. The next time the office asked for a paper, he found it in two minutes.",
sq:["What problem did the pile of mail cause?","What system did Barry set up?","How did it help next time?","What would your folder labels be?"],
act:{n:"Sort the Mail",m:"Pretend mail items written on cards or paper",s:["Make pretend mail cards: a phone bill, a credit card offer, a letter from Social Security, a birthday card, a store flyer, a medical bill, an insurance card in the mail, a jury duty notice.","Hand them out and have residents decide: ACTION, KEEP, or TRASH/SHRED.","Talk about each one.","Discuss what action each ACTION item needs and who could help.","Show how a simple folder or envelope system could work."]},
d:["Which item was hardest to sort?","What should happen to the credit card offer?","Who can help you with ACTION items?"],
disc:[["Why do people avoid opening mail?","Fear of bad news, bills, feeling overwhelmed."],["What could happen if you ignore important mail?","Lost benefits, missed deadlines, late fees."],["Where is a safe place to keep important papers here?","Locked drawer, with staff, a folder in a safe place."],["How do you replace a lost ID or Social Security card?","Ask the social worker; there are offices that help."],["Why should you never carry your Social Security card daily?","If lost or stolen, it can lead to identity theft."]],
take:"Open mail right away, sort it into Action, Keep, and Shred, and keep important papers in labeled folders in a safe place.",
tryit:"Gather your important papers this week and put them in one folder or envelope, with staff help if needed."},

{t:"Being a Good Tenant",
goal:"Today we will learn what landlords expect and how to be a good tenant, so we can keep housing in the future.",
warm:["Have you ever rented an apartment or room?","What makes a good neighbor?","What do you think landlords worry about most?"],
terms:[
["Tenant","a person who rents a place to live.","When you sign a lease and pay rent for an apartment, you are a tenant.","What responsibilities does a tenant have?"],
["Landlord","the person or company that owns the property and rents it to you.","The landlord collects rent and is responsible for major repairs like plumbing and heating.","What should a landlord fix?"],
["Lease","a written contract between a tenant and landlord.","It lists the rent amount, due date, length of stay, rules about pets, guests, and smoking.","Why should you read a lease before signing?"],
["Security deposit","money paid before moving in that the landlord holds in case of damage or unpaid rent.","Pay a $500 deposit. If you leave the place clean and undamaged, you can get it back.","How can you get your deposit back?"],
["Eviction","when a landlord legally makes a tenant leave.","Not paying rent, damaging the property, or breaking lease rules can lead to eviction, which can make it hard to rent again.","What can lead to eviction?"]],
story:"When Rhonda got her first apartment, she let a friend stay with her, even though her lease said no long-term guests. The friend was loud and got in fights in the parking lot. Neighbors complained, and the landlord gave Rhonda a warning. Rhonda asked her friend to leave and talked with the landlord. She kept her apartment, but she learned to read her lease and protect her housing.",
sq:["What lease rule did Rhonda break?","What happened as a result?","How did she save her housing?","What would you have done?"],
act:{n:"Landlord Says, Tenant Does",m:"None",s:["Read situations and ask: What should the tenant do?","Your sink is leaking. (Report it to the landlord in writing.)","Rent is due Friday but you will be $50 short. (Call the landlord before Friday to explain and make a plan.)","Your friend wants to move in. (Check the lease and ask the landlord first.)","Your neighbor plays loud music at midnight. (Talk politely or tell the landlord.)","You are moving out. (Give notice, clean, take photos, return keys.)","Discuss each answer."]},
d:["Which situation would be hardest for you?","Why should you report repairs in writing?","What did you learn about keeping housing?"],
disc:[["What makes someone a good tenant?","Pays rent on time, keeps place clean, follows rules, respects neighbors."],["Why is rental history important?","Future landlords check it; evictions follow you."],["What should you do if a landlord is not making repairs?","Put it in writing, ask a case manager or legal aid for help."],["What life skills from here will help you live on your own?","Cleaning, budgeting, cooking, getting along."],["What is your housing goal?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Pay rent on time, follow the lease, report repairs in writing, respect neighbors, and talk to your landlord early when problems come up.",
tryit:"Think about what kind of housing you want someday and tell a staff member about your goal."},

{t:"Reporting Repairs and Maintenance",
goal:"Today we will learn how to notice and report things that need fixing in our home, before they become big problems.",
warm:["Have you ever had something break in your room?","What did you do about it?","What do you know how to fix yourself?"],
terms:[
["Maintenance","keeping a building and its equipment working well.","Changing light bulbs, fixing leaks, and replacing air filters are maintenance.","What maintenance does a home need?"],
["Work order or request","a written or spoken request asking for something to be fixed.","Tell staff or fill out a request: Room 6, toilet keeps running, started Monday.","What details should a repair request include?"],
["Emergency repair","a problem that is dangerous or causes damage quickly.","A gas smell, flooding, no heat in winter, sparks from an outlet, or a broken lock. Report immediately.","What would count as an emergency here?"],
["Small fixes","simple things you can safely do yourself.","Plunging a toilet, replacing a light bulb (with power off), or tightening a loose knob with permission.","What small fix can you do?"],
["Do not hide damage","telling staff when you break something.","If you crack a window or spill on the carpet, saying so right away is better than it being found later.","Why is honesty about damage important?"]],
story:"Mr. Curtis noticed a small leak under his bathroom sink but did not mention it. Weeks later, the cabinet floor was rotted and moldy, and the repair was big and expensive. The maintenance worker said, If you had told me the first day, it would have been a 10-minute fix. Now Curtis reports anything he notices right away.",
sq:["What did Mr. Curtis notice?","Why do you think he did not say anything?","What happened because he waited?","What lesson did he learn?"],
act:{n:"Write a Repair Request",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Teach the four parts of a repair request: Where, What, When it started, How bad.","Example: Room 3 bathroom. Faucet drips nonstop. Started yesterday. Not urgent but wasting water.","Read situations and have residents write a request: light flickering in the hallway; toilet overflowing; smell of gas near the stove; window will not lock.","Ask which ones are emergencies (overflowing toilet, gas smell, window that will not lock).","Share a few requests out loud."]},
d:["Which situations were emergencies?","What are the four parts of a good request?","Who do you report repairs to here?"],
disc:[["Why do small problems become big problems?","Water damage, mold, fires, safety risks grow over time."],["What should you do if you smell gas?","Do not use switches or flames, leave the area, tell staff, call 911 or the gas company."],["How does reporting repairs show responsibility?","It protects your home and others."],["What can happen if tenants hide damage?","Bigger costs, lost deposit, eviction."],["What home skill would you like to learn?","Let residents share."]],
take:"See something, say something. Report repairs right away with where, what, when, and how bad.",
tryit:"Look around your room this week and report anything that needs fixing to staff."},

{t:"Getting Ready to Live More Independently",
goal:"Today we will think about the skills that help a person live more independently and set one goal to practice.",
warm:["What does independence mean to you?","Where would you like to live in the future?","What is one thing you already do well on your own?"],
terms:[
["Independent living","living with less help from others, such as in your own apartment or a supported apartment.","Some people move from assisted living to a supported apartment where staff check in a few times a week.","What would independent living look like for you?"],
["Daily living skills","everyday tasks like cooking, cleaning, managing money, medicine, and getting around.","Doing your own laundry, making a grocery list, and keeping appointments are daily living skills.","Which daily living skill are you strongest in?"],
["Support level","how much help a person needs to be safe and well.","Some people need help with medicine; others just need help with transportation. Everyone is different.","What kind of support helps you most right now?"],
["Practice","doing a skill over and over until it becomes easy.","Practicing laundry every week here makes it easier when you live on your own.","What skill could you practice here?"],
["Step-by-step goal","a big goal broken into small steps.","Big goal: my own apartment. Steps: save money, learn to cook 5 meals, manage my own appointments, get my ID.","What is one step toward your bigger goal?"]],
story:"When Tony came to the facility, he said he wanted his own place someday but did not know where to start. With his team, he made a list of skills to practice: cooking simple meals, doing his laundry, and keeping track of his appointments. Each month he worked on one. After a year, he moved into a supported apartment. He still checks in with his case manager, but he says, I do my own thing now.",
sq:["What was Tony's big goal?","How did he break it into steps?","How long did it take?","What support does he still have?"],
act:{n:"Independence Skills Checklist",m:"Printed or written checklist",s:["Write a list of skills on the board: cooking, laundry, cleaning, budgeting, paying bills, taking medicine, keeping appointments, using transportation, shopping, handling mail.","Have each resident rate themselves for each skill: I do this well, I'm learning, or I need help.","Have them circle one skill they want to practice next.","Help them write one small step for that skill.","Encourage them to share their goal with their care team."]},
d:["Which skill are you most proud of?","Which skill do you want to practice next?","What is your first small step?"],
disc:[["Is independence the goal for everyone?","Everyone's goals are different; independence can mean making more choices."],["What fears come with moving to a more independent setting?","Loneliness, managing money, emergencies."],["What support would you want if you lived on your own?","Case manager, check-ins, family, church."],["How can living here prepare you?","Practicing skills, building routines, support."],["What makes you feel independent right now?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Independence is built one skill at a time. Practice here, ask for help, and take small steps toward your goals.",
tryit:"Practice your chosen skill at least once this week and tell staff how it went."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Independent Living Skills", [
{t:"Planning Meals for the Week",
goal:"Today we will learn how to plan meals ahead so we eat better, waste less, and spend less.",
warm:["What did you eat yesterday?","Do you usually plan meals or decide when you are hungry?","What is a meal you could eat every week and not get tired of?"],
terms:[
["Meal plan","a list of what you will eat for each meal during the week.","Monday: oatmeal, turkey sandwich, spaghetti. Tuesday: eggs, leftover spaghetti, chicken and rice.","How could a meal plan make life easier?"],
["Leftovers on purpose","cooking extra so you have food for another meal.","Cook a big pot of chili on Sunday and eat it for lunch Monday and Tuesday.","What meals make good leftovers?"],
["Staples","basic foods you keep on hand all the time.","Rice, pasta, oats, canned beans, peanut butter, eggs, frozen vegetables.","What staples would you always keep?"],
["Theme nights","giving each night a type of meal so planning is easier.","Meatless Monday, Taco Tuesday, Soup Wednesday, Leftover Friday.","What theme night would you enjoy?"],
["Using what you have","checking your fridge and cabinets before planning.","You have rice, eggs, and frozen peas, so you can make fried rice instead of buying something new.","What could you make with rice, eggs, and vegetables?"]],
story:"Every evening, Kevin stood in front of the fridge with no idea what to eat, then ordered takeout. He spent over $200 a month on delivery. Staff helped him plan five simple dinners and make one grocery list. He cooked a big batch of chicken and rice on Sunday. By the end of the month, he had saved over $100 and felt less stressed at dinnertime.",
sq:["What was Kevin's problem every evening?","What did planning change?","How much did he save?","What meal would you plan for this week?"],
act:{n:"Build a Week of Dinners",m:"Board with 7 days; paper and pens",s:["Draw seven boxes for the days of the week.","Ask the group to suggest simple, affordable dinners.","Fill in the week, using at least two leftover nights and one theme night.","Under the plan, write a grocery list for all the meals together.","Have residents copy a plan they would like to use."]},
d:["Was it easier than you thought?","Which meal are you most excited about?","How did leftovers help?"],
disc:[["Why do people end up eating fast food often?","No plan, tired, no food at home."],["How does meal planning help health?","More balanced meals, fewer snacks, less salt and sugar."],["How does it save money?","Fewer impulse buys, less waste, less takeout."],["What if you do not feel like eating what you planned?","Swap days; plans can be flexible."],["What meals remind you of home?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A simple plan, a list, and cooking extra on purpose can save money and stress all week.",
tryit:"Plan three meals for this week on paper and share your plan with staff."},

{t:"Waking Up and Starting the Day",
goal:"Today we will learn ways to wake up on time and start the day with a calm, steady routine.",
warm:["Are you a morning person or a night person?","How do you wake up: alarm, staff, or on your own?","What makes mornings hard for you?"],
terms:[
["Alarm","a sound that wakes you up at a set time.","A phone alarm or simple alarm clock set for 7:30 a.m. every day.","How many alarms do you need?"],
["Snooze","hitting a button to sleep a few more minutes.","Hitting snooze five times can make you feel groggier and late.","Do you hit snooze? How do you feel after?"],
["Alarm across the room","putting the alarm where you have to get up to turn it off.","Once you are standing, you are more likely to stay up.","Would that trick work for you?"],
["Morning light","opening blinds or going outside soon after waking.","Sunlight tells your brain it is time to be awake and helps you sleep better at night.","Do you open your blinds in the morning?"],
["Consistent wake time","waking at the same time every day, even weekends.","Waking at 7:30 every day trains your body clock so mornings get easier.","What wake-up time would work for you every day?"]],
story:"Jerome often slept through breakfast and morning medicine, then felt sluggish all day. He tried putting his alarm across the room, opening the blinds as soon as he got up, and drinking a glass of water. It was hard the first week. By the third week, he was waking up before the alarm and making it to breakfast every day.",
sq:["What problems did oversleeping cause Jerome?","What three things did he try?","How long did it take to feel easier?","Which of those would you try?"],
act:{n:"Design Your First 30 Minutes",m:"Paper and pens",s:["Ask everyone to write their ideal wake-up time.","Then list what they will do in the first 30 minutes: turn off alarm, open blinds, drink water, use the bathroom, wash face, get dressed, go to breakfast.","Add one thing that makes the morning feel good: music, prayer, stretching, coffee with a friend.","Have volunteers share their routines.","Encourage them to set their alarm tonight."]},
d:["What wake-up time did people choose?","What feel-good activity did you add?","What will be the hardest part?"],
disc:[["How does your morning affect the rest of your day?","Sets your mood and energy."],["How can medicine affect waking up?","Some cause drowsiness; ask the nurse about timing."],["What can you do the night before to make mornings easier?","Lay out clothes, go to bed on time, set alarm."],["Why is a regular wake time good for mental health?","Steady sleep, steadier mood."],["What is your favorite part of the morning?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Same wake time every day, alarm across the room, blinds open, and a glass of water. Mornings get easier with practice.",
tryit:"Set an alarm for the same time every morning this week."},

{t:"Using a Smartphone for Daily Life",
goal:"Today we will learn helpful ways to use a phone for reminders, calendars, maps, and staying in touch, while staying safe.",
warm:["Do you have a cell phone? What do you use it for most?","What is something you wish you knew how to do on your phone?","What frustrates you about phones?"],
terms:[
["App","a program on your phone that does a specific job.","The clock app sets alarms, the calendar app holds appointments, and Maps gives directions.","What apps do you use most?"],
["Reminder or alarm","a phone notice that helps you remember something.","Set an alarm called Call pharmacy for 10 a.m. Tuesday.","What could you set a reminder for?"],
["Contacts","saved names and phone numbers in your phone.","Save the facility number as Rose of Sharon and your doctor's office under the doctor's name.","Whose numbers should you save?"],
["Data and Wi-Fi","data is the internet from your phone plan; Wi-Fi is internet from a router.","Watching videos on data can use up your plan quickly. Use Wi-Fi when you can.","Why might your phone bill go up?"],
["Phone safety","protecting your phone and information.","Use a screen lock, do not click strange links, and do not download apps strangers send you.","How do you keep your phone safe?"]],
story:"Ms. Doris got a smartphone from her daughter but only used it to answer calls. A volunteer showed her how to set alarms for her appointments, save numbers in her contacts, and video call her grandkids. She said, I feel like a whole new world opened up. Now she sees her great-grandson every Sunday on video.",
sq:["How was Ms. Doris using her phone at first?","What did she learn?","What did video calling give her?","What would you like to learn to do on a phone?"],
act:{n:"Phone Skills Practice",m:"A staff phone or residents' own phones (optional)",s:["If residents have phones, help each one practice: set an alarm with a label, add a contact, and find the calendar app.","If not, demonstrate on a staff phone and talk through the steps.","Show how to set a screen lock.","Ask residents to name the three most important contacts to save.","Pair residents who are good with phones with those who want help."]},
d:["What new skill did you learn?","Who helped you or who did you help?","What contacts will you save?"],
disc:[["How can a phone help with independence?","Reminders, maps, staying in touch, information."],["How can a phone cause problems?","Scams, overspending on data, too much screen time, conflict on social media."],["What should you do if you lose your phone?","Tell staff, call your carrier to lock it."],["How much phone time is healthy?","Let residents share; balance with people and activities."],["Who can help you learn more phone skills?","Staff, volunteers, library classes, family."]],
take:"A phone can be a powerful tool. Use alarms, a calendar, and contacts, and protect it with a lock and smart choices.",
tryit:"Set one phone alarm with a label for something important this week."},

{t:"Preventing Bugs and Pests",
goal:"Today we will learn how bugs and pests get into homes and simple habits that keep them away.",
warm:["Have you ever lived somewhere with bugs or mice?","What pest bothers you the most?","What do you think attracts pests?"],
terms:[
["Pest","an unwanted animal or insect in the home.","Roaches, ants, mice, bed bugs, and fruit flies are common pests.","Which pests are most common in Tennessee?"],
["Food source","anything pests can eat.","Crumbs, open snack bags, dirty dishes, pet food, and trash that is not emptied.","Where might crumbs collect in a bedroom?"],
["Sealed containers","containers with tight lids.","Keeping cereal, chips, and sugar in sealed containers keeps ants and roaches out.","What food do you keep in your room?"],
["Bed bugs","tiny insects that hide in mattresses and furniture and bite at night.","Small itchy bites in a line and tiny dark spots on sheets can be signs. Tell staff right away; it is not about being dirty.","What should you do if you think you have bed bugs?"],
["Clutter","piles of stuff that give pests places to hide.","Stacks of papers, bags, and clothes on the floor make perfect hiding spots.","How does clutter help pests?"]],
story:"Ms. Paula kept snacks in her dresser drawers and under her bed. One day she found ants marching into her drawer and later saw a roach. Staff helped her clean out the drawers and gave her a plastic container with a lid. She started eating snacks in the dining room. The ants were gone in a week.",
sq:["What attracted the pests?","What did Paula change?","Why did eating in the dining room help?","What would you change in your room?"],
act:{n:"Pest Detective",m:"Board",s:["Draw a simple bedroom on the board: bed, dresser, trash can, nightstand, closet.","Ask residents to point out where pests could find food, water, or hiding spots.","Write a fix next to each problem: sealed container, empty trash, clear clutter, wipe spills.","Talk about what to do if they see a pest: tell staff.","Have each resident name one change for their room."]},
d:["What hiding places surprised you?","What will you change in your room?","Why is telling staff early important?"],
disc:[["How can pests affect health?","Allergies, asthma, bites, germs on food."],["Why might someone not report pests?","Embarrassment; remind them it is not their fault."],["How do pests spread in a shared home?","From room to room; everyone's habits matter."],["What can you do when you bring things home from stores or donations?","Check items, wash clothes, inspect furniture."],["How can we work together to keep pests out?","Keep food in kitchen, clean common areas, report quickly."]],
take:"No food, no water, no clutter means no pests. Keep snacks sealed and tell staff right away if you see bugs.",
tryit:"Check your room this week for open food and put it in a sealed container."},

{t:"Organizing Your Belongings",
goal:"Today we will learn how to organize our things so our space is calm and we can find what we need.",
warm:["What item do you lose most often?","Do you consider yourself organized?","What is one thing you treasure most in your room?"],
terms:[
["Organize","putting things in order so they are easy to find and use.","Keeping all medicine papers in one folder, all socks in one drawer, all toiletries in one basket.","What do you already keep organized?"],
["Declutter","removing things you do not need or use.","Throwing away broken items, donating clothes that do not fit, and recycling old papers.","What could you let go of?"],
["Keep, donate, toss","three piles for sorting your things.","A shirt that fits: keep. Shoes that are too small but good: donate. A broken lamp: toss.","Which pile is hardest for you?"],
["Daily drop spot","one place for keys, wallet, glasses, and phone.","A small bowl on the dresser where these items always go.","Where could your drop spot be?"],
["Storage","containers, bins, or shelves to hold things.","Shoe boxes, plastic bins, and hooks are cheap ways to store items.","What storage would help your space?"]],
story:"Ms. Angela kept losing her glasses, ID, and keys. She spent a lot of time searching and got anxious before outings. Staff gave her a small dish for her nightstand and said, Everything important goes here, every time. It took practice, but now she grabs everything from one spot and leaves on time without stress.",
sq:["What problem did Ms. Angela have?","What simple tool helped?","Why did it take practice?","What items would go in your drop spot?"],
act:{n:"Keep, Donate, Toss Practice",m:"Pretend item cards or a list",s:["Read a list of pretend items: a sweater that does not fit, a broken phone charger, a photo of family, five pens that do not work, a good pair of shoes you never wear, expired lotion, a favorite blanket, old magazines.","Have the group decide: Keep, Donate, or Toss.","Talk about the feelings that come up with letting go.","Ask each resident to pick one small area in their room to declutter this week.","Offer staff help with bags and donation drop-off."]},
d:["Which item caused the most debate?","Why is it hard to let things go?","What area will you organize?"],
disc:[["How does clutter affect mood and stress?","Feels overwhelming, harder to relax."],["Why do people hold on to things?","Memories, fear of needing it later, habit."],["How can organizing help with safety?","Fewer trip hazards, easier to find medicine and ID."],["How can you keep things organized long-term?","A place for everything, five-minute tidy, regular declutter."],["What is something special you keep and why?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Give everything a home, use a daily drop spot, and let go of what you do not need.",
tryit:"Choose one drawer or shelf this week and organize it."},

{t:"Basic First Aid",
goal:"Today we will learn simple first aid for small injuries and when to get staff or call 911.",
warm:["Have you ever helped someone who was hurt?","What is in a first aid kit?","What makes you nervous about emergencies?"],
terms:[
["First aid","the first help given to someone who is hurt or sick, until professional help arrives.","Pressing on a cut to stop bleeding, cooling a burn with water, or helping someone sit down if they feel faint.","When might you need first aid?"],
["Minor cut","a small cut or scrape.","Wash with soap and water, press with a clean cloth to stop bleeding, cover with a bandage, and tell staff.","What should you do for a small cut?"],
["Minor burn","a small burn, like touching a hot pan.","Run cool (not ice-cold) water over it for 10 minutes. Do not use butter, ice, or toothpaste. Tell staff.","Why not use ice?"],
["Choking","when something blocks the throat and the person cannot breathe or talk.","If someone cannot cough, speak, or breathe and is holding their throat, yell for staff and call 911. Trained staff will do back blows or abdominal thrusts.","What is the sign for choking?"],
["Call for help","knowing when to get staff or 911.","Heavy bleeding, trouble breathing, someone not waking up, chest pain, or a fall with a head injury. Get staff and call 911.","What would you say when you call 911?"]],
story:"At lunch, Mr. Simmons started choking on a piece of meat. He grabbed his throat and could not speak. Ms. Darla, sitting next to him, remembered the choking sign from group and yelled for staff right away. A trained caregiver helped clear his airway. Mr. Simmons was fine. He thanked Darla for acting fast.",
sq:["What was the sign Mr. Simmons was choking?","What did Ms. Darla do?","Why was it important to act fast?","How can we prevent choking at meals?"],
act:{n:"First Aid Scenarios",m:"Band-aids or a small first aid kit to show (optional)",s:["Show what is in a basic first aid kit: bandages, gauze, tape, gloves, antiseptic wipes.","Read scenarios and have the group say what to do: a small cut on a finger; a burn from a coffee spill; someone feels dizzy and pale; someone is choking and cannot talk; someone falls and hits their head.","For each, ask: Can we handle it, or do we get staff or call 911?","Practice what to say on a 911 call: My name is ___. I am at ___ address. Someone is ___. They are breathing or not breathing.","Remind everyone to always tell staff."]},
d:["Which scenario was hardest?","Do you know the address of this building for a 911 call?","How do you feel about helping in an emergency now?"],
disc:[["Why should you wear gloves when helping someone bleeding?","Protect from germs and blood-borne illness."],["Why is staying calm important?","Clear thinking, calms the hurt person."],["How can we prevent choking?","Small bites, chew well, sit up, do not talk with food in mouth."],["What should you never do in first aid?","Move someone with a possible neck injury, give food or drink to someone choking or unconscious."],["Would you like to learn CPR? Where could you learn?","Red Cross classes; let residents share interest."]],
take:"For small injuries: clean, cover, and tell staff. For big emergencies: get staff and call 911. The Rose of Sharon address is 5410 Lee Avenue, Chattanooga.",
tryit:"Memorize the address of where you live so you can give it in an emergency."},

{t:"Getting Your ID and Important Documents",
goal:"Today we will learn which documents are important, why we need them, and how to replace them if lost.",
warm:["What ID do you carry?","Have you ever needed an ID and did not have it?","What can you not do without an ID?"],
terms:[
["Photo ID","a card with your picture that proves who you are, like a driver's license or state ID card.","You need a photo ID to pick up some prescriptions, open a bank account, apply for jobs, and vote in Tennessee.","When have you needed your ID?"],
["Birth certificate","the official record of when and where you were born.","It is often needed to get a state ID or passport. You can order a copy from the state where you were born.","Do you know where your birth certificate is?"],
["Social Security card","the card with your Social Security number.","Needed for jobs and benefits. Keep it locked up, not in your wallet.","Where should it be kept?"],
["Replacement","getting a new copy of a lost or stolen document.","A lost Tennessee ID can be replaced at a driver services center; a Social Security card can be replaced through Social Security. The social worker can help.","Who could help you replace a document?"],
["Proof of address","a document that shows where you live.","A letter from the facility, a bank statement, or a benefits letter with your current address.","Why might you need proof of address?"]],
story:"Mr. Curtis lost his wallet with his ID. When he tried to pick up a prescription and apply for a job, he could not. He felt stuck because he needed his birth certificate to get a new ID, and he did not know where it was. The social worker helped him order his birth certificate, then took him to get a new state ID. It took six weeks, but he got it done. Now he keeps copies of everything in a locked file.",
sq:["What could Mr. Curtis not do without his ID?","Why did it take so long to replace?","Who helped him?","What does he do now to protect his documents?"],
act:{n:"Document Checklist",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Write a checklist on the board: Photo ID, Birth certificate, Social Security card, Medicaid/TennCare or Medicare card, Benefits award letter, Bank card.","Have each resident mark what they have, what they are missing, and what they are not sure about.","Talk about safe places to keep each item.","Encourage residents to share their missing items with the social worker.","Remind everyone that copies help if originals are lost."]},
d:["Which documents are people missing most?","Where will you keep your documents?","Who will you talk to about replacing missing ones?"],
disc:[["Why are documents so important?","They unlock benefits, jobs, housing, health care, voting."],["Why is replacing documents hard?","You often need one document to get another; it takes time and sometimes fees."],["How can you protect your documents?","Locked storage, copies, never carry Social Security card."],["What should you do if your ID is stolen?","Tell staff, report it, replace it."],["How does having your documents make you feel?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Keep your ID, birth certificate, Social Security card, and insurance cards safe and copied. If something is missing, the social worker can help you replace it.",
tryit:"Check which documents you have and talk to the social worker about anything missing."},

{t:"Making a Weekly Chore Chart",
goal:"Today we will learn how a chore chart helps us keep up with tasks and share work fairly.",
warm:["What chore do you actually enjoy?","What chore do you dislike most?","Growing up, did your family use a chore chart?"],
terms:[
["Chore","a regular task to keep a home running.","Making the bed, doing laundry, taking out trash, wiping counters.","What chores do you do now?"],
["Chore chart","a chart that lists chores and when they will be done.","Monday: laundry. Tuesday: clean bathroom sink. Wednesday: dust. Thursday: change sheets.","How would a chart help you?"],
["Daily vs. weekly","some chores are done every day, others once a week.","Daily: make bed, wash dishes. Weekly: change sheets, clean the floor.","Which chores are daily for you?"],
["Accountability","being responsible for doing what you said you would.","Checking off chores on the chart shows yourself and others you followed through.","Who helps keep you accountable?"],
["Reward","something enjoyable after finishing a task.","After my chores, I get to watch my favorite show or have a coffee.","What reward motivates you?"]],
story:"Roommates Will and Andre argued every week about whose turn it was to take out the trash and clean the bathroom. They made a chart together with their names and days and taped it to the door. Each person checked off their chores. The arguments stopped, and Andre said, Now we just look at the chart.",
sq:["What were Will and Andre arguing about?","How did they solve it?","Why does a chart reduce arguments?","Would a chart help you?"],
act:{n:"My Chore Chart",m:"Paper with a grid (days across the top), markers",s:["Have residents draw a grid with days of the week across the top.","List 3 to 5 chores down the side.","Assign each chore to a day (some daily, some weekly).","Add a column for a reward at the end of the week.","Encourage them to hang it where they see it daily."]},
d:["What chores did you include?","What reward did you choose?","Where will you hang your chart?"],
disc:[["Why do chores matter for mental health?","Sense of accomplishment, cleaner space, routine."],["What happens when chores pile up?","Stress, mess, arguments, pests."],["How can you stay motivated?","Music while cleaning, rewards, doing it with a friend."],["How should chores be shared fairly?","Rotate, agree together, use a chart."],["What chore are you proud you can do?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A chore chart turns a messy week into a simple plan. Check it off, reward yourself, and share the work fairly.",
tryit:"Hang your chore chart and check off each chore you finish this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Coping Skills & Emotional Wellness", [
{t:"Naming Your Feelings",
goal:"Today we will learn more words for our feelings, because naming a feeling is the first step to handling it.",
warm:["How are you feeling right now, in one word?","Is it easy or hard for you to talk about feelings?","Growing up, were feelings talked about in your home?"],
terms:[
["Emotion","a feeling that comes from what happens to us or what we think about.","Happy, sad, angry, scared, and surprised are emotions. They come and go like weather.","Which emotions do you feel most often?"],
["Feelings vocabulary","having many words to describe how you feel.","Instead of just bad, you might say frustrated, lonely, disappointed, nervous, or overwhelmed.","Can you think of three words that mean more than just mad?"],
["Body clues","physical signs that show what we are feeling.","Anger might feel like a hot face and clenched fists. Anxiety might feel like a tight chest and fast heart.","Where do you feel anger in your body?"],
["Name it to tame it","saying the feeling out loud or to yourself helps calm it down.","Saying I'm feeling anxious right now gives your brain a little space to calm.","Have you ever felt better just by saying how you feel?"],
["All feelings are OK","feelings are not good or bad. What we DO with them matters.","It is OK to feel angry. It is not OK to throw things. We can feel anger and still choose a calm action.","What is the difference between a feeling and an action?"]],
story:"Ronnie would get quiet and then suddenly blow up at people. Staff asked him what he felt before he exploded, and he said, I don't know, just bad. They gave him a feelings chart with faces and words. Over a few weeks, he started pointing to words like embarrassed and left out. When he could name it, he could tell staff, and they could help before he exploded.",
sq:["What would happen to Ronnie when he felt bad?","What tool helped him?","What feelings did he discover were underneath?","Why did naming his feelings help?"],
act:{n:"Feelings Charades",m:"Feeling words written on slips of paper",s:["Write feelings on slips: happy, nervous, angry, proud, lonely, excited, embarrassed, bored, frustrated, calm, surprised, disappointed.","Volunteers pick a slip and act out the feeling with face and body only, no words.","The group guesses the feeling.","After each one, ask: When might someone feel this way?","Keep going until most people have had a turn."]},
d:["Which feeling was easiest to guess?","Which was hardest?","Which feeling do you want to understand better?"],
disc:[["Why do some people hide their feelings?","Fear of judgment, being taught not to show them, feeling weak."],["What can happen when feelings get bottled up?","Explosions, illness, depression, using substances."],["Who do you feel safe sharing feelings with?","Let residents share."],["How can you tell someone how you feel without blaming?","I feel ___ when ___."],["What feeling did you have most this week?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Every feeling is OK. Name it to tame it, then choose a healthy action.",
tryit:"Three times this week, stop and name exactly what you are feeling, using a more specific word than good or bad."},

{t:"Managing Anger",
goal:"Today we will learn how anger works and practice ways to cool down before we say or do something we regret.",
warm:["What is one small thing that annoys you?","How do you usually show anger?","How do you feel after you have lost your temper?"],
terms:[
["Anger","a strong feeling that comes when we feel wronged, threatened, or frustrated.","Someone cuts in front of you in the meal line and you feel heat rise in your chest.","Is anger always bad?"],
["Trigger","something that sets off anger.","Being disrespected, being told no, loud noises, being touched without permission, feeling ignored.","What are your anger triggers?"],
["Warning signs","body and thought signals that anger is building.","Clenched jaw, fast breathing, hot face, raised voice, thinking He did that on purpose!","What is your first warning sign?"],
["Time-out","stepping away from a situation to cool down.","I need a minute. I'll be back. Then walk away, breathe, and return when calm.","Where could you go for a time-out here?"],
["Cool-down tools","things you can do to calm your body.","Count to 10 slowly, take 5 deep breaths, squeeze a stress ball, splash cold water on your face, walk.","Which cool-down tool works for you?"]],
story:"During a card game, another resident accused Leon of cheating. Leon felt his face get hot and his fists clench. In the past he would have yelled and flipped the table. This time he said, I need a minute, and walked to the porch. He took deep breaths and counted to 20. When he came back, he calmly said, I wasn't cheating, but let's start a new game. Staff praised him later.",
sq:["What triggered Leon?","What warning signs did he notice?","What did he do instead of yelling?","How do you think he felt afterward?"],
act:{n:"The Anger Thermometer",m:"Board: draw a thermometer from 0 to 10",s:["Draw a thermometer. 0 = calm, 5 = irritated, 8 = very angry, 10 = out of control.","Ask residents what they look and feel like at 2, 5, and 8. Write it next to the numbers.","Ask: At what number should you use a cool-down tool? (Around 4 or 5, before it is too high.)","Practice together: 5 slow breaths, then count backward from 10.","Have each resident say their number for when they will take a time-out."]},
d:["What number do you usually notice your anger?","What tool will you use at that number?","Why is it easier to calm down at 4 than at 9?"],
disc:[["What is the difference between anger and aggression?","Anger is a feeling; aggression is hurting or threatening."],["What are the costs of losing your temper here?","Hurt relationships, discharge, legal trouble, regret."],["What is a healthy way to express anger?","Calmly using I statements, talking to staff, writing it out."],["How can you respond if someone else is angry at you?","Stay calm, give space, get staff."],["What would you tell your younger self about anger?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Anger is normal. Notice your warning signs early, take a time-out, cool down your body, and then talk.",
tryit:"Practice 5 slow breaths the next time you feel irritated, before it grows."},

{t:"Coping with Anxiety",
goal:"Today we will learn what anxiety is and practice tools to calm our body and mind when we feel worried or panicky.",
warm:["What does nervous feel like in your body?","What situations make you feel anxious?","What helps you feel safe?"],
terms:[
["Anxiety","a feeling of worry, fear, or unease, often about what might happen.","Feeling sick to your stomach before a doctor's visit, or lying awake worrying about money.","When do you feel anxious most?"],
["Panic attack","a sudden wave of intense fear with strong body symptoms.","Racing heart, shaking, sweating, trouble breathing, feeling like something terrible is happening. It feels scary but passes, usually within 10 to 20 minutes.","Have you ever felt something like this?"],
["Avoidance","staying away from things that cause anxiety.","Skipping the dining room because crowds feel scary. It helps for a moment but makes anxiety stronger over time.","What do people avoid because of anxiety?"],
["Box breathing","breathing in a pattern to calm the body.","Breathe in for 4, hold for 4, out for 4, hold for 4. Imagine tracing a box.","Want to try it now?"],
["Worry time","setting aside 15 minutes a day to worry so worries do not take over all day.","When a worry pops up at noon, tell yourself, I'll think about that at 4 o'clock during worry time.","Could worry time help you?"]],
story:"Ms. Grace felt so nervous in crowds that she ate every meal alone in her room. She started feeling lonely and sad. With her counselor and staff, she made a plan: first, sit at the edge of the dining room for 5 minutes, using box breathing. Each week she stayed longer. After two months, she ate with two friends at a corner table.",
sq:["What was Ms. Grace avoiding?","What did avoiding cost her?","How did she slowly face her fear?","What tool did she use?"],
act:{n:"Box Breathing and 5-4-3-2-1",m:"None",s:["Draw a square on the board. Lead box breathing: in 4 (up one side), hold 4 (across), out 4 (down), hold 4 (across). Repeat 4 times.","Then lead the 5-4-3-2-1 grounding exercise. Name 5 things you see, 4 things you can touch, 3 things you hear, 2 things you smell, 1 thing you taste.","Have volunteers share their answers out loud.","Ask how their body feels now.","Encourage them to use these when worry shows up."]},
d:["Which tool did you like better?","How did your body feel afterward?","When could you use these this week?"],
disc:[["What are physical signs of anxiety?","Fast heart, sweating, stomach upset, shaking, tight chest."],["Why does avoidance make anxiety worse?","The brain learns the thing is dangerous."],["What unhelpful thoughts feed anxiety?","What if the worst happens? Everyone is looking at me."],["What can you tell yourself during anxiety?","This feeling will pass. I am safe right now."],["Who can you go to when anxiety feels too big?","Staff, nurse, counselor."]],
take:"Anxiety is uncomfortable but not dangerous, and it passes. Breathe in a box, ground yourself with 5-4-3-2-1, and take small steps instead of avoiding.",
tryit:"Use box breathing once a day this week, even when you feel calm, so it is ready when you need it."},

{t:"Lifting a Low Mood",
goal:"Today we will talk about low moods and depression, and learn small actions that can help us feel a little better.",
warm:["What is something that always makes you smile?","How do you know when you are having a down day?","What have you done in the past to feel better?"],
terms:[
["Low mood","feeling sad, flat, tired, or uninterested for a while.","Not wanting to get out of bed, not enjoying TV or food, feeling heavy.","What does a low mood feel like for you?"],
["Depression","a health condition where low mood, low energy, and loss of interest last at least two weeks and affect daily life.","It is not weakness or laziness. It is treatable with support, medicine, therapy, and daily habits.","Why do you think people call depression laziness?"],
["Behavioral activation","doing small activities even when you do not feel like it, because action can lift mood.","You do not wait to feel better to take a walk. You take a walk, and it helps you feel a little better.","What activity could you do even on a low day?"],
["Pleasant activity","something you enjoy or used to enjoy.","Listening to old music, sitting in the sun, playing cards, calling a friend, coloring, praying.","What activities did you enjoy before?"],
["Reach out","telling someone you are struggling.","If you feel hopeless or have thoughts of hurting yourself, tell staff right away or call or text 988.","Who would you tell if you felt hopeless?"]],
story:"Mr. Paul had been in his room for days, sleeping and not eating much. A caregiver asked him to just sit on the porch with her for 5 minutes. He did not want to, but he went. The sun felt nice, and a bird was singing. The next day, she asked him to walk to the mailbox. A week later, he came to group. He said, I didn't feel like it, but each little thing helped a little.",
sq:["What was Mr. Paul's low mood like?","What small step did the caregiver suggest?","Why do you think small steps worked better than big ones?","What small step could help you on a low day?"],
act:{n:"My Mood Menu",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Ask each resident to draw a menu with three sections: Appetizers (1 to 5 minutes), Main Course (15 to 30 minutes), Dessert (special treats).","Appetizers: drink water, open blinds, stretch, listen to a song.","Main Course: walk, shower, call someone, play cards, color.","Dessert: a favorite snack, a phone call with family, an outing.","Have residents fill in their own and share one item from each section."]},
d:["What is on your appetizer list?","Which main course would help you most?","When will you use your menu?"],
disc:[["Why is it hard to do things when we feel low?","Low energy, no motivation, feeling it won't help."],["What thoughts come with low mood?","I'm worthless, nothing will change; remind them thoughts are not facts."],["How can others help someone with a low mood?","Invite gently, sit with them, do not judge."],["When should someone tell staff about a low mood?","When it lasts more than two weeks or includes thoughts of self-harm."],["What gives you hope?","Let residents share."]],
take:"When mood is low, action comes first and motivation follows. Start small, choose from your mood menu, and reach out.",
tryit:"Do one appetizer from your mood menu every day this week."},

{t:"Grounding: Coming Back to the Present",
goal:"Today we will learn grounding skills that help us feel calm and present when we feel overwhelmed, upset, or far away.",
warm:["Have you ever felt like your mind was racing or far away?","What helps you feel calm and steady?","What is something you love to touch, like a soft blanket or a smooth stone?"],
terms:[
["Grounding","using your senses to bring your mind back to the here and now.","Feeling your feet on the floor and naming objects in the room when you feel panicked or upset.","Why might our senses help us calm down?"],
["Present moment","what is happening right now, not in the past or future.","Right now, I am sitting in a chair, it is afternoon, and I am safe.","How often is your mind in the present?"],
["5-4-3-2-1","a grounding exercise using all five senses.","5 things you see, 4 you can touch, 3 you hear, 2 you smell, 1 you taste.","Which sense do you notice most?"],
["Physical grounding","using your body to feel steady.","Press your feet into the floor, hold a cold drink, squeeze a pillow, or run your hands under water.","What physical grounding might work for you?"],
["Mental grounding","using thinking games to focus your mind.","Name all the colors in the room, count backward from 100 by 7s, or name every state you can think of.","Which thinking game would you try?"]],
story:"Ms. Tonya sometimes felt overwhelmed by bad memories, like they were happening again. Her counselor taught her to hold a smooth stone and describe it: cool, smooth, gray with a white stripe. Then she would name five things in the room. She kept the stone in her pocket. When she felt a memory coming, she held it and grounded herself. She said it helped her feel like she was back in the room.",
sq:["What would happen to Ms. Tonya?","What tool did she use?","How did the stone help?","What object could help you?"],
act:{n:"Grounding Practice Circuit",m:"Small objects with different textures (a stone, cotton ball, a cold cup of water, a piece of fabric)",s:["Lead 5-4-3-2-1 together out loud.","Pass around objects with different textures. Ask each person to describe theirs using three words.","Have everyone press their feet into the floor and name how it feels.","Play a mental grounding game: go around the circle naming foods from A to Z.","Ask which technique felt the most calming."]},
d:["Which technique felt best?","How did your body feel after?","When might you use grounding?"],
disc:[["When might someone need grounding?","Panic, flashbacks, anger, overwhelming stress, feeling unreal."],["Can grounding be done anywhere?","Yes, quietly, even in public."],["What could you keep in your pocket to help?","A stone, a small fabric, a photo, a bead."],["How can staff help someone who seems overwhelmed?","Speak calmly, guide grounding, give space."],["What brings you peace?","Let residents share."]],
take:"When your mind is racing or far away, use your senses to come back: feel your feet, name what you see, hold something real.",
tryit:"Choose a small grounding object and carry it with you this week."},

{t:"Positive Self-Talk",
goal:"Today we will learn how the way we talk to ourselves affects how we feel, and practice kinder, more helpful self-talk.",
warm:["What is a nice thing someone said to you that you still remember?","Do you talk to yourself in your head? Is it mostly kind or harsh?","What would your best friend say about you?"],
terms:[
["Self-talk","the words you say to yourself in your mind.","I can't do anything right, or I made a mistake, but I can fix it.","What do you usually say to yourself when you make a mistake?"],
["Negative self-talk","harsh, unfair, or hopeless thoughts about yourself.","I'm stupid. Nobody likes me. I'll never get better.","Where do you think those thoughts come from?"],
["Thoughts are not facts","just because we think something does not make it true.","Thinking Everyone hates me does not mean it is true. Look for evidence.","Can you think of a time a negative thought was not true?"],
["Reframing","changing a negative thought into a fair, helpful one.","I always mess up becomes I messed up this time, and I'm learning.","How could you reframe I'm a failure?"],
["Affirmation","a short, positive statement you repeat to yourself.","I am worthy. I am doing my best. I can handle today.","What affirmation feels true for you?"]],
story:"Every time DeAndre made a mistake, he would mutter, You idiot. You can't do anything. He felt worse and worse. His counselor asked him, Would you say that to your little brother? He said, Never. She said, Then try talking to yourself like you'd talk to him. DeAndre started saying, It's OK, man, try again. His frustration went down, and he kept trying more things.",
sq:["What did DeAndre say to himself?","What question did the counselor ask?","How did he change his self-talk?","How did it affect him?"],
act:{n:"Flip the Thought",m:"Board, paper, pens",s:["Write negative thoughts on the left side of the board: I'm a failure. Nobody cares about me. I can't do this. I always mess things up. I'm not good enough.","For each one, ask the group to help flip it into a fair, kind thought. Example: I can't do this becomes This is hard, but I can try one step.","Write the new thought on the right side.","Ask each resident to write one affirmation for themselves on paper.","Invite volunteers to read their affirmation out loud while the group listens."]},
d:["Which flipped thought did you like best?","How did it feel to say your affirmation out loud?","Where could you post your affirmation?"],
disc:[["Why are we harder on ourselves than on others?","Old messages, past criticism, perfectionism."],["How does negative self-talk affect mood and behavior?","Sadness, giving up, isolating."],["Does positive self-talk mean pretending everything is fine?","No; it means being fair and kind, not fake."],["How can we encourage each other here?","Compliments, praise effort, kind words."],["What is one kind thing you can say about yourself right now?","Let each willing resident share."]],
take:"Talk to yourself like you would talk to someone you love. Thoughts are not facts, and you can flip them.",
tryit:"Write your affirmation on a card and read it every morning this week."},

{t:"Practicing Gratitude",
goal:"Today we will learn how noticing good things, even small ones, can improve our mood and outlook.",
warm:["What is one good thing that happened today?","Who is someone you are thankful for?","What simple pleasure do you enjoy?"],
terms:[
["Gratitude","noticing and appreciating the good things in your life.","Feeling thankful for a warm bed, a good meal, or a friend who checks on you.","What are you grateful for today?"],
["Small blessings","little things that are easy to miss.","A hot cup of coffee, a funny show, sunshine, a clean shirt, a text from family.","What small blessing did you notice this week?"],
["Gratitude list","writing down a few things you are thankful for each day.","Today: 1. Pancakes at breakfast. 2. My sister called. 3. My knee hurt less.","How might writing it down help?"],
["Thank-you","telling someone you appreciate them.","Saying to a caregiver, Thank you for helping me with my laundry. It meant a lot.","Who could you thank this week?"],
["Balance","gratitude does not erase hard things; it helps us see good alongside them.","I'm struggling with my health, and I'm also grateful for my friends here.","Can someone feel grateful and sad at the same time?"]],
story:"Ms. Bernice was going through a hard season. Her nurse suggested writing three good things every night before bed. At first, she could only think of one: dinner was good. After a few weeks, she noticed more: a laugh with her roommate, a cardinal at the window, a phone call from her son. She said she still had hard days, but her nights felt more peaceful.",
sq:["What was Ms. Bernice going through?","What did the nurse suggest?","How did it change over time?","What three good things could you write tonight?"],
act:{n:"Gratitude Circle",m:"A ball or soft object to pass",s:["Sit in a circle. Explain that whoever holds the ball shares one thing they are grateful for.","Go around the circle at least twice.","Encourage small things: a meal, a song, a person, a memory.","Then have each resident write a short thank-you note to someone in the facility or family.","Offer to help deliver notes."]},
d:["What did you hear that made you smile?","Was it hard to think of things?","How did it feel to write a thank-you?"],
disc:[["Why is it easier to notice bad things than good things?","The brain is wired to look for danger."],["How can gratitude help on hard days?","Gives perspective, small moments of peace."],["How does saying thank you affect relationships?","People feel valued; closer bonds."],["Is it OK to not feel grateful sometimes?","Yes; feelings are real; gratitude is a practice, not a rule."],["What tradition of thankfulness did you grow up with?","Let residents share, including faith traditions."]],
take:"Look for the good, even small things, and say thank you. Gratitude does not fix everything, but it helps us see more clearly.",
tryit:"Write three good things before bed every night this week."},

{t:"Dealing with Loneliness",
goal:"Today we will talk about loneliness, why it happens, and ways to feel more connected.",
warm:["When do you feel most connected to other people?","What time of day or year feels loneliest?","Who was someone you felt really close to in your life?"],
terms:[
["Loneliness","feeling disconnected or without close relationships, even when people are around.","You can be in a room full of people and still feel lonely if no one really knows you.","Have you ever felt lonely in a crowd?"],
["Isolation","being physically alone or withdrawing from others.","Staying in your room all day and skipping meals and groups.","How can isolation make loneliness worse?"],
["Connection","feeling close to, understood by, or part of something bigger than yourself.","A good talk with a roommate, a hug from family, singing in church, being on a team.","Where do you feel connection?"],
["Reaching out","taking the first step to connect.","Saying, Want to play cards? or sitting with someone new at lunch.","What is an easy way to reach out here?"],
["Belonging","feeling accepted as part of a group.","Feeling like you matter in this house, this group, your church, or your family.","Where do you feel you belong?"]],
story:"Mr. Albert's wife passed away and his children lived far away. At Rose of Sharon, he mostly stayed in his room. One day a resident named Gus asked him if he knew how to play dominoes. Albert said he used to be the champ in his neighborhood. Now the two play every afternoon, and others started joining. Albert said, I came here thinking I'd be alone. Now I have a crew.",
sq:["Why was Mr. Albert lonely?","What small thing changed it?","How did his life change?","Who could you invite to do something with you?"],
act:{n:"Find Someone Who",m:"A sheet with prompts for each resident, pens",s:["Make a sheet with prompts: Find someone who likes the same music as you. Find someone born in the same month. Find someone who has a pet or had one. Find someone who likes to cook. Find someone who grew up in another state. Find someone with the same favorite food.","Residents walk or wheel around and ask each other questions, writing names.","If mobility is limited, go around the circle and let people raise hands.","Share surprising things in common.","Encourage people to follow up with someone they connected with."]},
d:["What did you learn about someone today?","What did you have in common with someone?","Who might you talk to more this week?"],
disc:[["Why can it be hard to make friends as an adult?","Fear of rejection, trust issues, past hurts, mental health."],["How does loneliness affect health?","Sadness, poor sleep, worse physical health."],["What activities here help people connect?","Groups, meals, games, outings, church."],["How can we include people who seem lonely?","Invite them, say hello, sit with them."],["What is one thing you could offer someone as a friend?","Let residents share their strengths."]],
take:"Loneliness is common and painful, but connection can start with one small invitation. Reach out and let others in.",
tryit:"Invite one person to do something with you this week: a game, a walk, or a meal."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Coping Skills & Emotional Wellness", [
{t:"Coping with Grief and Loss",
goal:"Today we will talk gently about grief, the different ways people grieve, and healthy ways to honor our losses.",
warm:["What is a happy memory of someone you love?","How did your family handle loss when you were growing up?","What helps you when you miss someone?"],
terms:[
["Grief","the deep sadness and other feelings we have after a loss.","Grief can come after a death, but also after losing a home, a job, health, a relationship, or custody of children.","What kinds of losses can cause grief?"],
["Stages of grief","common feelings people go through, like denial, anger, bargaining, sadness, and acceptance.","These do not happen in order. Someone may feel angry one day and peaceful the next, then sad again.","Have you felt more than one of these at once?"],
["Triggers of grief","things that bring back strong feelings of loss.","A holiday, a birthday, a song, a smell, or the anniversary of a death.","What brings up memories for you?"],
["Honoring a loved one","doing something to remember someone.","Looking at photos, telling stories about them, cooking their favorite meal, visiting their grave, or praying for them.","How do you honor someone you lost?"],
["Grief support","people and places that help with grief.","Talking with staff, a counselor, a chaplain, a support group, or friends who listen.","Who helps you with grief?"]],
story:"When Ms. Viola's sister died, she did not cry at first. She felt numb. Months later, at Thanksgiving, she suddenly could not stop crying when she smelled sweet potato pie, her sister's favorite. A caregiver sat with her and asked her to tell a story about her sister. Ms. Viola laughed and cried while she talked. She said, I didn't know I still needed to grieve. Thank you for letting me remember her.",
sq:["How did Ms. Viola feel at first?","What triggered her grief months later?","How did the caregiver help?","Why do you think telling a story helped?"],
act:{n:"Memory Sharing",m:"Paper, pens, crayons (optional)",s:["Explain gently that sharing is optional and anyone can pass.","Ask each resident to think of someone or something they have lost.","Invite them to write or draw a favorite memory, or something they wish they could say to that person.","Invite volunteers to share their memory with the group.","Close with a moment of silence or a short reflection: We remember the people we love."]},
d:["How did it feel to remember?","What did you notice when others shared?","Is there anything you want to do to honor your loved one?"],
disc:[["Is there a right way or a time limit to grieve?","No; everyone grieves differently."],["What unhealthy ways do people cope with grief?","Drinking, using, isolating, anger."],["How can we support someone who is grieving?","Listen, say their loved one's name, be present, do not rush them."],["What should we avoid saying to someone grieving?","Get over it, they're in a better place if the person does not share that belief; just listen."],["When should someone get extra help for grief?","When it stops them from functioning or they have thoughts of self-harm; tell staff."]],
take:"Grief is love with nowhere to go. There is no right way or timeline. Remembering and sharing helps, and you do not have to grieve alone.",
tryit:"Do one small thing this week to honor someone you miss."},

{t:"Handling Disappointment",
goal:"Today we will learn healthy ways to cope when things do not go the way we hoped.",
warm:["What is something you were really looking forward to that did not happen?","How do you usually react when plans change?","What helps you bounce back?"],
terms:[
["Disappointment","sadness or frustration when something does not turn out as hoped.","Your family said they would visit Sunday but canceled.","What disappointments are common here?"],
["Expectations","what we think will happen.","Expecting a visit, a phone call, or a certain answer from the doctor.","How can expectations set us up for disappointment?"],
["Flexibility","being able to adjust when plans change.","The outing is canceled because of rain, so you play a board game instead.","When have you been flexible?"],
["Plan B","a backup plan.","If my sister cannot visit, I'll call her and plan a video chat.","What could be a Plan B for a canceled outing?"],
["Bounce back","recovering and moving forward after a letdown.","Feeling sad for a while, then finding something else to look forward to.","How long does it usually take you to bounce back?"]],
story:"Lamont was supposed to go on a fishing trip with the facility, but it was canceled because of a storm. He was very upset and stormed off to his room. After a while, a caregiver came by and suggested they watch a fishing show and plan the trip for next week. Lamont agreed. He told the group later, I was mad, but it helped to have something else to look forward to.",
sq:["Why was Lamont disappointed?","How did he react at first?","What helped him bounce back?","What would you have done?"],
act:{n:"Plan B Brainstorm",m:"Board",s:["Read disappointing situations: your visitor cancels; you did not get the room you wanted; your favorite meal is not served; your outing is canceled; your appointment is moved.","For each, ask the group to come up with a Plan B and a kind thing to tell yourself.","Write answers on the board.","Ask: Which Plan B ideas could work for many situations?","Have each resident pick one Plan B they will remember."]},
d:["Which situation would be hardest for you?","What Plan B ideas did you like?","What can you tell yourself when disappointed?"],
disc:[["Is it OK to feel disappointed?","Yes; it is a normal feeling."],["What unhealthy reactions do people have to disappointment?","Yelling, giving up, isolating, blaming."],["How can we comfort someone who is disappointed?","Listen, acknowledge the feeling, help find a Plan B."],["How can lowering expectations help without losing hope?","Hope for the best, prepare for changes."],["What is something you are looking forward to now?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Disappointment is part of life. Let yourself feel it, then find a Plan B and something new to look forward to.",
tryit:"The next time a plan changes, come up with a Plan B within 10 minutes."},

{t:"Taming Worry",
goal:"Today we will learn the difference between helpful and unhelpful worry, and tools to keep worry from running our lives.",
warm:["What do you worry about most?","Does worrying ever help you?","What do you do when you cannot stop thinking about something?"],
terms:[
["Worry","repeated thoughts about things that might go wrong.","What if my check is late? What if I get sick? What if they don't call back?","Is worry about the past, present, or future?"],
["Helpful worry","worry that leads to action.","Worrying about a bill leads you to call and set up a payment plan.","When has worry helped you take action?"],
["Unhelpful worry","worry that goes in circles without solving anything.","Lying awake thinking about everything that could go wrong tomorrow.","What unhelpful worries keep you up?"],
["Control circle","sorting things into what you can control and what you cannot.","I can control taking my medicine and calling my doctor. I cannot control the weather or what others think.","What is something you cannot control that you worry about?"],
["Worry jar","writing worries on paper and putting them in a jar to set them aside.","Writing I'm worried about my son and putting it in the jar, then saying, I'll deal with this tomorrow at 10.","Could a worry jar help you?"]],
story:"Mr. Hector worried every night about his health, his money, and his kids. He could not sleep. His counselor taught him to draw two circles: inside, what he could control; outside, what he could not. He wrote his worries in the right circle. For things inside, he made one small step. For things outside, he practiced letting go and praying. He started sleeping better.",
sq:["What did Mr. Hector worry about?","What tool did his counselor teach?","What did he do with worries he could not control?","Which of your worries are inside your circle?"],
act:{n:"Circle of Control",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident draw a big circle with a smaller circle inside it.","Label the inside: Things I Can Control. Label the outside: Things I Can't Control.","Ask them to write their worries in the correct area.","For each worry inside the circle, write one small action step.","For worries outside, write one way to let go: pray, breathe, talk to someone, distract."]},
d:["Were more worries inside or outside your circle?","What action step did you write?","How does it feel to see worries on paper?"],
disc:[["Why does the mind keep worrying?","It tries to protect us from danger."],["How does worry affect the body?","Tension, poor sleep, stomach problems."],["What is a good way to stop a worry loop?","Write it down, take one action, distract, talk to someone."],["How can faith or spirituality help with worry?","Let residents share their beliefs respectfully."],["Who can you talk to about big worries?","Staff, counselor, social worker, chaplain."]],
take:"Act on what you can control, let go of what you cannot, and write worries down so they do not take over your mind.",
tryit:"Write your worries on paper before bed and put them in a jar or drawer for the night."},

{t:"Building Self-Esteem",
goal:"Today we will talk about self-esteem, how it is built, and practical ways to see our own worth.",
warm:["What is something you are good at?","What is a compliment you received that you remember?","What makes you feel proud?"],
terms:[
["Self-esteem","how you value and see yourself.","Someone with healthy self-esteem might think, I have strengths and weaknesses, and I'm worthy of respect.","How would you describe your self-esteem?"],
["Strengths","things you do well or good qualities you have.","Being kind, funny, a good cook, a hard worker, a good listener, faithful, honest.","What are three of your strengths?"],
["Comparison","measuring yourself against others.","Seeing others with families, cars, or jobs and thinking you are less than them. Everyone's path is different.","How does comparison affect you?"],
["Accomplishment","something you did that you can be proud of.","Getting through a hard week, finishing a project, staying sober, helping someone.","What is an accomplishment you are proud of?"],
["Accepting compliments","saying thank you instead of brushing off kind words.","Instead of Oh, it's nothing, say Thank you, I appreciate that.","Why is it hard for some people to accept compliments?"]],
story:"Ms. Rochelle always said, I'm not good at anything. During a group, residents went around naming each other's strengths. People said she was a great listener, had a beautiful laugh, and always made sure new residents felt welcome. Rochelle cried and said, I didn't know anyone saw that in me. She wrote the list down and kept it in her Bible.",
sq:["What did Ms. Rochelle believe about herself?","What did others see in her?","Why do you think she did not see those things?","What do you think others see in you?"],
act:{n:"Strength Spotlight",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Each resident writes their name at the top of a paper.","Pass the papers around the circle. Each person writes one kind, true thing about the person whose name is on the paper.","Remind everyone: only kind, specific comments.","When papers return to their owner, give time to read silently.","Invite volunteers to read one comment that surprised them, and practice saying thank you."]},
d:["How did it feel to read your paper?","Was it easier to write about others or receive comments?","What comment will you remember?"],
disc:[["What lowers self-esteem?","Criticism, trauma, mental illness, comparison, mistakes."],["What builds self-esteem?","Accomplishments, kind people, helping others, self-care, faith."],["How can we build each other up here?","Compliments, encouragement, celebrating wins."],["What is the difference between confidence and arrogance?","Confidence respects others; arrogance puts others down."],["What would you like to believe about yourself?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You have value just as you are. Notice your strengths, celebrate small wins, and accept kind words with a thank you.",
tryit:"Read your Strength Spotlight paper every morning this week."},

{t:"Journaling Your Thoughts",
goal:"Today we will learn how writing down our thoughts and feelings can help us understand ourselves and feel calmer.",
warm:["Have you ever kept a diary or journal?","Do you like writing or drawing?","Where do you go in your mind when you need to think?"],
terms:[
["Journal","a notebook where you write thoughts, feelings, or events.","Writing a few sentences each night about your day and how you felt.","What could you write about?"],
["Prompt","a question or idea to help you start writing.","Today I felt ___ because ___. Something that made me smile was ___.","Which prompt would you choose?"],
["Venting on paper","writing down angry or sad feelings instead of acting on them.","Writing a letter to someone who hurt you that you never send.","How could writing help with anger?"],
["Patterns","things that repeat over time.","After journaling for two weeks, you notice you feel worse every Monday, or after phone calls with a certain person.","Why would it help to notice patterns?"],
["No rules","journaling does not need correct spelling, full sentences, or good handwriting.","You can write lists, single words, or draw pictures. It is just for you.","Does spelling worry stop you from writing?"]],
story:"Mr. Terrence did not like talking about feelings in group. His counselor gave him a notebook and said, Just write three sentences a night. At first he wrote, Day was OK. Food was bad. Tired. Over time he wrote more. He noticed he felt angry every time he skipped breakfast. He started eating breakfast and his mornings went better. He said, The notebook listened better than people do sometimes.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Terrence start journaling?","How did his writing change over time?","What pattern did he discover?","What did he mean about the notebook listening?"],
act:{n:"Five-Minute Journal",m:"Paper or notebooks, pens, calm music if available",s:["Give each resident paper and a pen.","Write these prompts on the board: 1. Right now I feel ___. 2. Today something good was ___. 3. Something on my mind is ___. 4. Tomorrow I hope ___.","Give 5 quiet minutes to write or draw. Play soft music if possible.","Remind them spelling does not matter, and they do not have to share.","Invite volunteers to share one line if they want."]},
d:["How did it feel to write quietly?","Which prompt was easiest?","Would you try journaling on your own?"],
disc:[["How is journaling different from talking?","Private, no interruptions, can be honest."],["What if someone cannot write easily?","Draw, use a few words, dictate to staff, voice record."],["How can you keep your journal private?","Keep it in a safe place; others should respect privacy."],["When might journaling bring up hard feelings?","Writing about trauma; talk to a counselor if it gets too heavy."],["What time of day would work best for you to journal?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Writing is a safe place to put your thoughts. A few lines a day can help you understand yourself and calm your mind.",
tryit:"Write or draw in a journal for 5 minutes every night this week."},

{t:"Music, Art, and Creativity for Coping",
goal:"Today we will explore how music, art, and creative activities can help us cope with stress and express ourselves.",
warm:["What song always lifts your mood?","Did you ever draw, paint, sing, or play an instrument?","What creative thing would you like to try?"],
terms:[
["Creative expression","using art, music, writing, dance, or crafts to show feelings.","Drawing a storm when you feel stressed, or writing a poem about someone you love.","How do you express yourself?"],
["Music and mood","music can change how we feel.","An upbeat song can give energy; a slow song can calm us down or help us cry when we need to.","What song do you play when you are sad?"],
["Flow","being so focused on an activity that you lose track of time.","Coloring for an hour without noticing time passing, feeling calm and focused.","What activity gives you flow?"],
["Process, not product","the value is in doing it, not in making it perfect.","Your drawing does not have to look good. The calm you feel while drawing is the point.","Do you worry about being good at art?"],
["Playlist","a list of songs chosen for a purpose.","A calm-down playlist, a get-up-and-go playlist, a memories playlist.","What would be on your calm-down playlist?"]],
story:"Ms. Lucille rarely spoke in groups. During an art activity, she painted a garden with bright flowers. When asked about it, she shared that it was her grandmother's garden where she felt safe as a child. It was the most she had ever shared. After that, she started joining art group every week, and she slowly talked more in other groups too.",
sq:["How was Ms. Lucille different during art?","What did her painting show?","Why do you think art helped her share?","What creative activity might help you?"],
act:{n:"Draw Your Feeling",m:"Paper, crayons, markers, colored pencils; music",s:["Play calm music in the background if possible.","Ask residents to draw how they feel today using colors, shapes, or scenes. It does not need to look like anything.","Allow 8 to 10 minutes of quiet drawing.","Invite volunteers to share what they drew and why they chose those colors.","Ask each resident to name one song for a group calm-down playlist."]},
d:["What colors did you choose and why?","How did drawing feel?","What songs made the group playlist?"],
disc:[["How can music help when you feel anxious or angry?","Distraction, calming rhythm, expressing feelings."],["Why do some people avoid art?","Fear of being bad at it, feeling silly."],["What creative activities could we do more of here?","Singing, painting, crafts, poetry, dancing."],["How does music connect us to memories?","Songs bring back times and people."],["What talent would you like to share with the group someday?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Creativity is for everyone. Music, art, and writing help us feel, heal, and connect. It is about the process, not perfection.",
tryit:"Make a short list of 3 songs that calm you, and listen to one when you feel stressed."},

{t:"Mindfulness: Living in the Moment",
goal:"Today we will learn what mindfulness is and practice simple ways to slow down and pay attention to the present.",
warm:["When was the last time you felt truly relaxed?","Does your mind wander a lot during the day?","What do you notice when you slow down?"],
terms:[
["Mindfulness","paying attention to the present moment, on purpose, without judging it.","Eating a meal slowly and noticing the taste, smell, and warmth of the food instead of rushing.","What could you do mindfully today?"],
["Autopilot","doing things without really noticing.","Eating a whole bag of chips while watching TV without tasting them.","When are you on autopilot?"],
["Non-judgment","noticing thoughts and feelings without labeling them as good or bad.","Instead of I'm terrible for feeling angry, notice I'm feeling angry right now.","Why might non-judgment help?"],
["Mindful breathing","focusing on your breath as it goes in and out.","Feeling cool air in your nose and your belly rising, and gently coming back when your mind wanders.","What happens when you focus on breathing?"],
["Body scan","slowly noticing each part of your body from head to toe.","Noticing tight shoulders or a clenched jaw, and letting them relax.","Where do you hold tension?"]],
story:"Mr. Desmond always felt rushed and irritable. He ate fast, smoked fast, and talked fast. In a mindfulness group, he tried eating one raisin slowly, noticing its wrinkles, smell, and taste. He laughed and said, I never really tasted a raisin before. He started taking one mindful minute each morning with his coffee. He said it helped him start the day calmer.",
sq:["How did Mr. Desmond live before?","What did he notice with the raisin?","What new habit did he start?","Where could you add a mindful minute?"],
act:{n:"Mindful Moment and Body Scan",m:"A small food item like a raisin, grape, or mint for each person (check diets and allergies)",s:["Give each resident a small food item. Ask them not to eat it yet.","Guide them: look at it closely, feel it, smell it, put it on your tongue without chewing, then chew slowly and notice the taste.","Next, lead a 3-minute body scan: notice your feet, legs, belly, hands, shoulders, jaw, forehead. Let each part soften.","End with three mindful breaths.","Ask how it felt."]},
d:["What did you notice that you usually miss?","How did your body feel after the body scan?","Where could you use mindfulness in your day?"],
disc:[["Why is it hard to stay in the present?","Worries about the future, regrets about the past, distractions."],["How can mindfulness help with stress and anger?","Slows reactions, gives a pause."],["What daily activities could be done mindfully?","Showering, eating, walking, washing dishes."],["What should you do when your mind wanders?","Gently bring it back, without judgment."],["What is a peaceful moment you had recently?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Mindfulness means slowing down and noticing right now, without judgment. Even one mindful minute can calm your day.",
tryit:"Take one mindful minute each day this week during a meal, shower, or cup of coffee."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Coping Skills & Emotional Wellness", [
{t:"Healthy vs. Unhealthy Coping",
goal:"Today we will look at the different ways people cope with stress and learn to choose coping that helps instead of hurts.",
warm:["What do you do when you have a bad day?","What did the adults around you do to cope when you were young?","What is one way you cope that you are proud of?"],
terms:[
["Coping","what we do to handle stress, pain, or hard feelings.","Taking a walk after an argument, or calling a friend after bad news.","What is your go-to way of coping?"],
["Healthy coping","ways that help us feel better without causing harm later.","Exercise, prayer, talking, music, deep breathing, journaling, rest, hobbies.","Which healthy coping skill do you use most?"],
["Unhealthy coping","ways that feel good now but cause problems later.","Drinking, using drugs, overeating, yelling, isolating, overspending, self-harm.","Why do unhealthy ways feel good at first?"],
["Short-term vs. long-term","how something affects us now versus later.","A drink may calm nerves for an hour, but leads to hangovers, money problems, and more anxiety.","Can you think of something that helps short-term but hurts long-term?"],
["Swap","replacing an unhealthy habit with a healthy one.","When I want to yell, I go outside and walk for five minutes instead.","What swap could you make?"]],
story:"Whenever Mr. Andre felt stressed, he went to the corner store and bought a 40-ounce beer. It calmed him for a while, but his stress came back worse, and he spent money he needed. In group, he decided to try a swap: when stressed, he would call his cousin or walk around the block first. Some days he still struggled, but he cut down a lot and felt prouder of himself.",
sq:["What was Mr. Andre's unhealthy coping?","What did it cost him?","What swap did he try?","Why was progress better than perfection for him?"],
act:{n:"Sort the Coping",m:"Two columns on the board: Helps Me, Hurts Me Later",s:["Read a list of coping behaviors one at a time: going for a walk, drinking alcohol, calling a friend, smoking, praying, yelling at someone, listening to music, staying in bed all day, journaling, eating a whole box of cookies, talking to staff, punching a wall.","Have the group decide which column each belongs in.","Talk about any that could go either way (like TV or sleep).","Ask each resident to name one unhealthy habit they want to swap and what they will do instead.","Write the swaps on the board."]},
d:["Were any hard to sort?","What swap did you choose?","Who can help you stick to it?"],
disc:[["Why do people keep using unhealthy coping even when they know it hurts?","Habit, quick relief, addiction, no other tools."],["How can you make healthy coping easier to choose?","Plan ahead, keep tools nearby, have support."],["What gets in the way of healthy coping?","Low energy, cravings, no privacy, no one to talk to."],["How can friends help each other cope well?","Encourage, invite, do activities together."],["What healthy coping skill would you like to learn?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Everyone copes somehow. Choose ways that help today AND tomorrow, and swap one unhealthy habit at a time.",
tryit:"Use your healthy swap at least once this week when you feel stressed."},

{t:"Building Your Coping Toolbox",
goal:"Today we will put together a personal coping toolbox, a list of skills and items we can use when times get hard.",
warm:["What is in a regular toolbox?","What tool or skill has helped you through a hard time?","What do you reach for when you feel upset?"],
terms:[
["Coping toolbox","a collection of skills, items, and people that help you handle stress.","A box with a stress ball, a favorite photo, a list of calming songs, a prayer card, and a phone number of a friend.","What would you put in your toolbox?"],
["Calming tools","things that slow you down.","Deep breathing, soft music, a warm drink, a weighted blanket, a quiet place.","What calms you best?"],
["Distraction tools","things that take your mind off a problem for a while.","Puzzles, games, TV, cleaning, coloring, walking.","What distracts you in a healthy way?"],
["Comfort tools","things that make you feel safe and cared for.","A soft blanket, a photo of family, a favorite lotion, a Bible or prayer book.","What brings you comfort?"],
["People tools","people you can call or talk to.","A caregiver, the nurse, a counselor, a family member, a friend, or the 988 crisis line.","Who are your people tools?"]],
story:"Ms. Desiree made a coping box from a shoebox. Inside she put a stress ball, a photo of her grandkids, lavender lotion, a list of three songs, and a card with her sister's phone number and 988. One night she felt panicky. She opened the box, held the photo, used the lotion, and called her sister. She said, I didn't have to think. It was all right there.",
sq:["What did Ms. Desiree put in her box?","Why was it helpful to have it ready?","Which tools did she use that night?","What would be the first item in your box?"],
act:{n:"Make a Toolbox Card",m:"Index cards or paper folded in quarters, pens, markers",s:["Have each resident fold paper into four boxes.","Label the boxes: Calm Me, Distract Me, Comfort Me, People I Can Call.","Have them fill in at least two things for each box.","Encourage them to add 988 to the People box.","Invite them to decorate it and keep it by their bed or in their pocket."]},
d:["Which box was easiest to fill?","Which was hardest?","Where will you keep your card?"],
disc:[["Why is it important to plan coping tools before you need them?","When upset, it is hard to think clearly."],["What tools work quickly in public?","Breathing, grounding, a stress ball, music in earbuds."],["What if a tool stops working?","Try another one; that is why we have a toolbox."],["How can staff support your toolbox?","Remind you of it, help gather items."],["What new tool did you hear about today?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Build your coping toolbox before you need it: things that calm, distract, comfort, and people you can call.",
tryit:"Gather two real items for your coping toolbox this week and keep them together."},

{t:"Handling Criticism and Feedback",
goal:"Today we will learn how to listen to feedback, tell helpful from hurtful criticism, and respond calmly.",
warm:["How do you usually feel when someone corrects you?","What is a piece of advice that helped you?","Who gives you honest feedback?"],
terms:[
["Feedback","information about how you are doing, given to help you improve.","A staff member says, You did a great job making your bed. Next time, remember to put the dirty clothes in the hamper.","What feedback have you received recently?"],
["Constructive criticism","feedback meant to help, given respectfully.","Your music is a little loud for the hallway. Could you turn it down after 9?","How is this different from an insult?"],
["Hurtful criticism","comments meant to put you down.","You're so stupid, you can't do anything right.","How should you respond to hurtful comments?"],
["Defensiveness","reacting with anger or excuses instead of listening.","Snapping back, It's not my fault! before hearing the person out.","Why do we get defensive?"],
["Calm response","listening, pausing, and replying respectfully.","Thank you for telling me. I'll work on that. Or, I hear you, but I see it differently.","What calm response could you use?"]],
story:"At his volunteer job, Mr. Raymond's supervisor told him he needed to come back from breaks on time. Raymond felt embarrassed and wanted to argue. Instead, he took a breath and said, You're right. I'll set an alarm on my phone. The next week, his supervisor praised him for always being on time. Raymond said, If I'd argued, I might've lost that job.",
sq:["What feedback did Mr. Raymond get?","How did he feel at first?","How did he respond?","What would have happened if he argued?"],
act:{n:"Feedback Practice",m:"None",s:["Read examples of feedback and ask the group: Is this helpful or hurtful?","Your room needs to be cleaned before inspection. (Helpful.)","You're lazy and gross. (Hurtful.)","You talked over others in group today; try to wait your turn. (Helpful.)","Nobody wants you here. (Hurtful, tell staff.)","Then role-play: one person gives helpful feedback, the other practices a calm response: Thank you for telling me. I'll work on that.","Switch roles."]},
d:["How did it feel to receive feedback in the role-play?","What made the response calm?","How can you respond to hurtful comments?"],
disc:[["Why is feedback hard to hear?","It hurts our pride, reminds us of past criticism."],["How can feedback help us grow?","We learn what to improve, build skills."],["How should you give feedback to others?","Kindly, privately, specifically, with a suggestion."],["What can you do if criticism feels unfair?","Stay calm, explain your side respectfully, talk to staff."],["What is a compliment you could give someone today?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Helpful feedback is a gift. Pause, listen, and respond calmly. Hurtful words say more about the speaker than about you.",
tryit:"The next time someone gives you feedback, pause and say: Thank you, I'll think about that."},

{t:"Patience and Frustration",
goal:"Today we will learn about frustration, why patience is hard, and ways to stay calm when things take longer than we want.",
warm:["What makes you impatient?","How do you act when you have to wait?","Who is the most patient person you know?"],
terms:[
["Frustration","the feeling when something blocks what you want or need.","Waiting 30 minutes for your medicine pass, or a computer that won't load.","What frustrates you most here?"],
["Patience","staying calm while waiting or dealing with difficulties.","Waiting your turn in the bathroom without banging on the door.","Is patience something you are born with or can learn?"],
["Frustration tolerance","how much frustration you can handle before reacting.","Low tolerance: yelling when the remote does not work. Higher tolerance: taking a breath and changing the batteries.","How would you rate your frustration tolerance?"],
["Waiting activities","things to do while waiting.","Bring a puzzle book to appointments, hum a song, chat with someone, do breathing.","What could you do while waiting?"],
["Perspective","seeing the bigger picture.","Will this matter in a week? Usually a long wait is annoying but not a disaster.","How could perspective help with frustration?"]],
story:"Ms. Cora hated waiting at the doctor's office. Once she waited 90 minutes and yelled at the front desk staff, then felt embarrassed. The next time, she brought a crossword book and a snack, and asked staff at check-in how long the wait might be. She still waited an hour, but she stayed calm and even finished two puzzles.",
sq:["What frustrated Ms. Cora?","How did she react the first time?","What did she do differently?","What could you bring to make waiting easier?"],
act:{n:"Frustration Stations",m:"A tricky puzzle, a knot to untie, a stack of cards to sort (optional)",s:["Give small groups a mildly frustrating task: untangle a knotted string, stack cups into a tower, or sort a deck of cards by suit with one hand.","Ask them to notice their feelings while they work.","After 3 minutes, stop and ask what they felt in their body and mind.","Practice a calm reset: three deep breaths and the phrase, This is hard, and I can handle it.","Try the task again for 2 minutes and compare."]},
d:["What did frustration feel like in your body?","Did the calm reset help the second time?","What helps you stay patient?"],
disc:[["Why are we impatient more when tired, hungry, or in pain?","Fewer resources to cope."],["How does impatience affect relationships?","Snapping at people, hurt feelings."],["What can you say to yourself when frustrated?","This is temporary. I can handle it."],["How can we be patient with each other here?","Remember everyone is waiting; be kind to staff and peers."],["When is it OK to speak up instead of being patient?","When it is unsafe or unfair; do it calmly."]],
take:"Frustration is normal, and patience can be practiced. Breathe, bring something to do, and ask: Will this matter in a week?",
tryit:"The next time you have to wait, use the time for a breathing exercise or a small activity."},

{t:"Coping with Distressing Thoughts and Voices",
goal:"Today we will talk respectfully about distressing thoughts, including hearing voices for those who experience them, and share coping strategies that many people find helpful.",
warm:["What helps you when your mind feels noisy or busy?","Have you ever had a thought that would not go away?","What makes you feel calm and safe?"],
terms:[
["Intrusive thoughts","unwanted thoughts that pop up and feel upsetting.","A sudden worry that something bad will happen, or a scary image that will not leave.","Do you think most people have intrusive thoughts?"],
["Hearing voices","hearing a voice or sounds others do not hear. Many people with certain mental health conditions experience this.","Some voices are neutral, some are mean or scary. Having voices does not make someone a bad person.","How do you think it feels to hear voices others cannot?"],
["Reality check","comparing a thought or experience with what others see and hear.","Asking a trusted staff member, Did you hear that too? or checking whether something makes sense.","Who could you do a reality check with?"],
["Distraction","shifting focus to something else to turn down distress.","Humming, listening to music with headphones, talking with someone, reading out loud.","What distraction might help with a busy mind?"],
["Tell your team","letting your nurse, doctor, or counselor know when thoughts or voices change.","If voices get louder or tell you to hurt yourself or others, tell staff right away. Your team can help.","Why is it important to tell your team about changes?"]],
story:"Mr. Jamal hears voices sometimes. When they get loud, he used to argue with them out loud, which upset other residents. With his counselor, he learned some strategies: he hums a gospel song, listens to music with headphones, and tells a staff member, The voices are loud today. The staff member stays nearby and lets the nurse know. Jamal says he feels more in control and less alone.",
sq:["What did Mr. Jamal used to do?","What strategies did he learn?","Why does telling staff help?","How can the group support someone like Jamal?"],
act:{n:"Turn Down the Noise",m:"Music player or phone with calm music; paper and pens",s:["Explain that everyone has a busy or upsetting mind sometimes, so these tools are for everyone. No one has to share personal experiences.","Try three tools together for 1 minute each: humming a familiar song together; counting backward from 50 out loud as a group; listening to a calm song.","After each, ask how loud or busy their mind felt from 1 to 10.","Have each person write down their top two tools.","Remind everyone of who to tell when thoughts feel scary or unsafe."]},
d:["Which tool helped quiet your mind most?","Did humming or counting surprise you?","Who will you tell if thoughts become frightening?"],
disc:[["Why might someone hide that they hear voices or have scary thoughts?","Fear of judgment, the hospital, or medicine changes."],["How can we make it safe to talk about this here?","No teasing, keep what is shared private, be kind."],["What should you do if voices or thoughts tell you to hurt yourself or someone else?","Tell staff immediately; call 988."],["How does stress affect thoughts and voices?","Stress, poor sleep, and missed medicine can make them worse."],["What helps you feel grounded and safe?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Distressing thoughts and voices are health experiences, not character flaws. Use your tools, do a reality check, and always tell staff when things get louder or scary.",
tryit:"Practice one of your two tools this week, even on a calm day."},

{t:"Coping with Change",
goal:"Today we will talk about how change affects us and how to handle changes in our lives with more ease.",
warm:["What is the biggest change you have had in your life?","Do you like change or prefer things to stay the same?","What change are you facing right now?"],
terms:[
["Change","when something becomes different.","A new roommate, a new medicine, a staff member leaving, moving to a new place, or a new routine.","What changes happen often here?"],
["Transition","the time of adjusting to a change.","The first few weeks in a new home when everything feels unfamiliar.","How long does it take you to adjust?"],
["Uncertainty","not knowing what will happen.","Waiting to hear about housing or a test result.","How does uncertainty make you feel?"],
["Adapt","adjusting to new conditions.","Learning the new schedule and finding a new favorite spot to sit.","When have you adapted well to something?"],
["Anchors","things that stay the same and help you feel steady during change.","Your morning coffee, your faith, a favorite song, a friend, your routine.","What are your anchors?"]],
story:"When Ms. Alice's favorite caregiver moved to another job, Alice felt sad and angry and did not want to talk to the new caregiver. Her counselor helped her name her feelings and reminded her of her anchors: her morning prayer, her puzzles, and her friend down the hall. Slowly, Alice got to know the new caregiver and found out they both loved gospel music. Now they sing together on Sundays.",
sq:["What change did Ms. Alice face?","How did she react at first?","What anchors helped her?","How did things turn out?"],
act:{n:"My Anchors",m:"Paper, pens; draw an anchor shape if you like",s:["Have each resident draw a simple anchor or a box.","Inside, write 5 anchors: things, people, or routines that stay steady for them.","Around the outside, write a change they are dealing with or worried about.","Ask: How can your anchors help you with this change?","Invite volunteers to share."]},
d:["What anchors did people list?","What change are you facing?","How can your anchors help?"],
disc:[["Why is change hard even when it is good?","The unknown, losing what was familiar."],["What are signs someone is struggling with change?","Irritability, withdrawing, trouble sleeping."],["How can we welcome new residents or staff?","Say hello, show them around, include them."],["What change in your life turned out better than expected?","Let residents share."],["What can you control during a big change?","Your routine, your attitude, who you reach out to."]],
take:"Change is part of life. Name your feelings, lean on your anchors, and give yourself time to adjust.",
tryit:"Do one of your anchor activities every day this week."},

{t:"Finding Joy and Laughter",
goal:"Today we will explore how joy and laughter help our health and find ways to bring more of them into our days.",
warm:["What is the funniest thing that has happened to you?","Who makes you laugh the most?","What simple thing brings you joy?"],
terms:[
["Joy","a feeling of happiness and delight.","Hearing a favorite song, a baby's laugh, sunshine after rain, a good meal.","What brought you joy this week?"],
["Laughter","a natural reaction to something funny.","Laughing lowers stress, relaxes muscles, and helps people feel closer.","How do you feel after a good laugh?"],
["Savoring","slowing down to enjoy a good moment fully.","Sitting with your coffee in the sun and really noticing how good it feels.","What moment could you savor today?"],
["Play","doing something just for fun.","Board games, card games, dancing, silly jokes, tossing a ball.","What did you love to play as a kid?"],
["Joy list","a list of things that make you happy.","Gospel music, peach cobbler, my grandson's voice, a clean bed, ballgames.","What would be on your joy list?"]],
story:"The residents at one home were grumpy during a long, gray winter. A caregiver started Joke of the Day at breakfast. At first, people rolled their eyes. Then a resident named Mr. Otis started bringing his own corny jokes. Soon everyone was groaning and laughing together. Staff noticed fewer arguments and more people coming to breakfast.",
sq:["What was the mood like in the home?","What did the caregiver try?","How did Mr. Otis help?","What changed in the home?"],
act:{n:"Joke and Joy Circle",m:"A few clean, simple jokes written down ahead of time",s:["Start by reading a clean, silly joke (for example: Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field.).","Invite residents to share any clean joke or funny story.","Then go around the circle and have each person name three things for their joy list.","Write a group joy list on the board.","End with a group laugh: everyone fake laughs for 10 seconds, which usually turns into real laughter."]},
d:["What joke made you laugh?","What was on the group joy list?","How do you feel now compared to the start of group?"],
disc:[["Why is laughter good for health?","Reduces stress hormones, eases pain, boosts mood."],["Is it OK to laugh during hard times?","Yes; joy and sadness can live together."],["What kinds of humor should we avoid?","Jokes that put people down, mean teasing."],["How can we bring more joy into our home?","Music, games, celebrations, jokes, decorations."],["What is one thing you can do for fun this week?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Joy and laughter are medicine. Look for small joys, savor them, and share a laugh with someone.",
tryit:"Do one thing from your joy list every day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Addiction & Recovery", [
{t:"What Is Addiction?",
goal:"Today we will learn what addiction is, how it affects the brain, and why recovery is possible.",
warm:["What do you think of when you hear the word addiction?","What things can people get addicted to?","How has addiction touched your life or community? You can keep it general."],
terms:[
["Addiction","a condition where a person keeps using a substance or doing a behavior even though it causes harm.","Someone keeps drinking every day even after losing a job, getting sick, and hurting family.","Can someone be addicted to things that are not drugs?"],
["Substance","a drug or chemical that changes how the brain and body feel.","Alcohol, nicotine, marijuana, cocaine, meth, opioids like pain pills or heroin, and some prescription pills.","Which substances are most common in our area?"],
["Brain reward system","the part of the brain that makes us feel pleasure and want to repeat things.","Drugs flood this system with feel-good chemicals. Over time, the brain needs more to feel normal, and normal pleasures feel dull.","Why might everyday fun stop feeling good during addiction?"],
["Tolerance","needing more of a substance to get the same effect.","At first, two beers made Ray feel relaxed. A year later, he needed eight.","Why is tolerance dangerous?"],
["Recovery","the ongoing process of getting healthy and living without the harmful use of substances.","Recovery can include treatment, meetings, counseling, medicine, faith, new friends, and new habits. Millions of people are in recovery.","What does recovery mean to you?"]],
story:"Tina started drinking at parties in her twenties. Over time, she drank alone every night to sleep. She lost her apartment and her relationship with her kids. She thought she was a bad person. In treatment, she learned addiction is a health condition that changes the brain, and that it can be treated. Tina has been sober three years. She calls her kids every Sunday and says, I'm not a bad person getting good. I'm a sick person getting well.",
sq:["How did Tina's drinking change over time?","What did she believe about herself?","What did she learn in treatment?","What does her quote mean to you?"],
act:{n:"Myth or Fact",m:"None",s:["Read each statement and have the group say MYTH or FACT.","Addiction is just a lack of willpower. (Myth; it is a health condition.)","Anyone can become addicted. (Fact.)","You have to hit rock bottom before you can recover. (Myth.)","Relapse means treatment failed. (Myth; relapse can be part of recovery.)","Addiction changes the brain. (Fact.)","Prescription pills cannot be addictive. (Myth.)","Discuss each answer."]},
d:["Which myth have you heard most?","Which fact surprised you?","How might believing myths keep someone from getting help?"],
disc:[["Why do people start using substances?","Pain, stress, trauma, curiosity, friends, mental illness, boredom."],["What makes it hard to stop?","Cravings, withdrawal, friends who use, stress, shame."],["What helps people recover?","Support, treatment, meetings, purpose, new habits, faith, medication."],["How does shame affect someone with addiction?","Keeps them hiding and using; compassion helps."],["What message of hope would you give someone struggling?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Addiction is a health condition that changes the brain, not a moral failure. Recovery is real, and it happens one day at a time.",
tryit:"Think about what recovery or a healthy life looks like for you, and share it with someone you trust."},

{t:"Triggers and Cravings",
goal:"Today we will learn what triggers cravings and how to plan for them so they do not lead to use.",
warm:["What makes you crave something, like a snack or a cigarette?","Where are you when cravings hit hardest?","What has helped you get through a craving before?"],
terms:[
["Craving","a strong urge or desire to use a substance.","Feeling like you need a drink so badly you cannot think of anything else.","How long do you think a craving lasts?"],
["External trigger","something outside of you that sparks a craving.","Seeing a liquor store, hanging out with old using friends, payday, a party, or hearing a certain song.","What external triggers are around here?"],
["Internal trigger","a feeling or thought inside you that sparks a craving.","Feeling bored, lonely, angry, anxious, or even very happy and wanting to celebrate.","What feelings make cravings stronger for you?"],
["Avoid, change, or cope","three ways to handle triggers.","Avoid: take a different route than past the bar. Change: go to the cookout but bring your own soda and a sober friend. Cope: call someone when lonely.","Which approach do you use most?"],
["Cravings pass","cravings rise like a wave and fall, often within 15 to 30 minutes.","If you can ride it out without using, it gets weaker. Each time you do, the next one is a little easier.","What could you do for 20 minutes during a craving?"]],
story:"Every Friday, Marcus got paid and walked past the liquor store. Every Friday, he bought a bottle. His counselor helped him see that payday and that route were his triggers. Marcus started having his check direct deposited, taking a different street, and going to a Friday evening meeting. He said, I didn't become stronger. I just stopped walking into the fire.",
sq:["What were Marcus's triggers?","What three changes did he make?","What did he mean by I just stopped walking into the fire?","What fire do you need to stop walking into?"],
act:{n:"Trigger Map",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident fold a paper in half.","On the left side, write PEOPLE, PLACES, THINGS, FEELINGS. List one or two triggers under each.","On the right side, next to each trigger, write a plan: avoid it, change it, or a coping skill.","Invite volunteers to share one trigger and plan.","Remind residents that triggers do not have to involve drugs; they can be for any unhealthy habit."]},
d:["Which trigger is hardest to avoid?","What plan are you most confident about?","Who can you tell about your triggers?"],
disc:[["Why are feelings sometimes stronger triggers than places?","You cannot walk away from your own feelings."],["What is HALT?","Hungry, Angry, Lonely, Tired: check these before cravings."],["How can someone ride out a craving?","Distract, call someone, leave the situation, breathe, pray."],["How can people around you help or hurt your recovery?","Supportive people help; using friends make it harder."],["What gives you strength during a craving?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Know your triggers. Avoid what you can, change what you can, and use coping skills for the rest. Cravings always pass.",
tryit:"The next time you have a craving, set a timer for 20 minutes and do something else until it rings."},

{t:"Riding Out the Urge",
goal:"Today we will practice urge surfing and other tools to get through cravings without acting on them.",
warm:["Have you ever wanted something badly and waited it out?","What did it feel like afterward?","What helps you stay patient with yourself?"],
terms:[
["Urge surfing","noticing a craving like a wave, watching it rise, peak, and fall, without acting on it.","Instead of fighting the craving, you sit and notice: my mouth is watering, my chest is tight. Then you notice it slowly fading.","What does a craving feel like in your body?"],
["Peak","the strongest point of a craving.","The craving is worst around 10 to 15 minutes in, then begins to drop.","Why is it important to know the craving will peak and fall?"],
["Play the tape forward","thinking through what will really happen if you use.","One drink becomes ten, I spend my money, I miss my medicine, I feel ashamed tomorrow.","What happens when you play the tape forward?"],
["Delay","putting off a decision for a set time.","I won't decide for 30 minutes. In that time, I'll call my sponsor and take a walk.","How might delaying help?"],
["Reward yourself","celebrating each time you get through a craving.","A favorite snack, a call to a loved one, marking a calendar, or telling the group.","What reward motivates you?"]],
story:"Ms. Janelle was 60 days sober when she smelled marijuana outside and felt a huge craving. She remembered urge surfing. She sat on a bench, breathed, and described the feeling to herself: My heart is racing. I want it bad. Then she played the tape forward: losing her housing, starting over. After 15 minutes, the urge faded. She called her sponsor and celebrated with an ice cream.",
sq:["What triggered Ms. Janelle?","What did she do instead of using?","What happened to the craving?","How did she reward herself?"],
act:{n:"Practice Urge Surfing",m:"A small treat placed in front of each person (optional, check diets) or imagination",s:["Explain: We'll practice resisting a small urge.","If using treats, place one in front of each person and ask them NOT to eat it for 5 minutes. Otherwise, ask them to imagine their favorite food in front of them.","Guide: Notice the urge. Where do you feel it? Is it getting stronger? Breathe slowly. Describe it to yourself.","Every minute, ask them to rate the urge from 1 to 10.","After 5 minutes, ask how the urge changed. Then they may enjoy the treat."]},
d:["Did the urge go up, down, or both?","What helped you wait?","How could you use this with a real craving?"],
disc:[["Why does fighting a craving sometimes make it stronger?","Focusing on not doing it keeps it in mind; surfing accepts it."],["What thoughts make cravings feel unbeatable?","I can't stand this. Just one won't hurt."],["What true thoughts can replace those?","This will pass. I've gotten through it before."],["Who could you call during a craving?","Sponsor, staff, counselor, friend."],["What would you like to celebrate in your recovery?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Cravings are waves. Notice them, breathe, play the tape forward, delay, and they will pass. Celebrate every wave you ride out.",
tryit:"The next time you feel any urge, rate it from 1 to 10 every few minutes and watch it change."},

{t:"Saying No to Drugs and Alcohol",
goal:"Today we will practice clear, confident ways to say no when someone offers drugs or alcohol.",
warm:["Has anyone ever pressured you to do something you didn't want to do?","How easy is it for you to say no to friends?","What is one thing you've said no to that you're proud of?"],
terms:[
["Refusal skills","ways to say no clearly and confidently.","No thanks, I don't drink. said with eye contact and a steady voice.","Why does how you say no matter as much as what you say?"],
["Peer pressure","pushing from others to join in.","Come on, just one hit. Don't be lame. We're all doing it.","What does peer pressure sound like?"],
["Broken record","repeating your answer calmly without explaining.","No thanks. No thanks. I said no thanks.","Why might explaining too much backfire?"],
["Exit plan","a plan for leaving a situation where people are using.","Telling your ride ahead of time, I'll text you if I need to leave early. Or saying, I have to get back for my medicine.","What could your exit plan be?"],
["Offer an alternative","suggesting something else to do.","No thanks, but do you want to go get some food?","What alternative could you offer?"]],
story:"Lonnie ran into an old friend at the corner store who offered him a pill. Lonnie had practiced in group. He looked at his friend and said, No thanks, man, I'm doing good now. His friend said, Come on, one won't hurt. Lonnie said, No thanks. I gotta get back. He walked away. On the way home he called his counselor and told her. She said, That's huge. Lonnie said he felt strong.",
sq:["What did Lonnie's friend offer?","What did Lonnie say?","How did he handle the second push?","What did he do afterward?"],
act:{n:"Refusal Role-Play",m:"None",s:["Ask for volunteers to play the person offering and the person refusing.","Scenario 1: A friend offers a beer at a cookout.","Scenario 2: Someone offers a pill to help you relax.","Scenario 3: A family member says, You're no fun anymore.","The refuser uses: a clear no, eye contact, the broken record, an alternative, or an exit.","After each round, the group gives positive feedback."]},
d:["Which scenario felt most real?","Which technique felt most natural to you?","How did it feel to say no out loud?"],
disc:[["Why is it harder to say no to friends and family?","Fear of losing them, wanting to belong."],["What should you do if someone keeps pushing?","Leave, tell staff, avoid that person."],["Do you owe anyone an explanation?","No; No thanks is enough."],["How can you find friends who support your recovery?","Meetings, church, groups, activities."],["What would you say to a younger person facing pressure?","Let residents share."]],
take:"No thanks is a complete sentence. Say it clearly, repeat it, offer an alternative, and leave if you need to.",
tryit:"Practice your no-thanks line out loud three times this week."},

{t:"Making a Relapse Prevention Plan",
goal:"Today we will learn what relapse is, the warning signs that come before it, and how to make a plan to protect our recovery.",
warm:["What helps you stay on track with any goal?","What does relapse mean to you?","What has helped people you know stay sober?"],
terms:[
["Relapse","returning to substance use after a period of not using.","After six months sober, Jake drank at a wedding and kept drinking for a week.","How might someone feel after a relapse?"],
["Lapse","a single slip that does not turn into a full return to old patterns.","Having one drink, then calling your sponsor the next morning and getting back on track.","What is the difference between a lapse and a relapse?"],
["Warning signs","changes that often come before relapse.","Skipping meetings, isolating, romanticizing old using days, stopping medicine, hanging out with old friends, saying I can handle one.","What warning signs have you noticed in yourself or others?"],
["Relapse prevention plan","a written plan of your triggers, warning signs, coping skills, and support people.","My warning sign: skipping meals and staying in my room. My action: call my sponsor and go to a meeting that day.","What would go in your plan?"],
["Getting back up","what to do if a lapse happens.","Be honest, tell your support person right away, go to a meeting, and remember one slip does not erase your progress.","Why is honesty after a slip so important?"]],
story:"Renee had been clean for a year when she started skipping her meetings because she felt fine. She stopped calling her sponsor and began hanging out with her cousin who still used. One night she used again. The next morning she felt ashamed, but she called her sponsor instead of hiding. Her sponsor said, You're back. That's what matters. Renee went to a meeting that afternoon and updated her plan.",
sq:["What warning signs did Renee show?","What did she do after she used?","How did her sponsor respond?","What would you add to Renee's plan?"],
act:{n:"Write My Plan",m:"A simple plan template on paper",s:["Hand out a plan sheet with these lines: My triggers are ___. My warning signs are ___. When I notice a warning sign I will ___. My support people are ___ (with phone numbers). My reasons for staying in recovery are ___.","Give 10 minutes to fill it out. Help anyone who needs it.","Invite volunteers to share their reasons for recovery.","Encourage residents to give a copy to staff or their counselor.","Remind them a plan can be for any unhealthy habit, not just drugs."]},
d:["What are your strongest reasons for recovery?","Which warning sign do you need to watch for?","Who will you share your plan with?"],
disc:[["Why do people stop doing what kept them sober?","Feeling cured, overconfidence, life gets busy."],["What does shame do after a slip?","Keeps people using and hiding."],["How can we support someone who has relapsed?","Welcome them back, no judgment, help them reconnect."],["Is relapse a failure?","No; it is information about what to adjust."],["What keeps you motivated?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Relapse starts before the first drink or drug. Know your warning signs, have a written plan, and if you slip, tell someone right away and get back up.",
tryit:"Give a copy of your plan to a staff member or counselor this week."},

{t:"Alcohol and Your Body",
goal:"Today we will learn how alcohol affects the body and brain, and why it is especially risky with medications and mental health conditions.",
warm:["Why do people drink alcohol?","What are some drinks people commonly have?","What have you noticed about how alcohol affects people?"],
terms:[
["Standard drink","a measure of how much alcohol is in a drink.","One 12-oz beer, one 5-oz glass of wine, or one 1.5-oz shot of liquor each have about the same alcohol. A 40-oz malt liquor can be 4 or 5 standard drinks.","How many standard drinks are in a 40?"],
["Depressant","a substance that slows down the brain and body.","Alcohol slows reaction time, judgment, and breathing. That is why it is dangerous to drive or mix with other depressants.","How does slowed judgment lead to trouble?"],
["Alcohol and medication","many medicines are dangerous with alcohol.","Alcohol with anxiety pills, sleeping pills, pain pills, or some mood medicines can cause extreme sleepiness, trouble breathing, or overdose. It can also stop medicines from working.","Why do medicine bottles warn against alcohol?"],
["Long-term effects","harm that builds over years.","Liver damage, high blood pressure, memory loss, stomach problems, depression, and certain cancers.","What long-term effects have you seen?"],
["Alcohol withdrawal","symptoms when someone who drinks heavily stops suddenly.","Shaking, sweating, anxiety, and in serious cases seizures. Heavy drinkers should stop only with medical help.","Why is alcohol withdrawal dangerous?"]],
story:"Mr. Frank drank a few beers every night with his medicine because he thought it helped him sleep. He started waking up confused and fell twice in the bathroom. His doctor explained that alcohol was mixing badly with his medicine and making his depression worse. With the nurse's help, Frank stopped drinking safely. His sleep got better, and he stopped falling.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Frank drink?","What problems did it cause?","What did the doctor explain?","Why did Frank need help to stop?"],
act:{n:"Count the Drinks",m:"Board",s:["Draw pictures or write names of drinks: 12-oz beer, 24-oz tall can, 40-oz malt liquor, 5-oz glass of wine, a pint of liquor, a shot.","Ask the group to guess how many standard drinks are in each.","Reveal: 12-oz beer = 1. 24-oz can = 2 (or more if strong). 40-oz = 4 to 5. 5-oz wine = 1. Pint of liquor = about 8 to 11. Shot = 1.","Discuss how quickly drinks add up.","Ask: How does this change how you think about one drink?"]},
d:["Which number surprised you?","How might people underestimate how much they drink?","What did you learn about alcohol and medicine?"],
disc:[["How does alcohol affect mental health?","Worsens depression and anxiety, disrupts sleep, can trigger psychosis or suicidal thoughts."],["Why do people think alcohol helps them sleep?","It makes you drowsy but ruins deep sleep."],["What are signs someone has had too much?","Slurred speech, stumbling, vomiting, passing out; get help if they can't be woken."],["Why is drinking against the rules in many group homes?","Safety, medicines, a stable environment for everyone."],["What do you enjoy doing that doesn't involve alcohol?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Alcohol slows the brain and body, mixes dangerously with many medicines, and makes mental health symptoms worse. If you want to stop, get medical help to do it safely.",
tryit:"Ask the nurse whether any of your medicines are dangerous with alcohol."},

{t:"Opioids, Overdose, and Naloxone",
goal:"Today we will learn about opioids, the signs of an overdose, and how naloxone (Narcan) can save a life.",
warm:["What have you heard about the opioid crisis?","Do you know someone affected by opioids? You can keep it general.","What would you do if you found someone unresponsive?"],
terms:[
["Opioids","drugs that relieve pain and can cause a strong high, including prescription pain pills, heroin, and fentanyl.","Oxycodone, hydrocodone, morphine, heroin, and fentanyl are all opioids.","Why are prescription opioids still risky?"],
["Fentanyl","a very strong opioid that is often mixed into other drugs without people knowing.","Fake pills that look like real prescription pills, or other street drugs, may contain fentanyl. A tiny amount can kill.","Why is fentanyl so dangerous?"],
["Overdose","when too much of a drug overwhelms the body, often slowing or stopping breathing.","Signs: not waking up, very slow or no breathing, blue or gray lips or fingertips, tiny pupils, gurgling or snoring sounds.","What are the signs of an opioid overdose?"],
["Naloxone (Narcan)","a medicine, often a nasal spray, that can reverse an opioid overdose.","Spray it in one nostril, call 911, and give a second dose in 2 to 3 minutes if the person does not wake up. It is safe even if you are not sure it is an opioid overdose.","Do you know where naloxone is kept here?"],
["Never use alone","a safety rule for people who still use drugs.","If someone is going to use, having someone nearby who has naloxone and can call 911 can save their life. Tennessee also has Good Samaritan protections when you call for help in an overdose.","Why is using alone so dangerous?"]],
story:"At a bus stop, Ms. Tasha saw a young man slumped over, lips turning blue. She had been trained on Narcan at her group home and carried it in her purse. She called 911, sprayed it into his nose, and talked to him. After a couple of minutes he gasped and woke up. Paramedics took over. Tasha said, I was so scared, but I'm glad I knew what to do.",
sq:["What signs did Tasha notice?","What steps did she take?","Why was it important she carried Narcan?","How would you feel in her shoes?"],
act:{n:"Overdose Response Steps",m:"Board; a naloxone training device if available",s:["Write the steps on the board: 1. Check: shout their name and rub the breastbone firmly. 2. Call 911. 3. Give naloxone: one spray in one nostril. 4. Support breathing if trained, or put them on their side. 5. Stay with them. 6. Give a second dose in 2 to 3 minutes if no response.","Read through the steps twice with the group.","If a training device is available, let residents practice holding it.","Quiz the group by reading steps out of order and having them put them in order.","Ask staff to confirm where naloxone is kept at the facility."]},
d:["Which step do you think is most important?","Do you feel more ready to help someone?","Where can people get free naloxone?"],
disc:[["Why do people hesitate to call 911 during an overdose?","Fear of police; Good Samaritan laws offer some protection."],["Why is it important not to leave someone alone after naloxone?","It can wear off before the opioid does."],["How can we reduce stigma for people who use opioids?","Treat them as people, offer help, not judgment."],["What treatment options exist for opioid addiction?","Medications like buprenorphine or methadone, counseling, programs; ask the social worker."],["What message would you share about fentanyl?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Know the signs of overdose: no response, slow or no breathing, blue lips. Call 911, give naloxone, and stay with the person. You can save a life.",
tryit:"Ask staff where naloxone is kept here, and how you can get trained."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Addiction & Recovery", [
{t:"People, Places, and Things",
goal:"Today we will talk about how the people, places, and things around us can support or threaten recovery and healthy choices.",
warm:["Who in your life makes you feel your best?","What place makes you feel peaceful?","What object reminds you of a hard time?"],
terms:[
["People","the friends, family, and others we spend time with.","Old using buddies can pull you back. Supportive friends, staff, and a sponsor help you move forward.","Who supports your healthy choices?"],
["Places","locations linked to using or to healthy living.","A bar, a certain street corner, or a friend's house where people use. Healthy places: church, the library, the park, meetings.","What places do you want to avoid?"],
["Things","objects that remind you of using.","A lighter, pipe, rolling papers, a certain song, cash in your pocket.","What things might be triggers?"],
["Setting boundaries","deciding what you will and won't do with certain people.","I love you, but I can't come over if you're using.","Who might you need a boundary with?"],
["New playground","building a new set of healthy people, places, and things.","New friends at meetings, a new hobby, a new route home, a new favorite coffee shop.","What new healthy place could you go?"]],
story:"When Derrick got out of treatment, he went back to the same apartment complex where his friends still used. Within a month he was using again. After his second treatment, he moved into a group home across town, changed his phone number, and started going to a men's group at a church. He said, I had to change my whole playground. It was lonely at first, but now I have real friends.",
sq:["Why did Derrick relapse the first time?","What did he change the second time?","Why was it lonely at first?","What part of your playground might need to change?"],
act:{n:"My Circle",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident draw three circles inside each other like a target.","In the center: people who strongly support you.","Middle circle: people who are OK but not always helpful.","Outer circle: people who pull you toward unhealthy choices.","Ask them to reflect: Who should I spend more time with? Who do I need a boundary with?"]},
d:["Was it hard to place some people?","Who is in your center?","What boundary might you need to set?"],
disc:[["Why is it so hard to let go of old friends?","History, loyalty, loneliness, feeling judged by new people."],["How can you set a boundary with a family member who uses?","Clear limits, meet in safe places, short visits."],["Where can you meet new healthy friends?","Meetings, church, volunteer work, groups here."],["What healthy places in Chattanooga do you enjoy?","Let residents share."],["How can we be good people for each other here?","Support, encouragement, honesty."]],
take:"Recovery often means changing people, places, and things. Protect your progress by building a new, healthy playground.",
tryit:"Spend time this week with someone from the center of your circle."},

{t:"Building a Support Network",
goal:"Today we will learn why support is important for recovery and wellness, and how to build a strong network of people who have our back.",
warm:["Who is someone you can always count on?","Is it easy or hard for you to ask for help?","Who has helped you in the past?"],
terms:[
["Support network","the group of people who help you stay well.","Family, friends, staff, a counselor, a sponsor, a pastor, a doctor, and peers in groups.","Who is in your network now?"],
["Sponsor or mentor","someone further along in recovery who guides you.","In 12-step programs, a sponsor is someone with longer sobriety you can call when struggling.","What would you want in a sponsor or mentor?"],
["Peer support","help from people with similar life experiences.","A peer support specialist is someone in recovery who is trained to help others.","Why might advice from a peer feel different?"],
["Professional support","help from trained helpers.","Counselors, doctors, nurses, case managers, social workers.","Who are your professional supports?"],
["Two-way street","healthy relationships go both ways.","Your friend listens to you, and you listen to them. You both give and receive.","How do you support others?"]],
story:"When Ms. Gail came to the facility, she said, I don't need anybody. She had been hurt by many people. Slowly, she got to know her counselor, a peer support worker, and a woman from church who visited on Sundays. When her brother died, Gail did not use. She called her peer support worker and went to church. She said, For the first time, I had people to lean on, and I didn't fall.",
sq:["Why did Ms. Gail think she didn't need anybody?","Who became part of her network?","How did her network help when her brother died?","Who could you add to your network?"],
act:{n:"My Support Web",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident write their name in the middle of a page.","Draw lines out like a web to the people who support them. Label each: family, friend, staff, professional, faith, peer.","Next to each name, write how they help (listens, gives rides, prays, gives advice).","Circle any gaps: areas where you would like more support.","Write one step to strengthen your web, like going to a meeting or calling a relative."]},
d:["How many people did you put in your web?","Where are your gaps?","What step will you take?"],
disc:[["Why do people try to do it alone?","Pride, distrust, shame, past hurt."],["How do you know if someone is a healthy support?","They respect you, encourage good choices, are honest."],["How can you ask for help without feeling like a burden?","Remember others want to help; you would help them."],["What support groups are available in our area?","AA, NA, church groups, NAMI; ask the social worker."],["How can you be a support for someone else?","Let residents share."]],
take:"No one recovers alone. Build a web of family, friends, peers, and professionals, and be a support for others too.",
tryit:"Reach out to one person in your support web this week."},

{t:"Recovery Meetings and Support Groups",
goal:"Today we will learn what recovery meetings and support groups are like, and how to find one that fits.",
warm:["Have you ever been to a support group or meeting?","What did you think of it?","What would make you want to go to a meeting?"],
terms:[
["12-step program","a recovery program with 12 steps, like Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) or Narcotics Anonymous (NA).","Members share their experiences, work the steps, and support each other. Meetings are free.","What have you heard about AA or NA?"],
["Open vs. closed meeting","open meetings welcome anyone; closed meetings are only for people who want to stop using.","A family member can go to an open AA meeting to learn more.","Why might someone prefer an open meeting?"],
["Sharing","talking about your experiences in a meeting.","You can share or just listen. Many people say, I'll pass, at first.","Would you feel comfortable sharing?"],
["Other options","there are many kinds of support groups.","SMART Recovery uses practical tools, Celebrate Recovery is church-based, and NAMI groups support mental health.","Which kind of group sounds best for you?"],
["Anonymity","keeping who you see and what you hear in meetings private.","What's said here stays here. This makes meetings feel safe.","Why is anonymity important?"]],
story:"Leroy was scared to go to his first NA meeting. He sat in the back and didn't say a word. People welcomed him and gave him a list of phone numbers. He came back the next week and the next. By the third month, he shared his story for the first time. Afterward, a young man came up and said, You just told my story. Leroy realized his pain could help someone else.",
sq:["How did Leroy feel at his first meeting?","What made him come back?","What happened when he shared?","Why does sharing help others too?"],
act:{n:"Mock Meeting",m:"Chairs in a circle",s:["Explain you will practice what a meeting feels like. Sharing is optional.","Read an opening: Welcome. This is a safe place. What is said here stays here. We share one at a time and do not interrupt or give advice.","Give a topic: What is one thing that helps you get through a hard day?","Go around the circle. Each person can share briefly or say, I pass.","Close by thanking everyone and reading a simple hopeful line: Keep coming back."]},
d:["How did the practice meeting feel?","What would make a real meeting easier for you?","What did you learn from someone else's share?"],
disc:[["Why do meetings help people stay sober?","Support, accountability, hope, structure."],["What fears keep people from going?","Being recognized, speaking in public, religion, not fitting in."],["What if you don't connect with the first meeting you go to?","Try others; each group is different."],["How can staff help you get to a meeting?","Find times, arrange rides, go the first time with you."],["What would you want to share someday?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Meetings and support groups are free, welcoming places of hope. You can listen first and share when ready. Try more than one to find your fit.",
tryit:"Ask staff about recovery or support meetings nearby, or attend one this week."},

{t:"HALT: Hungry, Angry, Lonely, Tired",
goal:"Today we will learn the HALT check-in, a simple tool to notice our basic needs before they lead to poor choices.",
warm:["How do you act when you are really hungry?","What do you do when you are very tired?","Which one affects your mood the most: hunger, anger, loneliness, or tiredness?"],
terms:[
["HALT","a quick check: am I Hungry, Angry, Lonely, or Tired?","Before you snap at someone or reach for a drink, stop and ask: H-A-L-T?","Why might these four make cravings and bad moods worse?"],
["Hungry","needing food or water, or emotional nourishment.","Skipping breakfast can make you irritable by noon. Hunger can also be for comfort or attention.","What happens to your mood when you skip a meal?"],
["Angry","feeling upset, resentful, or frustrated.","Holding a grudge all day can build until it explodes.","What helps you let go of anger?"],
["Lonely","feeling disconnected or isolated.","Sitting in your room all weekend with no one to talk to.","What helps when you feel lonely?"],
["Tired","physically or mentally worn out.","After a bad night's sleep, everything feels harder and cravings feel stronger.","How does tiredness affect your choices?"]],
story:"One afternoon, Ms. Ruby felt a strong urge to smoke and wanted to yell at her roommate. She remembered HALT. She realized she had skipped lunch, was angry about a phone call, and hadn't slept well. She ate a sandwich, called her sister about the phone call, and took a 20-minute nap. When she woke up, the urge was gone and she was not angry at her roommate anymore.",
sq:["What did Ms. Ruby feel at first?","Which HALT needs did she discover?","What did she do for each one?","How did it change her afternoon?"],
act:{n:"HALT Check-In",m:"Board with H, A, L, T written down the side",s:["Explain HALT and write the letters on the board.","Have each resident rate themselves right now from 0 to 5 for each letter.","For each letter, brainstorm quick fixes: Hungry: snack, water. Angry: walk, talk, write. Lonely: call, visit a friend, join an activity. Tired: rest, early bedtime.","Have each resident pick one fix for their highest letter.","Practice saying HALT out loud as a group."]},
d:["Which letter was highest for you today?","What quick fix will you use?","When could you use HALT during your day?"],
disc:[["Why do basic needs affect decisions?","The brain has less control when needs are not met."],["How can you plan ahead to avoid HALT?","Regular meals, sleep routine, daily connection, dealing with anger early."],["How can HALT help in relationships?","Avoid arguments when tired or hungry."],["How can staff help with HALT needs?","Snacks, quiet time, conversation."],["Which letter is hardest for you to manage?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Before you react, HALT. Are you Hungry, Angry, Lonely, or Tired? Take care of the need first.",
tryit:"Check HALT at least twice a day this week, especially before making a big decision."},

{t:"Recovery and Mental Health Together",
goal:"Today we will learn how substance use and mental health conditions affect each other, and why treating both matters.",
warm:["Why do you think so many people with mental health struggles also use substances?","What have you noticed about how substances affect mood?","What helps both your mind and body feel healthy?"],
terms:[
["Co-occurring disorders","having both a mental health condition and a substance use problem.","Someone with depression who also drinks heavily, or someone with anxiety who uses marijuana every day.","Why might these often go together?"],
["Self-medicating","using substances to try to manage mental health symptoms.","Drinking to calm anxiety or using stimulants to feel energy during depression.","Does self-medicating usually work long-term?"],
["Vicious cycle","when two problems feed each other.","Drinking to ease depression makes depression worse, which leads to more drinking.","Can you think of other vicious cycles?"],
["Integrated treatment","treating mental health and substance use together, by the same team or a team that works together.","A counselor who addresses both your bipolar disorder and alcohol use in the same plan.","Why is treating both together helpful?"],
["Medication and substances","drugs and alcohol can interfere with mental health medicines.","Using marijuana or alcohol can make psychiatric medicines less effective and can trigger symptoms like paranoia.","What should you tell your doctor about substance use?"]],
story:"Carlos had schizophrenia and used marijuana to cope with anxiety. He thought it helped, but his voices kept getting worse and he ended up in the hospital twice. In a program that treated both, he learned that marijuana could make his symptoms worse. With support, he cut back and then stopped. His medicine worked better, and he stayed out of the hospital for a year.",
sq:["Why did Carlos use marijuana?","What did it do to his symptoms?","What kind of program helped?","What changed when he stopped?"],
act:{n:"The Cycle",m:"Board",s:["Draw a circle with four arrows. Label: Feel bad, Use substance, Feel better briefly, Feel worse later.","Walk through an example together, such as anxiety and drinking.","Ask the group to suggest where healthy coping could break the cycle.","Write healthy replacements next to the Use substance arrow: call someone, breathe, talk to the nurse, go to a meeting.","Have each resident think of their own cycle privately and one place to break it."]},
d:["Where is the easiest place to break the cycle?","What healthy replacement would work for you?","Why is honesty with your treatment team important?"],
disc:[["Why might people hide substance use from their doctors?","Fear of judgment or losing medicine; doctors need to know to keep you safe."],["What symptoms can substances worsen?","Depression, anxiety, paranoia, voices, mania, sleep problems."],["What are healthier ways to manage symptoms?","Medicine, counseling, coping skills, routine, support."],["How does stable mental health help recovery?","Fewer cravings, more coping ability."],["What hope do you have for your mental health and recovery?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Mental health and substance use affect each other. Be honest with your team, treat both together, and use healthy coping to break the cycle.",
tryit:"Be honest with your nurse or doctor about any substance use at your next visit."},

{t:"Filling Free Time in Recovery",
goal:"Today we will find healthy, enjoyable ways to fill free time, since boredom is a major trigger for unhealthy choices.",
warm:["What do you do when you are bored?","What hobbies did you have before?","What would you try if money were no issue?"],
terms:[
["Boredom","feeling restless with nothing interesting to do.","Long weekend afternoons with nothing planned can lead to cravings or trouble.","When are you most bored?"],
["Hobby","an activity done for enjoyment in free time.","Gardening, fishing, puzzles, drawing, cooking, cards, music, reading, crocheting.","What hobbies do you enjoy?"],
["Structure","having a plan or routine for your time.","Saturday: morning walk, laundry, lunch, library at 2, call family at 5.","How does structure help in recovery?"],
["Free activities","fun things that cost little or no money.","Parks, walking trails, free museum days, the library, church events, community centers, volunteering.","What free activities are near here?"],
["Purpose","doing something that feels meaningful.","Volunteering at a food bank, helping a neighbor, mentoring a newcomer, caring for plants.","What gives you a sense of purpose?"]],
story:"When Mr. Bobby stopped drinking, he realized he had no idea what to do with his evenings. He felt bored and restless. His counselor asked what he loved as a kid. He said fishing and building model cars. With staff help, he got a cheap model kit and joined the facility fishing outing. Building models gave him something to do with his hands at night. He said, I forgot how much I liked it.",
sq:["Why was Mr. Bobby restless?","What question did his counselor ask?","What hobbies did he return to?","What did you love doing as a kid?"],
act:{n:"Activity Bingo",m:"Bingo cards with activities (draw a 4x4 grid on paper), pens",s:["Have each resident draw a 4x4 grid.","Call out activities and let residents write ones they would enjoy in the squares: walk, puzzle, cards, cook, draw, music, read, garden, call family, volunteer, fish, crochet, church, library, dance, write.","Ask: Which have you done in the past month? Mark those squares.","Ask each person to circle 2 squares they will try this week.","Share the circles with the group."]},
d:["Which activities have you done recently?","What two will you try?","Who would like to do an activity together?"],
disc:[["Why is boredom dangerous for recovery?","Restlessness, cravings, falling into old habits."],["What gets in the way of trying new hobbies?","Money, energy, confidence, transportation."],["How can we do more activities together here?","Suggest them to staff, form a group."],["What skill could you teach someone else?","Let residents share."],["How does having a purpose help mental health?","Meaning, self-worth, motivation."]],
take:"Boredom is a trigger. Fill your time with hobbies, structure, and purpose, and you will protect your recovery and enjoy life more.",
tryit:"Try one new or old hobby this week."},

{t:"Gambling, Lottery, and Other Behavioral Addictions",
goal:"Today we will learn about addictions that do not involve substances, like gambling, and how to spot when a habit becomes a problem.",
warm:["Have you ever bought a lottery ticket or scratch-off? What happened?","What habits can people get hooked on besides drugs?","What makes gambling exciting for people?"],
terms:[
["Behavioral addiction","when a person cannot stop a behavior even though it causes harm.","Gambling, shopping, video games, or phone use that takes over a person's life and money.","What behaviors could become addictive?"],
["Odds","the chance of winning.","The odds of winning a big Powerball jackpot are about 1 in 292 million. Scratch-offs pay back much less than people spend over time.","What do those odds mean in real life?"],
["Chasing losses","gambling more to try to win back money already lost.","Losing $20 on scratch-offs, then buying $40 more to win it back, and losing that too.","Why is chasing losses dangerous?"],
["Near miss","almost winning, which tricks the brain into thinking a win is close.","Two cherries and a lemon on a slot machine feel close, but it is still a loss.","How does a near miss keep people playing?"],
["Warning signs","signs a habit is becoming a problem.","Hiding it, borrowing money to do it, feeling anxious when not doing it, spending bill money on it.","What warning signs would you watch for?"]],
story:"Mr. Clarence bought scratch-offs every day with his spending money. Sometimes he won $5 or $10, which kept him hooked. He started borrowing from other residents and could not buy his toiletries. When staff helped him add it up, he had spent over $900 in six months and won back about $300. He decided to limit himself to one ticket a week and put the rest in savings.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Clarence keep buying tickets?","How did it affect his life?","What did the math show?","What limit did he set?"],
act:{n:"The Lottery Math",m:"Board",s:["Ask: If someone buys $5 of scratch-offs a day, how much is that a week? ($35) A month? (about $150) A year? (about $1,825)","Explain that, on average, lotteries pay back much less than people spend.","Ask: If they win back about a third, how much is lost in a year? (about $1,200)","Ask the group what $1,200 could buy instead.","Discuss the feelings that come with winning and losing."]},
d:["Were you surprised by the yearly total?","What would you do with that money instead?","How does a small win keep people playing?"],
disc:[["Is buying a lottery ticket always a problem?","Not always; it becomes a problem when it causes harm or loss of control."],["Why is gambling advertised so much?","Lottery and casinos make big profits."],["What can someone do if gambling is a problem?","Set limits, avoid stores, call the Tennessee REDLINE at 1-800-889-9789, Gamblers Anonymous."],["What other behaviors can become addictive?","Phone, shopping, video games, food."],["How can someone get the thrill of excitement in healthier ways?","Games for fun, sports, competitions without money."]],
take:"Any behavior can become an addiction if we lose control. Know the odds, watch for warning signs, set limits, and ask for help.",
tryit:"If you gamble or buy lottery tickets, write down how much you spend this week and add it up."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Addiction & Recovery", [
{t:"Marijuana: Facts and Myths",
goal:"Today we will look at the facts about marijuana, how it can affect mental health and medications, and how to make informed choices.",
warm:["What have you heard people say about marijuana?","Why do you think people use it?","Do you think marijuana is harmless? Why or why not?"],
terms:[
["THC","the chemical in marijuana that causes the high.","Today's marijuana and vapes often have much more THC than in the past, which makes effects stronger.","Why might stronger THC matter?"],
["Legal status","whether something is allowed by law.","Marijuana is still illegal for recreational use in Tennessee, and it is against the rules in most group homes.","What could happen if someone is caught with it?"],
["Psychosis risk","marijuana, especially high-THC types, can trigger or worsen paranoia, hallucinations, and psychosis in some people.","Someone with schizophrenia or bipolar disorder may have more symptoms or hospital stays when using.","Why might people with certain conditions be more at risk?"],
["Cannabis use disorder","when someone cannot control their marijuana use and it causes problems.","Using every day, needing more, feeling irritable without it, spending bill money on it.","Can marijuana be addictive?"],
["Motivation and memory","marijuana can affect drive, focus, and memory.","Using daily can make it harder to remember appointments, follow through on goals, or learn new skills.","How might this affect someone's goals?"]],
story:"Dante smoked weed every day and said it calmed his anxiety. But he started feeling like people were watching him, and he stopped leaving his room. His doctor explained that the strong THC was likely making his paranoia worse. Dante cut back with support from his counselor. After a few weeks, the paranoia eased and he started going to the gym with a friend.",
sq:["Why did Dante say he used?","What new symptoms appeared?","What did his doctor explain?","What happened when he cut back?"],
act:{n:"Fact or Myth",m:"None",s:["Read each statement and have the group answer FACT or MYTH.","Marijuana is natural, so it is always safe. (Myth.)","Marijuana can make some mental health symptoms worse. (Fact.)","People cannot get addicted to marijuana. (Myth.)","Marijuana can affect memory and focus. (Fact.)","It is legal to use recreationally in Tennessee. (Myth.)","Driving high is safe if you feel fine. (Myth.)","Discuss each answer."]},
d:["Which myth have you heard most often?","Which fact surprised you?","How might this information affect your choices?"],
disc:[["Why do people believe marijuana is harmless?","It's natural, legal in some states, common."],["What are healthier ways to manage anxiety or sleep?","Breathing, routine, medicine, counseling, exercise."],["Why should you tell your doctor about marijuana use?","It can affect medicines and symptoms."],["What are the legal and housing risks?","Charges, losing housing, probation violations."],["What are your goals, and how might substances affect them?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Marijuana is not harmless, especially for people with mental health conditions. It can worsen symptoms, affect medicine, and is illegal in Tennessee. Be honest with your doctor.",
tryit:"If you have questions about how marijuana affects your health, ask the nurse or your doctor."},

{t:"Stimulants: Meth and Cocaine",
goal:"Today we will learn how stimulant drugs like methamphetamine and cocaine affect the body and mind, and the risks they carry.",
warm:["What have you heard about meth or cocaine?","Why do you think people use stimulants?","How have these drugs affected communities you know?"],
terms:[
["Stimulant","a drug that speeds up the brain and body.","Meth, cocaine, and crack make the heart race, increase energy, and reduce sleep and appetite.","What are some effects of speeding up the body?"],
["Crash","the low that follows a stimulant high.","After the high, a person may feel exhausted, very depressed, irritable, and crave more.","Why does the crash make people use again?"],
["Paranoia and psychosis","feeling watched or in danger, seeing or hearing things that are not there.","Using meth for days without sleep can cause someone to believe people are after them.","Why might lack of sleep make this worse?"],
["Heart risks","stimulants can cause heart attacks, strokes, and dangerous heart rhythms, even in young people.","A 35-year-old man had a heart attack after using cocaine.","Why are stimulants hard on the heart?"],
["Contamination","street drugs are often mixed with other substances, including fentanyl.","People who thought they were using cocaine have died from fentanyl overdose.","What does this mean for someone who uses?"]],
story:"Keisha started using meth to stay awake for long work shifts. Soon she was up for days at a time. She lost weight, her teeth started to break, and she felt like people were following her. She lost her job and her kids went to her mother. In treatment, she learned to sleep and eat again. It took a long time, but her paranoia faded. She now visits her kids every weekend.",
sq:["Why did Keisha start using?","How did meth affect her body and mind?","What did she lose?","What gave her hope?"],
act:{n:"Effects Timeline",m:"Board",s:["Draw a line labeled: First use, Weeks later, Months later, Years later.","Ask the group to name effects at each stage: First use: energy, confidence. Weeks: less sleep, weight loss, cravings. Months: paranoia, money problems, relationship problems. Years: heart problems, dental damage, memory loss, losing housing.","Then draw a second line: Recovery starts, Weeks later, Months later, Years later.","Fill it with healing: sleep returns, appetite returns, thinking improves, relationships rebuild.","Point out that the brain and body can heal."]},
d:["What stood out about the first timeline?","What gives hope in the recovery timeline?","What would you tell someone just starting to use?"],
disc:[["Why are stimulants so addictive?","Intense high, crash, strong cravings."],["What should you do if someone is having a bad reaction?","Get staff, call 911 for chest pain, seizures, high fever, or extreme agitation."],["Is there treatment for stimulant addiction?","Yes; counseling, contingency management, support groups."],["How do stimulants affect mental health conditions?","Can trigger mania, psychosis, worsen anxiety and depression."],["What helps people stay away from stimulants?","Support, sleep, meaningful activities, avoiding triggers."]],
take:"Stimulants speed up the body until it breaks down, and street drugs may contain fentanyl. Recovery is possible, and the brain and body can heal.",
tryit:"Think of one reason you value your health and write it down."},

{t:"Prescription Drug Misuse",
goal:"Today we will learn how prescription medicines can be misused and how to use our own medicines safely.",
warm:["Have you ever been prescribed a medicine that made you feel really good or relaxed?","Why do you think some people misuse prescription pills?","Who should decide how much medicine you take?"],
terms:[
["Misuse","taking a medicine in a way not prescribed.","Taking more than prescribed, taking it more often, crushing or snorting it, or using someone else's prescription.","What are examples of misuse?"],
["Commonly misused medicines","types of prescription drugs people often misuse.","Opioid pain pills, benzodiazepines like Xanax or Klonopin, sleep pills, and stimulants like Adderall.","Why might these be misused?"],
["Dependence","when the body gets used to a medicine and has withdrawal without it.","Someone taking a benzodiazepine daily for a long time may need to slowly reduce it with a doctor.","Why should you never stop certain medicines suddenly?"],
["Doctor shopping","going to multiple doctors to get more prescriptions.","Visiting several urgent care clinics for pain pills. This is illegal and tracked in Tennessee.","Why is doctor shopping risky?"],
["Safe use","taking medicines exactly as prescribed and being honest with your doctor.","If your pain medicine does not work, tell your doctor instead of taking extra.","What does safe use look like for you?"]],
story:"After surgery, Mr. Jerome was given pain pills. When the pain went away, he kept taking them because they helped him relax. When the prescription ran out, he felt sick and anxious and started buying pills on the street. A friend found him unresponsive one night, and he survived because of Narcan. In treatment he said, I never thought I'd be an addict. It started with a doctor's prescription.",
sq:["How did Jerome's use start?","When did it become misuse?","What happened when the pills ran out?","What could have helped earlier?"],
act:{n:"Safe Medicine Habits",m:"Board",s:["Write two columns: SAFE and NOT SAFE.","Read behaviors and have the group sort them: taking medicine as directed; taking a friend's pill; telling the doctor about side effects; saving old pills for later; mixing pills with alcohol; asking the pharmacist questions; taking extra when stressed; using medicine take-back boxes for old pills.","Discuss each.","Ask staff or note where medicine disposal options are available.","Remind everyone medicine is managed by staff here for everyone's safety."]},
d:["Which behavior was surprising?","What is one safe habit you will keep?","Why is medicine take-back important?"],
disc:[["Why do people think prescription pills are safer than street drugs?","A doctor prescribed them; they look official."],["What should you do if you feel you need more medicine than prescribed?","Talk to your doctor or nurse."],["How can old pills in a home be dangerous?","Others may take them; kids, visitors, misuse."],["What are signs someone may be misusing medicine?","Running out early, seeming high, mood swings, asking others for pills."],["How can we support each other in using medicine safely?","Honesty, no sharing, encouraging talks with the nurse."]],
take:"Prescription medicines are powerful. Take them exactly as prescribed, never share them, and tell your doctor if something is not working.",
tryit:"Ask the nurse if any of your medicines can cause dependence, and how to take them safely."},

{t:"Shame, Guilt, and Forgiveness in Recovery",
goal:"Today we will talk about the difference between shame and guilt, and how forgiveness of ourselves and others supports healing.",
warm:["What does forgiveness mean to you?","Is it easier to forgive others or yourself?","What helps you move on from a mistake?"],
terms:[
["Guilt","feeling bad about something you did.","I feel bad that I lied to my mom. Guilt can lead us to make things right.","How can guilt be helpful?"],
["Shame","feeling that you are bad as a person.","I'm a terrible person. I don't deserve anything good. Shame often keeps people stuck and using.","Why is shame more harmful than guilt?"],
["Amends","making things right with people you have hurt, when it is safe and appropriate.","Apologizing sincerely, paying back money, or changing behavior.","What is the difference between saying sorry and making amends?"],
["Self-forgiveness","letting go of punishing yourself for past mistakes while still learning from them.","I made mistakes when I was using. I am not that person today. I am growing.","Why is self-forgiveness hard?"],
["Forgiving others","letting go of resentment toward someone who hurt you, for your own peace. It does not mean what they did was OK.","Holding on to anger is like drinking poison and expecting the other person to get sick.","What does that saying mean to you?"]],
story:"Ms. Tonya missed most of her daughter's childhood because of her addiction. Even after two years sober, she felt she did not deserve her daughter's love. Her counselor helped her see the difference between guilt and shame. Tonya wrote her daughter a letter making amends, without expecting anything back. Months later, her daughter called and invited her to her son's birthday party. Tonya cried and said, I'm learning I can be a good grandma now.",
sq:["What was Tonya feeling?","How did shame affect her?","What step did she take to make amends?","How did self-forgiveness help her?"],
act:{n:"Letter of Release",m:"Paper, pens, envelopes (optional)",s:["Explain that this letter is private and will not be shared or mailed unless the writer chooses.","Ask residents to write a short letter, either to themselves forgiving a past mistake, or to someone they are working to forgive.","Suggested start: I am letting go of ___ because ___.","Give 8 to 10 quiet minutes.","Offer the option to tear up the letter as a symbol of release, keep it, or talk to a counselor about it."]},
d:["How did writing the letter feel?","Did you choose to keep or tear up your letter?","What does letting go mean to you today?"],
disc:[["How does shame keep people stuck?","Hiding, isolating, using to numb the pain."],["What helps reduce shame?","Talking openly, being heard without judgment, support groups."],["Does forgiving someone mean you have to trust them again?","No; forgiveness and trust are different."],["When is it not safe to make amends directly?","If it would cause harm to you or others; talk to a counselor."],["What would you say to someone who feels they can't be forgiven?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Guilt says I did something bad; shame says I am bad. You are not your worst mistake. Make amends where you can, and forgive yourself so you can grow.",
tryit:"Say one kind, forgiving sentence to yourself each morning this week."},

{t:"Addiction and the Family",
goal:"Today we will talk about how addiction affects families and how families can heal together.",
warm:["Who in your family are you closest to?","How do families show love?","How has family helped or hurt you on your journey?"],
terms:[
["Family disease","addiction affects the whole family, not just the person using.","Kids worry, partners lose trust, parents feel guilty, and money problems hit everyone.","How can one person's addiction affect a whole family?"],
["Enabling","doing things that make it easier for someone to keep using, even with good intentions.","Giving money that gets spent on drugs, making excuses, or cleaning up their messes.","Why do family members enable?"],
["Rebuilding trust","slowly earning back trust through consistent actions.","Showing up when you say you will, being honest, staying sober over time.","How long does rebuilding trust take?"],
["Healthy boundaries in families","limits that protect everyone.","A mother says, I love you, but I won't give you money. I'll buy you groceries instead.","What boundary would help your family?"],
["Family support resources","groups and services that help families.","Al-Anon and Nar-Anon are support groups for family members. Family therapy can help everyone heal.","How could these groups help families?"]],
story:"Mr. Rodney's daughter stopped talking to him after he missed her graduation because he was high. When he got sober, he wanted her back right away, but she was not ready. His counselor told him to focus on being consistent. He sent her a short card every month, without pressure. After a year, she called him. They met for lunch. He said, I wanted it fast, but trust takes the time it takes.",
sq:["How had Rodney's addiction hurt his daughter?","What did his counselor suggest?","Why was consistency important?","What does trust takes the time it takes mean?"],
act:{n:"Building Trust Bricks",m:"Paper cut into rectangles, markers, tape",s:["Give each resident 3 or 4 paper rectangles (bricks).","On each brick, write one action that builds trust: showing up on time, keeping promises, being honest, saying sorry, staying sober, listening.","Take turns reading bricks out loud and taping them to the wall or board to build a wall together.","Point out that trust is built brick by brick, one action at a time.","Ask each person which brick they want to work on this week."]},
d:["Which brick do you think is most important?","Which brick will you work on?","How long do you think it takes to build trust back?"],
disc:[["How can families heal after addiction?","Honesty, time, therapy, support groups, consistency."],["What if a family is not ready to reconnect?","Respect their pace, keep doing the work."],["How can you support family members without enabling?","Offer help that does not involve money for using; encourage treatment."],["What do you wish your family understood?","Let residents share."],["Who is your family of choice?","Let residents share; friends and community count too."]],
take:"Addiction hurts families, and families can heal. Trust is rebuilt one consistent action at a time, and everyone deserves support.",
tryit:"Do one trust-building action for someone you care about this week."},

{t:"Celebrating Milestones",
goal:"Today we will celebrate progress, big and small, in recovery and wellness, and learn why recognizing wins matters.",
warm:["What is something you have accomplished that you are proud of?","How do you like to celebrate good news?","Who would you call first with good news?"],
terms:[
["Milestone","an important point of progress.","30 days sober, one year without a hospital stay, getting your ID, finishing a class, or making a new friend.","What milestones have you reached?"],
["Small wins","little steps of progress that add up.","Going to group three days in a row, taking your medicine every day this week, or saying no to a craving.","What small win did you have this week?"],
["Chip or token","in recovery programs, a coin given to mark time in sobriety.","A 30-day chip, a 90-day chip, a one-year coin.","What does a chip or token mean to people?"],
["Healthy celebration","celebrating without substances or harmful behaviors.","A special meal, a phone call, a new book, an outing, a party with soda and cake.","How could you celebrate in a healthy way?"],
["Looking back","comparing where you are now to where you started.","A year ago I was in the hospital. Today I'm leading a group.","How have you changed since last year?"]],
story:"Ms. Gloria never celebrated anything because she felt she had not done enough. In group, her peers asked her to share what had changed in a year. She said she had not been to the hospital, had reconnected with her son, and learned to cook. The group cheered for her. She laughed and said, I didn't realize how far I'd come until I said it out loud.",
sq:["Why didn't Ms. Gloria celebrate before?","What progress had she made?","How did the group react?","Why does saying it out loud help?"],
act:{n:"Wall of Wins",m:"Sticky notes or paper squares, tape, markers",s:["Give each resident 2 or 3 sticky notes.","Ask them to write a win, big or small, from the past month or year.","Have each person read one win out loud as they stick it on the wall.","After each win, the group claps or cheers.","Leave the Wall of Wins up if staff allow, so residents can add more over time."]},
d:["Which win inspired you?","How did it feel to be cheered for?","What win do you want to add next month?"],
disc:[["Why do people overlook their own progress?","Focusing on mistakes, comparing, waiting for big changes."],["How does celebrating help motivation?","Gives hope, energy to keep going."],["What are healthy ways to celebrate here?","Special meals, outings, recognition, parties."],["How can we celebrate each other more?","Notice and say it, cards, group shout-outs."],["What milestone are you working toward next?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Every step forward counts. Celebrate your wins in healthy ways and cheer each other on.",
tryit:"Write down one win every day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Safety & Violence Prevention", [
{t:"Warning Signs Before Violence",
goal:"Today we will learn to recognize warning signs that a situation may become violent, in ourselves and others, so we can step in early and stay safe.",
warm:["Have you ever been in a situation that felt like it was about to get out of hand?","What did you notice before it happened?","What helps you feel safe here?"],
terms:[
["Violence","using force to hurt, threaten, or scare someone.","Hitting, pushing, throwing things, threatening, or breaking someone's belongings.","What kinds of violence have you seen?"],
["Escalation","when a situation keeps getting more intense.","A disagreement turns into yelling, then pointing fingers in faces, then shoving.","What are the steps of escalation you've seen?"],
["Body warning signs","physical clues someone is getting close to losing control.","Clenched fists, red face, pacing, staring, standing too close, heavy breathing, puffing up.","Which signs do you notice first in others?"],
["Verbal warning signs","words or tone that show rising anger.","Yelling, cursing, threats like You better watch yourself, or going very quiet and cold.","What words are red flags?"],
["Step back and get help","leaving and alerting staff when you see warning signs.","Instead of jumping in, say, I'm going to step away, and find a caregiver right away.","Why is getting staff better than handling it yourself?"]],
story:"In the TV room, Mr. Lewis and Mr. Clay argued over the remote. Mr. Clay stood up, clenched his fists, and started pacing. Ms. Pearl, sitting nearby, saw the signs. She quietly left and told a caregiver. The caregiver came in calmly, offered Mr. Clay a walk outside, and the situation cooled off before anyone got hurt. Later, Mr. Clay thanked Ms. Pearl.",
sq:["What warning signs did Mr. Clay show?","What did Ms. Pearl do?","How did the caregiver help?","Why did Mr. Clay thank her later?"],
act:{n:"Spot the Signs",m:"None",s:["Read short situations and ask the group to name the warning signs and what to do.","Someone is pacing and muttering after a phone call. (Signs: pacing, muttering. Action: give space, tell staff.)","Two residents are standing nose to nose, voices rising. (Action: step away, get staff.)","Someone slams a door and punches a pillow. (Action: give space, tell staff, check later.)","You notice your own heart racing and fists clenching. (Action: walk away, breathe, find staff.)","Discuss each one."]},
d:["Which signs are easiest to spot?","What would you do if you noticed signs in yourself?","Who would you go to?"],
disc:[["Why do people ignore early warning signs?","Hoping it will pass, not wanting to snitch, fear."],["Why should you not try to physically break up a fight?","You could get hurt; staff are trained."],["How can we help someone who is getting upset?","Give space, speak calmly, get staff."],["What makes our home feel safe?","Rules, staff, respect, calm spaces."],["What do you need to feel safe here?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Violence rarely comes out of nowhere. Watch for body and word warning signs, step back, and get staff early.",
tryit:"Notice your own warning signs this week and step away when you feel them."},

{t:"Calming Things Down: De-escalation",
goal:"Today we will learn how to calm tense situations with our words and body language, keeping ourselves and others safe.",
warm:["What helps you calm down when someone is upset with you?","What makes an argument worse?","Who is someone who stays calm under pressure?"],
terms:[
["De-escalation","lowering the tension in a situation.","Speaking softly, giving space, and listening instead of arguing back.","When have you calmed a situation down?"],
["Calm voice","speaking slowly and quietly.","If someone yells and you speak softly, they often lower their voice to hear you.","Why does a soft voice help?"],
["Open body language","a relaxed posture that is not threatening.","Hands open and visible, standing at an angle instead of face-to-face, keeping about an arm's length or more of space.","What body language makes things worse?"],
["Validating","showing you understand how someone feels, even if you disagree.","I can see you're really upset. That would frustrate me too.","Does validating mean you agree?"],
["Offering choices","giving options to help someone feel in control.","Would you like to talk about it now, or take a walk first?","Why do choices help an upset person?"]],
story:"Ms. Brenda was upset because her check was late and she yelled at a caregiver. The caregiver did not yell back. She lowered her voice and said, I can tell you're really worried about your money. Let's sit down and I'll help you call the office, or we can wait for the social worker at 2. Which would you like? Ms. Brenda took a deep breath, sat down, and chose to call right away.",
sq:["Why was Ms. Brenda upset?","What did the caregiver do instead of yelling?","How did validating help?","How did offering choices help?"],
act:{n:"Calm vs. Heated Role-Play",m:"None",s:["Choose two volunteers. One plays an upset person (staged, not too intense): Someone took my seat!","First, the other volunteer responds in a heated way (crossing arms, raising voice). Ask the group how it felt.","Then the same volunteer responds calmly: soft voice, open hands, validating, offering a choice.","Ask the group to compare.","Repeat with new volunteers and new situations: someone used my cup, I didn't get my snack, you're always in the bathroom."]},
d:["What difference did you notice between the two responses?","Which de-escalation tool felt most natural?","When could you use these skills?"],
disc:[["Why is it hard to stay calm when someone yells at you?","Our own fight-or-flight kicks in."],["What should you avoid saying to an upset person?","Calm down! You're overreacting. Threats or insults."],["When should you stop trying to de-escalate and get staff?","When there are threats, weapons, or physical aggression."],["How can de-escalation skills help in your family or friendships?","Fewer fights, better relationships."],["What calms you down when you're upset?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Lower your voice, open your hands, give space, validate feelings, and offer choices. Calm is contagious.",
tryit:"Use a calm voice and a validating sentence the next time someone is upset with you."},

{t:"Personal Space and Boundaries",
goal:"Today we will learn about personal space and boundaries, and how to respect our own and others' limits.",
warm:["How close is too close when someone talks to you?","How do you feel when someone touches your things without asking?","What is something you need to feel comfortable in your space?"],
terms:[
["Personal space","the area around your body that feels like yours.","Most people feel comfortable with about an arm's length between them and someone they don't know well.","How much space do you like?"],
["Boundary","a limit that tells others how you want to be treated.","Please knock before coming into my room. Please don't touch my hair.","What is one boundary you have?"],
["Physical boundaries","limits about your body and touch.","Some people like hugs; others prefer a handshake or wave. Always ask before touching.","Do you like hugs?"],
["Property boundaries","limits about your belongings.","Ask before borrowing, return things on time, and do not go into others' rooms or drawers.","Why do property boundaries matter in a shared home?"],
["Respecting no","accepting someone's no without arguing or pushing.","If someone says no to a hug or to lending something, you say OK and step back.","How do you feel when someone respects your no?"]],
story:"Mr. Eddie liked to give big bear hugs to everyone. One resident, Ms. Faye, always stiffened up. One day she said, Eddie, I don't like hugs. Can we do a high-five instead? Eddie felt a little hurt at first, but he said, OK, high-five it is. Now they high-five every morning and Faye smiles. Eddie learned that respecting her boundary made their friendship stronger.",
sq:["What did Ms. Faye not like?","How did she ask for what she wanted?","How did Eddie respond?","Why did it make their friendship stronger?"],
act:{n:"Comfort Zone Walk",m:"Open space",s:["Have two volunteers stand on opposite sides of the room.","One walks slowly toward the other. The standing person says Stop when they start to feel uncomfortable.","Measure the distance with eyes or steps and talk about how everyone's comfort zone is different.","Repeat with different pairs.","Practice phrases for setting a boundary: Please give me a little space. I'd prefer a handshake. Please ask before using my things."]},
d:["Was everyone's comfort zone the same?","How did it feel to say Stop?","Which boundary phrase will you use?"],
disc:[["Why are boundaries important in a group home?","Shared spaces, privacy, respect, safety."],["What should you do if someone keeps ignoring your boundary?","Repeat it firmly and tell staff."],["How can you tell if you're crossing someone else's boundary?","They step back, look uncomfortable, say no."],["Can boundaries change?","Yes; people can change their minds."],["What boundary do you want others to know about you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Everyone has the right to their own space and limits. Ask before touching or borrowing, respect no, and speak up for your own boundaries.",
tryit:"Practice telling someone one of your boundaries kindly this week."},

{t:"Healthy vs. Abusive Relationships",
goal:"Today we will learn the signs of healthy relationships and the warning signs of abuse, and where to get help.",
warm:["What makes a relationship healthy?","What did you learn about relationships growing up?","What is a quality you look for in a friend or partner?"],
terms:[
["Healthy relationship","a relationship with respect, trust, honesty, and safety.","Partners listen to each other, make decisions together, and support each other's goals and friendships.","What does respect look like in a relationship?"],
["Abuse","a pattern of behavior used to control or harm another person.","Abuse can be physical, emotional, verbal, sexual, or financial.","Can abuse happen without hitting?"],
["Control","when one person tries to make all the decisions for the other.","Checking your phone, telling you who you can see, controlling your money, getting angry when you spend time with friends.","Why is control a red flag?"],
["Emotional abuse","hurting someone with words and manipulation.","Constant put-downs, name-calling, threats, blaming you for everything, making you feel crazy.","How can emotional abuse affect a person?"],
["Getting help","reaching out for safety and support.","Tell staff, call 911 if in danger, or call the National Domestic Violence Hotline at 1-800-799-7233 (or text START to 88788).","Who could you tell if you were being hurt?"]],
story:"Ms. Kayla's boyfriend visited every weekend. At first he was sweet. Over time, he started taking her spending money, telling her she was stupid, and getting angry if she talked to other residents. Kayla thought it was normal. After a group like this, she realized these were warning signs. She talked to the social worker, who helped her make a safety plan. Kayla said, I didn't know I deserved better.",
sq:["How did the relationship change over time?","What warning signs were there?","What helped Kayla see the problem?","What does I didn't know I deserved better mean?"],
act:{n:"Green Flags, Red Flags",m:"Two signs or board columns: Green Flag and Red Flag",s:["Read behaviors and ask the group to call out GREEN FLAG (healthy) or RED FLAG (warning sign).","Listens when you talk. (Green.)","Checks your phone without asking. (Red.)","Supports your goals. (Green.)","Takes your money. (Red.)","Makes you feel afraid. (Red.)","Respects when you say no. (Green.)","Isolates you from friends and family. (Red.)","Apologizes and changes behavior after a mistake. (Green.)","Discuss each."]},
d:["Which red flags are easy to miss?","Which green flags matter most to you?","Who could you talk to if you saw red flags?"],
disc:[["Why do people stay in abusive relationships?","Fear, love, money, low self-esteem, nowhere to go."],["Why is it never the victim's fault?","Abuse is a choice made by the abuser."],["How can you help a friend in an abusive relationship?","Listen, believe, do not judge, help them find resources, tell staff."],["Can abuse happen in friendships and families too?","Yes."],["What do you deserve in a relationship?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Healthy relationships are built on respect, trust, and safety. Control and fear are red flags. You deserve to be safe, and help is available.",
tryit:"Think of one green flag you want in your relationships and one red flag you won't accept."},

{t:"Bullying and Teasing",
goal:"Today we will talk about the difference between friendly joking and bullying, how bullying hurts, and what to do about it.",
warm:["What is the difference between playing around and being mean?","Were you ever teased as a kid? How did it feel?","What makes someone a good friend?"],
terms:[
["Bullying","repeated, intentional behavior meant to hurt, scare, or control someone who has less power.","Calling someone names every day, spreading rumors, taking their things, or leaving them out on purpose.","What are some forms of bullying?"],
["Friendly teasing","joking where both people are laughing and no one is hurt.","Two friends who tease each other about their favorite sports teams, and both enjoy it.","How can you tell if teasing has gone too far?"],
["Power imbalance","when one person has more power, like being bigger, more popular, or having something the other needs.","A resident who controls the TV and threatens others who want to change the channel.","Why does power matter in bullying?"],
["Bystander","someone who sees bullying happen.","Bystanders can make bullying worse by laughing, or better by speaking up and getting help.","What can a bystander do?"],
["Reporting","telling staff about bullying.","Telling staff is not snitching; it is keeping people safe.","Why do people avoid reporting bullying?"]],
story:"A newer resident, Mr. Samuel, had a stutter. Another resident, Rick, started imitating him at meals, and some people laughed. Samuel stopped coming to dinner. Ms. Annie noticed and said to Rick, That's not funny. Leave him alone. She also told a caregiver. Staff talked to Rick, and Annie invited Samuel to sit with her. Samuel started coming to dinner again.",
sq:["How was Samuel being bullied?","How did it affect him?","What did Ms. Annie do?","Why did her actions make a difference?"],
act:{n:"Joking or Bullying?",m:"None",s:["Read each situation and ask: Is it friendly joking or bullying? Why?","Two friends laugh about each other's bad singing. (Joking, if both enjoy it.)","Someone calls another resident a name every day and others laugh. (Bullying.)","A person keeps taking someone's dessert. (Bullying.)","A resident jokes about themselves. (Joking.)","Someone spreads a rumor about a roommate. (Bullying.)","Practice what a bystander could say: Hey, that's not cool. Come sit with us."]},
d:["Which situation was hard to decide?","What makes it bullying instead of joking?","What would you say as a bystander?"],
disc:[["How does bullying affect mental health?","Anxiety, depression, isolation, anger."],["Why do people bully others?","Insecurity, feeling powerful, past hurt."],["What should you do if you are being bullied?","Tell staff, do not fight back, stay near supportive people."],["How can we make this a place where bullying isn't accepted?","Speak up, include others, report."],["Have you ever stood up for someone? How did it feel?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Bullying is repeated hurt using power. If you see it, speak up kindly and tell staff. Everyone deserves to feel safe and respected here.",
tryit:"Include someone who seems left out this week."},

{t:"Fire Safety and Evacuation",
goal:"Today we will review how to prevent fires, what to do if there is a fire, and how to evacuate safely.",
warm:["Have you ever been in a fire drill or a real fire?","Where is the nearest exit from this room?","What do you think causes most home fires?"],
terms:[
["Fire prevention","actions that stop fires from starting.","Never leave cooking alone, keep things away from heaters, never smoke in bed, do not overload outlets.","Which fire risk is most common here?"],
["Smoke alarm","a device that beeps loudly when it senses smoke.","When the alarm sounds, act right away. Never remove batteries or cover alarms.","What should you do when you hear the alarm?"],
["Evacuation route","the planned way out of a building.","Know two ways out of every room. Follow staff and the exit signs.","What are the two exits from your room?"],
["Meeting place","a set spot outside where everyone gathers so staff can count people.","At Rose of Sharon, staff will tell you the meeting spot. Never go back inside for belongings.","Why is a meeting place important?"],
["Stop, drop, and roll","what to do if your clothes catch fire.","Stop where you are, drop to the ground, cover your face, and roll until the fire is out.","Can you show what stop, drop, and roll looks like?"]],
story:"During a fire drill, Mr. Willie stayed in his room because he thought it was just a drill. Staff had to go back to get him, which took precious time. Afterward, staff explained that in a real fire, smoke can fill a hallway in under a minute. At the next drill, Willie was the first one out and helped a resident with a walker. He said, I'll take every drill serious now.",
sq:["Why did Mr. Willie stay in his room?","Why is that dangerous?","How did he change?","How can we take drills seriously?"],
act:{n:"Exit Walk-Through",m:"A simple floor plan drawn on the board, or walk the hallway if staff approve",s:["Draw a simple floor plan of the building or this wing.","Ask residents to point out their room, the nearest exits, and a second way out.","Review the steps: hear alarm, leave right away, feel doors for heat with the back of your hand, stay low under smoke, follow staff, go to the meeting place, do not go back in.","Practice stop, drop, and roll with a volunteer (if safe and able).","Ask staff to confirm the outdoor meeting place."]},
d:["Do you know both exits from your room?","Where is our meeting place?","What would you do if smoke filled the hallway?"],
disc:[["Why should you never go back inside a burning building?","Smoke and fire spread fast; people die going back."],["Why stay low in smoke?","Clean air is near the floor."],["Why is smoking in bed so dangerous?","Falling asleep can start a fire."],["What should you do if you see a fire hazard?","Tell staff right away."],["How can we help residents who move slowly during an evacuation?","Follow staff instructions; staff will assist."]],
take:"Prevent fires, know two ways out, leave immediately when the alarm sounds, stay low, go to the meeting place, and never go back inside.",
tryit:"Find both exits from your room and from the dining room this week."},

{t:"Tornado and Severe Weather Safety",
goal:"Today we will learn what to do during tornadoes and severe storms, which are common in Tennessee.",
warm:["Have you ever been through a tornado or a bad storm?","What did you do?","How do you find out about severe weather?"],
terms:[
["Tornado watch","conditions are right for a tornado to form. Be ready.","The TV says there's a tornado watch for Hamilton County until 8 p.m. Stay alert and know where to go.","What should you do during a watch?"],
["Tornado warning","a tornado has been seen or shown on radar. Take shelter now.","Sirens sound, phones buzz with an alert, and staff call everyone to the safe area.","What is the difference between a watch and a warning?"],
["Safe area","the lowest floor, in an inside room or hallway away from windows.","An inside hallway or bathroom on the lowest floor. Get down low and cover your head.","Where is the safe area in our building?"],
["Duck and cover","protecting your head and neck.","Crouch low, put your arms over your head and neck, or cover with a blanket or pillow.","Why protect your head?"],
["Power outage","when electricity goes out.","Use flashlights, not candles. Keep phones charged when storms are coming, and stay away from downed power lines.","Why are candles dangerous during outages?"]],
story:"In April 2011, many tornadoes hit the Chattanooga area. One woman said she heard the sirens and almost ignored them because there had been so many false alarms. Her neighbor knocked on her door and said, Come on, get in the hallway! Minutes later, the tornado took off part of her roof. She was safe because she listened. She now takes every warning seriously.",
sq:["Why did the woman almost ignore the sirens?","What saved her?","What can we learn from her story?","Do you remember any big storms in Tennessee?"],
act:{n:"Tornado Drill Practice",m:"Space to crouch, or chairs",s:["Review: When there is a warning, staff will call everyone to the safe area.","Ask: Where in this building is the safe area? (Staff confirm.)","Practice the duck-and-cover position for those who are able: crouch low, arms over head. Those who cannot crouch can lean forward in a chair and cover their head.","Talk about what to bring if there is time: shoes, glasses, medicine (staff), a blanket.","Quiz: Watch or warning? Read situations and let the group answer."]},
d:["Do you know where the safe area is?","How did the duck-and-cover position feel?","What would you bring if you had time?"],
disc:[["Why do people ignore weather warnings?","False alarms, not believing it will hit them."],["How can you find out about severe weather?","TV, radio, phone alerts, staff."],["What should you do after a storm?","Stay inside until staff say it is safe, avoid downed lines, report damage."],["How can we help each other during a storm?","Stay calm, follow staff, help others move to the safe area."],["What makes storms scary for you?","Let residents share feelings."]],
take:"A watch means be ready; a warning means take shelter now. Go to the lowest inside area, away from windows, and cover your head.",
tryit:"Ask staff to show you the safe area for severe weather in our building."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Safety & Violence Prevention", [
{t:"Staying Safe in the Community",
goal:"Today we will learn simple habits that help us stay safe when we are out walking, shopping, or riding the bus.",
warm:["Where do you go when you leave the facility?","Have you ever felt unsafe while out? What happened?","What do you do to feel safe in public?"],
terms:[
["Situational awareness","paying attention to what is happening around you.","Looking up from your phone, noticing who is nearby, and knowing where exits are.","How often are you looking at your phone when walking?"],
["Buddy system","going places with a friend.","Two residents walk to the store together instead of alone, especially in the evening.","Why is a buddy safer?"],
["Trust your gut","listening to that uneasy feeling that something is wrong.","A man keeps following you through the store. You feel uneasy, so you go to the cashier and ask for help.","Have you ever had a gut feeling that turned out right?"],
["Well-lit routes","choosing paths with lights and other people around.","Taking the main street instead of a dark shortcut through an empty lot.","What routes near here feel safest?"],
["Check-in","telling someone where you are going and when you will be back.","Signing out with staff: Going to Dollar General, back by 3:30.","Why is checking in important?"]],
story:"Mr. Darius was walking back from the store at dusk, looking at his phone. Two men approached and asked for his phone. He gave it to them and was not hurt, but he was shaken. Afterward, he started going out in daylight, walking with a buddy, keeping his phone in his pocket, and signing out with staff. He said, I learned my stuff isn't worth my life, but I can make myself a harder target.",
sq:["What made Mr. Darius an easier target?","Why was giving up his phone a smart choice?","What changes did he make?","What did he mean by a harder target?"],
act:{n:"Safe or Risky?",m:"None",s:["Read each situation and let the group answer SAFE or RISKY and why.","Walking alone at night while wearing earbuds. (Risky.)","Going to the store with a friend in daylight. (Safe.)","Counting your cash at the bus stop. (Risky.)","Telling staff where you are going. (Safe.)","Accepting a ride from someone you just met. (Risky.)","Walking confidently on a main road. (Safe.)","Discuss how to make the risky ones safer."]},
d:["Which risky habit is most common?","What safety habit will you use?","What should you do if someone tries to rob you?"],
disc:[["Why is giving up belongings better than fighting back?","Belongings can be replaced; you cannot."],["What should you do if someone follows you?","Go into a store, near people, call staff or 911."],["How can you keep money and cards safe when out?","Front pocket, only carry what you need."],["Who can you call if you feel unsafe?","911, the facility phone number."],["What places in the community feel safe to you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Stay aware, use the buddy system, trust your gut, stick to well-lit routes, and always check in with staff.",
tryit:"Sign out with staff and tell them your return time every time you leave this week."},

{t:"Online and Social Media Safety",
goal:"Today we will learn how to stay safe online and on social media, protect our privacy, and avoid online conflicts.",
warm:["What apps or websites do you use?","Have you ever had a bad experience online?","What do you like about social media?"],
terms:[
["Social media","apps and websites where people share posts, photos, and messages.","Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, and Snapchat.","Which do you use?"],
["Privacy settings","controls that decide who can see your posts and information.","Setting your Facebook to Friends Only so strangers cannot see your posts.","Do you know how to check your privacy settings?"],
["Oversharing","posting too much personal information.","Posting your address, your full birthday, when you get your check, or that you are home alone.","Why is oversharing risky?"],
["Online scams and catfishing","fake people or offers online.","Someone you've never met says they love you after two weeks and asks you to send money. That is a romance scam.","What are signs someone online might be fake?"],
["Think before you post","pausing before posting or commenting.","Asking yourself: Would I be OK if my family, doctor, or a future landlord saw this? Am I posting while angry?","Have you ever regretted something you posted?"]],
story:"Ms. Rhonda met a man on Facebook who said he was a soldier overseas. He sent sweet messages every day. After a month, he said he needed $300 for a plane ticket to visit her. Rhonda almost sent the money, but she showed the messages to a caregiver first. The caregiver helped her search his photo online, and it belonged to someone else. Rhonda blocked him and felt relieved.",
sq:["What made the man seem real?","What did he ask for?","What smart thing did Rhonda do?","What are the warning signs of a romance scam?"],
act:{n:"Post or Don't Post?",m:"None",s:["Read each possible post and ask: POST or DON'T POST?","A picture of your lunch. (Probably fine.)","Just got my check! Going shopping! (Don't post, it tells people you have cash.)","Your full address and phone number. (Don't post.)","A picture of you at a community event. (Usually fine, think about who can see it.)","An angry rant about a staff member or resident by name. (Don't post.)","A happy birthday message to your sister. (Fine.)","Discuss each."]},
d:["Which post would you have posted without thinking?","Why can angry posts cause problems?","What will you check on your privacy settings?"],
disc:[["Why might people be meaner online than in person?","Distance, no faces, feeling anonymous."],["What should you do if someone harasses you online?","Do not respond, block, screenshot, tell staff."],["How can social media affect mood?","Comparing, conflict, staying up late; also connection."],["Why should you not post about other residents?","Privacy, respect, rules."],["What is a positive way you use social media?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Protect your privacy, think before you post, never send money to people you have not met, and block and report anyone who makes you uncomfortable.",
tryit:"Check your privacy settings on one app this week, with staff help if needed."},

{t:"Respect and Consent",
goal:"Today we will talk about respect and consent in all relationships, and why everyone has the right to say yes or no about their own body and belongings.",
warm:["What does respect mean to you?","How do you show someone you respect them?","How do you feel when someone doesn't ask your permission?"],
terms:[
["Consent","giving clear permission for something.","Asking, Can I hug you? and waiting for a clear yes before hugging.","What does a clear yes look like?"],
["Freely given","consent must be given without pressure, threats, or tricks.","A yes that comes after someone begs or threatens is not real consent.","Why does pressure cancel consent?"],
["Can change your mind","consent can be taken back at any time.","Someone can say yes to holding hands and then say, Actually, I'd like to stop. That must be respected.","How should someone respond when you change your mind?"],
["Capacity","being able to understand and make a decision.","Someone who is very drunk, high, asleep, or confused cannot give consent.","Why can't someone who is drunk give consent?"],
["Respecting no","accepting no without anger or pushing.","If someone says no to sitting next to you, you say OK and move without making them feel bad.","How do you feel when someone respects your no?"]],
story:"At a facility dance, Mr. Terrence asked Ms. Joy to dance. She smiled and said, Yes! Halfway through the song, she said she was tired and wanted to sit down. Terrence said, No problem, thanks for the dance. Later, another resident tried to pull Joy back to the floor even after she said no. Staff stepped in. Joy said she felt safe with Terrence because he respected her.",
sq:["How did Terrence ask for consent?","What did he do when Joy changed her mind?","What did the other resident do wrong?","Why did Joy feel safe with Terrence?"],
act:{n:"Asking and Answering",m:"None",s:["Practice everyday consent with simple, non-romantic examples.","Pairs practice asking: Can I sit here? Can I borrow your pen? Can I give you a high-five? Can I change the channel?","Partners practice answering yes, no, or maybe later.","The asker practices responding respectfully: OK, thanks for letting me know.","Discuss how consent applies to touch, belongings, and personal space."]},
d:["How did it feel to be asked?","How did it feel to hear no?","Why is asking important even for small things?"],
disc:[["Why is consent important in all relationships, not just romantic ones?","Respect, safety, trust."],["What should you do if someone touches you without consent?","Say stop, move away, tell staff."],["How can someone ask for consent without it feeling awkward?","Simple, direct questions; it gets easier with practice."],["Why should you tell staff about unwanted touching?","To keep you and others safe."],["What does a respectful relationship look like to you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Always ask, wait for a clear yes, and respect no. Consent can change at any time. Everyone has the right to decide about their own body and belongings.",
tryit:"Ask before borrowing, touching, or changing something this week, even small things."},

{t:"Protecting Yourself from Abuse and Exploitation",
goal:"Today we will learn about abuse and exploitation of vulnerable adults, the warning signs, and how to report it.",
warm:["Who do you trust to help you with important things?","Has anyone ever taken advantage of your kindness?","Who would you tell if someone was hurting you?"],
terms:[
["Vulnerable adult","an adult who may be at higher risk of being harmed because of age, illness, or disability.","Many residents in care homes are considered vulnerable adults and have special legal protections.","Why might some adults be at higher risk?"],
["Exploitation","taking advantage of someone, often for money.","A relative who takes someone's benefit check and gives them nothing, or a friend who pressures someone to buy them things.","What are examples of financial exploitation?"],
["Neglect","failing to give someone the care they need.","Leaving someone without food, medicine, or hygiene help.","Who could be responsible for neglect?"],
["Abuse","hurting someone physically, emotionally, or sexually.","Hitting, yelling and threatening, humiliating, or unwanted sexual touching.","What are the signs someone is being abused?"],
["Reporting","telling someone who can help.","Tell staff, the administrator, or the social worker. In Tennessee, you can also call Adult Protective Services at 1-888-277-8366.","Why might someone be scared to report?"]],
story:"Ms. Hazel's nephew offered to help with her money. Over a few months, she noticed her bank account kept getting low, and he showed up with new shoes and a new phone. When she asked, he got angry and said she owed him for his help. Hazel felt confused and embarrassed. She told the social worker, who helped her change her bank access and report it. Hazel said, I was ashamed, but it wasn't my fault.",
sq:["What warning signs did Hazel notice?","How did her nephew react when she asked?","Who did she tell?","Why was it not her fault?"],
act:{n:"Is This OK?",m:"None",s:["Read each situation and let the group decide: OK or NOT OK, and who to tell.","A family member helps you pay bills and shows you the receipts. (OK.)","A friend keeps borrowing money and never pays it back. (Not OK, tell staff.)","Someone threatens to tell lies about you unless you give them your snacks. (Not OK.)","A caregiver helps you shower and respects your privacy. (OK.)","Someone touches you in a way you don't like. (Not OK, tell staff right away.)","A visitor asks you to sign a paper you don't understand. (Not OK until someone you trust explains it.)"]},
d:["Which situation was hardest to decide?","Who would you tell first?","What would make it easier to speak up?"],
disc:[["Why are people often exploited by someone they know?","Trust, access, family pressure."],["Why might someone not report abuse?","Fear, shame, love for the person, fear of losing support."],["What should you never sign without help?","Legal papers, contracts, bank forms."],["How can we look out for each other?","Notice changes, listen, tell staff."],["What makes you feel protected here?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You have the right to be safe and treated with respect. If anyone hurts you, takes your money, or makes you afraid, tell staff or call Adult Protective Services. It is never your fault.",
tryit:"Memorize the name of one staff person you would tell if someone was hurting you."},

{t:"Keeping Your Belongings Safe",
goal:"Today we will learn how to protect our belongings from loss and theft in a shared home and in the community.",
warm:["What is your most important belonging?","Have you ever had something lost or stolen?","How do you keep your things safe now?"],
terms:[
["Valuables","items that are worth money or are very important to you.","A phone, cash, ID, jewelry, photos, and medicine.","What valuables do you have?"],
["Secure storage","a safe place to keep things.","A locked drawer, a lockbox, or asking staff to hold onto cash or important items.","Where do you keep valuables?"],
["Labeling","putting your name on your things.","Writing your name on your phone charger, cup, and clothes tags.","What could you label?"],
["Temptation","leaving things out where others could take them.","Leaving cash on your bed while you go to the bathroom.","What items are most often taken in group homes?"],
["Reporting missing items","telling staff right away when something is missing.","Saying calmly, My headphones are missing from my room since this morning, instead of accusing someone.","Why should you report instead of accusing?"]],
story:"Mr. Gary left his phone charging in the day room overnight. In the morning, it was gone. He was angry and accused his roommate, which started a fight. Later, a staff member found the phone in the lost and found; another resident had picked it up by mistake. Gary apologized to his roommate. He now charges his phone in his room and labeled it with his name.",
sq:["How did Gary lose his phone?","What mistake did he make when it was missing?","How was it found?","What does he do differently now?"],
act:{n:"Safe Spot Plan",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident list their 5 most important belongings.","Next to each, write where they keep it now.","Then write a safer place if needed (locked drawer, with staff, in pocket).","Discuss labeling and what items need labels.","Practice reporting a missing item calmly, without accusing."]},
d:["What item needs a safer spot?","What will you label?","How would you report something missing?"],
disc:[["Why is it risky to accuse someone without proof?","Fights, broken trust, could be wrong."],["How can we respect each other's belongings?","Don't touch without asking, return lost items to staff."],["What should you do if you find something that isn't yours?","Give it to staff."],["How can you avoid lending items you might not get back?","Set boundaries, only lend what you can lose."],["How does losing something affect your mood?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Keep valuables locked up or with staff, label your things, report missing items calmly, and never accuse without proof.",
tryit:"Label three of your belongings this week."},

{t:"Speaking Up: Reporting Concerns",
goal:"Today we will learn how and when to report concerns about safety, and why speaking up protects everyone.",
warm:["Have you ever seen something that didn't seem right?","What did you do?","What makes it hard to speak up?"],
terms:[
["Concern","something that worries you about safety or wellbeing.","Seeing a resident who hasn't eaten in days, smelling smoke, or seeing someone being bullied.","What kinds of concerns should be reported?"],
["Snitching vs. reporting","snitching is getting someone in trouble for small things; reporting is protecting someone from harm.","Telling staff that someone has a knife or is being hurt is reporting, not snitching.","What is the difference?"],
["Who to tell","the right person for the concern.","Caregivers for everyday concerns, the nurse for health, the administrator or social worker for serious issues, 911 for emergencies.","Who would you tell about a health concern?"],
["Grievance","a formal complaint about your care or treatment.","If you feel you are being treated unfairly, you can file a grievance with the administrator. You will not be punished for it.","Did you know you have the right to file a grievance?"],
["Retaliation","punishing someone for speaking up. It is not allowed.","No one should treat you worse for making a report.","What should you do if someone retaliates?"]],
story:"Ms. Lorraine noticed that her roommate, Ms. Bea, was not eating and was crying at night. Lorraine did not want to be nosy, but she was worried. She told a caregiver quietly. The nurse checked on Bea and found she had a tooth infection that hurt too much to eat. Bea got treatment and was eating again in a few days. She thanked Lorraine for caring enough to say something.",
sq:["What did Ms. Lorraine notice?","Why was she unsure about speaking up?","What happened because she did?","How did Bea feel about it?"],
act:{n:"Who Do I Tell?",m:"Board with columns: Caregiver, Nurse, Administrator/Social Worker, 911",s:["Read situations and have the group decide who to tell.","Someone's door is broken and won't lock. (Caregiver.)","A resident is having chest pain. (911 and caregiver.)","You feel a staff member treated you unfairly. (Administrator.)","A friend says they want to hurt themselves. (Caregiver right away, nurse.)","Your medicine looks different. (Nurse.)","Someone is threatening another resident with a weapon. (Staff and 911.)","Practice a reporting sentence: I'm worried about ___ because ___."]},
d:["Which situation was hardest to decide?","Do you know how to file a grievance here?","How does it feel to know you can't be punished for reporting?"],
disc:[["Why do people stay silent when they see a problem?","Fear of retaliation, being labeled a snitch, not wanting to get involved."],["How does reporting help the whole home?","Keeps everyone safe, fixes problems early."],["What if you report and nothing changes?","Follow up, go to the next level, ask the social worker."],["Can you report anonymously?","Ask staff about options."],["What concern would you feel comfortable reporting now?","Let residents share in general terms."]],
take:"Speaking up about safety is caring, not snitching. Tell the right person, and remember you cannot be punished for reporting.",
tryit:"Learn who the administrator and social worker are, and how to reach them."},

{t:"Who's at the Door? Visitor Safety",
goal:"Today we will learn how to handle visitors and strangers safely, both here and in our own homes someday.",
warm:["Who usually visits you?","Do you open the door when someone knocks?","Have you ever had an unexpected visitor that felt uncomfortable?"],
terms:[
["Visitor policy","the rules about guests at a facility.","Visitors sign in at the front, visit in common areas during certain hours, and follow house rules.","Why do facilities have visitor rules?"],
["Verify identity","checking who someone is before letting them in.","A person says they are from the gas company. Ask for ID and call the company's real number before opening the door.","How can you check if someone is who they say they are?"],
["Unexpected visitor","someone who shows up without notice.","A stranger at the door asking to come in to use the bathroom or the phone.","What should you do?"],
["Door-to-door sales and scams","people selling things or asking for donations at the door.","Someone offers cheap roofing work if you pay cash today. Don't sign or pay at the door.","Why should you avoid paying at the door?"],
["Let staff handle it","in a group home, staff answer the door and greet visitors.","If someone you don't know is at the door, get staff instead of opening it.","Why is it safer for staff to handle the door?"]],
story:"One afternoon, a man knocked on the facility side door and told Mr. Floyd he was there to visit a resident and asked to be let in. Floyd almost opened it, but remembered the visitor rule. He said, Please go to the front door, staff will help you. Staff later found out the man was not allowed to visit due to a safety order. Floyd's choice protected another resident.",
sq:["What did the man ask Mr. Floyd to do?","What rule did Floyd remember?","How did his choice protect someone?","What would you have done?"],
act:{n:"Door Scenarios",m:"None",s:["Read scenarios and have residents practice responses.","Someone you don't know knocks and asks to come in. (Get staff.)","A person in a uniform says they need to check the gas line. (Get staff; staff will verify.)","A friend visits and wants to go to your room. (Follow visitor policy.)","Someone says they're selling candy for a school. (Politely say no thanks or get staff.)","Your family member brings a stranger. (Staff should be informed.)","Practice the phrase: Please go to the front door. Staff will help you."]},
d:["Which scenario would be hardest for you?","What phrase will you use?","Why are visitor rules there?"],
disc:[["How do visitor rules protect everyone?","Keep out people who could harm residents."],["What should you do if a visitor makes you uncomfortable?","End the visit, tell staff."],["How can you safely meet new people from outside?","In public places, with staff knowledge."],["When living on your own, how would you handle a knock at the door?","Look through the peephole, talk through the door, verify, don't open for strangers."],["Who are visitors you look forward to?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Let staff answer the door, follow visitor rules, verify identity, and never let a stranger in. It protects you and everyone here.",
tryit:"Learn our visitor hours and rules this week by asking staff."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Safety & Violence Prevention", [
{t:"Resolving Conflicts Peacefully",
goal:"Today we will learn step-by-step ways to work out disagreements without fighting.",
warm:["What do people argue about most here?","How did people in your family handle disagreements?","Have you ever solved a conflict well? What happened?"],
terms:[
["Conflict","a disagreement between people with different needs or wants.","Two roommates disagree about when the light should be turned off.","Is conflict always bad?"],
["Cool down first","waiting until both people are calm before talking.","Let's talk about this after dinner when we're both calmer.","Why does talking while angry usually fail?"],
["I statement","sharing your feelings without blaming.","I feel frustrated when the light is on late because I can't sleep, instead of You're so selfish.","How could you turn a blaming sentence into an I statement?"],
["Active listening","listening to understand, not just to reply.","Repeating back: So you need the light on because you like to read before bed?","How do you know someone is really listening?"],
["Compromise","each person gives a little to find a solution.","Light off at 10:30, and the reader uses a small book light after that.","When have you compromised?"]],
story:"Ms. Tasha and Ms. Robin shared a room and argued nightly about the TV volume. One night it almost got physical. A caregiver sat with them the next day. Each one used I statements. Tasha said, I feel stressed when it's loud because I can't sleep. Robin said, I feel lonely at night and the TV helps. They agreed Robin would use headphones after 9. The arguments stopped.",
sq:["What was the conflict?","What did each person need?","How did I statements help?","What was the compromise?"],
act:{n:"Conflict Steps Practice",m:"Board with steps written",s:["Write the steps: 1. Cool down. 2. Each person shares with an I statement. 3. The other repeats back what they heard. 4. Brainstorm solutions together. 5. Pick one both can live with.","Choose a pretend conflict: who gets the recliner, borrowed items not returned, loud music, shared bathroom time.","Have two volunteers walk through the steps while the group coaches.","Try another conflict with new volunteers.","Ask the group to name one compromise that surprised them."]},
d:["Which step was hardest?","Did the solution feel fair to both people?","When could you use these steps?"],
disc:[["What makes conflicts worse?","Yelling, name-calling, bringing up the past, involving others."],["When should you ask staff to help with a conflict?","When it keeps happening, gets heated, or feels unsafe."],["Is it OK to agree to disagree?","Yes, sometimes that is the solution."],["How can apologizing help?","Repairs relationships; shows maturity."],["What conflict skill do you want to get better at?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Cool down, use I statements, listen and repeat back, and look for a compromise. Ask staff for help if it gets heated.",
tryit:"Use an I statement in your next disagreement."},

{t:"Making a Personal Safety Plan",
goal:"Today we will create a personal safety plan for times when we feel unsafe, overwhelmed, or in crisis.",
warm:["What does feeling safe mean to you?","Where do you go when you need to feel safe?","Who do you trust most?"],
terms:[
["Safety plan","a written list of steps to take when you are in danger or crisis.","When I feel like hurting myself, I will: 1. Use my coping skills. 2. Go to a common area. 3. Tell staff. 4. Call 988.","Why write it down ahead of time?"],
["Warning signs","thoughts, feelings, or situations that come before a crisis.","Not sleeping, feeling hopeless, isolating, thinking about using.","What are your warning signs?"],
["Safe people","people you can reach out to.","A caregiver, the nurse, a counselor, a family member, a friend.","Who are your safe people?"],
["Safe places","places where you feel calmer and more protected.","The day room with others around, the porch, the chapel, the office.","Where is a safe place for you here?"],
["Crisis lines","phone numbers for immediate help.","988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline (call or text), 911 for emergencies, and the facility phone.","Have you saved these numbers?"]],
story:"Mr. Kenneth sometimes had dark thoughts late at night. His counselor helped him write a safety plan on a card. One night, he felt hopeless. He took out his card. Step 1: listen to gospel music. Step 2: go sit in the day room near the night caregiver. Step 3: tell her how he feels. He did all three. The caregiver called the on-call nurse, and Kenneth got through the night. He said, The card thought for me when I couldn't.",
sq:["What was on Kenneth's card?","Why did it help to have it written?","Who helped him that night?","What does The card thought for me mean?"],
act:{n:"Write Your Safety Plan",m:"Index cards or a safety plan template, pens",s:["Hand out cards with these headings: My warning signs. Things I can do on my own. People and places that help. Staff I can tell. Crisis numbers: 988 and 911. Reasons to keep going.","Give 10 minutes to fill them in. Help those who need it.","Remind residents the plan is private and personal.","Encourage them to share a copy with their counselor or a staff member they trust.","Invite volunteers to share one reason to keep going."]},
d:["How did it feel to write your plan?","Where will you keep it?","Who will you share it with?"],
disc:[["Why is it hard to think clearly in a crisis?","Strong emotions take over."],["What should you do if a friend shares that they are in crisis?","Stay with them, get staff, call 988 or 911."],["Why is it important to tell staff even if you feel embarrassed?","Your safety matters more."],["How can we make this home feel safer for everyone?","Kindness, checking on each other, reporting concerns."],["What gives you hope?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A safety plan is a map for hard moments. Write it now, keep it close, and use it. You are never alone; 988 is available any time.",
tryit:"Keep your safety plan card in your pocket or by your bed."},

{t:"Dating Safety",
goal:"Today we will talk about healthy, safe dating, including meeting new people, setting boundaries, and spotting red flags.",
warm:["What qualities would you want in a partner?","How do people meet these days?","What did dating look like when you were younger?"],
terms:[
["Healthy dating","getting to know someone with respect, honesty, and fun, at a comfortable pace.","Going for coffee, talking on the phone, meeting each other's friends over time.","What makes a date feel good?"],
["Meet in public","the first several dates should be in public places.","A restaurant, a park in the daytime, or a church event, not someone's home.","Why is meeting in public safer?"],
["Tell someone","letting a trusted person know where you are going and who with.","Telling staff: I'm meeting Jim at Krystal on Brainerd Road at 2, back by 4.","Why tell someone your plans?"],
["Moving too fast","when someone pushes for commitment, money, or closeness very quickly.","Saying I love you after a week, asking to move in, or asking for money early on.","Why is moving too fast a red flag?"],
["Your own pace","you decide how fast to go in a relationship.","It is OK to say, I like you, but I want to take things slow.","How would you say you want to go slow?"]],
story:"Ms. Jackie met a man at church who seemed kind. For their first date, they had lunch at a busy restaurant, and she told staff her plans. After a few dates, he started asking her to borrow money and got angry when she said no. Jackie remembered the red flags from group. She ended things and told staff. Later she met a different man who respected her pace and never asked for money.",
sq:["What safe choices did Jackie make for the first date?","What red flags appeared?","How did she handle it?","What did the second relationship look like?"],
act:{n:"Date Safety Checklist",m:"Board, paper",s:["Ask the group to build a checklist for a safe first date.","Ideas: meet in public, daytime, tell staff, have your own ride, keep your phone charged, don't share your address yet, don't lend money, avoid alcohol and drugs, leave if you feel uncomfortable.","Have residents copy the checklist.","Discuss red flags and green flags in dating.","Practice saying: I'm not comfortable with that, and I'm going to head home now."]},
d:["Which checklist item is most important to you?","What red flag would make you leave?","How would you end a date that felt wrong?"],
disc:[["Why do some people ignore red flags?","Loneliness, hope, flattery."],["What should you do if someone pressures you?","Say no, leave, tell staff."],["How can a partner support your recovery and mental health?","Respect treatment, encourage healthy choices."],["Is it OK to be single?","Yes; a healthy relationship with yourself matters most."],["What makes a relationship last?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Date in public, tell someone your plans, go at your own pace, watch for red flags like pressure or asking for money, and trust your gut.",
tryit:"Write down three qualities you want in a partner and three things you won't accept."},

{t:"Understanding Trauma and Feeling Safe",
goal:"Today we will gently learn what trauma is, how it can affect us, and ways to feel safer in our bodies and surroundings.",
warm:["What helps you feel safe and calm?","What is a place where you have felt safe?","What does safe mean to you?"],
terms:[
["Trauma","a deeply upsetting experience that overwhelms a person's ability to cope.","Abuse, violence, accidents, losing a loved one suddenly, or living in danger can all cause trauma.","How common do you think trauma is?"],
["Trauma response","how the body and mind react to reminders of trauma.","Jumping at loud noises, feeling on edge, nightmares, shutting down, or feeling angry for no clear reason.","Why might the body react even when the danger is over?"],
["Trigger","a reminder of the trauma that brings back strong feelings.","A certain smell, a raised voice, a person who looks like someone from the past.","How can triggers catch us off guard?"],
["Feeling safe","the sense that you are protected in your body and surroundings.","Sitting with your back to the wall, having a calm object, being near trusted people.","What helps you feel safe here?"],
["Healing","trauma can be healed with time, support, and treatment.","Trauma therapy, support groups, coping skills, faith, and safe relationships help many people heal.","What does healing look like?"]],
story:"Mr. Calvin would get very angry and leave the room whenever someone slammed a door. People thought he was just grumpy. His counselor helped him understand that loud bangs reminded him of violence in his childhood home. He learned to notice the trigger, breathe, and remind himself, I'm safe now. Staff also put soft-close pads on the doors. He said, It helps to know I'm not crazy. My body's just trying to protect me.",
sq:["What was Mr. Calvin's trigger?","Why did he react so strongly?","What did he learn to do?","How did staff help?"],
act:{n:"My Safe Place",m:"Paper, crayons or markers, calm music",s:["Ask residents to close their eyes if comfortable, or look at the floor.","Guide them to imagine a safe, peaceful place, real or imagined. What do they see? Hear? Smell? Feel?","After 2 minutes, have them open their eyes and draw or write about their safe place.","Share if they want.","Explain they can visit this place in their mind whenever they need to calm down."]},
d:["What was your safe place like?","How did your body feel while imagining it?","When could you use your safe place?"],
disc:[["Why is it important not to judge people's reactions?","They may be trauma responses we don't understand."],["How can we help create a safe home for everyone?","Calm voices, knocking, respecting space, predictability."],["Why should you not push someone to talk about trauma?","It should be on their terms, with professional support."],["Where can someone get help for trauma?","Counselor, therapist, social worker."],["What helps you feel safe in your body?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Trauma responses are the body trying to protect us. With safety, support, and coping tools, healing is possible.",
tryit:"Visit your safe place in your mind for 2 minutes each day this week."},

{t:"Weapons and Dangerous Items",
goal:"Today we will talk about why weapons and dangerous items are not allowed here and how this keeps everyone safe.",
warm:["What makes you feel safe here?","Why do you think some people carry weapons?","Have you ever felt like you needed protection?"],
terms:[
["Weapon","any object used to hurt or threaten someone.","Knives, guns, box cutters, brass knuckles, and even everyday items if used to threaten.","What items could become weapons?"],
["Contraband","items not allowed in a facility.","Weapons, illegal drugs, alcohol, and sometimes lighters or certain medications not managed by staff.","Why do facilities have contraband rules?"],
["Safety for everyone","rules that protect residents with many different needs.","A resident in crisis could use a knife to hurt themselves or others, even if the owner meant no harm.","How do rules protect people in crisis?"],
["Feeling protected without weapons","finding safety through people, rules, and skills instead of weapons.","Staff on duty, locked doors at night, de-escalation skills, and reporting concerns.","What makes you feel protected here?"],
["Reporting weapons","telling staff right away if you see a weapon.","If you see someone with a knife, move away and tell staff. Do not try to take it.","Why should you not try to take a weapon away?"]],
story:"A new resident brought a pocket knife and kept it under his pillow because he had been robbed before. One night, during an argument with his roommate, he pulled it out. Staff calmed the situation and no one was hurt. Afterward, staff explained the rule and why it mattered. They also helped him feel safer by changing his room and checking in each night. He said, I didn't feel safe, but the knife made things more dangerous.",
sq:["Why did the resident have a knife?","What almost happened?","How did staff help him feel safer?","What did he realize?"],
act:{n:"Safety Without Weapons",m:"Board",s:["Ask: What makes people feel they need a weapon? Write answers (past trauma, fear, feeling unprotected).","Then ask: What can help people feel safe here instead? Write answers (staff, locks, buddies, talking, reporting, safety plans).","Review the facility's rule about weapons and contraband.","Practice what to do if you see a weapon: stay calm, move away, tell staff.","Discuss where to keep everyday items like scissors or razors safely."]},
d:["What helps you feel safest here?","Why do you think weapons make situations more dangerous?","What would you do if you saw a weapon?"],
disc:[["How do weapons increase danger in arguments?","Quick, permanent harm."],["What should you do if someone threatens you?","Get away, tell staff, call 911 if needed."],["How can we make sure everyone feels safe?","Respect, rules, staff, communication."],["What if someone feels unsafe because of their past?","Talk to staff and counselor about extra support."],["What does safety mean in a community like ours?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Weapons make situations more dangerous, not less. Safety comes from people, rules, and skills. If you see a weapon, move away and tell staff.",
tryit:"Tell staff about anything in your space that worries you for safety."},

{t:"Being an Upstander",
goal:"Today we will learn how to safely help when we see someone being mistreated, instead of just watching.",
warm:["Have you ever seen someone being treated unfairly?","Did anyone help?","What makes people stay quiet?"],
terms:[
["Bystander","someone who sees something wrong but does nothing.","People laughing or looking away while someone is picked on.","Why do people stay bystanders?"],
["Upstander","someone who safely steps up to help.","Saying Leave her alone or telling staff when you see someone being bullied.","When have you been an upstander?"],
["Distract","changing the subject or interrupting to stop a situation.","Hey, Ms. Carol, can you help me with this puzzle? to pull someone away from a bully.","How could a distraction help?"],
["Delegate","getting someone else to help, like staff.","Going straight to a caregiver and saying, There's a problem in the TV room.","Why is delegating a strong choice?"],
["Check in afterward","talking to the person who was mistreated later.","Are you OK? That wasn't fair. I'm here if you want to talk.","How might checking in help?"]],
story:"In the dining room, a resident kept making fun of Ms. Ella's walker. Others laughed. Ms. Diane felt uncomfortable. She stood up and said to Ella, Come sit with me, I saved you a seat. Then she quietly told a caregiver. Later, she asked Ella, Are you OK? Ella teared up and said, Thank you for seeing me. The teasing stopped after staff talked with the resident.",
sq:["What was happening to Ms. Ella?","What three things did Ms. Diane do?","Why did it matter so much to Ella?","What would you have done?"],
act:{n:"Upstander Practice",m:"None",s:["Teach three upstander moves: Distract, Delegate (get staff), Check in.","Read situations: someone is being laughed at; someone's snack is being taken; someone is being yelled at; someone is left out of a game.","For each, ask volunteers to show what an upstander might do.","Discuss when it is safe to speak up directly and when to get staff.","Praise every volunteer."]},
d:["Which upstander move felt easiest?","When is it better to get staff than to speak up?","How would you check in with someone later?"],
disc:[["Why does one upstander make a difference?","Others often follow; the victim feels supported."],["What risks should you think about before stepping in?","Your own safety; get staff if there is danger."],["How do bystanders unintentionally make things worse?","Laughing, watching, staying silent."],["How can we make upstanding normal here?","Praise it, practice it, model it."],["Who has been an upstander for you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Don't just watch. Distract, get staff, and check in afterward. One upstander can change everything.",
tryit:"Look out for someone this week and be an upstander if you see something unfair."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Communication & Relationships", [
{t:"Active Listening",
goal:"Today we will learn how to really listen to others so they feel heard and our relationships get stronger.",
warm:["Who is the best listener you know?","How can you tell when someone isn't listening to you?","How do you feel when someone really listens?"],
terms:[
["Active listening","giving someone your full attention and showing you understand.","Putting down your phone, looking at the person, nodding, and asking questions about what they said.","What does active listening look like?"],
["Eye contact","looking at the person who is talking.","Looking at someone's face while they talk shows you care. You don't have to stare; natural glances are fine.","Is eye contact easy or hard for you?"],
["Reflecting","repeating back what you heard in your own words.","So you're upset because your sister didn't call on your birthday?","Why does reflecting help?"],
["Open-ended question","a question that can't be answered with just yes or no.","How did that make you feel? or What happened next? instead of Were you mad?","Can you think of an open-ended question?"],
["Don't interrupt","letting the person finish before you speak.","Waiting until they pause instead of jumping in with your own story.","Why do people interrupt?"]],
story:"Ms. Patricia was upset after a call with her son and started telling her friend Ms. Lou about it. Lou kept looking at the TV and saying, Mm-hmm, and then started talking about her own son. Patricia felt ignored. Later, a caregiver sat down, turned toward her, and said, Tell me what happened. She listened, nodded, and said, That sounds really painful. Patricia said she felt so much better just being heard.",
sq:["How did Ms. Lou listen?","How did that make Patricia feel?","What did the caregiver do differently?","Why did being heard help so much?"],
act:{n:"Listening Pairs",m:"None",s:["Pair residents up.","Partner A talks for 2 minutes about a topic: My favorite holiday memory or A place I'd love to visit.","Partner B practices active listening: eye contact, nodding, no interrupting.","After 2 minutes, Partner B reflects back: What I heard is ___.","Switch roles. Then try a round where the listener is distracted on purpose, and compare how it felt."]},
d:["How did it feel to be really listened to?","How did it feel when your partner was distracted?","What part of active listening is hardest for you?"],
disc:[["Why is listening so important in relationships?","People feel valued, fewer misunderstandings."],["What gets in the way of listening?","Phones, TV, thinking of what to say next, strong feelings."],["How can you listen when you disagree?","Focus on understanding first, then respond."],["How can staff show they are listening to residents?","Eye contact, sitting down, reflecting."],["Who do you want to listen to better?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Listen with your eyes, ears, and heart. Put distractions down, don't interrupt, reflect what you heard, and ask open questions.",
tryit:"Have one conversation this week where you only listen and reflect, without sharing your own story."},

{t:"Speaking Up Assertively",
goal:"Today we will learn how to express our needs and feelings clearly and respectfully using assertive communication.",
warm:["Is it easy or hard for you to say what you need?","What happens when you keep your feelings inside?","Who do you know who speaks up well?"],
terms:[
["Assertive","expressing your thoughts, feelings, and needs honestly and respectfully.","I'd like to watch my show at 7. Can we take turns with the TV?","How is assertive different from bossy?"],
["I statement","a way to share feelings without blaming.","I feel ___ when ___ because ___. I would like ___.","Can you make an I statement about something small?"],
["Clear request","asking for exactly what you want.","Instead of Nobody ever helps me, say Could you help me carry my laundry on Tuesday?","Why do clear requests work better?"],
["Calm, steady voice","speaking in a normal volume and even tone.","Not too soft that you're ignored, not so loud you sound angry.","How does tone change how a message is heard?"],
["Self-respect","believing your needs matter too.","Speaking up because you are worth being heard.","Why do some people feel their needs don't matter?"]],
story:"Mr. Benny's roommate kept borrowing his soap and shampoo without asking. Benny didn't say anything but got angrier each week. Finally he exploded and yelled. Staff helped him practice an I statement. The next day he said calmly, I feel frustrated when my soap is used without asking, because I run out. I'd like you to ask first. His roommate apologized and started asking.",
sq:["What did Benny do at first?","What happened when he held it in?","What I statement did he use?","How did his roommate respond?"],
act:{n:"I Statement Builder",m:"Board with the formula written",s:["Write: I feel ___ when ___ because ___. I would like ___.","Give situations: someone cuts in line for meds; a friend is always late; a family member doesn't call back; your roommate leaves the light on.","Have residents build I statements for each.","Practice saying them out loud with a calm, steady voice.","Give feedback on tone and body language."]},
d:["Which situation was hardest to write an I statement for?","How did it feel to say it out loud?","Who will you use an I statement with?"],
disc:[["Why is holding feelings in harmful?","Builds resentment, leads to explosions."],["What if the other person doesn't respond well?","Stay calm, repeat, get staff if needed."],["How can being assertive help with doctors and staff?","Getting needs met, better care."],["Why is being assertive a form of respect?","Respects both yourself and the other person."],["What do you need to speak up about?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Your needs matter. Use I statements, make clear requests, and speak with a calm, steady voice.",
tryit:"Use one I statement this week about something small that bothers you."},

{t:"Passive, Aggressive, and Assertive",
goal:"Today we will learn three communication styles and practice choosing the assertive one.",
warm:["When you are upset, do you usually go quiet or get loud?","What communication style did your family use?","Who do you admire for how they handle conflict?"],
terms:[
["Passive","not expressing your needs; going along to avoid conflict.","Someone takes your seat and you say nothing, then feel resentful.","When are you passive?"],
["Aggressive","expressing needs in a way that disrespects or threatens others.","Yelling, Get out of my seat or else!","What are the costs of aggressive communication?"],
["Passive-aggressive","showing anger indirectly.","Saying Fine, whatever, then slamming doors or giving the silent treatment.","Why is passive-aggressive behavior confusing?"],
["Assertive","expressing needs clearly and respectfully.","Excuse me, I was sitting there. Would you mind moving?","How does assertive feel different from the others?"],
["Choosing your style","you can change how you communicate with practice.","Noticing you're about to yell, pausing, and choosing an assertive sentence instead.","Which style do you want to use more?"]],
story:"Three residents each had their dessert taken by someone at dinner. Ms. Ann said nothing and went to bed upset (passive). Mr. Joe yelled and threatened the person (aggressive), and got in trouble. Ms. Kay said, Excuse me, that's my dessert. Please give it back. Kay got her dessert and nobody got in trouble.",
sq:["How did each person respond?","Which style did each use?","What were the results of each?","Which response would you have chosen?"],
act:{n:"Which Style?",m:"None",s:["Read responses and have the group name the style: passive, aggressive, passive-aggressive, or assertive.","It's fine, I don't need anything. (Passive.)","You better stop or you'll regret it! (Aggressive.)","Sure, take my stuff, you always do. (Passive-aggressive.)","I'd like my charger back by tonight, please. (Assertive.)","After each, have a volunteer turn it into an assertive response.","Practice saying the assertive versions out loud."]},
d:["Which style do you use most?","Was it easy to turn responses into assertive ones?","What will you practice?"],
disc:[["Why do people become passive?","Fear of conflict, low self-worth, past punishment."],["Why do people become aggressive?","Feeling disrespected, past hurt, it got results before."],["What are the long-term results of each style?","Passive: resentment. Aggressive: broken relationships. Assertive: respect."],["How can we encourage assertiveness here?","Practice, model it, respond well when people speak up."],["What situation do you want to handle more assertively?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Passive keeps quiet, aggressive attacks, and passive-aggressive hides anger. Assertive is honest and respectful, and it is the style that works best.",
tryit:"Notice which style you use in conversations this week."},

{t:"Body Language",
goal:"Today we will learn how our bodies communicate and how to send clear, friendly signals.",
warm:["How can you tell someone is angry without them saying anything?","What does a friendly person look like?","Have you ever been misunderstood because of your face or posture?"],
terms:[
["Body language","messages we send with our bodies, faces, and gestures.","Crossed arms can look closed off. Leaning in and nodding can look interested.","What does your body language say right now?"],
["Facial expression","what your face shows.","A smile, a frown, raised eyebrows, rolling eyes.","Which facial expressions can cause conflict?"],
["Posture","how you hold your body.","Standing tall with shoulders relaxed looks confident. Slouching can look tired or uninterested.","How does posture affect how you feel?"],
["Tone of voice","how you say something, not just what you say.","Thanks said warmly versus thanks said with sarcasm mean very different things.","How can tone change a message?"],
["Mixed signals","when words and body language don't match.","Saying I'm fine while frowning with arms crossed.","Which do people believe more, words or body language?"]],
story:"Mr. Ray had a resting face that looked angry, and he often crossed his arms because he was cold. New residents avoided him, thinking he was mean. A caregiver gently told him. Ray started wearing a sweater, uncrossing his arms, and saying good morning with a small smile. A new resident sat by him at lunch and they became friends. Ray said, I'm not mad. I just didn't know my face was talking.",
sq:["Why did people think Ray was mean?","What did he change?","What happened after?","What does my face was talking mean?"],
act:{n:"Silent Message",m:"Feeling or message cards",s:["Write messages on cards: I'm bored. I'm interested. I'm nervous. I'm angry. I'm friendly. I'm sad. I'm proud.","Volunteers act out a message using only body language and facial expressions.","The group guesses the message.","Then practice friendly, open body language: uncrossed arms, relaxed shoulders, small smile, eye contact.","Try saying Good morning in a flat voice and then in a warm voice, and compare."]},
d:["Which message was easiest to guess?","What did you notice about tone of voice?","What body language do you want to change?"],
disc:[["Why is body language important in a group home?","Avoid misunderstandings and conflict."],["How can culture affect body language?","Eye contact, personal space, and gestures mean different things to different people."],["How can medicine or mental health affect facial expressions?","Some conditions or medicines make faces less expressive; don't judge."],["How can body language help in a job interview?","Confidence, interest, friendliness."],["What does welcoming body language look like?","Let residents demonstrate."]],
take:"Your body speaks before your words do. Relax your arms, lift your posture, soften your face, and match your tone to your words.",
tryit:"Greet three people this week with a smile and open body language."},

{t:"Making New Friends",
goal:"Today we will talk about how friendships start and practice ways to connect with new people.",
warm:["How did you meet your best friend ever?","What makes it hard to make friends as an adult?","What do you look for in a friend?"],
terms:[
["Acquaintance","someone you know a little bit.","A resident you say hi to at breakfast but don't know well.","Who are your acquaintances here?"],
["Common ground","interests or experiences you share with someone.","Both liking the same football team, both being grandparents, or both enjoying gospel music.","How do you find common ground?"],
["Conversation starter","an easy question or comment to begin talking.","What's your favorite thing on the menu? Did you see the game last night? I like your hat.","What conversation starter could you use?"],
["Invitation","asking someone to do something with you.","Want to play cards after lunch? or Would you like to walk with me?","Why is inviting someone a big step?"],
["Patience in friendship","friendships take time to grow.","Seeing someone at group every day for weeks before becoming close.","How long does it take you to trust a new friend?"]],
story:"When Ms. Rosa moved in, she didn't know anyone. She noticed another resident, Ms. Dee, always wore a church hat on Sundays. Rosa said, I love your hat. What church did you go to? They talked about church for 20 minutes. The next Sunday, Dee saved Rosa a seat to watch a church service on TV. Now they watch together every week.",
sq:["How did Rosa start the conversation?","What common ground did they find?","How did the friendship grow?","What could you compliment or ask someone about?"],
act:{n:"Conversation Starter Circle",m:"Conversation starter cards (optional)",s:["Go around the circle. Each person asks the person on their left a conversation starter question.","Use questions like: What is your favorite food? What music do you like? What's something you're good at? Where did you grow up?","After each answer, the asker says one thing they have in common, or asks a follow-up question.","Continue around the circle at least twice.","At the end, ask each person to name someone they learned something new about."]},
d:["What did you learn about someone?","What common ground did you find?","Who might you invite to do something?"],
disc:[["What fears get in the way of making friends?","Rejection, being judged, past hurt."],["What makes someone easy to be friends with?","Kindness, listening, humor, reliability."],["How can we welcome new residents?","Say hi, introduce them, include them."],["What activities here help people connect?","Groups, games, meals, outings."],["What kind of friend do you want to be?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Friendships start small: a hello, a question, finding common ground, and an invitation. Be patient and keep showing up.",
tryit:"Start a conversation with someone you don't know well this week."},

{t:"Being a Good Friend",
goal:"Today we will talk about the qualities of a good friend and how to keep friendships healthy.",
warm:["What is the nicest thing a friend has done for you?","What makes you trust a friend?","What do you bring to a friendship?"],
terms:[
["Loyalty","standing by a friend and not talking badly behind their back.","Defending your friend when someone gossips about them.","Why is loyalty important?"],
["Reliability","doing what you say you'll do.","If you promise to walk with a friend at 3, you show up at 3.","How does reliability build trust?"],
["Support","being there in good and hard times.","Visiting a friend who is sick, celebrating their good news, or just sitting with them.","How do you support friends?"],
["Give and take","both friends share, listen, and help.","One day you listen to their problems; another day they listen to yours.","What happens when a friendship is one-sided?"],
["Honesty with kindness","telling the truth gently.","I think you might want to change your shirt before your appointment; it has a stain.","Why is kind honesty important?"]],
story:"Mr. Hank and Mr. Leroy have been friends at the facility for three years. When Leroy was in the hospital, Hank called every day and saved his dessert for him. When Hank's birthday came, Leroy made him a card and convinced staff to get a cake. They don't agree on everything, especially football, but they always have each other's backs.",
sq:["How did Hank support Leroy?","How did Leroy show he cared?","What does having each other's backs mean?","What makes their friendship last?"],
act:{n:"Friendship Recipe",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Ask each resident to write a recipe for a good friendship.","Example: 2 cups of honesty, 1 cup of listening, a pinch of humor, 3 spoons of loyalty.","Have them include what NOT to add (gossip, jealousy, lies).","Share recipes out loud.","Create a group recipe on the board with the most popular ingredients."]},
d:["What ingredients did people use most?","What would you leave out of a friendship?","Which ingredient do you bring?"],
disc:[["How do you handle a disagreement with a friend?","Talk calmly, listen, forgive."],["What should you do if a friend is making unhealthy choices?","Express concern, encourage help, tell staff if unsafe."],["When is it time to step away from a friendship?","When it is harmful, abusive, or always one-sided."],["How can you be a friend to someone who is struggling?","Check in, listen, include them."],["Who has been a true friend to you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Good friends are loyal, reliable, supportive, and honest with kindness. Be the friend you want to have.",
tryit:"Do one kind thing for a friend this week."},

{t:"Apologizing Well",
goal:"Today we will learn how to give a sincere apology that helps repair relationships.",
warm:["Is it hard for you to say I'm sorry?","What makes an apology feel real?","Have you ever received an apology that didn't feel sincere?"],
terms:[
["Apology","saying you're sorry for something you did that hurt someone.","I'm sorry I yelled at you this morning.","Why are apologies important?"],
["Take responsibility","admitting what you did without excuses.","Instead of I'm sorry, but you made me mad, say I'm sorry I yelled. That wasn't OK.","Why do excuses weaken an apology?"],
["Name the hurt","showing you understand how your action affected them.","I know that embarrassed you in front of everyone.","How does naming the hurt help?"],
["Make it right","offering to fix it or change.","I'll replace your snack, and I'll take a break next time I'm upset.","What are ways to make things right?"],
["Fake apology","an apology that blames the other person.","I'm sorry you feel that way, or I'm sorry if you were offended.","How does a fake apology feel?"]],
story:"Ms. Carla borrowed Ms. Jean's sweater and spilled coffee on it. At first she said, Sorry, but it was already old. Jean was hurt. Later, Carla thought about it and came back. She said, I'm sorry I stained your sweater and made an excuse. That wasn't fair. I'll wash it, and if it doesn't come out, I'll replace it. Jean hugged her, and their friendship was stronger.",
sq:["What was wrong with Carla's first apology?","What made the second apology better?","How did Jean respond?","Why did it make their friendship stronger?"],
act:{n:"Apology Makeover",m:"Board",s:["Write the four parts of a good apology: 1. I'm sorry for ___ (specific). 2. I know it made you feel ___. 3. I will ___ to make it right. 4. Next time I will ___.","Read weak apologies and have the group fix them: Sorry, but you started it. I'm sorry you're upset. My bad.","Have volunteers role-play giving a full apology for a pretend situation (bumping someone's tray, forgetting to return something).","Practice receiving an apology: Thank you for apologizing.","Discuss when forgiveness takes time."]},
d:["Which part of an apology is hardest?","How did it feel to hear a full apology?","Who might you owe an apology?"],
disc:[["Why is it hard to apologize?","Pride, embarrassment, fear of looking weak."],["Does apologizing mean you were completely wrong?","No; you can apologize for your part."],["What if someone won't accept your apology?","Give them time; your job is to be sincere."],["How can actions show an apology is real?","Changed behavior over time."],["How do you feel after a sincere apology?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A real apology names what you did, acknowledges the hurt, offers to make it right, and is followed by changed behavior. No buts.",
tryit:"If you owe someone an apology, give a full, sincere one this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Communication & Relationships", [
{t:"Building and Rebuilding Trust",
goal:"Today we will learn how trust is built, how it gets broken, and how to rebuild it.",
warm:["Who do you trust most and why?","What makes you lose trust in someone?","Is it easy or hard for you to trust people?"],
terms:[
["Trust","believing someone is honest, reliable, and will not hurt you.","Trusting a friend to keep your secret or a staff member to follow through on a promise.","How do you know you can trust someone?"],
["Small actions","trust is built by many small acts over time.","Showing up on time, returning what you borrow, keeping private things private.","What small actions build trust?"],
["Broken trust","when someone lies, betrays, or lets you down.","A friend tells your private story to others.","How does broken trust feel?"],
["Consistency","doing the right thing again and again.","Keeping your word every time, not just once.","Why does consistency matter more than promises?"],
["Earning trust back","showing changed behavior over time after a mistake.","After lying, being honest even when it is hard, for weeks and months.","How long does it take to earn trust back?"]],
story:"Ms. Monique took money from her roommate's drawer when she was struggling. When she was caught, her roommate didn't want to speak to her. Monique apologized, paid the money back over three weeks, and asked staff to hold her own cash so there would be no question. Over months, she kept her word in small ways. Eventually her roommate said, I trust you again. Monique said it was the hardest thing she ever earned.",
sq:["How did Monique break trust?","What did she do to start repairing it?","Why did it take months?","What does hardest thing she ever earned mean?"],
act:{n:"Trust Bank Account",m:"Board",s:["Explain: Trust is like a bank account. Good actions make deposits. Hurtful actions make withdrawals.","Have the group list deposits: keeping promises, being honest, being on time, keeping secrets, apologizing.","List withdrawals: lying, gossiping, stealing, breaking promises, yelling.","Discuss: One big withdrawal can wipe out many deposits.","Ask each resident to name one deposit they will make this week."]},
d:["What surprised you about the trust bank?","Which deposit will you make?","Whose trust do you want to build?"],
disc:[["Why does broken trust take so long to fix?","Hurt, fear it will happen again."],["How can someone who has been hurt learn to trust again?","Slowly, with safe people, seeing consistent behavior."],["Is it OK not to trust someone who hurt you?","Yes; trust must be earned."],["How do you build trust with staff, and how do staff build it with you?","Honesty, follow-through, respect."],["What helps you trust someone?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Trust is built with small, consistent actions and lost quickly. If you break it, repair it with honesty and patience.",
tryit:"Make one trust deposit with someone this week."},

{t:"Family Relationships",
goal:"Today we will talk about family relationships, the joys and struggles, and ways to stay connected in healthy ways.",
warm:["Who is someone in your family you feel close to?","What is a favorite family tradition?","What makes family relationships complicated?"],
terms:[
["Family","the people we are related to or who we choose as family.","Parents, children, siblings, grandparents, aunts, cousins, or close friends who feel like family.","Who do you consider family?"],
["Family roles","the parts people play in a family.","The peacemaker, the caregiver, the black sheep, the funny one.","What role did you play in your family?"],
["Healthy contact","staying in touch in ways that feel good and safe.","A weekly phone call, cards on birthdays, visits in the common room.","What kind of contact works for you?"],
["Boundaries with family","limits that protect your wellbeing.","I'm happy to talk, but I won't discuss money. or I need to end the call if there's yelling.","What boundary might you need with family?"],
["Chosen family","people who are not related by blood but support you like family.","A best friend, a church family, residents and staff who care about you.","Do you have chosen family?"]],
story:"Mr. Marcus hadn't talked to his brother in five years after a fight. During the holidays, he felt the loss. With his counselor, he wrote a short letter saying he missed him and wanted to talk. His brother called a few weeks later. They agreed not to talk about the old fight yet, just catch up. Now they talk once a month. Marcus said, We're not perfect, but I've got my brother back.",
sq:["Why hadn't Marcus talked to his brother?","How did he reach out?","What boundary did they agree on?","What does We're not perfect, but I've got my brother back mean?"],
act:{n:"Family Tree of Support",m:"Paper, pens, markers",s:["Have residents draw a tree. Roots are where they come from, branches are people in their life now.","On the branches, write names of family and chosen family.","Next to each name, draw a heart for supportive, a circle for complicated, or a star for someone they want to reconnect with.","Invite volunteers to share one person on their tree.","Discuss one small step to connect with a star person."]},
d:["How many people are on your tree?","Who is a star person for you?","What small step could you take?"],
disc:[["Why can family be the most difficult relationships?","History, expectations, old hurts."],["How can you reconnect safely with family?","Small steps, letters, with counselor support, clear boundaries."],["What if family is not safe or healthy?","Chosen family, distance, protecting yourself."],["How can holidays be easier when family relationships are hard?","Plan ahead, connect with chosen family, coping skills."],["What do you love about your family?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Family can bring joy and pain. Stay connected in healthy ways, set boundaries when needed, and lean on chosen family too.",
tryit:"Reach out to one family member or chosen family member this week with a call, card, or message."},

{t:"Talking with Staff and Your Care Team",
goal:"Today we will learn how to communicate well with staff, nurses, and our care team so our needs are met.",
warm:["What is something you need help with regularly?","How do you usually ask staff for help?","What makes it easier to talk to someone in charge?"],
terms:[
["Care team","the people who help with your care.","Caregivers, the nurse, the social worker, the administrator, doctors, counselors, and case managers.","Who is on your care team?"],
["Right person, right question","knowing who to ask about what.","Medicine questions go to the nurse. Benefit questions go to the social worker. Daily needs go to caregivers.","Who would you ask about a missing check?"],
["Timing","choosing a good time to ask.","Waiting until after medicine pass instead of asking a caregiver during a busy rush.","When is a good time to ask staff for something?"],
["Clear, polite request","saying exactly what you need, respectfully.","Excuse me, when you have a minute, could you help me call my pharmacy?","How does being polite help?"],
["Follow up","checking back if something hasn't been done.","Hi, I asked yesterday about my glasses appointment. Do you know anything yet?","Why is following up OK?"]],
story:"Ms. Linda was frustrated because she had asked three different caregivers about her eye appointment and nobody followed through. She started yelling at the next caregiver. Later, the social worker explained that appointment scheduling was her job. Linda went to her calmly, explained what she needed, and wrote it down. The social worker scheduled it that day.",
sq:["Why was Ms. Linda frustrated?","What was the real problem?","What did she do differently?","What can we learn from this?"],
act:{n:"Who Do I Ask?",m:"Board with team roles: Caregiver, Nurse, Social Worker, Administrator",s:["Read requests and let the group decide who to ask.","I need more toilet paper. (Caregiver.)","My medicine makes me dizzy. (Nurse.)","My benefits letter is confusing. (Social worker.)","I want to file a complaint. (Administrator.)","I need a ride to an appointment. (Social worker or staff.)","Practice turning a frustrated statement into a clear, polite request: Nobody ever helps me! becomes Could you help me with ___ when you have a minute?"]},
d:["Did you learn who to ask about anything new?","How does a polite request change the response?","Who on your team do you talk to most?"],
disc:[["What makes communication with staff hard?","Busy schedules, shift changes, feeling ignored."],["How can you make sure your request isn't forgotten?","Write it down, ask the right person, follow up."],["What should you do if you feel staff aren't listening?","Calmly go to a supervisor or administrator."],["How can residents and staff build better relationships?","Respect, patience, appreciation both ways."],["What do you appreciate about staff here?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Ask the right person at the right time with a clear, polite request, and follow up if needed. Good communication gets needs met.",
tryit:"Make one clear, polite request to the right staff member this week."},

{t:"Giving and Receiving Compliments",
goal:"Today we will practice giving sincere compliments and accepting them gracefully.",
warm:["What is the best compliment you have ever received?","How do you usually respond to compliments?","Who could use a compliment today?"],
terms:[
["Compliment","saying something kind and positive about someone.","You have a great laugh. or You did a nice job cleaning the table.","Why do compliments matter?"],
["Sincere","honest and real.","Giving a compliment you truly mean, not just to get something.","How can you tell if a compliment is sincere?"],
["Specific compliment","naming exactly what you appreciate.","Instead of You're nice, say I appreciated how you helped Ms. Ann with her tray.","Why are specific compliments more meaningful?"],
["Character compliment","praising who someone is, not just how they look.","You're so patient with everyone, or You always make people feel welcome.","Why might character compliments mean more?"],
["Accepting gracefully","saying thank you instead of brushing it off.","Instead of Oh, this old thing, say Thank you, that's kind of you.","Why is it hard for some people to accept compliments?"]],
story:"Mr. Willie rarely smiled. One day, a resident told him, Willie, you always make sure the new folks have a seat at dinner. That's real kindness. Willie looked surprised and said, Nobody's ever said that to me. He smiled the rest of the day. The next week, he started complimenting others too.",
sq:["What compliment did Willie receive?","How did he react?","What changed in Willie afterward?","Why do compliments spread?"],
act:{n:"Compliment Circle",m:"None",s:["Sit in a circle.","Each person gives a sincere, specific compliment to the person on their right.","The person receiving says only, Thank you.","Go around again, this time with a character compliment.","Ask how it felt to give and to receive."]},
d:["Which was harder: giving or receiving?","What compliment will you remember?","Who will you compliment this week?"],
disc:[["How do compliments affect mood?","Lift spirits, build confidence."],["Why might people not give compliments?","Shy, not used to it, fear of seeming fake."],["What kinds of compliments should we avoid?","Comments on bodies that could make people uncomfortable."],["How can compliments help in a shared home?","Build kindness, reduce conflict."],["What compliment would you like to give a staff member?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Give sincere, specific compliments freely, and when you receive one, just say thank you.",
tryit:"Give one specific compliment every day this week."},

{t:"Handling Rejection",
goal:"Today we will talk about how to cope when someone says no or doesn't want the same relationship we do.",
warm:["What is a time someone said no to you?","How did it feel?","What helps you bounce back from hurt feelings?"],
terms:[
["Rejection","when someone says no to you or doesn't accept you.","Asking someone to sit with you and they say no, or not getting a job you wanted.","What kinds of rejection are common?"],
["Not personal","many rejections are about the other person's situation, not your worth.","A friend says no to a walk because they're tired, not because they don't like you.","How can you tell if a rejection is personal?"],
["Self-worth","your value doesn't change because someone says no.","Being turned down for a date doesn't mean you are unlovable.","How can you protect your self-worth?"],
["Respecting no","accepting a no without pressuring or getting angry.","OK, maybe another time.","Why is it important to accept no gracefully?"],
["Moving forward","trying again or trying elsewhere.","If one person doesn't want to play cards, ask someone else.","What helps you move forward?"]],
story:"Ms. Bonnie asked a group of residents if she could join their card game. One said, We already have four. Bonnie went to her room and cried, thinking, Nobody likes me. Later, a caregiver suggested she start her own game. She invited two newer residents. Now there are two card tables every afternoon, and Bonnie leads one.",
sq:["How did Bonnie feel after being turned down?","What did she tell herself?","Was the rejection personal?","How did she move forward?"],
act:{n:"Reframing Rejection",m:"Board",s:["Read rejection situations: a friend doesn't call back; someone says no to sitting with you; you don't get chosen for an outing; a family member cancels a visit.","For each, ask: What might you think at first? Write the hurtful thought.","Then ask: What are other possible reasons? Write kinder explanations.","Then ask: What could you do next? Write a forward step.","Practice responding to a no with OK, maybe another time."]},
d:["Which situation would hurt you most?","Did kinder explanations help?","What forward step will you remember?"],
disc:[["Why does rejection hurt so much?","We want to belong; it touches old wounds."],["How can rejection lead to unhealthy reactions?","Anger, isolation, using substances."],["How can we turn people down kindly?","Be honest and gentle, offer another option if you can."],["What helps you feel worthy after rejection?","Support, self-talk, faith, friends."],["Has a no ever led to something better?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Rejection hurts, but it doesn't define your worth. Often it isn't personal. Accept no gracefully and keep reaching out.",
tryit:"If you hear a no this week, say OK, maybe another time, and try something else."},

{t:"Getting Along with a Roommate",
goal:"Today we will learn how to share a room peacefully by communicating, setting agreements, and respecting each other.",
warm:["Have you ever had a roommate? What was it like?","What makes a good roommate?","What is your biggest pet peeve about sharing space?"],
terms:[
["Roommate agreement","a plan two roommates make about how they will share space.","Lights out at 10:30, TV volume low after 9, knock if the door is closed, keep your side tidy.","What would you put in a roommate agreement?"],
["Shared space","areas both people use.","The room itself, a closet, a dresser, the bathroom.","How do you divide shared space fairly?"],
["Privacy","having time and space to yourself.","Stepping out when your roommate is changing, or giving them quiet time after a hard phone call.","How can roommates give each other privacy?"],
["Noise and light","common causes of roommate conflict.","Phone calls late at night, a bright lamp, loud TV.","How could these be handled?"],
["Check-in talk","a regular short conversation about how things are going.","Every Sunday: Anything bothering you this week? Anything I can do differently?","Would a check-in talk help?"]],
story:"Mr. Dale liked to go to bed at 9, and his roommate, Mr. Chris, liked to stay up and watch TV. They argued every night. Staff helped them make an agreement: TV off at 10, headphones after 9, and a small lamp instead of the overhead light. They also agreed to a Sunday check-in. Dale said, We're not best friends, but we respect each other now.",
sq:["What was the conflict?","What was in their agreement?","Why did a Sunday check-in help?","What does respecting each other look like?"],
act:{n:"Write a Roommate Agreement",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have residents (individually or with their roommate if they are both present) write a roommate agreement.","Include: quiet hours, lights, visitors, shared items, cleaning, privacy, and how to bring up problems.","Share examples with the group.","Discuss what to do if the agreement is broken: talk first, then ask staff.","Encourage residents to share their agreement with their roommate and staff."]},
d:["What was the most important item in your agreement?","What would you do if it was broken?","Do you think your roommate would agree?"],
disc:[["Why can sharing a room be stressful?","Different habits, no privacy, different schedules."],["How can you bring up a problem without starting a fight?","I statements, calm timing, suggest a solution."],["When should you involve staff?","When talking doesn't work or it feels unsafe."],["How can you show respect for your roommate's things?","Ask first, don't touch, keep your side tidy."],["What's one nice thing you could do for your roommate?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Make a roommate agreement, respect privacy, keep noise and light considerate, and check in regularly.",
tryit:"Have a short, calm check-in with your roommate this week."},

{t:"Empathy: Walking in Someone Else's Shoes",
goal:"Today we will learn what empathy is and practice understanding how others feel.",
warm:["Has anyone ever truly understood how you felt? How did it feel?","What is the difference between feeling sorry for someone and understanding them?","When is it hard to understand someone else?"],
terms:[
["Empathy","trying to understand and share how someone else feels.","When a friend's pet dies, imagining how sad and lonely they must feel, and saying, That must be so hard.","Why is empathy important?"],
["Sympathy","feeling sorry for someone from a distance.","Oh, that's too bad. Empathy says, I can only imagine how painful that is. I'm here.","What's the difference between the two?"],
["Perspective-taking","imagining a situation from another person's point of view.","A resident snaps at you. Instead of snapping back, you wonder if they got bad news today.","How could perspective-taking prevent conflict?"],
["Empathy statement","words that show you understand.","That sounds really frustrating. I'd feel upset too. I'm sorry you're going through that.","What empathy statement could you use?"],
["Listening without fixing","just being with someone instead of giving advice right away.","Sometimes people don't want solutions; they want to feel heard.","Do you prefer advice or listening when you're upset?"]],
story:"Ms. Carol was grumpy every morning and snapped at people at breakfast. Most residents avoided her. One day, Mr. Ben sat next to her and said, Mornings seem hard for you. Carol paused, then shared that her joints hurt badly every morning. Ben said, That sounds really painful. I'm sorry. After that, people were gentler with Carol, and she began to smile more.",
sq:["How did others see Ms. Carol?","What did Mr. Ben do differently?","What did he learn?","How did empathy change things?"],
act:{n:"In Their Shoes",m:"Scenario cards",s:["Read short scenarios: a resident whose family didn't visit on their birthday; a new resident who doesn't know anyone; a staff member who has been yelled at all day; a resident who just got bad news from the doctor.","Ask: How might this person feel? What might they need?","Have volunteers practice an empathy statement for each.","Compare empathy statements with fixing statements and discuss the difference.","Ask each person to think of someone who might need empathy this week."]},
d:["Which scenario was easiest to understand?","How did empathy statements feel to say?","Who in your life needs empathy right now?"],
disc:[["Why is empathy hard sometimes?","When we're stressed, angry, or haven't experienced the same thing."],["Can you have empathy for someone you don't like?","Yes; it doesn't mean agreeing."],["How does empathy reduce conflict?","Understanding leads to patience."],["How can we show empathy to staff?","Recognize their hard work and stress."],["When has someone shown you empathy?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Empathy means trying to feel with someone. Listen, imagine their side, and say, That sounds hard. I'm here.",
tryit:"Use an empathy statement with someone this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Communication & Relationships", [
{t:"Gossip and Rumors",
goal:"Today we will talk about how gossip and rumors hurt people and our home, and what to do instead.",
warm:["Has a rumor ever been spread about you?","Why do you think people gossip?","What is the difference between sharing news and gossiping?"],
terms:[
["Gossip","talking about someone's private business when they're not there.","Did you hear what happened with Ms. Jones and her daughter?","Why is gossip tempting?"],
["Rumor","a story passed around that may not be true.","Someone says a resident is getting kicked out, and by dinner everyone believes it, but it isn't true.","How do rumors grow?"],
["Privacy","the right to keep personal things to yourself.","A resident's health, family problems, or money are their private business.","Why is privacy important here?"],
["Shutting down gossip","politely refusing to join in.","I'd rather not talk about her when she's not here. or Let's change the subject.","What could you say to stop gossip?"],
["Go to the source","if you have a concern, talk directly to the person or to staff.","Instead of talking about a roommate's behavior to others, talk to the roommate or to staff.","Why is going to the source better?"]],
story:"A rumor spread that Mr. Henry had stolen from the kitchen. People stopped talking to him and he didn't know why. Finally, a friend told him. Henry was hurt and angry. Staff looked into it and found the missing food had been moved by another staff member. The rumor was false. Henry said, That rumor took my friends away for two weeks. People apologized, but it took a while for him to trust them again.",
sq:["How did the rumor affect Henry?","Was the rumor true?","How long did the damage last?","What could people have done instead of spreading it?"],
act:{n:"Telephone Game",m:"None",s:["Sit in a circle. The facilitator whispers a long sentence to the first person, such as: Ms. Rose bought three red apples and a blue umbrella at the store on Tuesday afternoon.","Each person whispers what they heard to the next person, only once.","The last person says the sentence out loud.","Compare it to the original sentence.","Discuss how stories change as they are passed."]},
d:["How much did the message change?","How is this like a rumor?","How could a changed story hurt someone?"],
disc:[["Why does gossip make a home feel unsafe?","People worry about being talked about; trust breaks."],["How do you feel when you find out you were gossiped about?","Hurt, angry, embarrassed."],["What if someone tells you gossip?","Don't pass it on; change the subject."],["When is it OK to share something about someone?","When there's a safety concern; tell staff."],["How can we build a gossip-free home?","Let residents share ideas."]],
take:"Gossip and rumors hurt people and break trust. Don't spread them, shut them down kindly, and go to the source or staff with concerns.",
tryit:"If someone starts gossiping this week, change the subject."},

{t:"Keeping in Touch with Loved Ones",
goal:"Today we will learn different ways to stay connected with family and friends who live elsewhere.",
warm:["Who do you miss most?","How do you usually stay in touch?","When was the last time you wrote a letter?"],
terms:[
["Staying connected","keeping relationships alive even when apart.","Calling your sister every Sunday, sending a birthday card, or video chatting with grandkids.","Why is staying connected important?"],
["Phone call","talking by phone.","Scheduling a regular time for calls helps, like every Tuesday at 6.","Do you have a regular call with anyone?"],
["Letter or card","a written message sent in the mail.","A handwritten card means a lot because it takes time and effort. People often keep them for years.","Who might love to get a card from you?"],
["Video call","seeing someone on screen while you talk.","Using FaceTime, Zoom, or Facebook Messenger on a phone or tablet.","Would you like to learn to video call?"],
["Important dates","birthdays, anniversaries, and holidays.","Writing them on a calendar so you remember to reach out.","Whose birthdays do you want to remember?"]],
story:"Ms. Hattie's grandchildren lived in Atlanta. She felt forgotten. A volunteer helped her write short letters with stickers to each grandchild. A few weeks later, she got letters back with drawings. Now they write every month, and her grandson says her letters are his favorite mail. Hattie keeps their drawings on her wall.",
sq:["Why did Ms. Hattie feel forgotten?","What did she try?","How did her grandchildren respond?","Who could you write to?"],
act:{n:"Write a Card",m:"Cards or paper, envelopes, pens, stickers if available",s:["Give each resident a card or folded paper.","Ask them to choose someone to write to: family, friend, former teacher, or pen pal.","Give a simple structure: Hi ___. I've been thinking about you. Something new with me is ___. I hope you are ___. Love, ___.","Help with spelling and addresses as needed.","Offer to mail the cards."]},
d:["Who did you write to?","How did it feel to write?","How will you stay in touch going forward?"],
disc:[["What makes staying in touch hard?","No phone, no address, cost, old conflicts, depression."],["How can staff help residents stay connected?","Phone access, mailing, video calls, address help."],["What if someone doesn't respond?","It's OK; you reached out; try again later."],["How does staying connected help mental health?","Less lonely, more support, purpose."],["Who would you love to hear from?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Calls, cards, and video chats keep love alive across the miles. A little effort goes a long way.",
tryit:"Mail your card or make one call to a loved one this week."},

{t:"Small Talk and Starting Conversations",
goal:"Today we will practice small talk, the easy everyday conversations that help us connect with people.",
warm:["Do you like chatting with new people?","What is an easy thing to talk about with anyone?","What makes conversations awkward?"],
terms:[
["Small talk","light, friendly conversation about everyday topics.","The weather, food, sports, TV shows, or weekend plans.","Why is small talk useful?"],
["Opener","the first thing you say to start a conversation.","Good morning! How are you doing today? or That smells good, what are you eating?","What opener feels natural to you?"],
["Follow-up question","a question that keeps the conversation going.","Person says, I watched the Titans game. You ask, Who do you think will win next week?","What follow-up questions could you use?"],
["Taking turns","both people share and listen.","You share a little, then ask them a question, so one person isn't doing all the talking.","How do you know if you're talking too much?"],
["Polite ending","closing a conversation kindly.","It was nice talking with you. I'm going to head to my room. See you at dinner!","How do you end a conversation politely?"]],
story:"Mr. Jerome felt awkward around people and would sit silently in the day room. A caregiver taught him three easy openers. The next day, he said to a resident, That game looks fun, what is it? The resident explained dominoes and invited him to play. Jerome learned the game and now he's one of the regulars. He said, It just took one sentence.",
sq:["Why did Jerome sit silently?","What opener did he use?","What happened next?","What does It just took one sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"Small Talk Speed Rounds",m:"A timer",s:["Pair residents up.","Give a topic card for each round: food, music, weather, TV, sports, holidays, childhood games.","Each pair has 2 minutes to chat using an opener, follow-up questions, and taking turns.","When the timer rings, practice a polite ending and switch partners.","Do 3 or 4 rounds."]},
d:["Which topic was easiest?","What follow-up question worked well?","Who did you enjoy talking with?"],
disc:[["Why might small talk be hard for some people?","Anxiety, shyness, symptoms, not knowing what to say."],["What topics should you avoid with people you just met?","Very personal topics, money, heavy politics."],["How can small talk lead to friendship?","It builds comfort and finds common ground."],["How can you include someone quiet in a conversation?","Ask them a simple question."],["What topic do you love to talk about?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Small talk opens doors. Use an easy opener, ask follow-up questions, take turns, and end kindly.",
tryit:"Start a small talk conversation with someone new this week."},

{t:"Disagreeing Respectfully",
goal:"Today we will learn how to disagree with someone without hurting the relationship.",
warm:["What is something people often disagree about?","How do you usually act when someone disagrees with you?","Can you be friends with someone who sees things differently?"],
terms:[
["Disagreement","having a different opinion from someone else.","You think the Braves are the best team; your friend thinks the Yankees are.","Is disagreement normal?"],
["Respectful disagreement","sharing your view without insulting the other person.","I see it differently. Here's what I think.","What words show respect when you disagree?"],
["Attack the idea, not the person","talking about the opinion, not calling the person names.","I don't agree with that plan, instead of That's a stupid idea, you're stupid.","Why does this matter?"],
["Agree to disagree","accepting that you won't change each other's minds.","We see this differently, and that's OK.","When is agreeing to disagree the best choice?"],
["Hot topics","subjects that cause strong feelings.","Politics, religion, money, and family matters can heat up fast. Choose time and place carefully.","How should hot topics be handled in a group home?"]],
story:"During the news, Mr. Albert and Mr. Fred started arguing about politics. Voices got loud and others left the room. The next day, they talked again with staff nearby. Each shared his view without interrupting. They realized they both cared about lowering prices for seniors; they just disagreed on how. They shook hands and agreed to disagree. Now they joke about it.",
sq:["Why did the argument get loud?","What changed the second time?","What did they discover they had in common?","How did agreeing to disagree help?"],
act:{n:"Friendly Debate",m:"Board with fun, low-stakes topics",s:["Write fun topics: Is breakfast the best meal? Cats or dogs? Summer or winter? Is pineapple good on pizza? Coffee or tea?","Pick a topic and let residents choose sides.","Each side shares reasons using respectful phrases: I see it differently because..., I understand your point, and I think...","End each round with: We agree to disagree, and a handshake or high-five.","Try 3 or 4 topics."]},
d:["Was it easy to stay respectful?","Did anyone change their mind?","How can you use this in real disagreements?"],
disc:[["Why do disagreements turn into fights?","Feeling disrespected, ego, strong emotions."],["How can you tell when to walk away from a disagreement?","When voices rise or it feels personal."],["What phrases calm a disagreement?","I hear you. That's a fair point. Let's take a break."],["How can different opinions help a group?","New ideas, better decisions."],["What topic do you avoid here and why?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You can disagree and still respect each other. Attack ideas, not people, and it is OK to agree to disagree.",
tryit:"The next time you disagree with someone, say: I see it differently, and here's why."},

{t:"Jealousy and Envy",
goal:"Today we will talk about jealousy and envy, why we feel them, and healthy ways to handle them.",
warm:["Have you ever wished you had what someone else had?","What do people often feel jealous about?","How does jealousy feel in your body?"],
terms:[
["Envy","wanting something someone else has.","Feeling envious when another resident gets visitors every weekend and you don't.","What things do people envy?"],
["Jealousy","fear of losing someone or something to another person.","Feeling jealous when your friend spends time with a new resident.","How is jealousy different from envy?"],
["Comparison","measuring your life against someone else's.","Seeing photos of someone's family vacation online and feeling your life is worse.","How does comparison affect mood?"],
["Insecurity","not feeling good enough or secure in yourself.","Jealousy often grows from fear that you aren't enough.","How can insecurity lead to jealousy?"],
["Turning it around","using envy as information about what you want.","If you envy someone's visitors, maybe you want more connection. You could write to family or join a group.","What could your envy be telling you?"]],
story:"Ms. Linda felt jealous when her best friend at the facility, Ms. Faye, started spending time with a new resident. Linda stopped talking to Faye and said mean things about the new resident. Faye was hurt and confused. Linda talked to her counselor, who helped her see she was scared of losing her friend. Linda told Faye honestly, I got scared you'd forget about me. Faye said, Never. Come sit with us. Now the three are friends.",
sq:["Why was Linda jealous?","How did she act at first?","What was she really feeling underneath?","How did honesty fix things?"],
act:{n:"What Is Envy Telling Me?",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident fold a paper into two columns.","Left column: I feel envious or jealous when ___.","Right column: This might mean I want ___. One step I can take is ___.","Give an example: I feel envious when others go on outings. I want more fun. I can sign up for the next outing.","Invite volunteers to share."]},
d:["Was it hard to admit feeling envious?","What did your envy teach you?","What step will you take?"],
disc:[["Why is jealousy a normal feeling?","Everyone wants love, security, and good things."],["How can jealousy damage relationships?","Control, accusations, pushing people away."],["What helps when you feel jealous?","Talk honestly, build self-worth, gratitude."],["How does social media fuel envy?","People show only their best moments."],["What are you grateful to have right now?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Jealousy and envy are normal feelings that tell us what we want or fear. Be honest, focus on your own path, and take a step toward what you need.",
tryit:"When you feel envious this week, ask yourself: What do I want, and what step can I take?"},

{t:"Asking for What You Need",
goal:"Today we will practice asking clearly for what we need from others, without hinting, demanding, or giving up.",
warm:["Is it easy or hard for you to ask for help?","What do you do when you need something but don't ask?","Who taught you to ask, or not to ask, for help?"],
terms:[
["Need","something important for your health, safety, or well-being.","Needing quiet to sleep, help with laundry, or someone to talk to.","What do you need right now?"],
["Hinting","suggesting what you need without saying it directly.","Saying, Boy, I sure am cold, instead of Could I have another blanket?","Why does hinting often fail?"],
["Demanding","insisting in a way that disrespects others.","Get me a blanket now!","How do people react to demands?"],
["Direct request","clear, polite, specific ask.","Could you please help me get another blanket from the closet?","Why do direct requests work best?"],
["It's OK to ask","asking for help is a strength, not a weakness.","Strong people know when to ask for support.","Why do people believe asking is weak?"]],
story:"Mr. Tony had trouble cutting his meat because of shaky hands, but he was embarrassed to ask. He started skipping meals with meat and lost weight. A caregiver noticed and asked him about it. Tony admitted the problem. She said, You can always ask me. The next day he said, Could you please cut my chicken? It was easy, and he started eating full meals again.",
sq:["What did Tony need?","Why didn't he ask?","What happened because he didn't ask?","How did it change when he asked?"],
act:{n:"Hint to Ask",m:"Board",s:["Write hints on the board: I'm so bored. Nobody ever helps me. My room is freezing. I guess I'll just stay here alone.","For each, ask the group to turn the hint into a direct, polite request.","Example: I'm so bored becomes Would you like to play cards with me?","Practice saying the requests out loud.","Have each resident write one real direct request they want to make this week."]},
d:["Which hint do you use most?","How did the direct request feel to say?","What request will you make this week?"],
disc:[["What happens to relationships when needs go unspoken?","Resentment, misunderstandings."],["What if someone says no to your request?","Accept it, ask someone else, or ask later."],["How can you ask for emotional support?","I'm having a hard day. Could we talk for a few minutes?"],["Why do staff want residents to ask for what they need?","So they can help; they can't read minds."],["What is something you need but haven't asked for?","Let residents share."]],
take:"No one can read your mind. Ask directly, politely, and specifically. Asking for help is a strength.",
tryit:"Make one direct, polite request for something you need this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Community & Social Skills", [
{t:"Mealtime Manners",
goal:"Today we will review mealtime manners that make dining with others pleasant and respectful.",
warm:["What was dinnertime like in your home growing up?","What mealtime habit bothers you most?","What is your favorite meal to share with others?"],
terms:[
["Table manners","polite habits while eating with others.","Chewing with your mouth closed, using a napkin, saying please pass the salt instead of reaching across.","Which table manners did you learn as a child?"],
["Waiting your turn","letting others be served or go through the line in order.","Not cutting in the meal line and waiting until everyone at your table has food before eating, if that is the house custom.","Why does waiting your turn matter?"],
["Conversation at meals","pleasant talk that includes others.","Asking, How was your day? instead of talking about gross or upsetting topics.","What topics are good for mealtime?"],
["Cleaning up","taking care of your space after eating.","Pushing in your chair, clearing your plate, and wiping up spills.","Why should everyone clean up after themselves?"],
["Respecting food choices","not commenting negatively on others' food or diets.","Not saying, That looks disgusting, about someone's plate.","How does it feel when someone criticizes your food?"]],
story:"Mr. Calvin ate quickly, talked with food in his mouth, and reached across others for the hot sauce. Other residents moved to different tables. He noticed he was eating alone and felt hurt. A caregiver quietly gave him a few tips. Calvin started slowing down and asking for things to be passed. Within a couple of weeks, people sat with him again, and he said meals became his favorite part of the day.",
sq:["What habits pushed people away?","How did Calvin feel?","What did he change?","What happened after?"],
act:{n:"Manners Match-Up",m:"Board with two columns: Please Do and Please Don't",s:["Have the group list good mealtime manners and habits to avoid.","Please Do: napkin, chew with mouth closed, say please and thank you, ask for things to be passed, clean up, include others in conversation.","Please Don't: reach across, talk with full mouth, take food from others' plates, complain loudly about food, leave a mess.","Role-play asking someone to pass something politely.","Ask each person to pick one manner to practice at the next meal."]},
d:["Which manner is most important to you?","Which one is hardest to remember?","What manner will you practice?"],
disc:[["Why do manners matter in a shared home?","Respect, comfort, fewer conflicts."],["How can manners help in the community?","Restaurants, family dinners, job lunches."],["What should you do if someone else's manners bother you?","Kindly mention it privately or tell staff."],["How can we make mealtimes more enjoyable?","Good conversation, music, kindness."],["What's a mealtime tradition you love?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Good manners show respect and make meals more enjoyable for everyone: chew quietly, ask for things to be passed, include others, and clean up.",
tryit:"Practice one mealtime manner at every meal this week."},

{t:"Being On Time",
goal:"Today we will learn why being on time matters and practical tools to help us arrive when we should.",
warm:["Are you usually early, on time, or late?","How do you feel when someone keeps you waiting?","What makes you late most often?"],
terms:[
["Punctuality","being on time.","Arriving at group at 1:00 when it starts at 1:00, not 1:15.","Why do people value punctuality?"],
["Buffer time","extra time you plan in case something slows you down.","If the bus takes 20 minutes, leave 30 minutes early.","How much buffer time do you usually give yourself?"],
["Working backward","planning your steps from the time you need to arrive.","Appointment at 10. Ride leaves at 9:30. Need to be dressed by 9:15. Wake up at 8:15.","How could working backward help you?"],
["Reminders","alarms or notes that tell you when to get ready.","Setting a phone alarm 15 minutes before group.","What reminder works best for you?"],
["Communicating delays","letting people know if you'll be late.","Calling the doctor's office: I'm running 10 minutes late. Is that OK?","Why should you call if you're running late?"]],
story:"Ms. Tasha was always late to group and her appointments. Once, she was so late to the dentist they made her reschedule, and she had to wait another month with a toothache. A caregiver helped her work backward and set two alarms. She started arriving early. She said, I didn't know being on time could feel so relaxing.",
sq:["What happened when Tasha was late to the dentist?","What tools helped her?","How did being on time feel?","What makes you late?"],
act:{n:"Work Backward",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Give a pretend goal: Doctor appointment at 10:00 a.m., 20-minute ride, need to shower and eat breakfast first.","Have residents work backward to figure out what time to wake up, shower, eat, and leave.","Compare answers and discuss buffer time.","Try another: PSR group at 1:00, need to use the bathroom and find your glasses.","Ask residents to plan for a real upcoming event."]},
d:["What time did you need to wake up?","Did you include buffer time?","What real event will you plan for?"],
disc:[["How does being late affect others?","Wastes their time, feels disrespectful."],["How can being late affect your health care?","Missed or rescheduled appointments."],["Why is being on time important for jobs?","Employers count on you; can lose a job."],["What can staff do to help residents be on time?","Reminders, posted schedules."],["How do you feel when you arrive early?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Being on time shows respect for yourself and others. Work backward, add buffer time, set reminders, and call if you'll be late.",
tryit:"Arrive at every group and meal on time this week."},

{t:"Volunteering and Giving Back",
goal:"Today we will explore how volunteering helps our community and gives us purpose, and find ways we could give back.",
warm:["Have you ever volunteered or helped a cause?","Who has helped you in a big way?","What skill do you have that could help others?"],
terms:[
["Volunteering","giving your time to help others without being paid.","Sorting food at a food bank, helping at church, reading to children, or folding towels here.","Where could someone volunteer in Chattanooga?"],
["Purpose","a sense that your life matters and you contribute.","Helping others reminds us we have value to give.","How does helping others affect how you feel?"],
["Skills","things you are good at that can help.","Cooking, organizing, encouraging people, gardening, fixing things, singing.","What skills could you share?"],
["Giving back at home","helping right here in our community.","Welcoming new residents, helping set tables, watering plants, leading a group activity.","How could you give back here?"],
["Building a resume","volunteer work can show employers you are reliable.","Six months of volunteering at a thrift store shows responsibility and work skills.","How could volunteering help with future jobs?"]],
story:"Mr. Walter felt useless after retiring and moving to the facility. A staff member asked him to help water the plants on the porch every morning. He took it seriously. Then he started a small vegetable garden with staff permission. That summer he shared tomatoes with everyone. He said, I feel like I'm part of something again.",
sq:["Why did Walter feel useless?","What small job did he start with?","How did it grow?","What does feeling part of something mean to you?"],
act:{n:"Ways I Can Help",m:"Board, paper, pens",s:["Brainstorm volunteer ideas in three areas: Here at home, In the community, From my chair (writing cards, making crafts for kids in hospitals, praying for others).","Write all ideas on the board.","Have each resident choose one idea they could do this month.","Discuss what support they would need from staff.","Consider a group volunteer project, like making cards for a local nursing home."]},
d:["What volunteer idea excites you?","What support would you need?","Would the group like to do a project together?"],
disc:[["How does volunteering help mental health?","Purpose, connection, self-esteem, structure."],["What gets in the way of volunteering?","Transportation, health, confidence."],["Why does helping others make us feel good?","Brain releases feel-good chemicals; we feel useful."],["How have others given to you?","Let residents share gratitude."],["What cause matters most to you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Everyone has something to give. Volunteering builds purpose, skills, and connection, whether here at home or in the community.",
tryit:"Do one helpful act for our home or community this week."},

{t:"Going on Community Outings",
goal:"Today we will prepare for safe, fun community outings and review what is expected while we are out together.",
warm:["What outing would you love to go on?","What was the best outing you have been on?","What makes an outing stressful?"],
terms:[
["Outing","a planned trip away from the facility.","A trip to the park, a movie, a restaurant, the aquarium, or a church event.","What outings have you enjoyed?"],
["Group rules","expectations to keep everyone safe and on schedule.","Stay with the group, check in with staff, be back at the van on time, no substances.","Why do outings have rules?"],
["Preparing","getting ready ahead of time.","Use the bathroom, bring water, wear comfortable shoes, have your ID, take medicine if due.","What should you bring on an outing?"],
["Public behavior","how we act in public places.","Using inside voices, being polite to workers, waiting in line, cleaning up after ourselves.","How do our actions reflect on our home?"],
["Lost plan","what to do if you get separated.","Stay where you are, find a worker or security guard, call the facility number.","What would you do if you got separated?"]],
story:"On a trip to the Tennessee Aquarium, Mr. Jordan wandered off to look at the turtles and lost the group. He panicked at first. Then he remembered the plan: stay put and find a worker. An aquarium employee called the facility phone number on his card, and staff found him in 10 minutes. Jordan said, I was scared, but having the plan helped.",
sq:["What happened to Jordan?","What did he remember?","How did the card help?","What should everyone carry on an outing?"],
act:{n:"Outing Prep Checklist",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have the group build an outing checklist together.","Before: bathroom, medicine, water, comfortable shoes, jacket, ID card with facility number, money if needed.","During: stay with the group, follow staff, be polite, ask before going somewhere.","After: return on time, tell staff if you feel sick or something happened.","Practice what to say if lost: I'm from Rose of Sharon Senior Villa. Can you call this number?"]},
d:["What item is most important on the checklist?","What would you do if lost?","What outing should the group plan next?"],
disc:[["Why are outings good for mental health?","New experiences, fresh air, fun, connection."],["What makes outings hard for some people?","Crowds, anxiety, mobility, fatigue."],["How can we help each other on outings?","Buddy system, patience, help with mobility."],["What free or cheap outings are in Chattanooga?","Parks, Riverwalk, library, Coolidge Park, community events."],["What outing are you dreaming of?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Prepare before you go, stay with the group, be polite in public, and know your lost plan. Outings are better when everyone is safe.",
tryit:"Carry a card with the facility phone number every time you go out."},

{t:"Respecting Differences",
goal:"Today we will talk about the different backgrounds, beliefs, and abilities of people around us, and how to treat everyone with respect.",
warm:["Where did you grow up?","What is a tradition from your family or culture?","Have you ever felt different from people around you?"],
terms:[
["Diversity","differences among people.","People here come from different places, ages, races, religions, abilities, and life experiences.","What differences do you see in our home?"],
["Respect","treating others with dignity, even when they are different.","Listening to someone's story, using the name they prefer, and not making fun of how they talk or move.","How do you show respect?"],
["Stereotype","an unfair belief about a whole group of people.","Assuming all young people are lazy or all older people are grumpy.","Why are stereotypes harmful?"],
["Curiosity","wanting to learn about others.","Asking, What was it like growing up in Chicago? instead of judging.","What would you like to learn about someone here?"],
["Common ground","things we share despite differences.","Most people want to be loved, safe, and respected.","What do all of us have in common?"]],
story:"Mr. Earl grew up in rural Tennessee, and his new roommate, Mr. Luis, grew up in Puerto Rico. At first Earl was unsure about him. Then Luis shared some of his music and taught Earl a few Spanish words. Earl taught Luis how to play checkers the country way. They discovered they both loved baseball and fishing. Earl said, I almost missed out on a good friend because of what I assumed.",
sq:["How were Earl and Luis different?","How did they learn about each other?","What did they have in common?","What did Earl almost miss?"],
act:{n:"Same and Different",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Pair residents with someone they don't know well.","Each pair finds 3 things they have in common and 3 things that are different.","Share with the group.","Discuss: Were the differences interesting? Did the common things surprise you?","Invite residents to share a tradition or food from their background."]},
d:["What did you have in common with your partner?","What difference did you find interesting?","What tradition did someone share?"],
disc:[["Why do people fear what is different?","Unfamiliar, stereotypes, past experiences."],["How can stereotypes hurt someone in a group home?","Feeling unwelcome, isolated, disrespected."],["What should you do if someone makes a hurtful comment about a group?","Speak up kindly or tell staff."],["How do differences make a community stronger?","New ideas, skills, perspectives."],["What would you like others to know about you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Everyone deserves respect. Be curious instead of judging, avoid stereotypes, and look for common ground.",
tryit:"Learn one new thing about someone different from you this week."},

{t:"Everyday Courtesy",
goal:"Today we will practice everyday courtesies that make life smoother and kinder for everyone.",
warm:["What polite habit did you learn growing up?","What rude habit bothers you most?","When did a stranger's kindness make your day?"],
terms:[
["Courtesy","polite, considerate behavior toward others.","Holding the door, saying excuse me, letting someone go ahead of you.","What courtesy do you appreciate most?"],
["Magic words","please, thank you, excuse me, and I'm sorry.","Please pass the bread. Thank you for helping me. Excuse me, may I get by?","Why are these called magic words?"],
["Greeting","saying hello to acknowledge people.","Good morning! or Hi, how are you? when you see someone.","How does it feel when someone greets you?"],
["Holding the door","waiting a moment to keep a door open for someone behind you.","Especially kind for someone with a walker or full hands.","Who could you hold a door for here?"],
["Knocking","knocking and waiting before entering someone's room or an office.","Knock, knock, may I come in?","Why is knocking important?"]],
story:"A resident named Ms. June made it her goal to say good morning to every person she saw each day. At first, some people barely responded. After a few weeks, people started greeting her first and even each other. A staff member said the whole mood of mornings changed. June said, It doesn't cost a thing to be kind.",
sq:["What was June's goal?","How did people respond at first?","How did the mood change?","What does It doesn't cost a thing to be kind mean?"],
act:{n:"Courtesy Challenge",m:"None",s:["Act out short scenes and let the group suggest the courteous action.","Someone is behind you at the door. (Hold it.)","You bump into someone. (Excuse me, I'm sorry.)","You want to get past someone in the hallway. (Excuse me, may I get by?)","A staff member helps you. (Thank you.)","You want to enter the office. (Knock and wait.)","Have volunteers act out the courteous version of each."]},
d:["Which courtesy do you already do well?","Which one will you work on?","How does courtesy change a place?"],
disc:[["Why does courtesy matter even in small moments?","Builds respect and kindness."],["How do you respond when someone is rude?","Stay calm, don't match it, walk away if needed."],["Are courtesies the same everywhere?","Some differ by culture; respect is universal."],["How can courtesy help in jobs and appointments?","Good impressions, better service."],["Who is the most courteous person you know?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Please, thank you, excuse me, a greeting, a held door, and a knock. Small courtesies make a big difference.",
tryit:"Greet every person you see with a hello or good morning for one full day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Community & Social Skills", [
{t:"Being a Good Group Member",
goal:"Today we will talk about what helps a group like this one work well, and how each of us can make it better.",
warm:["What makes a group fun to be part of?","What makes a group frustrating?","What do you enjoy most about PSR groups?"],
terms:[
["Group member","a person who belongs to and takes part in a group.","Every resident who comes to PSR is a group member with something to offer.","What do you bring to this group?"],
["Participation","joining in by listening, sharing, or helping.","Answering a question, doing the activity, or encouraging someone else.","What does participation look like for you?"],
["Group norms","the agreed-upon rules for how a group behaves.","One person talks at a time, what's said here stays here, no put-downs, phones away.","What group norms are most important to you?"],
["Encouraging others","helping others feel comfortable to share.","Good point, Ms. Rose! or I'd love to hear what you think, Mr. Ben.","How can you encourage a quiet member?"],
["Respectful exit","leaving a group politely if you need to.","Quietly telling the facilitator, I need a break, I'll be back.","Why is it better than walking out abruptly?"]],
story:"In one PSR group, Mr. Dwayne used to sit in the back, make jokes during serious moments, and leave early. The facilitator asked him to help lead the warm-up one day. Dwayne surprised everyone by asking great questions. He started coming every day and sitting in the circle. He said, I didn't think I had anything to give until someone asked me.",
sq:["How did Dwayne act in group at first?","What changed for him?","Why did being asked to help matter?","What could you help with in group?"],
act:{n:"Create Our Group Norms",m:"Large paper, markers",s:["Ask the group: What rules help our PSR group work well?","Write each idea on large paper. Make them positive: We listen when others talk.","Vote on the top 6 to 8 norms.","Have each member sign the paper if they agree.","Ask staff to post the norms in the group room."]},
d:["Which norm matters most to you?","How will we remind each other kindly?","How does it feel to make the rules together?"],
disc:[["Why do some people find groups hard?","Anxiety, past bad experiences, symptoms, shyness."],["What can we do when someone disrupts the group?","Kind reminder, facilitator steps in, give them a role."],["How does group help us?","Learning, support, friendship, routine."],["What would make PSR even better?","Let residents share; facilitator notes ideas for staff."],["What have you learned from another group member?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Every member makes the group. Listen, join in, encourage others, and follow the norms we create together.",
tryit:"Encourage a quiet group member to share this week."},

{t:"Teamwork",
goal:"Today we will learn what makes teamwork successful and practice working together to reach a goal.",
warm:["Have you ever been on a team? What kind?","What makes a team successful?","What role do you usually play on a team?"],
terms:[
["Teamwork","people working together toward a shared goal.","A kitchen crew where one person cooks, one sets the table, and one washes dishes.","Where do you see teamwork here?"],
["Roles","the jobs each person does on a team.","Leader, helper, timekeeper, encourager, idea person.","What role fits you best?"],
["Cooperation","working together instead of competing.","Sharing supplies and helping someone who is struggling.","What makes cooperation hard?"],
["Communication","sharing ideas and information clearly.","I'll get the plates; can you grab the cups?","How does communication help teams?"],
["Shared credit","celebrating the team's success together.","We did it! instead of I did it all.","Why does sharing credit matter?"]],
story:"The residents decided to decorate the day room for a holiday party. At first, everyone did their own thing and it was messy and stressful. Then Ms. Pat suggested they assign jobs: some made paper chains, some hung them, some set up snacks, and one person picked music. The room looked beautiful, and everyone was proud. Ms. Pat said, We did it as a family.",
sq:["What went wrong at first?","What did Ms. Pat suggest?","What were the results?","Why did they feel like a family?"],
act:{n:"Team Tower Challenge",m:"Paper cups, or paper and tape, or index cards",s:["Divide residents into teams of 3 or 4.","Give each team the same materials (for example, 20 paper cups or 10 sheets of paper and tape).","Challenge: Build the tallest standing tower in 8 minutes.","Encourage teams to plan first and assign roles.","Measure towers and celebrate every team's effort."]},
d:["How did your team decide what to do?","What roles did people take?","What would you do differently next time?"],
disc:[["What makes teamwork break down?","Bossiness, not listening, someone not helping."],["How do you handle a teammate who isn't helping?","Ask them kindly to take a role, encourage."],["Where else in life do you need teamwork?","Jobs, families, church, roommates."],["How can our home work more like a team?","Chores, helping each other, communication."],["What did you enjoy about working together today?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Teams succeed when everyone has a role, communicates, cooperates, and shares the credit.",
tryit:"Help out on a team task at home this week, like setting tables or decorating."},

{t:"Good Sportsmanship",
goal:"Today we will practice being good winners and good losers when playing games and sports.",
warm:["What is your favorite game or sport?","How do you feel when you lose?","Who is a sore loser or a show-off you remember?"],
terms:[
["Sportsmanship","playing fair and treating others with respect, whether you win or lose.","Shaking hands after a game, saying good game, and following the rules.","What does good sportsmanship look like?"],
["Gracious winner","winning without bragging or teasing.","Good game, you almost had me! instead of Ha, you lose!","How do you feel when someone brags?"],
["Good loser","losing without anger or excuses.","Good game. Want to play again? instead of throwing cards or accusing someone of cheating.","Why is losing hard for some people?"],
["Playing fair","following the rules and being honest.","Not peeking at cards or changing the rules halfway.","Why does fair play matter even in fun games?"],
["Having fun","remembering the goal is enjoyment and connection.","Laughing together even when you lose.","What do you enjoy about games besides winning?"]],
story:"During bingo, Mr. Floyd lost five rounds in a row and started complaining loudly that it was rigged. Others stopped wanting to play with him. Staff gently talked with him. The next week, when he lost, he said, Not my day! Congratulations, Ms. Ruth! Everyone laughed, and Ruth gave him one of her prize candies.",
sq:["How did Floyd react to losing at first?","How did others respond?","What did he do differently the next week?","How did people react?"],
act:{n:"Game Time with Sportsmanship",m:"A simple game: cards, bingo, dominoes, or a quick trivia game",s:["Before playing, review: good winners say good game; good losers say congratulations.","Play a quick game for about 8 minutes.","Pause after each round for winners and losers to practice their phrases.","Give a sportsmanship award (a sticker or applause) to someone who showed great sportsmanship.","Discuss how the game felt."]},
d:["How did it feel to practice good sportsmanship?","Who showed great sportsmanship?","What will you say next time you lose?"],
disc:[["Why do some people get very upset over games?","Competitive, feel disrespected, past experiences."],["How can losing teach us life skills?","Handling disappointment, trying again."],["What should you do if someone cheats?","Calmly mention it, or ask staff to help."],["How do games help build community?","Fun, laughter, connection."],["What game should we play more often?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Win with grace, lose with grace, play fair, and remember the goal is fun together.",
tryit:"Play a game with someone this week and say good game at the end, win or lose."},

{t:"Ordering at a Restaurant",
goal:"Today we will practice how to order food at a restaurant, pay, and tip with confidence.",
warm:["What is your favorite restaurant?","What do you usually order?","What feels confusing or stressful about eating out?"],
terms:[
["Menu","the list of food and drinks with prices.","Look at the sections: appetizers, main dishes, sides, drinks, desserts.","How do you choose from a menu?"],
["Ordering","telling the server or cashier what you want.","I'd like the chicken sandwich with fries and a sweet tea, please.","What makes ordering easier?"],
["Special requests","asking for changes.","Could I have that without onions? or Can I get the dressing on the side?","When might you need a special request?"],
["The check","the bill showing what you owe.","Check that everything is correct before paying.","Why should you check the bill?"],
["Tipping","extra money left for the server at a sit-down restaurant.","A common tip is 15 to 20 percent. On a $10 meal, that's about $1.50 to $2.","How do you figure out a tip?"]],
story:"Ms. Ruby got nervous at restaurants and always let others order for her. On an outing, a caregiver encouraged her to order herself. Ruby practiced in the van: I'd like the cheeseburger, no tomato, and an iced tea, please. At the restaurant, she said it perfectly. The server smiled and brought it just right. Ruby beamed and said, I did it myself!",
sq:["Why did Ruby let others order?","How did she prepare?","How did it go?","What would help you feel confident ordering?"],
act:{n:"Restaurant Role-Play",m:"A simple pretend menu with prices on paper",s:["Make a menu: Burger $7, Chicken sandwich $6, Salad $5, Fries $2, Soda $1.50, Sweet tea $1.50, Pie $3.","Residents take turns as server and customer.","Customer orders a meal with a special request.","Server writes it down and reads it back.","Together, add up the total and figure a 15 percent tip."]},
d:["Was ordering easier or harder than you thought?","How much was your meal with tip?","What special request did you make?"],
disc:[["What is the difference between fast food and sit-down restaurants?","Ordering at the counter vs. a server; tipping."],["How can you stay on budget when eating out?","Check prices, water instead of soda, share a meal."],["What should you do if your order is wrong?","Politely tell the server."],["How should you treat restaurant workers?","Kindly and patiently."],["Where would you like to go for a group outing?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Look at the menu, order clearly with please and thank you, check the bill, and tip your server at sit-down restaurants.",
tryit:"Practice your order out loud before the next time you eat out."},

{t:"Using the Public Library",
goal:"Today we will learn about the many free services at the public library and how to use them.",
warm:["When was the last time you went to a library?","What did you like to read growing up?","What would you like to learn about?"],
terms:[
["Library card","a free card that lets you borrow books, movies, and more.","In Chattanooga, you can get a free library card with an ID and proof of address.","What would you borrow first?"],
["Borrowing and due dates","taking items home and returning them by a date.","Check out a book for 3 weeks. Return it on time or renew it.","What happens if you return something late?"],
["Free computers and Wi-Fi","libraries have free internet and computers.","Use them to look for jobs, print forms, email family, or learn something new.","What would you use a computer for?"],
["Programs and classes","free events at the library.","Book clubs, computer classes, crafts, movie showings, and help with job applications.","What class sounds interesting?"],
["Large print and audiobooks","options for people who have trouble reading small print.","Large print books and audiobooks you can listen to on a device.","Would large print or audiobooks help you?"]],
story:"Mr. Ronald hadn't been to a library in 30 years. On a facility outing, he got a library card and found a section of Westerns, his favorite. He also learned the library had free computer classes. He started going every Tuesday. He said, I didn't know so much was free. It's like a second living room.",
sq:["Why did Ronald get a library card?","What did he find?","What else did he discover?","What does a second living room mean?"],
act:{n:"Library Treasure Hunt",m:"A printed list of library services, or a phone to look up the Chattanooga Public Library website",s:["Read or show a list of free library services.","Have residents pick their top 3 services they'd like to use.","Talk about what is needed to get a card: ID and proof of address (staff can help with an address letter).","Share a favorite book, author, or type of story.","Suggest a group library outing to staff."]},
d:["Which library service surprised you?","What would you borrow first?","Would you like to go on a library outing?"],
disc:[["How can the library help with goals like jobs or learning?","Computers, classes, books, help desk."],["Why is the library good for mental health?","Quiet, free activities, learning, connection."],["How do you care for borrowed items?","Keep them dry, return on time."],["What library rules should we know?","Quiet voices, return items, respect the space."],["What topic would you love to learn about?","Let residents share."]],
take:"The library is free and full of books, movies, computers, classes, and help. All you need is a library card.",
tryit:"Ask staff about getting a library card or going on a library outing."},

{t:"Being a Good Neighbor",
goal:"Today we will talk about how to be a good neighbor, here at the facility and in any future home.",
warm:["Who was the best neighbor you ever had?","What makes a bad neighbor?","How do neighbors help each other?"],
terms:[
["Neighbor","someone who lives near you.","The resident in the next room, people on our street, or people in your future apartment building.","Who are your neighbors here?"],
["Quiet hours","times when noise should be kept low.","Usually late night and early morning, like 10 p.m. to 7 a.m.","Why do quiet hours matter?"],
["Shared areas","spaces everyone uses, like hallways, porches, and parking lots.","Picking up trash, not blocking walkways, and keeping the porch tidy.","How can we care for shared areas?"],
["Helping hand","small ways to help neighbors.","Carrying groceries, checking on someone who's sick, sharing a garden tomato.","How have you helped a neighbor?"],
["Handling neighbor problems","solving issues calmly.","Knocking and kindly asking someone to lower their music, or asking staff or a landlord to help.","How would you handle a noisy neighbor?"]],
story:"When Ms. Pearl moved into her own apartment after living at a facility, she didn't know her neighbors. She baked cookies and knocked on the door next to hers to introduce herself. The neighbor, an older man, became a good friend. When Pearl got sick, he brought her soup. She said, A good neighbor is like family next door.",
sq:["How did Pearl meet her neighbor?","How did the friendship help her?","What does family next door mean?","How could you be a good neighbor here?"],
act:{n:"Neighbor Scenarios",m:"None",s:["Read situations and ask what a good neighbor would do.","Your music is loud at 11 p.m. (Turn it down.)","A neighbor's trash blew into the yard. (Pick it up or let them know.)","A new person moves in next door. (Introduce yourself.)","Your neighbor's dog barks all day. (Talk kindly or ask the landlord.)","An elderly neighbor hasn't been seen in days. (Check on them or call for help.)","Practice introducing yourself to a new neighbor."]},
d:["Which situation would be hardest?","How would you introduce yourself?","What kind of neighbor do you want to be?"],
disc:[["How do good neighbors make a community safer?","Look out for each other, report problems."],["What makes neighbors argue?","Noise, parking, trash, pets."],["How can we be good neighbors to the homes around our facility?","Respect quiet, clean up, be friendly."],["How does knowing neighbors help in emergencies?","Someone can check on you."],["What is something kind a neighbor did for you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A good neighbor is friendly, considerate about noise and shared spaces, lends a hand, and solves problems calmly.",
tryit:"Say hello to a neighbor or resident you don't usually talk to this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Community & Social Skills", [
{t:"Faith and Spiritual Community",
goal:"Today we will talk respectfully about how faith and spirituality can be a source of strength and community, for those who choose it.",
warm:["What gives you strength in hard times?","Did you grow up with a faith tradition?","What does spirituality mean to you? It is OK if your answer is nothing."],
terms:[
["Faith","belief and trust in God or a higher power, or in a set of spiritual teachings.","For many, faith brings comfort, hope, and guidance.","How has faith helped people you know?"],
["Spirituality","a sense of connection to something bigger than yourself.","It can include religion, nature, meditation, music, or helping others.","Where do you feel peaceful or connected?"],
["Faith community","a group that shares beliefs and supports each other.","A church, mosque, synagogue, Bible study, prayer group, or online service.","Have you been part of a faith community?"],
["Respecting beliefs","honoring that people believe different things.","Not pushing your beliefs on others and not mocking theirs.","How can we respect different beliefs here?"],
["Spiritual practices","activities that nurture the spirit.","Prayer, reading scripture, singing hymns, meditation, gratitude, quiet reflection.","What practice helps you?"]],
story:"Ms. Ernestine missed her church after moving into the facility. She felt cut off from her church family. A caregiver helped her find her church's live stream on a tablet and arranged for a church member to pick her up once a month. Ernestine said, I got my church back, and my peace came with it.",
sq:["What did Ernestine miss?","How did staff help?","What changed for her?","What helps you feel connected spiritually?"],
act:{n:"Sources of Strength",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Remind everyone that all beliefs, including no religious belief, are welcome and respected.","Ask each resident to write or draw their sources of strength: faith, people, nature, music, values, memories.","Invite volunteers to share a favorite hymn, verse, saying, or quote that gives them hope (keep it brief).","Discuss ways to stay connected: services, TV broadcasts, visits, prayer partners.","Ask staff to note any resident who wants help connecting with a faith community."]},
d:["What gives you strength?","Did someone share something meaningful to you?","Would you like help connecting with a faith community?"],
disc:[["How can faith support recovery and mental health?","Hope, purpose, community, guidance."],["Can faith ever be used in hurtful ways?","Yes; judgment or shame; healthy faith brings peace."],["How can we be respectful when beliefs differ?","Listen, don't argue or push."],["What if someone has no faith or is unsure?","That is OK; everyone's path is personal."],["What brings you peace?","Let residents share."]],
take:"For many, faith and spirituality bring strength, hope, and community. Honor your own path and respect others' beliefs.",
tryit:"Spend a few minutes this week on whatever practice brings you peace."},

{t:"Behaving in Public Places",
goal:"Today we will review how to act in public places so outings go smoothly and we are treated with respect.",
warm:["Where do you like to go in public?","What behavior in public bothers you?","Have you ever felt people were staring at you?"],
terms:[
["Public place","anywhere the general public can go.","Stores, buses, parks, doctor's offices, restaurants, churches, libraries.","Which public places do you visit?"],
["Inside voice","speaking at a normal, low volume indoors.","Talking so the person next to you can hear, but not people across the room.","Why is volume important in public?"],
["Waiting in line","taking your place and waiting patiently.","Standing behind the last person, not cutting, and keeping some space.","What do you do while waiting in line?"],
["Appropriate topics","things that are OK to talk about in public.","Weather and plans are fine. Very personal medical or private matters are better kept for private settings.","What topics should stay private?"],
["Handling stares or comments","staying calm if people react to you.","Ignoring it, smiling, or saying Have a nice day, and telling staff if someone is rude or threatening.","How would you handle someone staring at you?"]],
story:"At the grocery store, Mr. Kenny loudly talked about his medication and his bowel problems with a staff member. Other shoppers looked uncomfortable. Later, the staff member kindly explained that those topics are better for private settings. Next time, Kenny whispered, Can we talk about that in the van? Staff gave him a thumbs-up.",
sq:["What was the issue?","How did the staff member handle it?","What did Kenny do the next time?","Why is privacy about health good for you too?"],
act:{n:"Public or Private?",m:"None",s:["Read topics or behaviors and have the group decide if they are fine in PUBLIC or better kept PRIVATE.","Talking about the weather. (Public.)","Discussing your medical diagnosis loudly. (Private.)","Saying excuse me when passing someone. (Public.)","Arguing with a friend. (Private, and calm.)","Changing clothes. (Private.)","Asking a cashier for help. (Public.)","Discuss any that caused debate."]},
d:["Which one caused debate?","Why does privacy protect you?","What will you remember for the next outing?"],
disc:[["Why do we act differently in public than at home?","Strangers around, rules, respect."],["How can symptoms make public places hard?","Anxiety, voices, crowds; use coping skills."],["What should you do if you feel overwhelmed in public?","Tell staff, step outside, breathe."],["How can our behavior help reduce stigma?","Showing people we are respectful community members."],["What public place do you feel most comfortable in?","Let residents share."]],
take:"In public, use your inside voice, wait your turn, keep private things private, and stay calm if others stare. Tell staff if you feel overwhelmed.",
tryit:"Use your inside voice and wait your turn on your next outing."},

{t:"Celebrating Traditions",
goal:"Today we will share and celebrate the traditions that matter to us and learn about others' traditions.",
warm:["What is your favorite holiday or celebration?","What food reminds you of a special day?","What tradition from your family do you miss?"],
terms:[
["Tradition","a custom or practice passed down over time.","Eating black-eyed peas on New Year's Day, a family reunion every summer, or singing a certain song at birthdays.","What traditions did your family have?"],
["Celebration","a special event to honor something or someone.","Birthdays, holidays, graduations, anniversaries.","How do you like to celebrate?"],
["Culture","the beliefs, customs, foods, and arts of a group of people.","Different cultures celebrate holidays like Christmas, Juneteenth, Kwanzaa, or Hanukkah.","What cultural celebration would you like to learn about?"],
["New traditions","new customs we create together.","A monthly birthday party at the facility, or Friday movie night.","What new tradition could our home start?"],
["Memories","traditions connect us to good memories and people we love.","Smelling sweet potato pie and remembering grandma's kitchen.","What memory does a tradition bring back for you?"]],
story:"The facility started a new tradition: on the last Friday of each month, they celebrated everyone's birthdays that month with cake and music. Mr. Clarence hadn't celebrated his birthday in ten years. When everyone sang to him, he got emotional. He said, I didn't think anybody would remember. Now he helps plan the parties.",
sq:["What tradition did the facility start?","Why was Clarence emotional?","How did he get involved?","Why are traditions meaningful?"],
act:{n:"Tradition Sharing Circle",m:"Paper, pens, crayons",s:["Ask each resident to think of a favorite tradition from their life.","Have them draw or write about it: what it is, who was there, what food or music was part of it.","Go around the circle and let each person share.","Ask the group: What new tradition could we start here?","Vote on one new tradition idea and share it with staff."]},
d:["What tradition did you enjoy hearing about?","What new tradition did the group choose?","How do traditions make a place feel like home?"],
disc:[["Why do traditions matter?","Connection, identity, joy, comfort."],["How can holidays be hard for some people?","Missing loved ones, memories; be gentle."],["How can we include everyone in celebrations?","Respect different beliefs, offer choices."],["What tradition would you like to pass on?","Let residents share."],["What makes a celebration special to you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Traditions connect us to our past and to each other. Share yours, learn others', and create new ones together.",
tryit:"Share a tradition or a favorite memory with someone this week."},

{t:"Acts of Kindness",
goal:"Today we will explore how small acts of kindness affect others and ourselves, and plan kind acts we can do.",
warm:["What is the kindest thing a stranger has done for you?","How do you feel after helping someone?","What small kindness could brighten someone's day?"],
terms:[
["Act of kindness","doing something nice for someone without expecting anything back.","Saving someone a seat, sharing an umbrella, writing a nice note.","What act of kindness have you done lately?"],
["Random kindness","an unexpected kind act.","Leaving a nice note on a staff member's desk.","Why do unexpected kindnesses feel special?"],
["Ripple effect","when one kind act leads to more kindness.","Someone helps you, so you help someone else, and it keeps spreading.","Have you seen a ripple effect?"],
["Kindness to yourself","treating yourself with care too.","Resting when tired, speaking gently to yourself, doing something you enjoy.","How can you be kind to yourself today?"],
["Kindness without money","most kindness costs nothing.","A smile, a compliment, listening, holding a door, helping with a chore.","What free kindness could you give?"]],
story:"A caregiver had a rough week, and residents noticed she seemed down. Ms. Ruth organized a card that everyone signed: Thank you for taking care of us. We love you. The caregiver cried when she read it. She said it was the best thing that had happened to her all year. Ruth said, We get so much care. It felt good to give some back.",
sq:["What did residents notice?","What did Ruth organize?","How did the caregiver react?","What does it feel good to give back mean?"],
act:{n:"Kindness Plan",m:"Paper, pens, and a card for a group project",s:["Brainstorm a list of kind acts for residents, staff, family, and strangers.","Have each resident choose two kind acts they will do this week.","Write them on a small card to keep.","As a group, make one card or sign for someone (a staff member, a sick resident, or a local fire station).","Plan to share how the kind acts went at the next group."]},
d:["Which kind acts did you choose?","Who did the group card go to?","How do you think it will make them feel?"],
disc:[["Why does kindness improve our own mood?","Purpose, connection, feel-good chemicals."],["Is it ever hard to be kind?","When we're hurting, angry, or tired."],["How can kindness change a home?","Less conflict, more trust."],["What is the difference between kindness and being a pushover?","Kindness includes boundaries."],["Who in your life is the kindest person you know?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Small acts of kindness cost nothing and change everything. Be kind to others and to yourself, and watch it ripple.",
tryit:"Do your two kind acts this week."},

{t:"Enjoying the Outdoors",
goal:"Today we will talk about the benefits of spending time outside and safe ways to enjoy nature.",
warm:["What is your favorite outdoor place?","Did you spend a lot of time outside as a child?","What do you enjoy about being outside?"],
terms:[
["Fresh air","clean outdoor air.","Sitting on the porch for 15 minutes can lift mood and help with sleep.","How do you feel after being outside?"],
["Sunlight and vitamin D","the sun helps our bodies make vitamin D, which supports bones and mood.","Getting a little morning sun can help your body clock and energy. Use sunscreen for longer time outdoors.","When do you usually get sunlight?"],
["Nature","plants, trees, birds, water, and other parts of the natural world.","Watching birds at a feeder or walking by the river can be calming.","What part of nature do you enjoy?"],
["Outdoor activities","things to do outside.","Walking, gardening, fishing, sitting in a park, picnics, bird watching.","What outdoor activity would you like to do?"],
["Outdoor safety","staying safe outside.","Water, sunscreen, a hat, proper shoes, checking the weather, staying with a buddy, and watching for uneven ground.","What safety steps do you take outside?"]],
story:"Ms. Sylvia spent most of her days inside and felt down. A caregiver invited her to sit on the porch each morning with coffee. Sylvia started noticing the birds and asked for a bird feeder. Soon other residents joined her to watch cardinals and blue jays. She said, I didn't realize how much I missed the outdoors.",
sq:["How did Sylvia feel spending days inside?","What small change did she make?","How did it spread to others?","What outdoor activity could help you?"],
act:{n:"Nature Moment",m:"If weather allows, take the group outside; otherwise, use a window or nature sounds",s:["If possible, move the group to the porch or yard for a few minutes (check weather and safety).","Do a 5-senses nature check: What do you see, hear, smell, feel, and what does the air taste like?","If inside, look out a window or play nature sounds and do the same.","Brainstorm outdoor activities the group could do: walks, a garden, picnics, a bird feeder.","Choose one to suggest to staff."]},
d:["How did it feel to focus on nature?","What did you notice?","What outdoor activity did the group choose?"],
disc:[["How does nature help mental health?","Lowers stress, improves mood and sleep."],["What gets in the way of going outside?","Weather, mobility, energy, safety."],["How can we make the outdoor spaces here more enjoyable?","Chairs, plants, feeders, shade."],["What parks in Chattanooga have you visited?","Coolidge Park, the Riverwalk, Lookout Mountain; let residents share."],["What is your favorite season to be outdoors?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Time outdoors lifts mood, helps sleep, and connects us to the world. Even 15 minutes on the porch makes a difference. Stay safe and enjoy.",
tryit:"Spend at least 15 minutes outside on three days this week, weather permitting."},

{t:"House Meetings: Having a Voice",
goal:"Today we will learn how to participate in house meetings so our ideas and concerns help shape our home.",
warm:["What is one thing you love about living here?","What is one thing you would change?","Have you ever been to a meeting where your voice was heard?"],
terms:[
["House meeting","a regular meeting where residents and staff discuss life in the home.","Talking about menu ideas, outings, chores, rules, and concerns.","What would you want to talk about at a house meeting?"],
["Agenda","a list of topics for a meeting.","1. Menu ideas. 2. Outing plans. 3. Bathroom schedule. 4. Open floor.","Why is an agenda helpful?"],
["Suggestion","an idea for improving something.","Could we have taco night once a week? or Could we get a second microwave?","What suggestion do you have?"],
["Voting","a fair way for a group to decide.","Raising hands to choose between a movie night or a game night.","Why is voting fair?"],
["Constructive feedback","sharing concerns with a possible solution.","The TV room gets loud at night. Could we have quiet hours after 10?","How is this better than just complaining?"]],
story:"Residents complained to each other about the food for months but never said anything at house meetings. Finally, Mr. Ross brought it up with a suggestion: a monthly resident-choice dinner. Staff agreed, and residents voted on the first menu: fried chicken and greens. Ross said, I learned complaining in the hallway changes nothing. Speaking up at the meeting did.",
sq:["What were residents doing before?","What did Ross suggest?","What happened?","What does his last sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"Mock House Meeting",m:"Board for the agenda",s:["Choose a resident to lead and one to take notes.","Build an agenda with the group: 3 or 4 topics.","For each topic, residents share ideas or concerns with possible solutions.","Practice voting on one decision.","Write a summary of ideas to share with staff after group."]},
d:["How did it feel to lead or take notes?","What idea came up that you liked?","Will you share ideas at a real house meeting?"],
disc:[["Why do people complain privately instead of speaking up?","Fear, feeling unheard, not knowing how."],["How can we make sure everyone gets a voice?","Take turns, ask quiet members, use a suggestion box."],["What should you do if your idea isn't chosen?","Accept the vote, try again later."],["How do house meetings build community?","Shared decisions, ownership, respect."],["What is one change you'd suggest for our home?","Let residents share; facilitator passes ideas to staff."]],
take:"Your voice matters. Bring ideas with solutions to house meetings, listen to others, vote fairly, and help shape our home.",
tryit:"Bring one suggestion with a solution to the next house meeting."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Goals, Motivation & Purpose", [
{t:"Setting SMART Goals",
goal:"Today we will learn how to set goals that are clear and reachable using the SMART method.",
warm:["What is something you have always wanted to do?","Have you ever set a goal and reached it?","What usually stops people from reaching goals?"],
terms:[
["Goal","something you want to achieve.","Getting my ID, walking every day, saving $50, or reconnecting with my daughter.","What is one goal you have?"],
["Specific","clear about exactly what you want.","Instead of get healthy, say walk around the building after lunch.","How could you make your goal more specific?"],
["Measurable","you can track it or count it.","Walk 10 minutes, 5 days a week. You can check off each day.","How would you measure your goal?"],
["Achievable and Relevant","possible for you right now, and it matters to you.","Running a marathon next month may not be achievable. Walking 10 minutes is. It matters because you want more energy.","Why should goals be realistic?"],
["Time-bound","has a deadline.","By the end of this month, I will walk 10 minutes, 5 days a week.","Why does a deadline help?"]],
story:"Ms. Karen said her goal was to be more social. Weeks passed and nothing changed. In group, she turned it into a SMART goal: I will sit with someone new at lunch twice a week for the next month. She tracked it on a calendar. By the end of the month, she had two new friends. She said, A vague goal kept me stuck. A clear goal got me moving.",
sq:["What was Karen's first goal?","Why didn't it work?","What was her SMART goal?","What changed?"],
act:{n:"Make It SMART",m:"Paper, pens, SMART letters on the board",s:["Write S-M-A-R-T down the board with each meaning.","Give a vague goal: I want to save money. Turn it into a SMART goal together: I will put $5 in my savings envelope every Friday for 8 weeks.","Have each resident write their own vague goal.","Then help them rewrite it using each SMART letter.","Invite volunteers to share their SMART goal."]},
d:["What was your vague goal?","What is your SMART goal?","How will you track it?"],
disc:[["Why do vague goals often fail?","No clear action, can't tell progress."],["What should you do if you fall behind on a goal?","Adjust, don't quit."],["Who can help you with your goal?","Staff, case manager, friends."],["How does reaching small goals build confidence?","Success breeds success."],["What goal matters most to you right now?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Goals work best when they are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound. Start small and track your progress.",
tryit:"Work on your SMART goal every day this week and check it off."},

{t:"Finding Your Purpose",
goal:"Today we will explore what gives our lives meaning and purpose, and how to find more of it each day.",
warm:["What makes you feel useful?","When do you feel most like yourself?","What would you want people to remember about you?"],
terms:[
["Purpose","a reason to get up in the morning; what gives life meaning.","Caring for others, faith, family, creating art, helping your community.","What gets you out of bed in the morning?"],
["Meaning","the sense that your life matters.","Knowing that your kindness helps someone, or that your story can help others.","Where do you find meaning?"],
["Passion","something you love doing.","Cooking, music, gardening, sports, fixing things, talking with people.","What are you passionate about?"],
["Contribution","what you give to others or the world.","Encouraging a new resident, sharing a skill, praying for others.","What do you contribute here?"],
["Purpose at any stage","purpose can change but never disappears.","A retired teacher reads to other residents; a former cook shares recipes.","How has your purpose changed over time?"]],
story:"Mr. Albert, a retired mechanic, felt he had no purpose after his illness. One day, a staff member's car wouldn't start. Albert walked out and talked her through checking the battery. It worked. Staff began asking him car questions, and he started a car talk group. He said, I thought my usefulness was over. Turns out it just changed shape.",
sq:["Why did Albert feel he had no purpose?","What happened with the car?","How did his purpose change shape?","What skill or gift do you still have?"],
act:{n:"Purpose Map",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Draw four circles on the board: What I Love, What I'm Good At, What People Need, What Matters to Me.","Have each resident fill in each circle for themselves.","Look for overlaps: what appears in more than one circle?","Help each person write one sentence: My purpose might include ___.","Share with the group if comfortable."]},
d:["What overlaps did you find?","What purpose sentence did you write?","How could you live that purpose this week?"],
disc:[["How does lack of purpose affect mental health?","Emptiness, depression, boredom."],["Can purpose be found in small things?","Yes; daily kindness, routines, caring for plants."],["How can illness or loss change purpose?","Old roles end, new ones begin."],["How can this home help residents find purpose?","Jobs, roles, groups, volunteering."],["What would you like your purpose to be?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Everyone has a purpose. Look where what you love, what you're good at, what others need, and what matters to you meet.",
tryit:"Do one thing this week that connects to your purpose sentence."},

{t:"Staying Motivated",
goal:"Today we will learn what motivation is, why it comes and goes, and how to keep going even when we don't feel like it.",
warm:["What motivates you to get things done?","When do you feel least motivated?","What gets you going on a hard day?"],
terms:[
["Motivation","the drive to do something.","Wanting to see your grandkids motivates you to stay healthy.","What motivates you most?"],
["Inner motivation","doing something because it matters to you.","Walking because you like how it feels, not because someone told you to.","Why does inner motivation last longer?"],
["Outer motivation","doing something for a reward or to avoid a consequence.","Earning points, a treat, or praise.","What rewards motivate you?"],
["Action before motivation","sometimes you have to start before you feel like it.","You don't feel like cleaning, but you start with one drawer, and then you keep going.","Has starting ever made you feel more motivated?"],
["Your why","the deep reason behind your goal.","I want to stay sober so I can be there for my daughter.","What is your why?"]],
story:"Mr. Derek wanted to get stronger so he could walk without his walker. Some days he didn't feel like doing his exercises. His physical therapist asked him to tape a photo of his son's baseball team to his wall, because Derek's dream was to walk to the bleachers and watch a game. When he didn't feel like exercising, he looked at the photo. Six months later, he walked to the bleachers.",
sq:["What was Derek's goal?","What was his why?","How did the photo help?","What could remind you of your why?"],
act:{n:"My Why Card",m:"Index cards, markers",s:["Have each resident write a goal on one side of a card.","On the other side, write their why: the deep reason it matters.","Encourage them to add a drawing or symbol.","Practice the two-minute rule: when you don't feel like doing something, do it for just two minutes.","Share a few why cards with the group."]},
d:["What is your why?","Where will you keep your card?","How could the two-minute rule help you?"],
disc:[["Why does motivation come and go?","Mood, energy, stress, health."],["What kills motivation?","Comparing, perfectionism, big goals, no support."],["How can friends help with motivation?","Encouragement, doing things together, accountability."],["How do you keep going after a setback?","Remember your why, start small again."],["What motivates you today?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Motivation comes and goes, but your why stays. Start small, act before you feel like it, and keep your reason in sight.",
tryit:"Use the two-minute rule on a task you've been avoiding."},

{t:"Overcoming Obstacles",
goal:"Today we will learn how to face obstacles that block our goals and find ways around them.",
warm:["What is an obstacle you have overcome in your life?","What gets in the way of your goals right now?","Who is someone you admire for overcoming hard things?"],
terms:[
["Obstacle","something that blocks your path to a goal.","No transportation, health problems, lack of money, fear, or not knowing how.","What obstacles do you face?"],
["Problem-solving","finding a way around, over, or through an obstacle.","No ride to the library? Ask staff, take the bus, or ask about a library outing.","How do you usually solve problems?"],
["Inner obstacles","obstacles inside us, like fear, doubt, or negative self-talk.","Thinking, I'll never be able to do it.","How can you deal with inner obstacles?"],
["Outer obstacles","obstacles outside us, like money, rules, or other people.","Not having an ID to apply for a job.","Who could help with outer obstacles?"],
["Detour","a different route to the same goal.","Can't afford a gym? Walk the hallways or do chair exercises.","What detour could help with your goal?"]],
story:"Ms. Tasha wanted to get her GED but had three obstacles: no transportation, trouble reading, and fear that she was too old. Her team helped her find a free online program she could do on the facility computer, a reading tutor from a volunteer group, and a GED graduate who was 62 to talk with her. Tasha is now halfway through her GED.",
sq:["What were Tasha's obstacles?","Which were inner and which were outer?","How was each one solved?","What obstacle would you like help with?"],
act:{n:"Obstacle Busters",m:"Board, paper",s:["Ask residents to share a goal and an obstacle (or use examples).","Write each obstacle on the board.","For each, the group brainstorms at least three ways around it.","Label obstacles as inner or outer.","Have each resident write their own obstacle and three possible detours."]},
d:["Which brainstorm surprised you?","What is your obstacle and detour?","Who will help you?"],
disc:[["Why do obstacles make people give up?","Feels impossible, discouragement."],["How can asking for help remove obstacles?","Others know resources and solutions."],["What inner obstacle is hardest for you?","Let residents share."],["How do past successes help with new obstacles?","Proof you can overcome."],["What obstacle are you proud of overcoming?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Obstacles are not stop signs; they are detour signs. Name the obstacle, brainstorm ways around it, and ask for help.",
tryit:"Take one step around an obstacle this week."},

{t:"Knowing Your Values",
goal:"Today we will identify our personal values and see how they can guide our choices.",
warm:["What matters most to you in life?","What is something you would never do, no matter what?","Who taught you right from wrong?"],
terms:[
["Values","beliefs about what is important in life.","Honesty, family, faith, kindness, independence, respect, hard work.","What are three of your values?"],
["Living your values","making choices that match what you believe.","If you value honesty, you return extra change the cashier gave you.","When did you make a choice based on your values?"],
["Values conflict","when two values pull you in different directions.","Valuing loyalty to a friend and honesty when the friend asks you to lie for them.","How do you handle a values conflict?"],
["Compass","values guide you like a compass guides a traveler.","When unsure what to do, ask: What would someone who values ___ do?","How could your values be a compass?"],
["Changing values","values can grow and change over time.","Someone who valued partying in their twenties may now value peace and health.","How have your values changed?"]],
story:"Mr. Isaac found a wallet with $80 in the facility parking lot. He was low on money and was tempted to keep it. But he valued honesty, because his grandmother always said, Do right even when no one is watching. He turned it in to staff. It belonged to a visitor, who came back to thank him and gave him a $20 reward. Isaac said, Even without the reward, I would've slept better.",
sq:["What tempted Isaac?","What value guided him?","Where did that value come from?","Why would he have slept better?"],
act:{n:"Values Sort",m:"A list of values printed or on the board",s:["Write 20 values on the board: honesty, family, faith, health, freedom, kindness, respect, loyalty, fun, independence, security, love, learning, hard work, creativity, peace, helping others, courage, humor, responsibility.","Have each resident pick their top 5.","Then narrow to their top 3.","Have them write one example of how they live each value.","Share top values with the group and look for common ones."]},
d:["Was it hard to choose only three?","What values does our group share?","How could your values help with a tough decision?"],
disc:[["What happens when we act against our values?","Guilt, regret, stress."],["How can values help in recovery and mental health?","Direction, motivation, self-respect."],["What should you do when others pressure you against your values?","Stand firm, say no, find support."],["How can we respect different values in a shared home?","Listen, find common ground."],["What value do you want to live more fully?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Your values are your compass. Know them, live by them, and let them guide your choices.",
tryit:"Make one choice this week based on one of your top three values."},

{t:"Discovering Your Strengths",
goal:"Today we will identify our personal strengths and learn to use them every day.",
warm:["What is something people often ask you for help with?","What did you do well as a child?","What compliment do you hear most often?"],
terms:[
["Strength","a positive quality or skill you have.","Being a good listener, cooking, making people laugh, staying calm, being organized.","What is one of your strengths?"],
["Character strengths","qualities of who you are.","Kindness, bravery, honesty, patience, humor, faith, gratitude.","Which character strengths do you have?"],
["Skills","things you've learned to do.","Cooking, fixing things, singing, drawing, math, caring for others.","What skills have you learned?"],
["Hidden strengths","strengths you may not notice in yourself.","Surviving hard times shows resilience. Asking for help shows courage.","What hard thing have you survived?"],
["Using strengths","putting your strengths into action.","A resident who is a good listener helps welcome new residents.","How could you use one strength this week?"]],
story:"Ms. Bertha said she had no strengths. Her counselor asked, Who do residents come to when they're upset? Bertha admitted, Me. They say I calm them down. Her counselor said, That's a strength: compassion. Bertha started helping welcome new residents. She said, I always thought of myself by what I couldn't do. Now I know what I can.",
sq:["What did Bertha believe at first?","What strength did her counselor point out?","How did she start using it?","How did it change how she saw herself?"],
act:{n:"Strength Spotting",m:"Paper, pens, list of strengths",s:["Write a list of strengths on the board: kind, funny, patient, brave, honest, creative, hard-working, good listener, loyal, organized, faithful, calm, helpful, curious, strong, caring.","Have each resident circle 3 strengths they have.","Then pair up and have partners name one more strength they see in each other.","Write: I can use my strength of ___ by ___.","Share with the group."]},
d:["Which strengths did you choose?","What strength did your partner see in you?","How will you use your strength?"],
disc:[["Why is it easier to see weaknesses than strengths?","Criticism, low self-esteem, focus on problems."],["How can knowing strengths help with goals?","Use them to overcome obstacles."],["How can strengths help in a job?","Show employers what you offer."],["How can we point out strengths in each other?","Compliments, specific praise."],["What strength are you most proud of?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You have real strengths. Know them, name them, and use them every day.",
tryit:"Use one of your strengths on purpose this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Goals, Motivation & Purpose", [
{t:"Building Healthy Habits",
goal:"Today we will learn how habits form and how to build healthy ones that stick.",
warm:["What is a habit you do every day without thinking?","What healthy habit do you wish you had?","What habit have you broken before?"],
terms:[
["Habit","something you do so often it becomes automatic.","Brushing your teeth after breakfast or checking your phone first thing in the morning.","What habits do you have?"],
["Cue","the trigger that starts a habit.","Finishing breakfast is the cue to take your vitamins.","What cues start your habits?"],
["Habit stacking","linking a new habit to an old one.","After I pour my morning coffee, I will write one thing I'm grateful for.","What habit could you stack?"],
["Start tiny","making a new habit so small it's easy.","One push-up, one minute of stretching, one glass of water.","Why does starting tiny work?"],
["Streak","doing a habit many days in a row.","Marking an X on a calendar each day you walk, and trying not to break the chain.","Would tracking a streak help you?"]],
story:"Mr. Sam wanted to drink more water but always forgot. He decided to stack it: every time he took his morning medicine, he would drink a full glass of water. He marked an X on his calendar each day. After 30 days, he didn't need the calendar anymore. He said, It's just part of my morning now. I don't even think about it.",
sq:["What habit did Sam want?","How did he stack it?","How did he track it?","How long did it take to become automatic?"],
act:{n:"Habit Stack Builder",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Explain the formula: After I ___ (current habit), I will ___ (new tiny habit).","Give examples: After I brush my teeth, I will stretch for one minute. After lunch, I will walk to the mailbox.","Have each resident write one habit stack.","Give each person a simple 14-day tracker (14 boxes) to mark their streak.","Share habit stacks with the group."]},
d:["What habit stack did you create?","Is it tiny enough to be easy?","Where will you keep your tracker?"],
disc:[["Why do new habits fail?","Too big, no cue, no tracking, giving up after missing a day."],["What should you do if you miss a day?","Never miss twice; start again."],["How long does it take to build a habit?","Weeks to months; it varies."],["How can you break a bad habit?","Remove the cue, replace it with something else."],["What healthy habit are you proud of?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Start tiny, link new habits to old ones, track your streak, and if you miss a day, start again.",
tryit:"Do your habit stack every day this week and mark your tracker."},

{t:"Holding On to Hope",
goal:"Today we will talk about what hope is, why it matters, and how to grow it even in hard times.",
warm:["What are you hopeful about right now?","Who gives you hope?","What helped you through your hardest time?"],
terms:[
["Hope","believing that good things are possible in the future.","Believing that your health can improve, that you can reconnect with family, or that tomorrow can be better.","What does hope feel like?"],
["Hopelessness","feeling that nothing will ever get better.","Thinking, What's the point? Nothing will change.","How does hopelessness affect people?"],
["Small hopes","hoping for little things.","Hoping for a good meal, a phone call, sunshine, or a good night's sleep.","What small thing are you hoping for this week?"],
["Hope builders","things that grow hope.","Remembering past successes, hearing others' recovery stories, setting small goals, faith, and support.","What builds your hope?"],
["Borrowing hope","leaning on others' hope when yours is low.","When you can't believe things will improve, trust someone who believes it for you.","Who could you borrow hope from?"]],
story:"When Mr. Clifton arrived at the facility, he said he had given up. His roommate, Mr. James, had been through similar struggles and was now doing well. James told him, I didn't believe it either, but I'll hold the hope for you until you can. Each week, James pointed out one small improvement in Clifton. Six months later, Clifton said, I borrowed his hope until I had my own.",
sq:["How did Clifton feel when he arrived?","What did James offer?","How did James help him see progress?","What does borrowing hope mean?"],
act:{n:"Hope Jar",m:"A jar or box, slips of paper, pens",s:["Give each resident 2 or 3 slips of paper.","On each slip, write something you hope for, big or small.","Read a few aloud (with permission) and place all slips in the jar.","Then write one slip of encouragement for someone else, like Don't give up or Better days are coming.","Let residents draw an encouragement slip to keep."]},
d:["What hope did you put in the jar?","What encouragement did you receive?","How did it feel to give hope to someone else?"],
disc:[["What steals hope?","Losses, illness, setbacks, negative people."],["What should you do if you feel hopeless?","Tell staff, talk to someone, call or text 988 if you have thoughts of not wanting to live."],["How can we give hope to each other?","Encourage, share stories, celebrate progress."],["How does hope help recovery?","Motivation to keep going."],["What story of hope inspires you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Hope is believing better is possible. Grow it with small goals and support, and when yours is low, borrow some from others.",
tryit:"Tell one person this week something you're hopeful about for them."},

{t:"Exploring Work and Careers",
goal:"Today we will explore the benefits of work, types of jobs and volunteer roles, and first steps toward employment.",
warm:["What was your favorite job ever?","What kind of work did you dream of as a kid?","What do you think work gives people besides money?"],
terms:[
["Employment","having a job that pays.","Part-time work at a grocery store, a restaurant, cleaning, or a warehouse.","What kinds of jobs interest you?"],
["Supported employment","programs that help people with disabilities find and keep jobs.","A job coach helps you apply, learn the job, and solve problems at work.","Would a job coach be helpful?"],
["Job skills","abilities employers want.","Being on time, following directions, getting along with others, and being reliable.","Which job skills do you have?"],
["Application and resume","forms and documents that show your experience.","A resume lists your jobs, skills, and volunteer work.","What would go on your resume?"],
["Benefits and work","working can affect disability benefits, but there are programs to help.","Ask the social worker about Ticket to Work and how earnings affect your benefits.","Who can you ask about work and benefits?"]],
story:"Mr. Andre hadn't worked in 10 years and thought no one would hire him. With help from a supported employment program, he started volunteering at a thrift store two mornings a week. After three months, the manager offered him a paid part-time job sorting donations. He said, Getting a paycheck again made me feel like a man again.",
sq:["Why did Andre think he couldn't work?","How did he start?","What happened after three months?","What did the paycheck mean to him?"],
act:{n:"Job Interest Inventory",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Ask residents to answer: Do you like working with people, things, or ideas? Inside or outside? Sitting or moving? Mornings or afternoons?","List jobs that match common answers: greeter, cashier, cook, janitor, landscaper, stocker, receptionist, dishwasher, peer support.","Have each resident circle 2 or 3 jobs that interest them.","Write a list of their job skills.","Discuss first steps: volunteering, talking to the social worker, getting an ID, making a resume."]},
d:["What kind of work interests you?","What skills do you already have?","What first step could you take?"],
disc:[["How can work help mental health?","Routine, purpose, social contact, income."],["What worries people about going back to work?","Losing benefits, stress, failing, symptoms."],["How can volunteering lead to work?","Builds skills, references, confidence."],["How do you explain a gap in work history?","Honestly and briefly: I was focusing on my health and now I'm ready."],["What job would you love to try?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Work and volunteering can bring purpose, routine, and income. Start small, know your strengths, and ask about supports and how work affects benefits.",
tryit:"Talk to the social worker or staff about one job or volunteer idea."},

{t:"Learning New Things",
goal:"Today we will talk about the joy and benefits of learning new skills at any age and explore ways to keep learning.",
warm:["What is something new you learned recently?","What did you love learning about in school?","What would you like to learn how to do?"],
terms:[
["Lifelong learning","continuing to learn throughout life.","Taking a class at 70, learning to use a tablet, or learning a new recipe.","Is anyone too old to learn?"],
["Brain health","learning keeps the brain active and healthy.","Doing puzzles, reading, and learning new skills help keep memory sharp.","What activities keep your brain busy?"],
["GED and adult education","programs to finish high school or improve reading and math.","Free GED and adult reading programs are available in Chattanooga.","Would you be interested in a class?"],
["Growth mindset","believing you can improve with effort.","I can't do this yet, instead of I can't do this.","How does the word yet change things?"],
["Learning resources","free places to learn.","Libraries, YouTube videos, community centers, churches, and volunteers.","Where could you learn something new?"]],
story:"Ms. Opal, age 68, always wished she could read better. A volunteer started reading with her twice a week. She practiced with large-print books. After a year, she read a full chapter book for the first time and read a Bible passage aloud at church. She cried and said, I thought it was too late. It wasn't.",
sq:["What did Ms. Opal want to learn?","How did she learn?","What did she accomplish?","What does It wasn't too late mean to you?"],
act:{n:"Teach Me Something",m:"None",s:["Ask volunteers to teach the group something small in 2 minutes: a word in another language, a card trick, a recipe tip, a fact, a dance step.","After each mini-lesson, the group tries it.","Talk about how it felt to learn something new and to teach.","Have each resident write one thing they want to learn this year.","Discuss how to start learning it."]},
d:["What did you learn today?","How did it feel to teach?","What do you want to learn next?"],
disc:[["Why do adults stop learning?","Fear of failure, embarrassment, busy lives."],["How does learning help mental health?","Confidence, purpose, focus."],["How can we support each other in learning?","Patience, encouragement, no teasing."],["What does yet mean in a growth mindset?","You're still learning; not there yet."],["What would you love to learn?","Let residents share."]],
take:"It's never too late to learn. Use the word yet, find free resources, and keep your brain growing.",
tryit:"Spend 10 minutes learning something new this week."},

{t:"Making Good Decisions",
goal:"Today we will learn a simple process for making decisions we feel good about.",
warm:["What is a good decision you made in your life?","What decision do you regret?","How do you usually make decisions: fast or slow?"],
terms:[
["Decision","choosing between options.","Whether to go on the outing or stay home, whether to call a family member, what to spend money on.","What decision are you facing now?"],
["Options","the different choices you could make.","For a free afternoon: nap, walk, call a friend, play cards, watch TV.","Why is it good to think of more than two options?"],
["Pros and cons","the good and bad points of each option.","Going on the outing. Pros: fun, fresh air. Cons: tiring.","How do pros and cons help?"],
["Consequences","what happens as a result of a choice.","Spending all your money on day one means nothing for the rest of the month.","How do you think about consequences?"],
["Impulse decisions","decisions made quickly without thinking.","Quitting a program because you had one bad day.","When are impulse decisions risky?"]],
story:"Ms. Rhonda got a call from an old friend inviting her to move in with him across town. She was excited and almost packed her bags. Her counselor asked her to make a pros and cons list first. Rhonda realized the friend still used drugs and the apartment had no elevator for her bad knees. She decided to stay and visit him in public instead. She said, I'm glad I slowed down.",
sq:["What decision did Rhonda face?","What did her counselor suggest?","What did the list show her?","What did she decide?"],
act:{n:"Decision Steps",m:"Paper, pens, steps on the board",s:["Write 5 steps: 1. What is the decision? 2. What are my options? 3. Pros and cons of each. 4. Which fits my values and goals? 5. Choose and check how it went.","Use a group example: Should I use my $20 for a new shirt or save it?","Walk through the steps together.","Have each resident use the steps on a real or pretend decision.","Share a few results."]},
d:["Did the steps help you think differently?","Which step was most useful?","When will you use this process?"],
disc:[["Why do strong emotions lead to poor decisions?","Thinking gets cloudy."],["When should you sleep on a decision?","Big choices, when upset."],["Who can help you think through big decisions?","Counselor, staff, trusted friend."],["What if you make a bad decision?","Learn from it, fix what you can."],["What decision are you proud of?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Slow down, list your options, weigh pros and cons, check your values, and ask for advice on big decisions.",
tryit:"Use the five steps on one decision this week."},

{t:"Problem-Solving Step by Step",
goal:"Today we will practice a step-by-step method for solving everyday problems.",
warm:["What is a problem you solved recently?","Do you tend to avoid problems or face them?","Who do you go to when you have a problem?"],
terms:[
["Problem","a situation that needs a solution.","Running out of money before the end of the month, or a roommate who snores.","What problem is on your mind?"],
["Define the problem","saying exactly what the problem is.","Instead of Everything's wrong, say I keep missing breakfast because I sleep through my alarm.","Why does defining the problem help?"],
["Brainstorm","listing many possible solutions without judging them.","Move the alarm across the room, ask staff to knock, go to bed earlier, use two alarms.","Why list many ideas?"],
["Try a solution","picking one and testing it.","Try moving the alarm across the room for one week.","What if the first solution doesn't work?"],
["Review","checking whether the solution worked.","After a week: Did I make breakfast? If not, try the next idea.","Why is reviewing important?"]],
story:"Mr. Greg kept getting into arguments in the TV room. He defined the problem: We argue about which show to watch every night at 7. He brainstormed: make a schedule, use headphones with his tablet, watch in a different room, take turns. The group agreed on a TV schedule. The arguments stopped. Greg said, Once I named the problem, I could actually fix it.",
sq:["How did Greg define the problem?","What ideas did he brainstorm?","What solution was chosen?","Why did naming the problem help?"],
act:{n:"Problem-Solving Practice",m:"Board",s:["Write the 5 steps: Define, Brainstorm, Choose, Try, Review.","Ask the group for a common problem in the home (keep it general).","Walk through each step as a group.","Write all brainstorm ideas without judging.","Choose one solution and plan how to try it and when to review."]},
d:["Which step was hardest?","How many ideas did we brainstorm?","What problem will you use these steps on?"],
disc:[["Why do people avoid problems?","Overwhelm, fear, don't know where to start."],["How does breaking a problem into steps help?","Less overwhelming, clear actions."],["What problems need staff help?","Safety, money, health, conflicts that keep happening."],["How can solving small problems build confidence?","Proof you can handle things."],["What problem are you proud of solving?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Define the problem, brainstorm ideas, choose one, try it, and review. If it doesn't work, try the next one.",
tryit:"Use the five steps on one problem this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Goals, Motivation & Purpose", [
{t:"Taking Responsibility",
goal:"Today we will talk about taking responsibility for our choices and how it gives us more control over our lives.",
warm:["What does being responsible mean to you?","Who is the most responsible person you know?","What responsibilities do you have now?"],
terms:[
["Responsibility","being accountable for your actions and duties.","Taking your medicine, keeping your room clean, and showing up to appointments.","What are you responsible for each day?"],
["Accountability","owning your choices, both good and bad.","Saying, I forgot to call, that's on me, instead of blaming others.","Why is accountability hard?"],
["Blaming","putting the fault on others.","It's staff's fault I missed the bus, when you overslept.","How does blaming keep us stuck?"],
["Locus of control","believing you have some control over what happens to you.","I can't control my diagnosis, but I can control whether I take my medicine and go to group.","What can you control in your life?"],
["Making it right","fixing a mistake when you can.","Replacing something you broke, apologizing, or changing what you do next time.","How do you make things right?"]],
story:"Mr. Paul often missed group and blamed staff for not reminding him. One day, his counselor asked, What part of this can you control? Paul admitted he could set his own alarm. He started doing it and made it to every group that week. He said, Blaming felt easier, but owning it gave me power.",
sq:["Who did Paul blame?","What question did his counselor ask?","What did Paul change?","What does owning it gave me power mean?"],
act:{n:"Control Circle",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident draw a circle.","Inside: things I can control (my words, my actions, taking medicine, my attitude).","Outside: things I can't control (other people, weather, the past, my diagnosis).","Pick one thing inside the circle to take responsibility for this week.","Share with the group."]},
d:["What was in your circle?","What will you take responsibility for?","How does focusing on what you can control feel?"],
disc:[["Why do people blame others?","Shame, fear of consequences, habit."],["How does taking responsibility build trust?","People see you are honest and reliable."],["Is it possible to take responsibility without beating yourself up?","Yes; own it, learn, move on."],["How can responsibility help you get more independence?","Shows you can handle more freedom."],["What responsibility are you proud of handling?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Taking responsibility gives you power. Focus on what you can control, own your mistakes, and make things right.",
tryit:"Take full responsibility for one task this week without reminders."},

{t:"Building Resilience",
goal:"Today we will learn what resilience is and how to bounce back from life's hard times.",
warm:["What is a hard time you made it through?","What helped you get through it?","What does the word bounce back mean to you?"],
terms:[
["Resilience","the ability to recover and grow after hardship.","Losing a job, getting sick, or going through a loss, and slowly finding your footing again.","Who is the most resilient person you know?"],
["Setback","something that slows down your progress.","A hospital stay, a relapse, a broken relationship.","How do you handle setbacks?"],
["Bounce forward","not just recovering, but growing stronger from hard times.","After a hard year, learning new coping skills and becoming more compassionate.","How have you grown from something hard?"],
["Support","resilience grows with people around you.","Friends, family, staff, faith, and community help us recover.","Who supports you in hard times?"],
["Self-care","taking care of your body and mind during tough times.","Sleep, eating, taking medicine, coping skills, asking for help.","What self-care helps most when life is hard?"]],
story:"Ms. Doris lost her home in a fire and spent weeks in a shelter. She felt hopeless. She kept going to church, took her medicine, and leaned on a case manager. Slowly, she found a place at Rose of Sharon. She started a prayer group and now helps others who have lost homes. She said, The fire took my house, but it didn't take me.",
sq:["What hardship did Doris face?","What helped her keep going?","How did she bounce forward?","What does it didn't take me mean?"],
act:{n:"My Resilience Story",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Ask each resident to think of a hard time they made it through.","Answer three questions on paper: What happened? What helped me get through? What did I learn or how did I grow?","Invite volunteers to share their stories.","As a group, list common things that helped people: faith, family, staff, routine, humor, hope.","Remind everyone: you've survived 100% of your hardest days."]},
d:["What helped people most?","What did you learn about yourself?","How can your past resilience help with what you face now?"],
disc:[["Is resilience something you're born with or something you build?","Mostly built over time."],["How can setbacks be part of growth?","Teach lessons, build strength."],["What hurts resilience?","Isolation, substance use, hopelessness."],["How can we build resilience together here?","Support, encouragement, routine, laughter."],["What is your source of strength?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Resilience is built, not born. Lean on support, take care of yourself, and remember all the hard days you have already survived.",
tryit:"Write down one hard time you overcame and keep it as a reminder of your strength."},

{t:"Starting Over",
goal:"Today we will talk about new beginnings and how it is never too late to start again.",
warm:["Have you ever had a fresh start? What was it like?","What would you do differently if you could start over in one area of life?","What does a new beginning mean to you?"],
terms:[
["Fresh start","a chance to begin again.","Moving to a new home, starting treatment, a new year, or even a new day.","Is every day a fresh start?"],
["Letting go of the past","accepting what happened without letting it control your future.","Not letting past mistakes define who you are today.","What could you let go of?"],
["Second chances","opportunities to try again.","A family member gives you another chance, or you give yourself one.","Who gave you a second chance?"],
["One day at a time","focusing on today instead of the whole future.","I don't have to fix my whole life today. I just have to do today well.","How does one day at a time help?"],
["New identity","seeing yourself in a new way.","Instead of I'm an addict or I'm a failure, saying I'm a person in recovery or I'm a work in progress.","How do you see yourself now?"]],
story:"Mr. Leon spent many years in and out of hospitals and jail. At 58, he came to Rose of Sharon and thought it was too late to change. He decided to focus on one day at a time: take medicine, go to group, help in the kitchen. A year later, he had reconnected with his son, earned trust from staff, and was leading a group. He said, I used to say it was too late. Now I say it's never too late.",
sq:["Why did Leon think it was too late?","What did he focus on?","What changed in a year?","What does his story teach?"],
act:{n:"Letter to My Future Self",m:"Paper, envelopes, pens",s:["Ask each resident to write a short letter to themselves one year from now.","Include: what you're starting today, what you hope will be different, and an encouraging message.","Seal it in an envelope and write the date to open it.","Offer for staff to store the letters safely if residents want.","Invite volunteers to share one line."]},
d:["How did it feel to write to your future self?","What are you starting today?","What encouraging message did you write?"],
disc:[["Why do people believe it's too late to change?","Age, past failures, shame."],["What makes a fresh start succeed?","Support, small steps, new habits, patience."],["How can you forgive yourself for the past?","Learn, make amends, focus on today."],["How can we support someone starting over here?","Welcome, encourage, believe in them."],["What fresh start are you excited about?","Let residents share."]],
take:"It is never too late to start over. Let go of the past, take one day at a time, and give yourself a second chance.",
tryit:"Each morning this week, say: Today is a fresh start."},

{t:"Dreaming Big: My Vision Board",
goal:"Today we will imagine our hopes and dreams and create a vision board to keep them in sight.",
warm:["If anything were possible, what would your life look like in 5 years?","What did you dream of being as a child?","What dream do you still have?"],
terms:[
["Vision","a picture of the future you want.","Seeing yourself in your own apartment, healthy, close with family, with a garden.","What is your vision?"],
["Vision board","a poster of pictures and words that show your dreams and goals.","Magazine pictures of a kitchen, a family hug, a garden, and the words peace and health.","Have you ever made one?"],
["Big dreams and small steps","dreams come true through small steps.","Dream: my own place. Steps: save money, learn to cook, get my ID.","What small step leads to your dream?"],
["Daily reminder","seeing your vision every day keeps you motivated.","Hanging your vision board where you see it when you wake up.","Where would you hang it?"],
["Permission to dream","it is OK to want good things for yourself.","You deserve to dream, no matter your past or your diagnosis.","Why might some people feel they can't dream?"]],
story:"Ms. Shirley made a vision board with a picture of a beach, a photo of her grandkids, a cookbook, and the word peace. She hung it by her bed. Every time she saw the beach, she remembered her goal to save for a trip. Two years later, she visited the beach with her daughter. She brought the vision board picture with her and took a photo holding it.",
sq:["What was on Shirley's vision board?","How did it help her?","What happened two years later?","What would you put on your board?"],
act:{n:"Make a Vision Board",m:"Poster board or paper, old magazines, scissors, glue sticks, markers",s:["Hand out paper and magazines.","Ask residents to cut or draw pictures and words that represent their dreams: home, family, health, faith, hobbies, places.","Glue them onto the paper. Add their own words with markers.","Ask each person to add one small step they can take toward their dream.","Share boards with the group and encourage hanging them in rooms."]},
d:["What is on your vision board?","Which dream means the most to you?","What small step did you write?"],
disc:[["Why is it important to dream?","Gives hope and direction."],["What gets in the way of dreaming?","Past disappointments, fear, low self-worth."],["How can dreams change over time?","Life changes; dreams can too."],["How can we support each other's dreams?","Encourage, celebrate steps, believe in each other."],["What dream would you like to share?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You deserve to dream. Picture the future you want, keep it where you can see it, and take small steps toward it.",
tryit:"Hang your vision board where you'll see it every day."},

{t:"Tracking Your Progress",
goal:"Today we will learn simple ways to track progress so we can see how far we have come and stay motivated.",
warm:["How do you know when you're making progress?","Have you ever looked back and realized how much you'd changed?","What do you want to track in your life?"],
terms:[
["Progress","moving forward toward a goal, even slowly.","Walking 5 minutes last month and 10 minutes this month is progress.","What progress have you made lately?"],
["Tracking","writing down or marking what you do.","Checking a box each day you take a walk, or writing your mood each night.","Why is tracking helpful?"],
["Mood tracker","a chart showing how you feel each day.","Coloring a box green for good days, yellow for OK days, and red for hard days.","Would a mood tracker help you?"],
["Before and after","comparing where you started to where you are now.","When I came here, I didn't leave my room. Now I go to group every day.","What is a before and after in your life?"],
["Celebrate progress, not perfection","praising effort and improvement, not just finished goals.","I walked 4 out of 7 days this week. That's great progress!","Why is perfection a trap?"]],
story:"Mr. Vernon felt like he wasn't getting better. His counselor gave him a simple mood tracker to color each day. After two months, they looked at it together. In the first month, most days were red. In the second month, most were yellow and green. Vernon said, I didn't feel it day to day, but the colors don't lie. I'm getting better.",
sq:["Why did Vernon feel he wasn't improving?","What tool did he use?","What did the tracker show?","Why is it hard to see progress day to day?"],
act:{n:"Make a Tracker",m:"Paper with a 30-day grid, colored pencils or markers",s:["Give each resident a 30-day grid.","Choose what to track: mood, a habit, sleep, steps, group attendance, or a goal.","Create a color code or check-mark system.","Fill in today's box together.","Discuss where to keep the tracker and when to fill it in each day."]},
d:["What did you choose to track?","When will you fill it in each day?","What do you hope to see at the end of the month?"],
disc:[["Why do we forget our progress?","We focus on what's still wrong."],["How can tracking help with doctor or counselor visits?","Shows patterns and changes."],["What if your tracker shows a bad week?","It's information, not failure; adjust."],["How can we celebrate each other's progress?","Notice it out loud, cheer."],["What progress are you proud of this year?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Track your progress so you can see it. Celebrate progress, not perfection. Small steps add up.",
tryit:"Fill in your tracker every day this week."},

{t:"Leaving a Legacy",
goal:"Today we will reflect on the legacy we want to leave: the way we want to be remembered and the difference we want to make.",
warm:["Who left a lasting impact on your life?","What lessons did your elders pass down to you?","What would you like people to say about you?"],
terms:[
["Legacy","what you leave behind for others: memories, lessons, kindness, and love.","A grandmother's recipes, a father's lessons on hard work, a friend's kindness that people remember.","Whose legacy has touched you?"],
["Life lessons","wisdom you have gained from experience.","Don't let pride keep you from asking for help. Treat people right.","What is one life lesson you've learned?"],
["Passing it on","sharing your wisdom, stories, and skills with others.","Teaching a younger resident to cook your famous cornbread, or telling your grandkids family stories.","What could you pass on?"],
["Everyday legacy","the way you treat people each day.","Being remembered as the one who always said good morning with a smile.","What everyday legacy are you building?"],
["It's not too late","your legacy is still being written.","No matter your past, your actions today shape how you are remembered.","What do you want to add to your legacy?"]],
story:"Mr. Odell had made many mistakes in his life and worried his family would only remember the bad. He started writing letters to his grandchildren, sharing stories about his childhood and the lessons he learned the hard way. He told them he loved them. After he passed away, his granddaughter read one of the letters at his service. She said, Grandpa taught me that it's never too late to do right.",
sq:["What did Odell worry about?","What did he do?","How did his grandchildren remember him?","What lesson did he leave?"],
act:{n:"My Legacy Letter",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Ask each resident to think about someone they want to pass wisdom to: a child, grandchild, friend, or future residents.","Write a short letter that includes: a life lesson, a favorite memory, and words of love or encouragement.","Allow quiet writing time.","Invite volunteers to read a line.","Offer to help mail or save the letters."]},
d:["Who did you write to?","What life lesson did you share?","How did it feel to write it?"],
disc:[["Why do people think about legacy?","Want their life to matter."],["Can someone with a hard past leave a good legacy?","Yes; change and love are powerful."],["How can our home have a legacy?","How we treat each other, traditions, kindness."],["What lessons do you want young people to know?","Let residents share."],["How do you want to be remembered?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Your legacy is still being written. Share your lessons, show love, and treat people well every day.",
tryit:"Share a life lesson or story with someone this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Nutrition & Fitness", [
{t:"Reading Food Labels",
goal:"Today we will learn how to read a Nutrition Facts label so we can make healthier food choices.",
warm:["Do you ever read the labels on food?","What do you look for on a label, if anything?","What food do you eat almost every day?"],
terms:[
["Nutrition Facts label","the box on packaged food that tells what is in it.","It shows serving size, calories, fat, sodium, sugar, protein, and more.","Where on a package do you find this label?"],
["Serving size","the amount the label is based on.","A bag of chips may say 1 serving = 15 chips, and the bag has 3 servings. If you eat the whole bag, triple everything.","Why does serving size matter?"],
["Calories","the energy in food.","An apple has about 95 calories. A large fast-food burger can have over 700.","Why do calories matter for weight?"],
["Sodium","salt. Too much raises blood pressure.","Look for 5 percent or less of the daily value per serving as low, and 20 percent or more as high.","Which foods do you think are high in sodium?"],
["Added sugar","sugar put into food during processing.","A flavored yogurt might have 15 grams of added sugar, almost 4 teaspoons.","How can you spot added sugar?"]],
story:"Ms. Brenda thought her favorite granola bar was healthy because the box said natural. A nurse showed her the label: 12 grams of added sugar and two servings per bar. Brenda started comparing labels at the store and found a bar with less sugar that she liked just as much. She said, The front of the box tells a story. The back tells the truth.",
sq:["Why did Brenda think the bar was healthy?","What did the label show?","What did she start doing?","What does her quote mean?"],
act:{n:"Label Detective",m:"Several food packages or label pictures",s:["Pass around real food packages or label examples (cereal, chips, soup, yogurt, crackers).","Have residents find: serving size, servings per container, calories, sodium, and added sugar.","Ask: If you ate the whole package, how much would you eat?","Compare two similar products and decide which is healthier.","Write the 5 percent low, 20 percent high rule on the board."]},
d:["What surprised you on a label?","Which product was healthier?","What will you check first next time?"],
disc:[["Why do companies put health words on the front?","To sell; check the label."],["Which numbers matter most for your health?","Depends; ask the nurse about sodium, sugar, or other limits for you."],["How can labels help on a budget?","Compare value and nutrition."],["What foods don't have labels?","Fresh fruits and vegetables, often the healthiest."],["What label will you check this week?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Check the serving size first, then look at calories, sodium, and added sugar. 5 percent is low, 20 percent is high.",
tryit:"Read the label on one food you eat often this week."},

{t:"Building a Healthy Plate",
goal:"Today we will learn how to build a balanced meal using the plate method.",
warm:["What did you eat for lunch today?","What does a healthy meal look like to you?","What food could you never give up?"],
terms:[
["Plate method","a simple way to build a balanced meal by dividing your plate.","Half the plate vegetables and fruit, one quarter protein, one quarter grains or starch.","What would your plate look like right now?"],
["Vegetables and fruit","plant foods full of vitamins, fiber, and water.","Green beans, salad, carrots, broccoli, apples, berries, bananas.","What vegetables do you enjoy?"],
["Protein","food that builds and repairs muscles.","Chicken, fish, eggs, beans, peanut butter, turkey, tofu.","What protein do you eat most?"],
["Grains and starches","foods that give energy.","Rice, bread, pasta, potatoes, corn, tortillas. Whole grains like brown rice and whole wheat bread have more fiber.","What grain or starch do you eat most?"],
["Balance","having a mix of foods instead of too much of one.","A plate of only fried chicken and mac and cheese is heavy on protein and starch with no vegetables.","How could you balance that plate?"]],
story:"Mr. Joe's dinner plate was usually piled with fried chicken, mashed potatoes, and rolls. He felt sluggish and his blood sugar was high. The nurse showed him the plate method. He started filling half his plate with green beans and salad first. He still ate chicken and potatoes, just less. He said he felt lighter and his numbers improved.",
sq:["What was on Joe's plate before?","What did the nurse teach him?","Did he give up his favorite foods?","How did he feel after?"],
act:{n:"Build a Plate",m:"Paper plates or drawn circles, markers, food pictures",s:["Give each resident a paper plate or circle.","Draw a line down the middle, then divide one half into two quarters.","Label: half Vegetables/Fruit, one quarter Protein, one quarter Grain/Starch.","Have residents draw or write foods they like in each section.","Share plates and discuss how to use the plate method at facility meals."]},
d:["What did you put on your plate?","Which section was hardest to fill?","How can you use this at dinner tonight?"],
disc:[["Why are vegetables often missing from plates?","Taste, habit, cost, not served."],["How can you add vegetables you like?","Season them, add to soups, raw with dip."],["Does a healthy plate mean no treats?","No; balance and portions."],["How can you use the plate method when you don't choose the menu?","Take more vegetables, smaller starch portion."],["What healthy meal do you love?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Half vegetables and fruit, a quarter protein, a quarter grains. Balance, not perfection.",
tryit:"Use the plate method at one meal every day this week."},

{t:"Healthy Snacks",
goal:"Today we will learn how to choose snacks that give energy and keep us full, without too much sugar, salt, or cost.",
warm:["What is your favorite snack?","When do you usually snack?","Do you snack because you're hungry or for other reasons?"],
terms:[
["Snack","food eaten between meals.","A piece of fruit at 3 p.m. or crackers before bed.","When do you snack most?"],
["Empty calories","foods with lots of calories but few nutrients.","Candy, chips, soda, and pastries fill you up briefly but don't nourish your body.","What empty-calorie snacks do you eat?"],
["Protein and fiber snacks","snacks that keep you full longer.","Peanut butter on apple slices, cheese and crackers, a hard-boiled egg, nuts, popcorn.","Which of these sound good?"],
["Portion control for snacks","eating a set amount instead of from the bag.","Putting a handful of chips in a bowl instead of eating straight from a family-size bag.","Why eat from a bowl instead of the bag?"],
["Planned snacks","deciding your snack ahead of time.","Packing a banana and peanut butter for the afternoon instead of buying from a vending machine.","Would planning snacks help you?"]],
story:"Ms. Tina ate two bags of chips and a candy bar every afternoon from the vending machine. She spent over $60 a month and felt tired and hungry again an hour later. She started keeping bananas, peanut butter, and popcorn in her room. She spent much less and said she didn't crash in the afternoons anymore.",
sq:["What did Tina snack on before?","How did it affect her money and energy?","What did she switch to?","What changed?"],
act:{n:"Snack Swap",m:"Board",s:["Write common snacks on the left: chips, candy bar, soda, cookies, pastry, ice cream.","Have the group suggest healthier swaps on the right: popcorn, fruit, water with lemon, graham crackers, yogurt, frozen banana.","Compare prices where possible.","Have each resident pick one swap to try this week.","If possible, offer a healthy snack sample (check diets and allergies)."]},
d:["Which swap sounds easiest?","Which one will you try?","Were healthy snacks cheaper or more expensive?"],
disc:[["Why do we crave salty or sweet snacks?","Taste, habit, stress, boredom."],["How can you tell if you are truly hungry?","Stomach growling, been hours since a meal; not just bored."],["How do snacks affect blood sugar and mood?","Sugary snacks spike and crash."],["What snacks fit a diabetic or low-salt diet?","Ask the nurse; generally fruit, nuts, veggies."],["What healthy snack would you like the facility to offer?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Choose snacks with protein or fiber, portion them into a bowl, and plan ahead to avoid the vending machine.",
tryit:"Try one healthy snack swap every day this week."},

{t:"Eating More Fruits and Vegetables",
goal:"Today we will learn why fruits and vegetables matter and easy, affordable ways to eat more of them.",
warm:["What fruit or vegetable did you eat as a kid?","What vegetable do you dislike?","What is your favorite fruit?"],
terms:[
["Vitamins and minerals","nutrients that keep the body working well.","Oranges have vitamin C for healing. Bananas have potassium for muscles. Leafy greens have iron.","Which fruits and vegetables help you most?"],
["Fiber","the part of plants that helps digestion and keeps you full.","Apples, beans, broccoli, and berries are high in fiber.","How does fiber help the body?"],
["Eat the rainbow","choosing different colors of produce.","Red tomatoes, orange carrots, yellow corn, green spinach, blue berries, purple grapes. Each color has different nutrients.","What colors did you eat today?"],
["Frozen and canned","affordable options that are still healthy.","Frozen vegetables are picked at peak freshness. Choose canned fruit in juice, not syrup, and low-sodium canned vegetables.","Do you think frozen vegetables are as healthy as fresh?"],
["Sneaking them in","adding produce to foods you already eat.","Adding spinach to spaghetti sauce, bananas to oatmeal, or frozen veggies to ramen.","How could you sneak in more vegetables?"]],
story:"Mr. Carl said he hated vegetables. In group, he tried roasted sweet potatoes with cinnamon and loved them. Then he tried green beans cooked with a little onion and garlic. He realized he hated the mushy canned vegetables he grew up with, not all vegetables. He said, I just hadn't met the right ones yet.",
sq:["Why did Carl think he hated vegetables?","What changed his mind?","What did he realize?","What vegetable deserves a second chance?"],
act:{n:"Rainbow Challenge",m:"Paper, colored pencils, or a chart with colors",s:["Draw a rainbow with 6 colors: red, orange, yellow, green, blue/purple, white/tan.","Have residents list fruits and vegetables for each color.","Mark which ones they eat now and circle one new one to try.","Discuss affordable ways to buy each (frozen, canned, in season).","Make a goal: eat 3 colors per day."]},
d:["Which color is missing from your diet?","What new food will you try?","How could the facility help with more produce?"],
disc:[["Why don't many people eat enough fruits and vegetables?","Cost, taste, habit, spoilage."],["How can you make vegetables taste better?","Seasoning, roasting, dips, soups."],["Are fruit juices as good as whole fruit?","Whole fruit has more fiber and less sugar."],["How can you buy produce on a budget?","Frozen, canned in juice, in season, farmers markets with SNAP."],["What fruit or vegetable dish reminds you of home?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Eat the rainbow. Fresh, frozen, and canned all count. Sneak produce into foods you love and give vegetables a second chance.",
tryit:"Eat at least 3 colors of fruits and vegetables each day this week."},

{t:"Fiber and Healthy Digestion",
goal:"Today we will learn how fiber, water, and movement help digestion and prevent constipation.",
warm:["What foods make you feel good after eating?","What foods upset your stomach?","How does your stomach feel when you're stressed?"],
terms:[
["Digestion","how the body breaks down food and gets rid of waste.","Food moves from the mouth to the stomach to the intestines, and waste leaves the body as stool.","Why is healthy digestion important?"],
["Constipation","having hard stools or going fewer times than usual.","Many medicines, low water, low fiber, and sitting a lot can cause constipation.","What helps when you're constipated?"],
["Fiber","plant material that helps stools move.","Oatmeal, beans, whole wheat bread, fruits, vegetables, and nuts. Add fiber slowly to avoid gas.","What high-fiber foods do you like?"],
["Water and digestion","water helps fiber work.","Fiber without enough water can make constipation worse. Drink water with high-fiber foods.","How much water do you drink?"],
["Tell the nurse","digestion problems can be a sign of a medication side effect or health issue.","Going more than three days without a bowel movement, blood in stool, or bad stomach pain should be reported.","Why might someone be embarrassed to report this?"]],
story:"Mr. Glen was often constipated and uncomfortable, but he was too embarrassed to say anything. He finally told the nurse, who explained that one of his medicines can cause it. She suggested more water, oatmeal for breakfast, prunes, and a daily walk, and she talked to his doctor. Within two weeks, he felt much better. He said, I wish I hadn't been too shy to ask.",
sq:["Why didn't Glen say anything?","What was causing his problem?","What helped?","Why is it OK to talk to the nurse about this?"],
act:{n:"Fiber Finder",m:"Board, food pictures or a list",s:["List foods on the board and have the group guess HIGH or LOW fiber: white bread, oatmeal, apple with skin, chips, black beans, cheese, broccoli, white rice, popcorn, prunes.","Reveal answers: high fiber includes oatmeal, apple with skin, beans, broccoli, popcorn, prunes.","Discuss easy swaps: whole wheat for white bread, brown rice for white rice.","Remind everyone: add fiber slowly and drink more water.","Do a gentle movement: a 2-minute seated twist and march to show how movement helps digestion."]},
d:["Which high-fiber food surprised you?","What swap could you make?","Why is water important with fiber?"],
disc:[["Why do medicines cause constipation?","They slow the gut."],["How does movement help digestion?","Gets the intestines moving."],["How does stress affect the stomach?","Upset stomach, diarrhea, constipation."],["When should digestive problems be reported?","Pain, blood, many days without going, weight loss."],["What foods make your stomach happy?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Fiber, water, and movement keep digestion healthy. Add fiber slowly, drink water, and tell the nurse about problems without embarrassment.",
tryit:"Add one high-fiber food and one extra glass of water each day this week."},

{t:"Protein for Strength",
goal:"Today we will learn why protein matters, especially as we age, and affordable ways to get enough.",
warm:["What protein foods do you like?","Do you feel stronger or weaker than a year ago?","What did you eat for breakfast today?"],
terms:[
["Protein","a nutrient that builds and repairs muscles, skin, and the immune system.","Eggs, chicken, fish, beans, milk, yogurt, cheese, peanut butter, and lentils.","Why do muscles need protein?"],
["Muscle loss","as we age, muscles weaken if we don't eat enough protein and stay active.","Older adults who eat protein at every meal and move daily keep strength longer.","How does muscle loss affect daily life?"],
["Protein at every meal","spreading protein throughout the day.","Eggs at breakfast, a turkey sandwich at lunch, beans with dinner.","Do you eat protein at breakfast?"],
["Plant protein","protein from plants.","Beans, lentils, peanut butter, nuts, and tofu are cheap and healthy proteins.","What plant proteins do you eat?"],
["Budget protein","affordable protein options.","Eggs, canned tuna, dried beans, peanut butter, and chicken thighs are low-cost.","Which budget protein would you try?"]],
story:"Ms. Agnes had been losing strength and had trouble getting out of chairs. The dietitian noticed she mostly ate toast and coffee for breakfast and soup for lunch. Agnes added an egg at breakfast, peanut butter on her toast, and a glass of milk with lunch, and she joined chair exercises. A few months later, she stood up more easily and felt stronger.",
sq:["What was Agnes eating?","What problem was she having?","What did she add?","What else helped her get stronger?"],
act:{n:"Protein Plan",m:"Paper, pens",s:["List protein foods on the board with approximate cost per serving: egg (about 25 cents), peanut butter (about 20 cents), canned tuna (about $1), dried beans (about 15 cents), chicken (about $1), milk (about 30 cents).","Have residents plan a day with protein at breakfast, lunch, and dinner.","Share plans with the group.","Discuss how to ask for protein options at facility meals.","Remind residents with kidney problems to follow the nurse's protein guidance."]},
d:["Which meal is hardest to add protein to?","What budget protein will you try?","How could protein help your strength?"],
disc:[["Why do some older adults not eat enough protein?","Low appetite, cost, trouble chewing."],["What if chewing meat is hard?","Eggs, yogurt, beans, soft fish, ground meat."],["Can you eat too much protein?","Some people with kidney issues need limits; ask the nurse."],["How does protein help healing?","Repairs skin and muscles, supports immunity."],["What is your favorite protein dish?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Eat protein at every meal to keep muscles strong. Eggs, beans, and peanut butter are cheap and easy. Pair protein with movement.",
tryit:"Add a protein food to your breakfast every day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Nutrition & Fitness", [
{t:"Eating Healthy on a Budget",
goal:"Today we will learn how to eat well without spending a lot of money.",
warm:["What is the cheapest meal you know how to make?","Do you think healthy food costs more?","What food do you buy most often?"],
terms:[
["Budget-friendly staples","cheap, healthy foods that last.","Oats, rice, dried beans, eggs, bananas, frozen vegetables, peanut butter, canned tuna.","Which staples do you buy?"],
["Cost per serving","how much one portion costs.","A $2 bag of dried beans makes about 12 servings, about 17 cents each. A $1 bag of chips is 1 or 2 servings.","Why is cost per serving useful?"],
["Cooking from scratch","making food from basic ingredients.","A pot of homemade chili costs less than buying canned chili or fast food.","What could you cook from scratch?"],
["SNAP and food assistance","programs that help pay for food.","SNAP (food stamps) on an EBT card, food banks, church pantries, and senior meal programs.","Who could help you find food assistance?"],
["Seasonal produce","fruits and vegetables that are cheaper when in season.","Watermelon in summer, apples in fall, citrus in winter.","What's in season right now?"]],
story:"Mr. Terrance thought eating healthy was only for rich people. A staff member took him shopping with $20. They bought oatmeal, bananas, eggs, a bag of rice, a bag of beans, frozen broccoli, and peanut butter. It made breakfasts and lunches for over a week. Terrance said, I spent $20 on fast food in two days before. This lasted me a week.",
sq:["What did Terrance believe?","What did they buy?","How long did it last?","What surprised him?"],
act:{n:"$20 Healthy Challenge",m:"Board, paper",s:["Tell the group: You have $20 to buy healthy food for a week of breakfasts and lunches.","List items with prices: oats $3, bananas $2, eggs $3, rice $2, dried beans $2, frozen veggies $2, peanut butter $3, bread $2, apples $3, canned tuna $1.","Have residents build their list and add it up.","Compare lists and discuss which gave the most meals.","Talk about local food pantries and SNAP."]},
d:["What did you buy with $20?","How many meals did it make?","Was it easier than you expected?"],
disc:[["Why do people think healthy food is expensive?","Fresh produce and organic labels look pricey."],["What healthy foods are cheap?","Beans, oats, eggs, bananas, frozen vegetables."],["How can cooking in batches save money?","One pot makes many meals."],["Where can people get free food in Chattanooga?","Food banks, churches, community pantries; ask staff."],["What budget meal do you love?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Healthy eating can be cheap. Buy staples like beans, oats, eggs, and frozen vegetables, cook from scratch, and use food assistance when needed.",
tryit:"Next time you shop, buy at least two budget-friendly healthy staples."},

{t:"Portion Sizes",
goal:"Today we will learn what a healthy portion looks like and simple ways to avoid overeating.",
warm:["Do you usually clean your plate?","Have restaurant portions gotten bigger over the years?","How do you know when you're full?"],
terms:[
["Portion","the amount of food you eat at one time.","A portion of pasta might be one cup, but many restaurant plates have three cups or more.","How big are your usual portions?"],
["Hand guide","using your hand to estimate portions.","Protein: the size of your palm. Grains: your fist. Fats like butter: the tip of your thumb. Vegetables: two open hands.","Can you show the hand guide?"],
["Fullness cues","signals that you have eaten enough.","Feeling satisfied, not stuffed; food tastes less exciting; you could stop.","What does satisfied feel like to you?"],
["Eating slowly","taking time to eat so your brain can catch up.","It takes about 20 minutes for your brain to know you're full. Put your fork down between bites.","How fast do you usually eat?"],
["Plate size","using smaller plates can help with portions.","The same food on a smaller plate looks like more and helps you eat less.","Would a smaller plate help you?"]],
story:"Mr. Harold always went back for seconds and sometimes thirds. He felt stuffed and uncomfortable after dinner and was gaining weight. He tried eating slower, putting his fork down between bites, and waiting 15 minutes before deciding on seconds. Most of the time, he didn't want seconds after waiting. He said, I was eating faster than my stomach could talk to me.",
sq:["What was Harold's habit?","What did he try?","What happened when he waited?","What does his quote mean?"],
act:{n:"Portion Guess",m:"Measuring cups, a plate, rice or cereal (or pictures)",s:["Pour what looks like a normal serving of cereal or rice into a bowl.","Measure it. Compare to the label serving size (often 1 cup or less).","Teach the hand guide: palm for protein, fist for grains, thumb for fats, two hands for veggies.","Have residents practice showing each portion with their hands.","Discuss the 20-minute rule and slowing down."]},
d:["Were your portion guesses bigger or smaller than a serving?","Which hand guide is easiest to remember?","Will you try waiting before seconds?"],
disc:[["Why do people eat past full?","Habit, fast eating, emotions, clean-plate rule."],["How can medicines affect appetite?","Some increase hunger; talk to the nurse."],["Is it OK to leave food on your plate?","Yes, or save it for later."],["How can you handle big restaurant portions?","Share, box half right away."],["What helps you feel satisfied?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Use your hand as a portion guide, eat slowly, wait 15 to 20 minutes before seconds, and stop when satisfied, not stuffed.",
tryit:"Put your fork down between bites at one meal each day this week."},

{t:"Eating Out Healthier",
goal:"Today we will learn how to make healthier choices at fast-food places and restaurants.",
warm:["What is your favorite fast-food place?","What do you usually order?","How often do you eat out?"],
terms:[
["Grilled vs. fried","grilled foods are usually lower in fat and calories than fried.","A grilled chicken sandwich vs. a crispy chicken sandwich can be a difference of 200 calories.","Which do you usually choose?"],
["Combo meals","a main item, side, and drink together.","A combo with large fries and a large soda can be over 1,200 calories.","How could you change a combo?"],
["Smart swaps","healthier choices on the menu.","Water instead of soda, side salad or fruit instead of fries, small instead of large.","What swap would you make?"],
["Sauces and dressings","toppings that add salt, sugar, and calories.","Ask for sauce or dressing on the side and use half.","Why do sauces matter?"],
["Planning ahead","looking at the menu before you go.","Deciding what to order before getting in line so you don't choose on impulse.","Would planning ahead help you?"]],
story:"Ms. Rochelle ate fast food on outings and always ordered a large combo. After learning about smart swaps, she ordered a grilled chicken sandwich, a side salad, and water. She still enjoyed her meal and saved money because water was free. She said, I didn't give up eating out. I just made it work for me.",
sq:["What did Rochelle usually order?","What did she switch to?","Did she give up eating out?","What swap would you make?"],
act:{n:"Menu Makeover",m:"A printed fast-food menu or a list of items with calories",s:["Show a fast-food menu or write typical items with approximate calories: large fries (about 500), small fries (about 230), regular soda large (about 300), water (0), crispy chicken sandwich (about 600), grilled chicken sandwich (about 400), side salad (about 50).","Build a typical combo and add up the calories.","Then build a healthier combo with swaps.","Compare totals and prices.","Have each resident write their go-to healthier order."]},
d:["How big was the difference?","What will your new order be?","Which swap is easiest for you?"],
disc:[["Why is fast food so tempting?","Cheap, fast, tasty, everywhere."],["How does fast food affect health over time?","Weight, blood pressure, blood sugar."],["Can you eat out and still eat healthy?","Yes, with smart choices."],["How can you handle peer pressure to supersize?","Know your order, say no thanks."],["What restaurant meal is worth a treat?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Choose grilled over fried, swap soda for water, pick small sizes, get sauce on the side, and plan your order ahead.",
tryit:"Make at least one smart swap the next time you eat out."},

{t:"Walking for Health",
goal:"Today we will learn the benefits of walking and how to build a safe walking habit.",
warm:["Where do you like to walk?","How far can you comfortably walk right now?","How do you feel after a walk?"],
terms:[
["Walking","one of the easiest and safest forms of exercise.","A 10-minute walk after meals can help blood sugar, digestion, and mood.","What do you like about walking?"],
["Pace","how fast you walk.","A good pace is one where you can talk but not sing.","How fast do you usually walk?"],
["Build up slowly","starting small and adding a little each week.","Week 1: 5 minutes. Week 2: 7 minutes. Week 3: 10 minutes.","Why is slow progress safer?"],
["Walking safety","staying safe while walking.","Good shoes, water, sunscreen, a buddy, well-lit paths, use your cane or walker, and stop if you feel chest pain or dizziness.","What safety steps matter most for you?"],
["Walking buddy","someone to walk with.","Walking with a friend makes it more fun and safer, and you are more likely to stick with it.","Who could be your walking buddy?"]],
story:"Ms. Clara could only walk to the end of the hallway when she arrived. She started walking the hallway twice a day with a caregiver. Each week, she added one more lap. After three months, she could walk around the whole building and out to the mailbox. She said, Every lap was a win.",
sq:["How far could Clara walk at first?","How did she build up?","What happened after three months?","What does every lap was a win mean?"],
act:{n:"Group Walk",m:"Safe walking space, chairs along the way for rest",s:["If safe, take the group on a short walk around the hallway or outside (5 to 10 minutes). Anyone can sit and rest at any time.","Use the talk test: we should be able to talk but not sing.","Back in the group, ask each person to rate how they feel from 1 to 10 before and after.","Help each resident set a walking goal: minutes per day and days per week.","Pair up walking buddies."]},
d:["How did you feel before and after?","What is your walking goal?","Who is your walking buddy?"],
disc:[["What are the benefits of walking?","Heart, mood, sleep, blood sugar, strength, balance."],["What gets in the way of walking?","Pain, weather, fatigue, fear of falling."],["What can you do on bad weather days?","Walk hallways, march in place, chair exercises."],["When should you stop walking and get help?","Chest pain, trouble breathing, dizziness."],["Where would you love to walk someday?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Walking is simple, free medicine. Start small, build slowly, stay safe, and bring a buddy.",
tryit:"Walk for your goal number of minutes on at least 4 days this week."},

{t:"Stretching and Flexibility",
goal:"Today we will learn simple, safe stretches that help us move more easily and feel less stiff.",
warm:["Where do you feel stiff in the morning?","Do you ever stretch? When?","What movements are hard for you now?"],
terms:[
["Flexibility","how easily your muscles and joints move.","Being able to reach a high shelf, turn your head to check traffic, or bend to tie your shoes.","Which movement do you want to make easier?"],
["Stretch","gently lengthening a muscle.","Reaching your arms overhead or rolling your shoulders.","How does a good stretch feel?"],
["Hold, don't bounce","stretching slowly and holding for 15 to 30 seconds.","Bouncing can strain muscles. Slow and steady is safer.","Why is bouncing risky?"],
["Gentle, not painful","stretching should feel like a mild pull, never sharp pain.","If it hurts, ease off. Stretching is not a contest.","How can you tell the difference between stretch and pain?"],
["Breathing while stretching","breathing slowly helps muscles relax.","Breathe in before the stretch, breathe out as you reach.","Do you hold your breath when you move?"]],
story:"Mr. Lonnie's back and neck were so stiff he could barely turn his head. A physical therapist taught him a few gentle seated stretches to do every morning. He did them while watching the news. After a month, he could look over his shoulder again and said his back pain went from a 6 to a 3.",
sq:["What problems did Lonnie have?","When did he do his stretches?","What changed after a month?","When could you fit in stretches?"],
act:{n:"Seated Stretch Routine",m:"Sturdy chairs without wheels",s:["Remind everyone: go slowly, no pain, follow any doctor's limits.","Neck: slowly tilt ear toward shoulder, hold 15 seconds, switch. Then slowly turn to look over each shoulder.","Shoulders: roll forward 10 times, backward 10 times. Shrug up and release 5 times.","Arms: reach both arms overhead and hold 15 seconds. Hug yourself and hold 15 seconds.","Back: sit tall, gently twist to each side, hold 15 seconds.","Legs: straighten one leg, point and flex foot 10 times, switch. Ankle circles."]},
d:["Which stretch felt best?","Did anything feel uncomfortable?","When could you do these each day?"],
disc:[["How can stretching help with pain and stiffness?","Loosens muscles, improves movement."],["Who should check with the doctor before stretching?","People with recent surgery, injuries, or certain conditions."],["How can stretching help stress?","Releases tension, calms breathing."],["How can stretching prevent falls?","Better range of motion and balance."],["What stretch will you do every day?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Stretch gently every day, hold without bouncing, breathe, and never push into pain.",
tryit:"Do the seated stretch routine every morning this week."},

{t:"Staying Strong at Any Age",
goal:"Today we will learn why muscle strength matters and simple strength exercises we can do safely.",
warm:["What daily tasks need strength?","Have you noticed getting weaker over time?","What would you like to be stronger for?"],
terms:[
["Strength training","exercises that make muscles work against resistance.","Standing up from a chair, lifting soup cans, or pushing against a wall.","What everyday movements use strength?"],
["Resistance","something muscles push or pull against.","Your own body weight, water bottles, soup cans, or resistance bands.","What could you use as weights at home?"],
["Repetitions (reps)","how many times you do an exercise.","10 sit-to-stands is 10 reps.","How many reps feels right for you?"],
["Rest days","giving muscles time to recover.","Do strength exercises 2 or 3 times a week, with a day of rest in between.","Why do muscles need rest?"],
["Functional strength","strength for real-life tasks.","Carrying groceries, climbing stairs, getting out of bed, lifting a laundry basket.","What task do you want to do more easily?"]],
story:"Ms. Lucille needed help getting out of chairs and off the toilet. She felt embarrassed and dependent. Staff helped her do sit-to-stand exercises three times a week. At first she could do 3. After two months, she could do 12. She started getting up on her own. She said, I got my dignity back one stand at a time.",
sq:["What could Lucille not do on her own?","What exercise did she do?","How did she progress?","What does dignity mean to you?"],
act:{n:"Strength Circuit",m:"Sturdy chairs, water bottles or soup cans",s:["Remind everyone: go at your own pace, stop if anything hurts, follow doctor limits.","Sit-to-stand: 5 to 10 reps.","Wall push-ups (or seated chest presses with hands pushing together): 5 to 10 reps.","Bicep curls with water bottles or soup cans: 10 reps each arm.","Seated leg lifts: straighten one leg and hold 3 seconds, 5 each leg.","Heel raises holding a chair: 10 reps. Rest between each exercise."]},
d:["Which exercise was hardest?","How many sit-to-stands could you do?","Which one will you practice?"],
disc:[["Why do we lose strength as we age?","Less activity, less protein, aging muscles."],["How does strength protect independence?","Getting up, walking, carrying, avoiding falls."],["Can people start strength training late in life?","Yes; muscles respond at any age."],["What gets in the way?","Pain, fear, not knowing how."],["What task do you want to be strong enough for?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Strong muscles keep us independent. Use your body weight and household items, start small, and do strength exercises 2 to 3 times a week.",
tryit:"Do sit-to-stands on three different days this week and count your reps."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Nutrition & Fitness", [
{t:"Breakfast: Starting the Day Right",
goal:"Today we will learn why breakfast matters and easy, healthy breakfast ideas.",
warm:["What did you eat for breakfast today?","Do you usually eat breakfast or skip it?","What was breakfast like in your home growing up?"],
terms:[
["Breakfast","the first meal of the day, which breaks the overnight fast.","Even a small breakfast gives your brain and body fuel to start the day.","Why is it called break-fast?"],
["Steady energy","feeling energized without crashing.","Oatmeal with peanut butter keeps energy steady. A sugary pastry gives a quick burst, then a crash.","How do you feel mid-morning after different breakfasts?"],
["Breakfast and medicine","many medicines are taken with food.","Taking morning medicine on an empty stomach can cause nausea for some people. Check with the nurse.","Do any of your medicines need food?"],
["Quick breakfasts","easy options for busy or low-energy mornings.","A banana and a cheese stick, yogurt with granola, a boiled egg and toast, peanut butter on whole wheat bread.","Which quick breakfast sounds good?"],
["Sugary cereals and pastries","breakfasts that are high in sugar and low in staying power.","A frosted cereal can have 12 grams of sugar per small serving.","What breakfast swap could you make?"]],
story:"Mr. Jamal skipped breakfast and drank two energy drinks every morning. By 11, he was shaky, irritable, and starving, and he'd eat chips from the vending machine. He started eating oatmeal with a banana and peanut butter and switched to one cup of coffee. He said his mornings felt calmer and he wasn't snapping at people before lunch anymore.",
sq:["What did Jamal have for breakfast before?","How did he feel by 11?","What did he change?","How did it affect his mood?"],
act:{n:"Build-a-Breakfast",m:"Board with three columns: Protein, Fiber/Grain, Fruit",s:["List options: Protein (eggs, peanut butter, yogurt, cheese, milk), Fiber/Grain (oatmeal, whole wheat toast, bran cereal), Fruit (banana, berries, apple, orange).","Have each resident build a breakfast with one item from each column.","Share combos out loud.","Discuss which work for low-energy mornings.","Ask staff about breakfast options at the facility."]},
d:["What breakfast did you build?","Which is easiest for busy mornings?","Will you try it this week?"],
disc:[["Why do people skip breakfast?","Not hungry, no time, nausea, habit."],["How does breakfast affect mood and focus?","Steadier mood, better concentration."],["What if you're not hungry in the morning?","Start small: a piece of fruit or glass of milk."],["How can breakfast help blood sugar?","Prevents big drops and spikes."],["What is your favorite breakfast?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A breakfast with protein, fiber, and fruit gives steady energy and a calmer morning. Even something small is better than nothing.",
tryit:"Eat breakfast with a protein food every day this week."},

{t:"Emotional Eating",
goal:"Today we will learn the difference between physical and emotional hunger and healthier ways to cope with feelings.",
warm:["What foods do you reach for when you're stressed?","Do you eat more or less when you're upset?","What is your favorite comfort food?"],
terms:[
["Physical hunger","the body's need for food.","Comes on slowly, any food sounds good, stomach growls, and you feel satisfied after eating.","How do you know you're physically hungry?"],
["Emotional hunger","wanting food to soothe feelings.","Comes on suddenly, craves specific comfort foods like chips or ice cream, and often leads to guilt.","How is emotional hunger different?"],
["Comfort food","foods we connect with good feelings.","Mac and cheese, fried chicken, sweet tea, or grandma's pound cake.","Is it OK to enjoy comfort food sometimes?"],
["Hunger check","pausing to ask if you're really hungry.","Before eating, ask: When did I last eat? Is my stomach growling? Or am I bored, sad, or stressed?","Would a hunger check help you?"],
["Other comfort","non-food ways to soothe feelings.","Calling a friend, a warm shower, a walk, music, prayer, journaling, a nap.","What non-food comfort works for you?"]],
story:"Every night after a hard phone call with her sister, Ms. Doreen ate a whole sleeve of cookies. She felt worse afterward. In group, she learned the hunger check. Next time, she paused and realized she wasn't hungry, she was hurt. She called a friend instead and had one cookie with tea. She said, I still love cookies. I just don't let them be my therapist anymore.",
sq:["What triggered Doreen's eating?","How did she feel afterward?","What did she do differently?","What does her last sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"Hungry or Feeling?",m:"Board with two columns",s:["Read situations and have the group decide: physical hunger or emotional hunger?","It's been 5 hours since lunch and your stomach is growling. (Physical.)","You just got off a stressful call and want ice cream. (Emotional.)","You're bored watching TV and want chips. (Emotional.)","You skipped breakfast and feel weak. (Physical.)","Brainstorm non-food comforts for each emotional situation.","Have each resident write their top two non-food comforts."]},
d:["Which feelings lead you to eat?","What non-food comforts did you choose?","How could a hunger check help you?"],
disc:[["Why does food comfort us?","Brain chemicals, memories, habit."],["Is emotional eating always bad?","Not always; it becomes a problem when it's the only coping tool."],["How can medicines affect hunger?","Some increase appetite; tell the nurse."],["How can we be kind to ourselves about eating?","No shame; learn and adjust."],["What comfort food memory do you have?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Check if you're hungry or feeling something. Enjoy comfort food sometimes, but have non-food ways to soothe your feelings too.",
tryit:"Do a hunger check before every snack this week."},

{t:"Food and Mood",
goal:"Today we will learn how what we eat and drink can affect our mood, energy, and mental health.",
warm:["What foods make you feel good?","What foods make you feel sluggish?","Have you noticed your mood changing when you're hungry?"],
terms:[
["Food and mood connection","what we eat affects brain chemicals and energy.","Skipping meals can make people irritable. Steady meals help steady moods.","Have you ever been hangry?"],
["Blood sugar swings","rapid ups and downs from sugary foods and drinks.","A soda gives a quick boost, then a crash with tiredness and crankiness.","How do you feel an hour after a sugary drink?"],
["Caffeine and anxiety","too much caffeine can cause jitters and worsen anxiety.","Three energy drinks a day might make someone shaky, anxious, and unable to sleep.","How much caffeine do you have each day?"],
["Brain foods","foods linked to better brain health.","Fish, nuts, leafy greens, berries, beans, and whole grains.","Which brain foods do you eat?"],
["Regular meals","eating at steady times through the day.","Breakfast, lunch, and dinner with a snack if needed, instead of skipping and binging.","Do you eat at regular times?"]],
story:"Mr. Kevin often felt anxious and jittery in the afternoons. His counselor asked what he ate and drank. He was skipping lunch and drinking three energy drinks. He cut back to one coffee in the morning and ate lunch every day. Within two weeks, his afternoon anxiety was much less. He said, I didn't realize my drinks were part of my anxiety.",
sq:["What symptoms did Kevin have?","What was he eating and drinking?","What changed?","What could your food be telling you about your mood?"],
act:{n:"Mood and Food Log",m:"Paper with a simple chart: Time, What I Ate/Drank, How I Felt",s:["Explain a simple food and mood log.","Fill in today's meals and snacks so far as a practice, with how each person felt afterward.","Ask volunteers to share any patterns they notice.","Discuss swaps that could help: water instead of soda, adding protein, regular lunch.","Encourage residents to keep the log for 3 days."]},
d:["Did you notice any patterns already?","What swap might help your mood?","Will you keep the log for 3 days?"],
disc:[["Why might people with depression eat more or less?","Changes in appetite, energy, comfort-seeking."],["How does alcohol affect mood?","It's a depressant; worsens mood and sleep."],["What role does water play in mood?","Dehydration causes tiredness and irritability."],["Should food replace medicine for mental health?","No; it supports, but medicine and treatment matter."],["What food makes you happiest?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Steady meals, less sugar and caffeine, more water and whole foods can help steady your mood and energy.",
tryit:"Keep a simple food and mood log for 3 days this week."},

{t:"Medications, Appetite, and Weight",
goal:"Today we will talk about how some medications can affect appetite and weight, and healthy ways to manage these changes with our care team.",
warm:["Have you noticed changes in your appetite over time?","How do you feel about your weight right now?","Who do you talk to about health changes?"],
terms:[
["Side effect on appetite","some medications can increase or decrease hunger.","Some mental health medicines make people feel much hungrier, especially for sweets and carbs.","Have you noticed an appetite change after a new medicine?"],
["Weight change","gaining or losing weight.","Gaining 15 pounds in a few months after starting a new medicine is something to tell the doctor about.","Why is it important to report weight changes?"],
["Don't stop your medicine","never stop a medicine because of weight gain without talking to your doctor.","The doctor may adjust the dose, switch medicines, or add support.","What could happen if someone stops suddenly?"],
["Healthy habits help","eating well and moving can reduce medication-related weight gain.","Swapping soda for water, eating more vegetables, and daily walks.","Which habit could you add?"],
["Body respect","treating your body with kindness, whatever its size.","Speaking kindly about your body and focusing on health, not just numbers.","How can we speak kindly about our bodies?"]],
story:"Ms. Angela gained 20 pounds after starting a new medicine and felt ashamed. She thought about stopping it. Instead, she told her nurse. The nurse talked to her doctor, who adjusted her medicine. Angela also started walking after lunch and swapped her sodas for flavored water. Her weight leveled off, and her mood stayed stable. She said, I'm glad I spoke up instead of quitting.",
sq:["What happened to Angela after a new medicine?","What did she almost do?","What did she do instead?","Why was speaking up better?"],
act:{n:"Healthy Habits Menu",m:"Board",s:["Brainstorm small habits that help with appetite and weight: water before meals, half plate vegetables, walk after meals, smaller plates, healthy snacks ready, regular sleep.","Have each resident choose two habits to try.","Practice what to say to the nurse: Since starting my new medicine, I've noticed ___. Can we talk about it?","Remind everyone that weight is only one part of health.","Encourage kind self-talk about bodies."]},
d:["Which habits did you choose?","How would you bring up a concern with the nurse?","How can we be kinder to our bodies?"],
disc:[["Why is weight gain from medicine so frustrating?","Feels unfair, affects self-esteem."],["Why is it important not to stop medicine on your own?","Symptoms can return."],["How does weight affect health?","Blood pressure, diabetes, joints; but health is more than weight."],["How can we avoid making negative comments about others' bodies?","Focus on kindness."],["What helps you feel good in your body?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Medicines can affect appetite and weight. Tell your nurse about changes, never stop medicine on your own, add small healthy habits, and treat your body with respect.",
tryit:"Try your two healthy habits every day this week."},

{t:"Healthy Cooking Swaps",
goal:"Today we will learn simple swaps to make favorite foods healthier without losing flavor.",
warm:["What is your favorite home-cooked meal?","Who taught you to cook?","What makes food taste good to you?"],
terms:[
["Cooking swap","replacing an ingredient with a healthier one.","Using olive oil instead of lard, or baked instead of fried.","What swap have you tried?"],
["Baking, grilling, roasting","cooking methods with less fat than frying.","Oven-baked chicken with a crispy coating can taste great without deep frying.","Have you tried oven-fried chicken?"],
["Seasoning instead of salt","using herbs and spices for flavor.","Garlic, onion, pepper, paprika, lemon, cumin, and herbs add flavor without the salt.","What seasonings do you love?"],
["Smoked turkey instead of pork","a Southern swap for seasoning greens and beans.","Smoked turkey wings or necks give that smoky taste with less fat than ham hocks.","Would you try this swap?"],
["Whole grains","using brown rice or whole wheat pasta instead of white.","Whole grains have more fiber and keep you full longer.","Have you tried brown rice?"]],
story:"Ms. Hattie made collard greens with ham hocks and lots of salt every Sunday. Her doctor said her blood pressure was too high. She was upset because greens were her tradition. Her granddaughter helped her try smoked turkey, onion, garlic, red pepper flakes, and a splash of vinegar. The family said they tasted just as good. Hattie said, I kept my tradition and my health.",
sq:["What was Hattie's tradition?","Why did she need to change it?","What swaps did she make?","How did the family react?"],
act:{n:"Swap It Out",m:"Board",s:["Write classic dishes on the board: fried chicken, mac and cheese, collard greens, sweet tea, mashed potatoes, spaghetti.","For each, brainstorm swaps as a group: oven-fried chicken; mac and cheese with whole wheat pasta and added broccoli; greens with smoked turkey; half-sweet tea; mashed potatoes with skins and less butter; spaghetti with extra vegetables in the sauce.","Discuss which swaps keep the flavor.","Have each resident pick one swap to suggest for the facility menu or their future cooking.","Share a family recipe memory if time allows."]},
d:["Which swap sounds tastiest?","What dish would you never change?","What swap will you suggest?"],
disc:[["Why are traditional foods so important?","Culture, memories, family."],["Can healthy food taste good?","Yes, with seasoning and good cooking."],["What makes people resist healthy changes?","Taste, tradition, habit."],["How can small swaps add up over time?","Less salt, fat, and sugar each day."],["What family recipe do you treasure?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You don't have to give up your favorite foods. Bake instead of fry, season instead of salt, and make small swaps that keep the flavor.",
tryit:"Suggest one healthy swap to staff for the facility menu this week."},

{t:"Moving to Music",
goal:"Today we will enjoy moving our bodies to music and learn how dance and rhythm boost mood and fitness.",
warm:["What song always makes you want to move?","Did you like to dance when you were younger?","What music did you grow up with?"],
terms:[
["Rhythm","the beat of music.","Clapping or tapping your foot to the beat of a song.","Can you clap a rhythm?"],
["Aerobic exercise","movement that gets your heart pumping.","Dancing, marching, and brisk walking are aerobic.","How does dancing feel compared to regular exercise?"],
["Chair dancing","dancing while seated.","Moving arms, shoulders, and feet to music while sitting in a chair.","Is chair dancing real exercise?"],
["Mood boost","music and movement release feel-good chemicals.","Even 5 minutes of dancing can lift a low mood.","How do you feel after dancing?"],
["No wrong moves","there's no right or wrong way to move to music.","Move however feels good to you. It's about fun, not looking perfect.","What keeps people from dancing?"]],
story:"At a facility music hour, Mr. Ellis sat with his arms crossed. When the staff played a Temptations song, he started tapping his foot. Soon, he was swaying his shoulders and singing along. By the end, he was doing chair dance moves and laughing. He said, That song took me right back to 1968. I forgot how good it feels to move.",
sq:["How did Ellis act at first?","What changed?","What did the song remind him of?","What song might get you moving?"],
act:{n:"Chair Dance Party",m:"Music player with upbeat songs residents know (oldies, Motown, gospel, country)",s:["Remind everyone to move at their own pace and stay seated if needed.","Play a warm-up song: tap toes and clap hands.","Play an upbeat song: march feet, roll shoulders, reach arms up and out.","Let residents call out moves for the group to copy.","Cool down with a slow song: gentle arm sways and deep breaths."]},
d:["How do you feel now compared to before?","What song got you moving most?","Would you like regular dance time here?"],
disc:[["Why does music make us want to move?","Rhythm connects to the brain and body."],["How can music help with memory?","Songs bring back memories and feelings."],["What gets in the way of dancing?","Embarrassment, pain, fear of falling."],["How can we make movement fun here?","Music, games, groups."],["What is your all-time favorite song?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Moving to music is exercise and joy at the same time. There are no wrong moves. Turn on a song and move.",
tryit:"Dance or move to one favorite song every day this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Rights, Resources & Advocacy", [
{t:"Knowing Your Resident Rights",
goal:"Today we will learn about the basic rights residents have in an assisted living facility and how to use them respectfully.",
warm:["What does the word rights mean to you?","What right is most important to you?","Have you ever felt your rights were not respected?"],
terms:[
["Resident rights","the protections every resident has under the law and facility policy.","The right to be treated with dignity, to privacy, to make choices, to voice complaints, and to be free from abuse.","Which of these rights matters most to you?"],
["Dignity","being treated with respect as a person.","Staff knocking before entering, speaking to you as an adult, and protecting your privacy during personal care.","How do you know you're being treated with dignity?"],
["Choice","the right to make decisions about your own life within safety rules.","Choosing your clothes, your activities, when to visit with family, and whether to join a group.","What choices do you make each day?"],
["Voicing concerns","the right to complain without punishment.","Telling the administrator about a problem or filing a grievance, and no one can treat you worse for it.","How would you voice a concern here?"],
["Responsibilities","rights come with responsibilities.","Respecting others' rights, following house rules, paying fees, and treating staff and residents with respect.","Why do rights come with responsibilities?"]],
story:"Ms. Velma felt a staff member was rude to her and talked down to her. She was afraid that if she complained, she'd get in trouble. A friend reminded her that she had the right to voice concerns without punishment. Velma calmly told the administrator what happened. The administrator listened, talked with the staff member, and checked back with Velma. Velma said, I felt respected because I was heard.",
sq:["What happened to Velma?","Why was she afraid to speak up?","What right did she use?","How was the concern handled?"],
act:{n:"Rights and Responsibilities Match",m:"Board with two columns",s:["Write rights on the left: privacy, respect, choice, voicing concerns, safety, visitors.","For each right, ask the group to name a matching responsibility: respect others' privacy, treat others with respect, follow safety rules, speak respectfully, help keep the home safe, follow visitor policies.","Discuss real examples of each.","Ask staff to share where the facility's posted resident rights are located.","Invite residents to ask questions (staff can answer specifics later)."]},
d:["Which right surprised you?","Which responsibility is most important?","Do you know where resident rights are posted here?"],
disc:[["Why is it important to know your rights?","So you can recognize when they're not respected and speak up."],["What should you do if you feel a right was violated?","Calmly talk to the administrator or social worker; you can contact the state Long-Term Care Ombudsman."],["How can we respect each other's rights?","Privacy, kindness, following rules."],["What is the Long-Term Care Ombudsman?","A free advocate for residents in care facilities; staff can share contact info."],["What right do you value most?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You have rights to dignity, privacy, choice, safety, and to voice concerns without punishment. With rights come responsibilities to respect others.",
tryit:"Find where resident rights are posted in our building and read them."},

{t:"Speaking Up for Yourself: Self-Advocacy",
goal:"Today we will learn how to advocate for ourselves, asking for what we need with confidence and respect.",
warm:["Have you ever had to stand up for yourself? What happened?","Who has advocated for you in the past?","What makes it hard to speak up for yourself?"],
terms:[
["Self-advocacy","speaking up for your own needs, rights, and wishes.","Telling your doctor, I'd like to try a different medicine because this one makes me very sleepy.","When have you advocated for yourself?"],
["Know what you need","being clear about what you want before you speak.","Writing down: I need a lower bed because I'm afraid of falling.","Why is knowing what you need the first step?"],
["Know who to ask","finding the person who can help.","The nurse for health, the social worker for benefits, the administrator for complaints.","Who would you ask about a room change?"],
["Calm and clear","speaking respectfully and directly.","I'd like to talk about my care plan. When is a good time?","Why does calm work better than angry?"],
["Bring support","having someone with you if needed.","Bringing a family member, peer, or advocate to an important meeting.","Who could support you at a meeting?"]],
story:"Mr. Charles was told he couldn't go on outings because of his walker. He felt left out. He wrote down why he wanted to go and some ideas, like bringing a portable chair. He asked to meet with the administrator and brought his social worker. They talked it through and agreed he could go on outings with an extra staff member. Charles said, I didn't yell. I just made my case.",
sq:["What problem did Charles face?","How did he prepare?","Who did he bring?","What was the result?"],
act:{n:"Make Your Case",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Teach 4 steps: 1. What do I need? 2. Why does it matter? 3. Who can help? 4. What will I say?","Practice with an example: wanting a different roommate, wanting a later bedtime, wanting to change a medication time.","Have each resident fill out the 4 steps for a real or pretend need.","Pair up and practice saying it out loud.","Give feedback: clear, calm, respectful."]},
d:["What need did you choose?","How did it feel to practice?","Who will you talk to?"],
disc:[["Why do people give up instead of advocating?","Fear, feeling powerless, past experiences."],["What should you do if your first request is denied?","Ask why, ask what could change the answer, go to the next level."],["How can writing things down help?","Clear thoughts, nothing forgotten."],["How can advocating help others too?","Changes can help everyone."],["What do you want to advocate for?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Know what you need, why it matters, who can help, and what you'll say. Be calm, clear, and bring support if you need it.",
tryit:"Advocate for one need this week using the 4 steps."},

{t:"Health Insurance Basics",
goal:"Today we will learn the basic words and programs related to health insurance, like Medicaid (TennCare) and Medicare.",
warm:["What health insurance do you know about?","Have you ever been confused by a medical bill?","Who helps you with insurance questions?"],
terms:[
["Health insurance","a program that helps pay for medical care.","It pays for doctor visits, hospital stays, and medicines, depending on the plan.","Why is health insurance important?"],
["Medicaid (TennCare)","a program for people with low income or disabilities. In Tennessee, it's called TennCare.","TennCare can cover doctor visits, prescriptions, hospital care, and rides to appointments.","Do you know if you have TennCare?"],
["Medicare","federal insurance for people 65 and older and some people with disabilities.","Part A covers hospitals, Part B covers doctors, Part D covers prescriptions.","Who qualifies for Medicare?"],
["Copay","a small amount you pay for a visit or medicine.","A $3 copay for a prescription.","Have you paid a copay?"],
["Insurance card","the card that shows your coverage.","Bring it to every appointment and the pharmacy. Keep it safe.","Where do you keep your insurance card?"]],
story:"Mr. Willie got a bill for $400 from an emergency room visit and panicked. He didn't know he had both Medicare and TennCare. The social worker helped him call, and it turned out the bill was sent to him by mistake before insurance processed it. It was covered. Willie said, I almost paid money I didn't owe because I didn't understand my insurance.",
sq:["Why did Willie panic?","What did he not know?","Who helped him?","What would you do with a confusing bill?"],
act:{n:"Insurance Word Match",m:"Words and meanings on separate cards or the board",s:["Write words on the left: TennCare, Medicare, copay, premium, prescription coverage, insurance card.","Write meanings on the right, mixed up.","Have residents match each word to its meaning.","Discuss any confusing words.","Remind residents: bring confusing bills or letters to the social worker."]},
d:["Which word was new?","Do you know what insurance you have?","Who will you ask if you get a confusing bill?"],
disc:[["Why is insurance so confusing?","Many words, many programs, rules change."],["What should you do before paying a medical bill?","Check with the social worker that insurance processed it."],["What services might TennCare cover that people don't know about?","Rides to appointments, dental for some, behavioral health."],["How can you protect your insurance information?","Don't share your number with strangers or callers."],["What insurance question do you have?","Let residents share; facilitator passes questions to the social worker."]],
take:"Know what insurance you have, keep your card safe, bring it to every visit, and ask the social worker before paying any medical bill.",
tryit:"Ask the social worker what insurance you have and what it covers."},

{t:"Understanding Your Care Plan",
goal:"Today we will learn what a care plan is and how to take an active part in planning our own care.",
warm:["Who decides what care you get?","Have you ever been part of a care planning meeting?","What would you want included in your care?"],
terms:[
["Care plan","a written plan describing the care and support you need.","It may include medicine times, help with bathing, diet, activities, and goals.","What do you think is in your care plan?"],
["Care plan meeting","a meeting with your care team to review and update your plan.","Meeting with the nurse, social worker, and administrator every few months.","Have you been to one?"],
["Person-centered","care planned around your wishes, preferences, and goals.","If you prefer showers at night, your plan says that.","What preferences would you add?"],
["Goals in your plan","what you are working toward.","Walking to the dining room on your own, managing your own money, or reconnecting with family.","What goal would you want in your plan?"],
["Your input","your voice in decisions about your care.","Saying, I'd like to try going to the dining room without my wheelchair.","How can you give input?"]],
story:"Ms. Thelma never went to her care plan meetings because she thought they were just paperwork. When she finally went, she learned her plan said she needed help bathing every day, but she only needed help with her back. She asked for a change, and the plan was updated. She said, I got some independence back just by showing up.",
sq:["Why didn't Thelma go to meetings before?","What did she find in her plan?","What changed?","Why is it important to show up?"],
act:{n:"My Care Wishes",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Have each resident write: My preferences (wake time, shower time, food, activities).","My goals for the next 3 months.","Things I can do on my own.","Things I need help with.","Questions for my care team. Encourage them to bring this to their next care plan meeting."]},
d:["What preferences did you list?","What goal will you share?","What question do you have for your team?"],
disc:[["Why should residents be involved in care planning?","It's your life; better care when your voice is included."],["What should you do if your plan doesn't match your needs?","Ask for a meeting to update it."],["How can family be involved?","With your permission, they can attend."],["How often should plans be reviewed?","Regularly and when things change."],["What would make your care better?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Your care plan is about you. Attend meetings, share your preferences and goals, and ask for changes when needed.",
tryit:"Ask staff when your next care plan meeting is and bring your care wishes."},

{t:"Privacy and Your Health Information",
goal:"Today we will learn about our right to privacy regarding our health information and personal matters.",
warm:["What information do you want to keep private?","How do you feel when people know your business?","Who do you trust with private information?"],
terms:[
["Health information","facts about your health, diagnoses, medicines, and treatment.","Your diagnosis, your medicine list, and your doctor's notes are private health information.","Who should be able to see your health information?"],
["HIPAA","a federal law that protects the privacy of health information.","Doctors and care facilities can't share your health information without permission except for treatment, payment, or legal reasons.","Have you heard of HIPAA?"],
["Release of information","a form giving permission to share your information with someone.","Signing a release so the facility can talk to your daughter about your care.","Who would you allow to receive your information?"],
["Respecting others' privacy","not sharing what you know about other residents.","Not telling others about a roommate's diagnosis or why someone went to the hospital.","Why is this important here?"],
["Privacy in daily life","personal space and dignity.","Closing the bathroom door, knocking, and keeping private conversations private.","How can privacy be protected in a shared home?"]],
story:"A resident overheard Mr. Lloyd's phone call with his doctor and told others about his diagnosis. Lloyd felt embarrassed and betrayed. Staff reminded everyone that health information is private and should never be gossiped about. They also set up a private room for phone calls. Lloyd said, My health is my business. I'll share it when I'm ready.",
sq:["How was Lloyd's privacy violated?","How did he feel?","What did staff do?","What does his statement mean?"],
act:{n:"Private or OK to Share?",m:"None",s:["Read situations and ask: Is this private or OK to share?","A resident's diagnosis. (Private.)","That there is a movie night Friday. (OK to share.)","Why someone went to the hospital. (Private unless they share it.)","Your own health story, if you choose. (Your choice.)","A roommate's medication. (Private.)","A resident's birthday, if they want it celebrated. (Ask them.)","Discuss each answer."]},
d:["Which one was hardest to decide?","Why should we not share others' health information?","Who would you give permission to share your information with?"],
disc:[["Why is health privacy important?","Prevents stigma, discrimination, embarrassment."],["What should you do if your privacy is violated?","Tell the administrator."],["How can you keep phone calls private?","Ask for a private space."],["What is the difference between your choice to share and others sharing for you?","Your story is yours to tell."],["How can we build a home where privacy is respected?","No gossip, knock, private spaces."]],
take:"Your health information is private and protected by law. Share only what you choose, and respect others' privacy too.",
tryit:"If someone starts talking about another resident's health, kindly change the subject."},

{t:"Finding Help: 211 and Community Resources",
goal:"Today we will learn how to find free help in our community for food, housing, bills, and more.",
warm:["Where would you go if you needed help with food or bills?","Have you ever gotten help from a community program?","What kind of help would be most useful to you?"],
terms:[
["Community resources","programs and services that help people with needs.","Food banks, clothing closets, utility help, legal aid, transportation, and support groups.","What community resources do you know about?"],
["211","a free phone number that connects you to local help.","Call or text 211 to ask about food, housing, utilities, health care, and more. In Chattanooga, it's run through United Way.","Have you ever called 211?"],
["Food pantry","a place that gives free food.","Chattanooga Area Food Bank partners with many pantries and churches.","Do you know a food pantry near here?"],
["Eligibility","whether you qualify for a program.","Some programs require proof of income, ID, or address.","What documents might you need?"],
["Following up","calling back if you don't hear from a program.","Hi, I applied last week for help with ___. Can you tell me the status?","Why is following up important?"]],
story:"When Ms. Gwen moved out of the facility into her own apartment, her electric bill was higher than she expected. She didn't know where to turn. She remembered learning about 211. She called, and they connected her with a utility assistance program. She got help paying the bill and was set up on a budget billing plan. She said, One phone call kept my lights on.",
sq:["What problem did Gwen have?","What did she remember?","What help did she get?","What does one phone call mean?"],
act:{n:"Resource Finder",m:"Board, or a phone to look up 211 resources together",s:["Write needs on the board: food, clothes, rent help, utility help, legal help, transportation, mental health, job help.","For each need, brainstorm where to get help: 211, food bank, churches, Legal Aid, CARTA, Centerstone or other mental health centers, career centers.","If possible, call or look up 211 together on speaker or screen to show how it works.","Practice a script for calling: Hi, I'm looking for help with ___. I live in ___. Can you tell me what's available?","Have residents write 211 on a card to keep."]},
d:["Which resource was new to you?","What would you call 211 about?","How did the call script feel?"],
disc:[["Why don't people use community resources?","Don't know about them, embarrassment, hard to reach."],["Is it OK to ask for help?","Yes; these programs exist for that reason."],["What should you have ready when you call?","Address, ID, income info, phone number."],["How can staff help connect residents to resources?","Social worker, rides, paperwork."],["What resource would help you most?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Help is out there. Call or text 211 to find food, housing, utility, and other help in our community, and follow up.",
tryit:"Write 211 on a card and keep it with you."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Rights, Resources & Advocacy", [
{t:"Your Right to Vote",
goal:"Today we will learn about our right to vote, how to register, and how voting gives us a voice in our community.",
warm:["Have you ever voted?","Why do you think voting matters?","What issues in our community do you care about?"],
terms:[
["Voting","choosing leaders and deciding on laws and issues.","Voting for mayor, governor, president, or on local issues like school funding.","Why do elections matter to you?"],
["Voter registration","signing up so you can vote.","In Tennessee, you can register online, by mail, or at the election commission. You need to register about 30 days before an election.","Are you registered to vote?"],
["Photo ID to vote","Tennessee requires a government photo ID to vote in person.","A Tennessee driver's license or state ID card, a passport, or a military ID.","Do you have a photo ID that works for voting?"],
["Absentee voting","voting by mail if you qualify.","In Tennessee, voters 60 and older and people with certain illnesses or disabilities may qualify to vote by mail.","Do you think you might qualify?"],
["Nonpartisan help","staff can help you register and get to the polls, but cannot tell you who to vote for.","Your vote is your private choice.","Why should voting choices stay private?"]],
story:"Mr. Harold hadn't voted in 20 years because he thought his vote didn't matter. He learned that a local election for county commission had been decided by fewer than 100 votes. With the social worker's help, he registered and got an absentee ballot. He said, I've got opinions. Might as well make them count.",
sq:["Why hadn't Harold voted?","What changed his mind?","How did he vote?","Why does every vote matter?"],
act:{n:"Voting Steps",m:"Board",s:["Write the steps: 1. Check if you're registered. 2. Register if needed. 3. Get a photo ID if you don't have one. 4. Learn about the candidates and issues. 5. Vote early, absentee, or on Election Day.","Discuss which step each person is on.","Talk about where to find nonpartisan information (sample ballots, local news, League of Women Voters).","Practice reading a sample ballot (staff can provide or draw one).","Remind residents staff can help with registration and rides."]},
d:["Which step are you on?","What issue matters most to you?","Would you like help registering?"],
disc:[["Why do some people not vote?","Feel it doesn't matter, no ID, no transportation, confusion."],["How can voting give people a voice?","Leaders listen to voters."],["What local issues affect our residents?","Bus routes, housing, health care, safety."],["How can we talk about politics respectfully?","Listen, don't attack, agree to disagree."],["What would you change in our city?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Voting is your right and your voice. Register, get a photo ID, learn about the issues, and ask staff for help getting your ballot.",
tryit:"Ask the social worker whether you are registered to vote."},

{t:"Free Legal Help",
goal:"Today we will learn about free legal help available for people with low income and when to seek it.",
warm:["Have you ever needed a lawyer?","What legal problems do people commonly face?","Where would you go for legal advice?"],
terms:[
["Legal problem","an issue involving laws, contracts, or court.","An eviction, a benefits denial, a debt collector suing, custody issues, or an old warrant.","What legal problems have you heard of?"],
["Legal Aid","free legal help for people with low income.","Legal Aid of East Tennessee serves Chattanooga and can help with housing, benefits, and consumer issues.","Did you know free legal help exists?"],
["Court date","a scheduled time to appear in court.","Missing a court date can lead to a warrant or losing your case automatically.","Why should you never ignore a court date?"],
["Read before you sign","never sign legal papers you don't understand.","Ask the social worker or Legal Aid to look at contracts, leases, or court papers first.","Have you ever signed something you didn't understand?"],
["Deadlines","legal matters have strict time limits.","An appeal of a benefits denial might have only 60 days, or less.","Why are deadlines important?"]],
story:"Ms. Rosa got court papers saying a debt collector was suing her for an old credit card. She was scared and ignored them. The social worker noticed and connected her with Legal Aid. The lawyer found that the debt was too old to be legally collected. Rosa's case was dismissed. She said, If I'd kept hiding those papers, I would've lost.",
sq:["What papers did Rosa get?","What did she do at first?","How did Legal Aid help?","What lesson did she learn?"],
act:{n:"Legal Help or Not?",m:"None",s:["Read situations and ask: Should this person get legal help? Who should they contact?","A landlord says you have 3 days to leave. (Yes, Legal Aid.)","A debt collector calls about an old bill. (Ask for it in writing; talk to the social worker or Legal Aid.)","You get court papers. (Yes, right away.)","Your SSI is cut off. (Yes, appeal quickly; social worker or Legal Aid.)","Someone wants you to sign a power of attorney. (Get advice first.)","Discuss each one."]},
d:["Which situation surprised you?","Who would you contact first?","Why is acting quickly important?"],
disc:[["Why do people ignore legal papers?","Fear, confusion, shame."],["How can a social worker help?","Read papers, connect to Legal Aid, meet deadlines."],["What should you bring to Legal Aid?","All papers, letters, ID, notes about what happened."],["Can staff give legal advice?","No, but they can connect you to help."],["What legal question do you have?","Let residents share in general terms."]],
take:"Never ignore legal papers. Read before you sign, watch deadlines, and contact the social worker or Legal Aid for free help.",
tryit:"If you have any legal papers you don't understand, bring them to the social worker this week."},

{t:"Rides to Medical Appointments",
goal:"Today we will learn about transportation options for getting to medical appointments.",
warm:["How do you usually get to your appointments?","Have you ever missed an appointment because you didn't have a ride?","What makes transportation stressful?"],
terms:[
["Non-emergency medical transportation","free rides to medical appointments for people with Medicaid.","TennCare members can schedule rides to covered appointments, usually a few days in advance.","Have you used a medical ride service?"],
["Scheduling ahead","booking your ride days before the appointment.","Call at least 3 days before with your appointment time and address.","Why should you book early?"],
["Pickup window","the time range when the ride may arrive.","The driver may come anytime within a set window, so be ready early.","How can you be ready for a pickup window?"],
["Return trip","the ride home after your appointment.","Some rides need you to call when you're done. Keep the number with you.","What should you do if your return ride is late?"],
["Other options","backup ways to get to appointments.","Facility staff, family, CARTA's Care-A-Van, or the city bus.","What backup ride options do you have?"]],
story:"Mr. Eugene missed his eye appointment because the ride never came. He was frustrated. The social worker helped him call the ride line to complain and rebook. She also showed him how to call the day before to confirm the ride. The next time, the ride arrived on time. Eugene said, Now I always confirm the day before.",
sq:["What happened to Eugene?","What did the social worker help with?","What new habit did he learn?","What would you do if a ride didn't come?"],
act:{n:"Book a Ride Role-Play",m:"None",s:["Review what you need to book a ride: insurance ID number, appointment date and time, doctor's address and phone, your address, any wheelchair or walker needs.","Pair up. One person is the ride scheduler, one is the caller.","Practice booking: Hi, I need a ride to an appointment on ___ at ___. The address is ___. I use a walker.","Practice confirming the day before.","Discuss what to do if the ride is late: call the ride line, tell staff, call the doctor's office."]},
d:["What information do you need to book a ride?","How did the role-play feel?","Who will you tell if your ride doesn't show?"],
disc:[["Why do missed rides happen?","Booking errors, driver shortages, not confirming."],["What should you do if you'll miss an appointment because of a ride?","Call the doctor's office right away."],["How can staff help with rides?","Booking, reminders, backup transportation."],["Why is getting to appointments important?","Health care, refills, follow-ups."],["What appointment do you have coming up?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Book medical rides early, confirm the day before, be ready during the pickup window, and keep the return ride number with you.",
tryit:"For your next appointment, ask staff how your ride is scheduled and confirm it the day before."},

{t:"Planning Ahead: Advance Directives",
goal:"Today we will gently learn what advance directives are and why planning ahead for health care decisions can give peace of mind.",
warm:["What does planning ahead mean to you?","Have you ever talked with family about your wishes for health care?","Why might people avoid these conversations?"],
terms:[
["Advance directive","a written document that says what kind of medical care you want if you can't speak for yourself.","It might say whether you want certain life-saving treatments, or who should make decisions for you.","Why might someone want an advance directive?"],
["Health care agent","the person you choose to make medical decisions for you if you can't.","Choosing your sister because she knows your wishes and will speak up for you.","Who would you trust with this role?"],
["Living will","a document that describes the treatments you do or don't want.","Whether you would want a breathing machine or feeding tube in certain situations.","Why is it helpful to decide ahead of time?"],
["Talking with loved ones","sharing your wishes so others understand.","I want you to know what matters to me if I get very sick.","How might this conversation help your family?"],
["You can change it","advance directives can be updated anytime.","If your wishes change, you can make a new document.","Why is it good to know you can change it?"]],
story:"When Ms. Bertha's husband got very sick, the family argued about his care because no one knew what he wanted. It was painful for everyone. Afterward, Bertha decided to fill out her own advance directive with the social worker and talk with her children. She said, I don't want my kids to argue or guess. I want them to know, so they can just love me.",
sq:["What happened in Bertha's family?","Why was it painful?","What did Bertha decide to do?","What does her last sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"What Matters Most",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Explain gently that this is not about legal forms today, just thinking about values.","Ask residents to complete these sentences privately: What matters most to me in life is ___. If I were very sick, I would want ___. The person I trust to speak for me is ___.","Invite volunteers to share one sentence if comfortable.","Explain that the social worker can help with official forms if they want.","Close with a calm breathing moment."]},
d:["How did it feel to think about these questions?","Who did you think of as your trusted person?","Would you like to talk to the social worker about forms?"],
disc:[["Why do people avoid planning ahead?","Uncomfortable, superstition, don't want to think about it."],["How can an advance directive help families?","Less conflict, less guilt, clear wishes."],["Who can help fill out these forms?","Social worker, doctor, lawyer."],["What should you do once you have one?","Give copies to your agent, doctor, and the facility."],["What gives you peace about the future?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Planning ahead lets your voice be heard even if you can't speak. Think about what matters to you, choose a trusted person, and talk with your loved ones.",
tryit:"If you'd like, ask the social worker about advance directive forms."},

{t:"Representative Payees",
goal:"Today we will learn what a representative payee is, how it works, and our rights when someone manages our benefits.",
warm:["Who helps you manage your money?","How do you feel about someone else handling your money?","What would you want to know about your money?"],
terms:[
["Representative payee","a person or organization chosen by Social Security to manage someone's benefits for them.","The payee receives the check, pays rent and bills, and gives the person spending money.","Do you have a payee?"],
["Payee's responsibilities","what a payee must do.","Use the money for your needs first: housing, food, medicine, clothing. Save the rest for you. Keep records.","What should a payee spend your money on?"],
["Your rights","what you are entitled to with a payee.","You have a right to know how your money is spent, to get spending money, and to ask for a different payee.","Did you know you have these rights?"],
["Accounting","the yearly report a payee gives Social Security.","The payee reports how they spent your benefits.","Why is accounting important?"],
["Becoming your own payee","showing you can manage your benefits again.","Working on budgeting skills and asking Social Security to review if you can manage your own money.","Is managing your own money a goal for you?"]],
story:"Ms. Patricia's payee was a relative who wouldn't tell her how her money was being spent. She often didn't get her spending money. With help from the social worker, she contacted Social Security, which changed her payee to an organization. Now she gets a monthly statement and her spending money on time. She's also practicing budgeting so she can become her own payee someday.",
sq:["What problem did Patricia have?","Who helped her?","What changed?","What is her long-term goal?"],
act:{n:"Payee Questions",m:"Paper, pens",s:["List questions residents can ask their payee: How much is my monthly benefit? What bills are being paid? How much spending money do I get and when? How much is saved for me?","Have residents write questions they want to ask.","Practice asking politely in pairs.","Discuss what to do if a payee won't answer: talk to the social worker or Social Security.","Discuss skills needed to become your own payee."]},
d:["What question do you want to ask?","How did practicing feel?","Is becoming your own payee a goal for you?"],
disc:[["Why might someone need a payee?","Help managing money for health reasons."],["What should you do if you think a payee is misusing money?","Tell the social worker; report to Social Security."],["How can a payee help you?","Bills paid on time, stability."],["What skills help you become your own payee?","Budgeting, paying bills, tracking spending."],["How do you feel about money independence?","Let residents share."]],
take:"A payee should use your benefits for your needs and tell you how. You have a right to know, to receive spending money, and to ask for a change.",
tryit:"Ask your payee or the social worker one question about your money this week."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Rights, Resources & Advocacy", [
{t:"Disability Rights and Accommodations",
goal:"Today we will learn about rights for people with disabilities and how to ask for accommodations.",
warm:["What makes a place easy or hard to get around?","Have you ever needed something changed to make life easier?","What does the word fair mean to you?"],
terms:[
["Disability","a physical or mental condition that affects daily activities.","Mobility problems, vision or hearing loss, mental illness, and chronic illness can be disabilities.","Why is mental illness considered a disability under the law?"],
["ADA","the Americans with Disabilities Act, a law that protects people with disabilities from discrimination.","It requires ramps, accessible bathrooms, and fair treatment at jobs and in public places.","What changes has the ADA brought?"],
["Accommodation","a change that helps a person with a disability have equal access.","A ramp, large-print forms, extra time, a quiet room, or a sign language interpreter.","What accommodation would help you?"],
["Asking for accommodations","requesting what you need.","Could I get this form in large print? or Can I have a seat near the door because of anxiety?","How could you ask for an accommodation?"],
["Discrimination","unfair treatment because of a disability.","Being refused housing or a job because of a mental health diagnosis.","What should you do if you face discrimination?"]],
story:"Mr. Darnell had trouble reading small print and felt embarrassed filling out forms at the doctor's office. A friend told him he could ask for help. At his next visit, he said, I have trouble with small print. Could someone help me with this form or give me a large-print copy? The receptionist helped right away. He said, I wish I'd known I could just ask.",
sq:["What was hard for Darnell?","What did he ask for?","How did the office respond?","What accommodation might help you?"],
act:{n:"Accommodation Brainstorm",m:"Board",s:["Write common challenges: trouble walking, poor eyesight, hearing loss, anxiety in crowds, trouble remembering, trouble reading.","For each, brainstorm accommodations: walker, ramp, large print, hearing aids, seat near exit, written reminders, someone to read aloud.","Practice asking: I have ___. Could I please have ___?","Have each resident write one accommodation they could ask for.","Discuss where to ask: doctor's office, bank, store, job."]},
d:["What accommodation would help you?","Where would you ask for it?","How did it feel to practice asking?"],
disc:[["Why do people avoid asking for accommodations?","Embarrassment, not knowing they can."],["What should you do if a business refuses a reasonable accommodation?","Ask for a manager; talk to the social worker or a disability rights group."],["How can accommodations help with jobs?","Equal opportunity to succeed."],["How can we make our own home more accessible?","Clear paths, good lighting, patience."],["What makes you feel included?","Let residents share."]],
take:"People with disabilities have rights to equal access. Ask clearly for accommodations: I have ___, could I please have ___?",
tryit:"Ask for one accommodation that would make something easier for you."},

{t:"Veterans Benefits and Support",
goal:"Today we will learn about benefits and support available to veterans and their families, and honor those who served.",
warm:["Did you or someone in your family serve in the military?","What do you know about veterans benefits?","How can we honor veterans?"],
terms:[
["Veteran","someone who served in the armed forces.","Army, Navy, Air Force, Marines, Coast Guard, and Space Force veterans.","Who is a veteran in your life?"],
["VA benefits","help from the Department of Veterans Affairs.","Health care, disability compensation, pension, housing help, and burial benefits.","What VA benefits have you heard of?"],
["DD-214","the paper that shows military service and discharge.","It is often needed to apply for VA benefits.","Do you know where your DD-214 is?"],
["Veteran service officer","a free helper who assists veterans with benefits paperwork.","Hamilton County has veteran service offices, and groups like the VFW and American Legion help too.","How could a service officer help?"],
["Veterans Crisis Line","24/7 support for veterans and families.","Dial 988 and press 1, or text 838255.","Why is a special line for veterans important?"]],
story:"Mr. Raymond served in the Army but never applied for VA benefits because he thought it was too complicated. A resident who was also a veteran told him about a free veteran service officer. With help, Raymond found his DD-214 and applied. He started getting VA health care and a small pension. He said, I served my country. It's OK to let my country serve me back.",
sq:["Why hadn't Raymond applied?","Who helped him?","What did he receive?","What does his last sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"Honor and Help",m:"Paper, pens, cards",s:["Invite any veterans to share their branch and years of service if they wish. Thank them as a group.","Review the steps to get benefits: find your DD-214, contact a veteran service officer, apply.","Write the Veterans Crisis Line on the board: 988, press 1.","For non-veterans, discuss family members who served and benefits that may help spouses.","Make thank-you cards for local veterans or a VA clinic."]},
d:["Who did you honor today?","What benefit step is new to you?","Who could you share this information with?"],
disc:[["Why don't many veterans use their benefits?","Don't know, paperwork, pride, bad past experiences."],["How can military service affect mental health?","PTSD, depression, adjustment."],["What support helps veterans most?","Peer support, VA care, community."],["How can we honor veterans in our home?","Recognize Veterans Day, listen to their stories."],["What does service mean to you?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Veterans have earned benefits and support. Find your DD-214, get free help from a veteran service officer, and remember the Veterans Crisis Line: 988, press 1.",
tryit:"If you or a family member is a veteran, ask the social worker about connecting with a veteran service officer."},

{t:"Peer Support and Advocacy Groups",
goal:"Today we will learn about peer support and advocacy groups that help people with mental health conditions find community and a voice.",
warm:["Have you ever felt understood by someone who went through the same thing?","What support groups have you heard of?","What would you want from a support group?"],
terms:[
["Peer support","help from someone with similar lived experience.","A peer support specialist who has lived with depression helps others build hope and skills.","Why might peer support feel different from staff support?"],
["NAMI","the National Alliance on Mental Illness, a group that offers free support, education, and advocacy.","NAMI Chattanooga offers support groups for people with mental illness and their families.","Have you heard of NAMI?"],
["Advocacy group","a group that works to improve laws, services, and attitudes.","Groups that push for better mental health services or fight stigma.","What would you want to change for people with mental illness?"],
["Lived experience","knowledge from going through something yourself.","Your experience with recovery can help someone just starting.","How could your experience help others?"],
["Warm line","a phone line to talk with a peer when you are struggling but not in crisis.","Some areas have peer-run warm lines for when you need someone to talk to.","When might a warm line be helpful?"]],
story:"Ms. Janet felt alone with her bipolar disorder until she joined a NAMI support group. Hearing others' stories helped her feel normal. After a few years, she trained to become a peer support specialist. Now she leads a group herself. She said, The thing I was most ashamed of became the thing that helps others most.",
sq:["How did Janet feel before joining?","What did the group give her?","What did she become?","What does her last sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"My Story Can Help",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Explain that everyone's experience has value.","Ask residents to write a short message to someone newly diagnosed or new to the facility, sharing one thing that helped them.","Invite volunteers to read their messages.","Discuss peer support and advocacy opportunities: groups, volunteering, training to be a peer specialist.","With permission, collect messages to share with new residents."]},
d:["What advice did you write?","How does it feel to know your story can help?","Would you be interested in a support group?"],
disc:[["Why is peer support powerful?","Shared experience, hope, understanding."],["How do advocacy groups fight stigma?","Education, sharing stories, changing laws."],["What makes a support group safe?","Confidentiality, respect, no judgment."],["How can residents support each other here?","Listen, share, encourage."],["What change would you advocate for?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You're not alone. Peer support and groups like NAMI offer community, hope, and a voice. Your lived experience can help others.",
tryit:"Ask staff about local support groups, or encourage someone who is new."},

{t:"Your Rights About Medications and Treatment",
goal:"Today we will learn about our rights to understand, ask questions about, and take part in decisions about our medications and treatment.",
warm:["Do you know why you take each of your medicines?","Have you ever had a question about your treatment you didn't ask?","Who makes decisions about your treatment?"],
terms:[
["Informed consent","agreeing to treatment after it has been explained to you.","Before starting a new medicine, the doctor explains what it's for, the benefits, risks, and other options.","Has a doctor explained a medicine to you this way?"],
["Right to information","you can ask what your medicines are and why you take them.","What is this pill for? What side effects should I watch for?","What would you like to know about your medicine?"],
["Shared decision-making","you and your doctor make decisions together.","The doctor offers options, and you talk about which fits your life and goals.","How could you take part in decisions?"],
["Right to refuse","adults generally have the right to say no to a treatment, after understanding the risks.","If you don't want a medicine, tell the nurse and doctor so they can explain the risks and discuss options, rather than secretly not taking it.","Why is it better to talk than to secretly skip?"],
["Second opinion","asking another doctor for their view.","Seeing a different psychiatrist if you have concerns about a diagnosis or treatment.","When might a second opinion help?"]],
story:"Mr. Tyrone didn't like how one of his medicines made him feel, so he started hiding pills instead of taking them. He got sick and ended up in the hospital. Afterward, his nurse explained that he had the right to talk to his doctor about it. At his next visit, he said, This medicine makes me feel like a zombie. Are there other options? His doctor changed his medicine. Tyrone said, I didn't know I was allowed to speak up.",
sq:["What did Tyrone do when he didn't like his medicine?","What happened?","What right did he learn about?","How did speaking up help?"],
act:{n:"Questions for My Doctor",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Write these questions on the board: What is this medicine for? What side effects should I watch for? How long will I take it? Are there other options? What happens if I stop?","Have each resident copy the questions they want to ask.","Practice in pairs: one plays the doctor, one asks.","Practice saying a concern: This medicine makes me ___. Can we talk about options?","Remind residents to talk to the nurse first if they have concerns."]},
d:["Which question is most important to you?","How did practicing feel?","Who will you talk to first?"],
disc:[["Why do people secretly stop medicines instead of speaking up?","Fear, feeling unheard, not knowing their rights."],["What are the risks of stopping medicine without telling anyone?","Symptoms return, withdrawal, hospital."],["How can you make sure your doctor hears you?","Write questions, bring support, be specific."],["When might a court or guardian be involved in treatment decisions?","In some situations; the social worker can explain your situation."],["What would you like to understand better about your treatment?","Let residents share."]],
take:"You have the right to understand your treatment, ask questions, and take part in decisions. Speak up instead of secretly stopping, and ask the nurse to help you talk to your doctor.",
tryit:"Ask the nurse what one of your medicines is for this week."},

{t:"Using Crisis and Support Lines",
goal:"Today we will learn the phone numbers and services available for crisis and emotional support, and when to use each one.",
warm:["Who would you call if you were really struggling at night?","Have you ever called a helpline?","What makes it hard to reach out for help?"],
terms:[
["988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline","a free, 24/7 call, text, or chat line for anyone in emotional distress or crisis.","Call or text 988 if you're thinking about suicide, feeling overwhelmed, or worried about someone.","Do you know how to reach 988?"],
["911","the emergency number for immediate danger to life or safety.","Call 911 for a medical emergency, violence, fire, or if someone is in immediate danger.","When should you call 911 instead of 988?"],
["Mobile crisis","a team that can come to you during a mental health crisis.","In Tennessee, the statewide crisis line can send a mobile crisis team: 855-274-7471.","How might a mobile crisis team help?"],
["Warm line or support line","a non-crisis line to talk when you need support.","When you're lonely or stressed but not in danger.","When might a support line help?"],
["Tell staff","staff here are always your first and closest help.","Staff can stay with you, call the nurse, and call for help.","Who is the staff member you'd go to?"]],
story:"One night, Ms. Kiara felt hopeless and didn't want to wake anyone. She remembered 988 from a group and sent a text. A trained counselor texted back, listened, and helped her make a plan to get through the night. The counselor also encouraged her to tell the night caregiver. Kiara did, and the caregiver sat with her. She said, I thought I'd be a bother. They were glad I reached out.",
sq:["How was Kiara feeling?","What did she do?","How did the counselor help?","What does her last sentence teach us?"],
act:{n:"Who Do I Call?",m:"Board with columns: Staff, 988, 911",s:["Read situations and ask who to contact.","A friend says they want to end their life. (Staff right away; 988; 911 if immediate danger.)","You feel lonely and can't sleep. (Staff, a support line, or 988.)","Someone is not breathing. (911 and staff.)","You're having a panic attack. (Staff, coping skills, 988 if needed.)","Someone is threatening others with a weapon. (911 and staff.)","Have each resident write 988 and the facility number on a card to keep."]},
d:["Which situation was hardest to decide?","Do you feel more comfortable reaching out now?","Where will you keep your card?"],
disc:[["Why do people hesitate to call crisis lines?","Fear of police, being a bother, shame."],["What happens when you call 988?","A trained counselor listens and helps; most calls are handled without emergency services."],["How can you help a friend in crisis?","Stay with them, get staff, help them call 988."],["Is it OK to call 988 for someone else?","Yes."],["What helps you get through the hardest nights?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Tell staff first. Call or text 988 for emotional crisis or distress, any time. Call 911 for immediate danger. Reaching out is never a bother.",
tryit:"Save 988 in your phone or keep it on a card with you."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Mind, Memory & Thinking", [
{t:"Keeping Your Brain Active",
goal:"Today we will learn why keeping the brain active matters and fun ways to exercise our minds every day.",
warm:["What activities keep your mind sharp?","Do you like puzzles, games, or reading?","What is something new you learned this year?"],
terms:[
["Brain fitness","keeping the brain healthy and active, like exercising a muscle.","Doing crosswords, learning a song, playing cards, or having a good conversation.","What is your favorite brain exercise?"],
["Neuroplasticity","the brain's ability to grow new connections at any age.","When you learn something new, like a card game, your brain builds new pathways.","Can older brains still learn?"],
["Mental stimulation","activities that challenge your thinking.","Puzzles, reading, learning a new skill, playing strategy games like checkers or chess.","What challenges your mind?"],
["Social connection","talking with others is great exercise for the brain.","A good conversation uses memory, attention, and language all at once.","Who do you enjoy talking with?"],
["Variety","doing different kinds of brain activities.","Mixing word games, number games, music, and art works more parts of the brain.","Do you usually do the same activity or mix it up?"]],
story:"Mr. Otis watched TV most of the day and felt foggy and forgetful. A caregiver invited him to a daily puzzle and trivia group. At first, he struggled. After a few weeks, he was answering questions quickly and remembering names better. He said, I thought my brain was just old. It was just bored.",
sq:["How did Otis spend his days?","What did he start doing?","What changed?","What does my brain was just bored mean?"],
act:{n:"Brain Warm-Up Circuit",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Round 1 (word): Name as many fruits as you can in 1 minute.","Round 2 (memory): Read a list of 6 items slowly (apple, hat, chair, blue, dog, river). Wait 30 seconds and have residents write down what they remember.","Round 3 (math): Count backward from 50 by 3s together.","Round 4 (creativity): Name 5 uses for a paper clip.","Talk about which round was easiest and hardest."]},
d:["Which round was easiest?","Which was hardest?","Which brain exercise will you do daily?"],
disc:[["What habits hurt brain health?","Isolation, too much TV, poor sleep, smoking, alcohol."],["How does physical exercise help the brain?","More blood flow, better mood and memory."],["What brain activities can we do more of here?","Games, trivia, reading groups, music."],["Does it matter if you get the answer wrong?","No; trying is what exercises the brain."],["What new thing would you like to learn?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Your brain can keep growing at any age. Challenge it with puzzles, conversation, music, and new skills every day.",
tryit:"Do one brain exercise every day this week."},

{t:"Memory Tricks",
goal:"Today we will learn simple memory tricks to help remember names, lists, and important information.",
warm:["What is something you often forget?","What is a favorite memory from childhood?","How do you try to remember things?"],
terms:[
["Repetition","saying or writing something several times.","When you meet someone, say their name back: Nice to meet you, Carol. Then use it again later.","Does repeating help you remember?"],
["Association","linking new information to something you already know.","Meeting a man named Mr. Baker and picturing him holding a loaf of bread.","Can you make an association for someone's name here?"],
["Chunking","breaking information into smaller groups.","A phone number like 4237505504 is easier as 423-750-5504.","What else could you chunk?"],
["Acronyms","using first letters to make a word.","F.A.S.T. for stroke signs, or HALT for hungry, angry, lonely, tired.","Do you remember any acronyms?"],
["Visualization","making a picture in your mind.","To remember to buy milk, eggs, and bread, picture a cow sitting on eggs eating bread.","Does picturing things help you remember?"]],
story:"Ms. Lorraine always forgot new residents' names and felt embarrassed. In group, she learned to repeat names and make associations. When she met Ms. Rose, she pictured a big red rose. When she met Mr. Hill, she pictured him climbing a hill. She started remembering everyone's name, and new residents felt welcomed by her.",
sq:["What did Lorraine struggle with?","What tricks did she use?","How did it affect new residents?","What association could you make for your own name?"],
act:{n:"Memory Trick Practice",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Name practice: Go around the circle. Each person says their name plus a word or picture to remember it (for example, Sunny Sam).","Chunking: Read a 10-digit number and have residents chunk it to remember.","Visualization: Read a shopping list of 5 items and ask residents to create a silly picture story to connect them.","Wait 3 minutes, then ask residents to recall the list.","Acronym: Make a group acronym for something useful, like the steps of a morning routine."]},
d:["Which trick worked best for you?","How many items did you remember?","Where could you use these tricks?"],
disc:[["Why do we forget things?","Stress, distraction, not paying attention, sleep, aging, medicine."],["How does stress affect memory?","Makes it harder to focus and recall."],["What should you do if memory problems get worse quickly?","Tell the nurse."],["How can we help each other remember?","Gentle reminders, patience."],["What is your earliest memory?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Repeat it, link it, chunk it, picture it. Memory tricks make remembering easier for everyone.",
tryit:"Use the repetition trick the next time you meet someone new."},

{t:"Using Lists and Reminders",
goal:"Today we will learn how to use lists, calendars, and reminders so we don't have to keep everything in our heads.",
warm:["Do you make lists? What kind?","What do you forget most often?","How do you remember important dates?"],
terms:[
["External memory","using tools outside your brain to remember.","Lists, calendars, sticky notes, alarms, and pill organizers.","What external memory tools do you use?"],
["To-do list","a list of tasks to complete.","Today: call pharmacy, wash clothes, return library book.","How does a to-do list help?"],
["Sticky note reminder","a note placed where you'll see it.","A note on your door: Take your ID!","Where could you put a sticky note?"],
["Routine spot","a specific place for important items.","Always putting your glasses on your nightstand and keys in a bowl.","What item do you lose most?"],
["Checking off","crossing items off as you finish them.","Crossing off tasks gives a sense of accomplishment.","How does it feel to check something off?"]],
story:"Mr. Benny kept missing his appointments and losing his glasses. Staff helped him set up a small whiteboard by his door with a daily to-do list and a basket for his glasses and wallet. Every morning, he checked the board. He said, My head used to be full of worries about forgetting. Now the board remembers for me.",
sq:["What did Benny keep forgetting?","What tools helped?","How did he use them?","What does the board remembers for me mean?"],
act:{n:"Make a Daily List",m:"Paper, pens, sticky notes",s:["Have each resident write a to-do list for tomorrow with 3 to 5 tasks.","Add check boxes next to each one.","Write one sticky note reminder for something important.","Decide where they will put their list and sticky note.","Choose a routine spot for one item they often lose."]},
d:["What is on your list for tomorrow?","Where will you put your sticky note?","What item needs a routine spot?"],
disc:[["Is using lists a sign of bad memory?","No; smart people use tools."],["How can lists reduce stress?","Less to hold in your head."],["What tools could staff help set up?","Whiteboards, calendars, pill organizers."],["How can phones help with reminders?","Alarms, calendars, notes."],["What would you like to remember better?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Let tools do the remembering. Use lists, sticky notes, calendars, and routine spots, and enjoy checking things off.",
tryit:"Write and use a to-do list every day this week."},

{t:"Brain-Healthy Habits",
goal:"Today we will learn daily habits that protect brain health and memory as we age.",
warm:["What do you think helps the brain stay healthy?","Do you know anyone who stayed sharp into old age?","What habits do you have that might help your brain?"],
terms:[
["Brain health","how well your brain works for thinking, memory, and mood.","A healthy brain helps you remember, learn, make decisions, and manage emotions.","What affects brain health?"],
["Sleep and the brain","the brain cleans itself and stores memories during sleep.","Getting 7 to 9 hours helps memory and mood.","How does poor sleep affect your thinking?"],
["Heart-brain connection","what's good for the heart is good for the brain.","Controlling blood pressure, not smoking, and exercising help prevent strokes and memory problems.","How are the heart and brain connected?"],
["Brain foods","foods that support brain health.","Leafy greens, berries, fish, nuts, beans, and olive oil.","Which brain foods do you eat?"],
["Protecting your head","preventing head injuries.","Preventing falls, wearing seat belts, and wearing helmets when biking.","Why are head injuries dangerous?"]],
story:"Ms. Harriet's mother had memory loss, and Harriet worried it would happen to her. Her doctor told her that healthy habits could lower her risk. Harriet started walking daily, eating more vegetables and fish, doing crossword puzzles, getting better sleep, and staying social at church. At 75, she still leads the facility's trivia night.",
sq:["What was Harriet worried about?","What did her doctor tell her?","What habits did she start?","How is she doing now?"],
act:{n:"Brain Habit Check",m:"Paper with a checklist",s:["List brain-healthy habits: sleep 7+ hours, walk or move daily, eat vegetables, do a brain game, talk with someone, manage stress, take medicines as prescribed, avoid smoking and alcohol, prevent falls.","Have residents check the habits they do now.","Circle one habit to improve.","Share with the group.","Make a group goal, like a daily walk or trivia time."]},
d:["How many habits do you already have?","Which one will you improve?","What group goal did we choose?"],
disc:[["Can we prevent all memory loss?","No, but healthy habits lower risk."],["How do loneliness and isolation affect the brain?","Increase risk of decline."],["Why are blood pressure and diabetes control important for the brain?","Protect blood vessels in the brain."],["How can mental health affect memory?","Depression and anxiety affect focus and memory."],["What brain habit will you start today?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Sleep, move, eat brain foods, stay social, keep learning, protect your heart and head. Small daily habits protect your brain.",
tryit:"Practice your chosen brain-healthy habit every day this week."},

{t:"Telling Your Life Story",
goal:"Today we will share memories from our lives, which exercises memory, builds connection, and honors our stories.",
warm:["Where did you grow up?","What was your first job?","What is a song that reminds you of your younger days?"],
terms:[
["Reminiscing","remembering and sharing past experiences.","Talking about your childhood home, your first car, or a holiday memory.","What do you love to remember?"],
["Life story","the collection of experiences that make you who you are.","Where you grew up, people you loved, challenges you overcame, things you're proud of.","What chapter of your life would you like to share?"],
["Memory triggers","things that bring back memories.","An old photo, a song, a smell like fresh bread, or a favorite food.","What triggers happy memories for you?"],
["Listening to stories","giving others attention as they share.","Asking, What happened next? or What was that like?","Why is listening to elders' stories important?"],
["Legacy of stories","stories passed down keep memories alive.","Telling grandkids how you met your spouse or what Chattanooga was like in the 1970s.","Who would you like to tell your stories to?"]],
story:"During a reminiscing group, the facilitator played a song from the 1960s. Mr. Ellis, who rarely talked, smiled and said, I danced to this at my prom. He told the group about his date, his borrowed suit, and how he got home late. Everyone laughed. Afterward, his caregiver said it was the most she'd ever heard him talk.",
sq:["What triggered Ellis's memory?","What did he share?","How did the group react?","Why do you think the song helped him talk?"],
act:{n:"Memory Lane",m:"Old photos, songs from past decades, or memory prompt cards",s:["Play a song from a past decade or show an old photo or object.","Use prompts: Describe your childhood home. What games did you play as a kid? What was your favorite meal growing up? Tell about a teacher you remember. What was your first job?","Each person picks a prompt and shares a short memory.","Encourage listeners to ask one follow-up question.","Thank each storyteller."]},
d:["What memory did you share?","What story from someone else stuck with you?","What song or object brought back memories?"],
disc:[["Why is reminiscing good for the brain?","Exercises memory, language, and emotions."],["How can sharing stories build friendships?","We learn about each other."],["What if a memory is painful?","It's OK to pass or share only what feels safe."],["How can we preserve our stories?","Writing, recording, telling family."],["What would you want your grandchildren to know about your life?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Your life story matters. Sharing memories keeps the brain active, builds connection, and passes on wisdom.",
tryit:"Share a favorite memory with a staff member or family member this week."},

{t:"Focus and Concentration",
goal:"Today we will learn what affects focus and practice ways to improve our attention.",
warm:["When is it hardest for you to concentrate?","What helps you focus?","Have you ever read a page and realized you didn't take in anything?"],
terms:[
["Concentration","the ability to focus on one thing.","Reading a book without your mind wandering, or following a recipe step by step.","How long can you usually focus?"],
["Distraction","something that pulls your attention away.","TV in the background, a phone buzzing, noise in the hallway, or worried thoughts.","What distracts you most?"],
["Single-tasking","doing one thing at a time.","Turning off the TV while filling out a form.","Why does single-tasking work better than multitasking?"],
["Breaks","short rests to recharge focus.","Working for 15 minutes, then taking a 2-minute stretch break.","How often do you need breaks?"],
["Symptoms and focus","mental health symptoms and some medicines can affect concentration.","Depression, anxiety, and racing thoughts can make focusing hard. Tell the nurse if it's a big problem.","How do your moods affect your focus?"]],
story:"Ms. Tamara tried to read her benefits letter while the TV was on and people were talking. She got frustrated and gave up. Her caregiver suggested taking it to a quiet room. Tamara read it in 10 minutes and understood it. She said, The letter wasn't hard. The room was too loud.",
sq:["Why couldn't Tamara understand the letter at first?","What helped?","What did she realize?","Where is a quiet spot you could use?"],
act:{n:"Focus Challenges",m:"Paper, pens, a simple word search or spot-the-difference page",s:["Give each resident a word search or simple puzzle.","Round 1: Do the puzzle for 3 minutes while the facilitator plays music and talks.","Round 2: Do the puzzle for 3 minutes in silence.","Compare how many words people found each round.","Discuss focus tips: quiet space, one task, breaks, writing things down."]},
d:["Which round was easier?","How much difference did the noise make?","What focus tip will you use?"],
disc:[["Why is multitasking hard on the brain?","Switching tasks wastes focus."],["How do phones affect concentration?","Notifications pull attention."],["How can staff help residents focus on important tasks?","Quiet spaces, help reading, breaking tasks down."],["What should you do if you can't concentrate for many days?","Tell the nurse."],["Where do you focus best?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Find a quiet space, do one thing at a time, take short breaks, and tell the nurse if focus is a big problem.",
tryit:"Do one important task this week in a quiet place with no TV."},

{t:"Word Games for the Brain",
goal:"Today we will enjoy word games that build vocabulary, memory, and quick thinking.",
warm:["What word game did you play growing up?","Do you like crosswords, word searches, or Scrabble?","What is your favorite word?"],
terms:[
["Vocabulary","the words you know and use.","Learning new words helps you understand more and express yourself better.","What is a word you learned recently?"],
["Synonym","a word that means the same thing as another word.","Happy and joyful. Big and large.","Can you name a synonym for tired?"],
["Antonym","a word that means the opposite.","Hot and cold. Up and down.","What is the opposite of brave?"],
["Rhyme","words that end with the same sound.","Cat, hat, bat. Rhymes help with memory and are fun.","What rhymes with day?"],
["Category game","naming items in a group.","Name 5 animals that live in the water.","What category would you choose?"]],
story:"Mr. Clyde struggled to find his words after a mild stroke and felt frustrated. His speech therapist suggested word games. He started playing category games and rhymes with other residents each afternoon. After a few months, his speech was smoother and he was the champion of the rhyming game. He said, The games made therapy feel like fun.",
sq:["Why did Clyde struggle with words?","What did his therapist suggest?","How did he improve?","Why might games be better than drills?"],
act:{n:"Word Game Round-Robin",m:"Paper, pens, a timer",s:["Round 1 – Categories: Name things in the kitchen. Go around the circle; no repeats.","Round 2 – Rhyme Time: Start with a word (light); each person says a rhyming word.","Round 3 – Opposites: The facilitator says a word, the group calls out the opposite.","Round 4 – Alphabet: Name a food for each letter of the alphabet as a group.","Celebrate effort and laughs."]},
d:["Which game was your favorite?","Which round was hardest?","Which game could we play more often?"],
disc:[["How do word games help the brain?","Memory, language, quick thinking."],["How can word games help with conversation?","More words, easier to express."],["Why do games feel easier than lessons?","Fun, less pressure."],["What word games can you play alone?","Crosswords, word searches, apps."],["What word describes you?","Let each resident share one word."]],
take:"Word games build vocabulary, memory, and quick thinking, and they're fun. Play a little every day.",
tryit:"Do a crossword, word search, or word game each day this week."},

{t:"Everyday Math",
goal:"Today we will practice simple math we use every day, like money, time, and measuring.",
warm:["Do you like math or not?","Where do you use math in your daily life?","What math was easy or hard in school?"],
terms:[
["Adding and subtracting money","figuring totals and change.","A $2.50 soda and a $1.25 bag of chips total $3.75. From $5, the change is $1.25.","Where do you add money in daily life?"],
["Telling time","reading clocks and figuring out time spans.","If group starts at 1:00 and lasts an hour, it ends at 2:00.","How long is it from 9:30 to 11:00?"],
["Measuring","using cups, spoons, and inches.","A recipe needs half a cup of milk, or a box is 12 inches wide.","When do you measure things?"],
["Estimating","making a close guess.","$3.89 plus $2.15 is about $6.","How does estimating help at the store?"],
["Calculator help","using a calculator or phone for harder math.","Tapping the calculator on your phone to check a total.","Do you know where the calculator is on a phone?"]],
story:"Ms. Doris avoided cooking because recipes had fractions and she felt bad at math. A caregiver showed her that measuring cups do the math for her: half a cup is just the cup marked 1/2. They made a simple banana bread together. Doris said, I thought I couldn't do math. Turns out I just needed the right tools.",
sq:["Why did Doris avoid cooking?","How did the caregiver help?","What did they make?","What tools help you with math?"],
act:{n:"Math in Real Life",m:"Paper, pens, play money or a clock (optional)",s:["Money: You buy bread for $2.29 and peanut butter for $3.49. About how much? Exact total? Change from $10?","Time: Your appointment is at 2:30 and the ride takes 25 minutes. When should you leave?","Measuring: A recipe needs 2 cups of rice. You want to make half. How much?","Estimating: Round $4.89, $1.99, and $3.10 and add them.","Let residents work in pairs, then share answers."]},
d:["Which problem was easiest?","Which was hardest?","Where will you use math this week?"],
disc:[["Why do people feel anxious about math?","Bad school experiences, fear of mistakes."],["How does math help with independence?","Money, time, cooking, medicine."],["Is it OK to use a calculator?","Yes, it's a tool."],["How can we help each other with math without judgment?","Patience, showing steps."],["What math skill would you like to improve?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Math is part of daily life: money, time, and measuring. Estimate first, use tools like calculators and measuring cups, and practice without fear.",
tryit:"Estimate your total before paying the next time you shop."}
]);
PSR_ADD("Mind, Memory & Thinking", [
{t:"Understanding Memory Changes",
goal:"Today we will learn the difference between normal memory changes and signs that need a doctor's attention.",
warm:["Do you ever walk into a room and forget why?","How do you feel when you forget something?","What do you know about dementia?"],
terms:[
["Normal memory changes","small slips that happen to everyone, especially with age.","Forgetting where you put your keys, or a name that comes back to you later.","What memory slips are normal?"],
["Dementia","a group of conditions that cause serious memory and thinking problems that affect daily life.","Getting lost in a familiar place, forgetting how to do everyday tasks, or not recognizing loved ones.","How is dementia different from normal forgetting?"],
["Other causes","memory problems can come from treatable causes.","Infections, dehydration, medicine side effects, depression, thyroid problems, or low vitamin B12.","Why is it important to see a doctor about memory changes?"],
["Warning signs","changes to report to the nurse or doctor.","Asking the same question many times, trouble following conversations, confusion about time or place, personality changes.","Who would you tell if you noticed these signs?"],
["Patience and dignity","treating people with memory problems kindly.","Gently reminding instead of saying, I already told you!","How should we treat someone with memory problems?"]],
story:"Ms. Ruby suddenly became confused and forgot her daughter's name. Her family feared it was dementia. The nurse sent her to the doctor, who found a urinary tract infection. After antibiotics, Ruby's memory returned to normal. The doctor said sudden confusion should always be checked quickly because it's often treatable.",
sq:["What happened to Ruby?","What did her family fear?","What was the real cause?","Why should sudden memory changes be reported quickly?"],
act:{n:"Normal or Get Checked?",m:"Board with two columns",s:["Read examples and have the group decide: NORMAL or GET CHECKED.","Forgot where you put your glasses. (Normal.)","Got lost walking to the bathroom you use every day. (Get checked.)","Couldn't remember an actor's name. (Normal.)","Suddenly confused about what year it is. (Get checked, quickly.)","Forgot an appointment once. (Normal.)","Asking the same question many times in an hour. (Get checked.)","Discuss each one."]},
d:["Which examples surprised you?","Who would you tell if you noticed warning signs?","Why should we be patient with forgetful friends?"],
disc:[["Why do people fear memory loss?","Losing independence, identity."],["How can stress and depression affect memory?","Harder to focus and remember."],["What treatable causes of confusion should staff watch for?","Infections, dehydration, medicine changes."],["How can we support someone with memory problems?","Patience, routines, gentle reminders, dignity."],["What helps you remember important things?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Some forgetting is normal. Sudden or serious memory changes should be checked by the nurse or doctor because many causes are treatable. Treat everyone with patience and dignity.",
tryit:"If you notice a big memory change in yourself or a friend, tell the nurse."},

{t:"Thinking Traps",
goal:"Today we will learn common thinking traps that make us feel worse and how to catch and change them.",
warm:["Have you ever assumed the worst and it turned out fine?","Do you ever think in all-or-nothing terms?","What thought often brings you down?"],
terms:[
["Thinking trap","a habit of thinking that isn't accurate and makes us feel worse.","My friend didn't text back, so she must hate me.","Have you fallen into a thinking trap?"],
["All-or-nothing thinking","seeing things as all good or all bad.","I missed one day of my walking plan, so I'm a total failure.","What would a fairer thought be?"],
["Mind reading","assuming you know what others think.","They're laughing, so they must be laughing at me.","How could you check if it's true?"],
["Catastrophizing","imagining the worst possible outcome.","My check is late, so I'll lose my housing and end up on the street.","What's a more likely outcome?"],
["Should statements","harsh rules for yourself or others.","I should never feel sad. They should always know what I need.","How do should statements make you feel?"]],
story:"When the doctor's office didn't call Mr. Dean back, he thought, They don't care about me. Nobody does. I'll never get better. He felt hopeless all day. His counselor helped him spot the traps: mind reading and catastrophizing. He reframed it: They're probably busy. I'll call again tomorrow. He called the next day and got an appointment.",
sq:["What thoughts did Dean have?","What thinking traps were they?","How did he reframe it?","What happened next?"],
act:{n:"Trap Spotting",m:"Board",s:["Write the four traps on the board.","Read thoughts and have the group name the trap: I ruined the whole day by spilling coffee (all-or-nothing). She frowned, so she's mad at me (mind reading). I have a headache, it's probably a brain tumor (catastrophizing). I should be able to handle everything alone (should).","For each, help the group create a fairer, kinder thought.","Have residents write a thought trap they often fall into and a fairer replacement.","Share if comfortable."]},
d:["Which trap do you fall into most?","What fairer thought did you write?","How did reframing change how you feel?"],
disc:[["Why do our minds fall into traps?","Stress, past experiences, depression, anxiety."],["How can you catch a trap in the moment?","Notice strong feelings and ask: Is this thought 100 percent true?"],["How do thinking traps affect relationships?","Misunderstandings, conflicts."],["Who can help you spot traps?","Counselor, friends, staff."],["What fairer thought helps you most?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Thoughts are not facts. Catch thinking traps like all-or-nothing, mind reading, catastrophizing, and should statements, and replace them with fairer thoughts.",
tryit:"Catch one thinking trap this week and write a fairer thought."},

{t:"Thoughts, Feelings, and Actions",
goal:"Today we will learn how our thoughts, feelings, and actions are connected, and how changing one can change the others.",
warm:["Have you ever had a thought that changed your mood instantly?","What do you do when you feel sad?","Can actions change feelings? How?"],
terms:[
["Thoughts","the words and ideas in our minds.","I'm going to have a terrible day. or I can handle this.","What thought did you wake up with today?"],
["Feelings","emotions we experience.","Sad, anxious, happy, angry, calm.","How do thoughts affect feelings?"],
["Actions","what we do.","Staying in bed, going for a walk, calling a friend, yelling.","How do feelings affect actions?"],
["The triangle","thoughts, feelings, and actions all affect each other.","Thought: Nobody likes me. Feeling: lonely. Action: stay in room. Result: more lonely.","Can you think of a triangle in your life?"],
["Changing the triangle","changing a thought or action can improve feelings.","Changing the action: going to the day room anyway. Result: someone says hi, and you feel less lonely.","Which corner is easiest to change?"]],
story:"Ms. Brenda woke up thinking, Today is going to be awful. She felt down and stayed in bed, which made her feel worse. Her counselor taught her the triangle. The next morning, she noticed the same thought. She changed her action: she got up, showered, and went to breakfast. A friend made her laugh. By lunch, her mood had lifted. She said, I couldn't change my feelings directly, but I could change what I did.",
sq:["What was Brenda's first thought?","What happened when she stayed in bed?","What did she change the next day?","What does her last sentence mean?"],
act:{n:"Draw the Triangle",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Draw a triangle on the board. Label the corners: Thought, Feeling, Action.","Walk through an example as a group: Thought: I'll fail at this. Feeling: nervous. Action: give up.","Then change the thought: I'll try my best. New feeling: hopeful. New action: try.","Have each resident draw their own triangle about a real situation.","Have them change one corner and write the new result."]},
d:["What situation did you choose?","Which corner did you change?","How would the outcome be different?"],
disc:[["Why can't we just choose our feelings?","Feelings come automatically; we can influence them through thoughts and actions."],["Which corner is easiest to change when depressed?","Often actions."],["How can staff help residents change actions?","Invitations, encouragement, routines."],["How can this help with anger?","Change the thought or take a time-out."],["What helpful action lifts your mood?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Thoughts, feelings, and actions are connected. When you can't change a feeling directly, change a thought or an action, and the feeling often follows.",
tryit:"When you feel down this week, change one action and notice what happens."},

{t:"Following Directions Step by Step",
goal:"Today we will practice listening to and following multi-step directions, a skill used for medicines, recipes, forms, and jobs.",
warm:["Have you ever followed directions and still gotten lost?","Do you prefer written or spoken directions?","What directions are hardest for you to follow?"],
terms:[
["Directions","instructions that tell you how to do something.","Take 1 pill twice daily with food, or turn left at the light, then right on Main Street.","What directions do you follow every day?"],
["Steps in order","doing each step in the right sequence.","Mixing ingredients before baking, not after.","Why does order matter?"],
["Listen for key words","words like first, next, then, last, before, and after.","First wash your hands, then put on gloves.","What key words help you follow directions?"],
["Ask to repeat","asking someone to say directions again.","Could you repeat that, please? or Could you write it down?","Why is it OK to ask?"],
["Write it down","writing down directions to check later.","Writing the steps for using the washing machine on an index card.","When would writing directions help?"]],
story:"Mr. Arthur's new job at a car wash had many steps. On his first day, he got confused and made mistakes. The next day, he asked his supervisor to show him once more and wrote the steps on a card. He kept the card in his pocket. By the end of the week, he didn't need the card anymore. His supervisor said he was one of the best new workers.",
sq:["What was hard for Arthur?","What did he do on the second day?","How did the card help?","What happened by the end of the week?"],
act:{n:"Follow Me",m:"Paper, pens",s:["Give a 3-step direction out loud: Write your name at the top of the paper, draw a circle in the middle, and draw a star in the bottom corner.","Check how many people got all three steps.","Give a 5-step direction and allow residents to ask you to repeat it or write it down.","Compare how writing it down helped.","Practice with a real-life example: medicine directions or recipe steps."]},
d:["How many steps could you remember?","Did writing it down help?","Where in life could you use this skill?"],
disc:[["Why do people struggle with directions?","Too many steps, distractions, anxiety."],["What should you do if you don't understand directions?","Ask to repeat, ask for a demonstration, write it down."],["How can following directions help with medicines?","Safety, correct use."],["How can following directions help at a job?","Good performance, trust from supervisor."],["What tool helps you remember directions?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Listen for key words, do steps in order, ask to repeat, and write it down. Following directions is a skill you can practice.",
tryit:"Write down the steps for one daily task this week."},

{t:"Reading for Fun and Learning",
goal:"Today we will explore how reading, at any level, can relax us, teach us, and keep our minds sharp.",
warm:["What did you like to read as a kid?","What do you like to read now: newspapers, magazines, the Bible, books?","Is reading easy, hard, or somewhere in between for you?"],
terms:[
["Reading for pleasure","reading something just because you enjoy it.","A mystery novel, a sports magazine, a cookbook, or comic strips.","What do you enjoy reading?"],
["Reading level","how complex the reading is.","Large-print books, easy readers for adults, and audiobooks make reading enjoyable for everyone.","What helps make reading easier for you?"],
["Audiobooks","books read aloud by a narrator.","Listening to a story from the library on a phone or CD player.","Have you tried audiobooks?"],
["Reading aloud","reading out loud to yourself or others.","Reading a short poem to the group, or a staff member reading the newspaper to a resident.","Do you enjoy being read to?"],
["Benefits of reading","how reading helps the brain and mood.","Reading reduces stress, builds vocabulary, improves focus, and takes you to new places.","How do you feel after reading something good?"]],
story:"Ms. Viola had stopped reading because her eyesight got worse. A volunteer brought her large-print books and introduced her to audiobooks. She started listening to mystery stories every night. She also joined a small group where a staff member read the newspaper aloud. She said, I got my stories back. I'm traveling again in my mind.",
sq:["Why did Viola stop reading?","What helped her start again?","What new habit did she form?","What does traveling in my mind mean?"],
act:{n:"Read-Aloud Circle",m:"A short story, poem, or newspaper article; large-print copies if possible",s:["Choose a short, uplifting story, poem, or article.","Read it aloud slowly, or invite volunteers to read a paragraph each (never force anyone).","Ask the group: What was your favorite part? How did it make you feel?","Share favorite books, authors, or types of stories.","Make a group reading wish list to share with staff or the library."]},
d:["What did you enjoy about the reading?","How did it feel to listen or read aloud?","What would you like to read next?"],
disc:[["Why do adults stop reading?","Vision, time, stress, bad school memories."],["How can reading help at bedtime?","Calms the mind instead of screens."],["How can we make reading easier for everyone?","Large print, audiobooks, reading groups."],["What types of reading count?","All of it: magazines, scripture, recipes, comics."],["What book changed your life or that you loved?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Reading is for everyone. Large print, audiobooks, and read-aloud groups make it easy to enjoy stories and keep learning.",
tryit:"Read or listen to something you enjoy for 10 minutes each day this week."},

{t:"Curiosity and Creative Thinking",
goal:"Today we will exercise our curiosity and creative thinking with fun challenges and questions.",
warm:["What is something you've always wondered about?","When was the last time you tried something new?","Do you think you are creative?"],
terms:[
["Curiosity","wanting to learn or understand something.","Wondering how birds know where to fly in winter, or asking a new resident about where they grew up.","What are you curious about?"],
["Creative thinking","coming up with new ideas or new ways to do things.","Using an old jar as a pencil holder or finding a new way to rearrange your room.","When have you been creative?"],
["Brainstorming","thinking of many ideas without judging them.","Listing 20 ideas for a group outing, even silly ones.","Why should we avoid judging ideas while brainstorming?"],
["Open mind","being willing to consider new ideas.","Trying a new food or activity you thought you wouldn't like.","When did being open-minded surprise you?"],
["What if questions","imagining possibilities.","What if we could plant a garden here? What if animals could talk?","What what-if question would you ask?"]],
story:"The residents said there was nothing to do on Sunday afternoons. A caregiver asked, What if we created something? The group brainstormed 25 ideas, including a talent show, a cooking contest, and a memory book of everyone's stories. They chose the talent show. Mr. Jesse, who had been quiet for months, played harmonica. Everyone cheered. He said, I didn't know I'd ever play for people again.",
sq:["What was the problem on Sundays?","How did the group solve it?","What surprised everyone?","How did creativity help Jesse?"],
act:{n:"Creative Challenges",m:"Paper, pens, a few everyday objects (cup, spoon, sock)",s:["Challenge 1: Hold up an everyday object. Name as many new uses as possible in 2 minutes.","Challenge 2: Answer what-if questions: What if you could have any job for a day? What if our home had a new room?","Challenge 3: Brainstorm 15 ideas for a new activity for our home.","Vote on the favorite idea and share it with staff.","Celebrate the most creative answers."]},
d:["What was the most creative idea?","Which what-if question was most fun?","What activity did the group choose?"],
disc:[["Why do adults stop being curious?","Busy, stressed, fear of looking silly."],["How does curiosity help mental health?","Engagement, hope, learning."],["How can we encourage creative ideas here?","Suggestion box, house meetings, trying new things."],["What gets in the way of trying new things?","Fear, routine, low energy."],["What new thing would you like to try?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Stay curious, ask what-if questions, and let your creativity out. New ideas can bring new joy.",
tryit:"Try one new thing this week, even something small."},

{t:"Trivia and Fun Facts",
goal:"Today we will enjoy a friendly trivia game that exercises memory, sparks conversation, and gets us laughing together.",
warm:["Do you like trivia or game shows?","What is a fun fact you know?","What topic do you know the most about?"],
terms:[
["Trivia","questions about facts from many topics.","Questions about history, music, sports, food, and geography.","What trivia topic would you choose?"],
["Recall","pulling information from memory.","Remembering the name of a singer or the year something happened.","How does recall exercise the brain?"],
["Teamwork in trivia","working together to find answers.","Teammates who know different topics help each other.","Why might a team do better than one person?"],
["Fun fact","an interesting piece of information.","Chattanooga's Lookout Mountain is home to Rock City, and on a clear day people say you can see seven states.","What fun fact do you know about Tennessee?"],
["Friendly competition","competing for fun while staying kind.","Cheering for the other team and laughing at wrong answers together.","How do we keep competition friendly?"]],
story:"Every Friday, the residents played trivia. Ms. Gloria, who was usually quiet, knew every answer about old movies and Motown music. Her team won three weeks in a row. Other residents started asking her about her favorite movies. Gloria said, I didn't know all that stuff in my head was worth anything. Turns out it makes me the trivia queen.",
sq:["What did Gloria know a lot about?","How did trivia change how others saw her?","How did she see herself?","What topic could make you a trivia champ?"],
act:{n:"Team Trivia",m:"Prepared trivia questions, paper, pens",s:["Divide into 2 or 3 teams.","Ask a mix of questions (examples): What city is called the Music City? (Nashville) What is the capital of Tennessee? (Nashville) Who sang What's Going On? (Marvin Gaye) How many days are in a leap year? (366) What river runs through Chattanooga? (Tennessee River) What is the largest planet? (Jupiter) What fruit is known for keeping the doctor away? (Apple) Who was the first U.S. president? (George Washington)","Let teams discuss and answer.","Keep score but celebrate every correct answer.","End with each person sharing one fun fact."]},
d:["Which question was hardest?","What fun fact did you learn?","Who on your team surprised you?"],
disc:[["How does trivia help memory?","Practices recall."],["Why is trivia good for socializing?","Teamwork, conversation, laughter."],["How do we include people who don't know many answers?","Teams, varied topics, encouragement."],["What topics should we include next time?","Let residents share."],["What is your favorite fun fact?","Let residents share."]],
take:"Trivia exercises memory, builds teamwork, and brings laughter. Everyone knows something worth sharing.",
tryit:"Learn a new fun fact this week and share it with someone."},

{t:"Finding Calm in a Busy Mind",
goal:"Today we will practice simple ways to quiet a busy or racing mind so we can think clearly and rest.",
warm:["Does your mind ever feel like it's racing?","When is your mind quietest?","What do you do to slow your thoughts?"],
terms:[
["Racing thoughts","thoughts that move fast and jump from one thing to another.","Lying in bed thinking about bills, family, appointments, and the past all at once.","When do you have racing thoughts?"],
["Brain dump","writing down everything on your mind to clear space.","Writing every worry and task on paper before bed.","Have you ever tried writing your thoughts down?"],
["Focus point","something to focus on to calm the mind.","A candle flame (or picture of one), a ticking clock, your breath, or a calming word like peace.","What focus point would work for you?"],
["Slow counting","counting slowly to steady the mind.","Counting breaths from 1 to 10, then starting over.","Does counting help you calm down?"],
["Talk to your team","racing thoughts that don't stop can be a symptom to share with the nurse.","If racing thoughts keep you up for several nights or come with high energy and little sleep, tell staff.","Why is it important to tell the nurse?"]],
story:"Ms. Dana had racing thoughts every night. She tried a brain dump: before bed, she wrote everything on her mind in a notebook. Then she did slow counting of her breaths. Some nights it worked right away; other nights it took a while. She also told her nurse, who talked to her doctor. Over time, she said, My mind feels less like a highway and more like a back road.",
sq:["What was Dana struggling with?","What did she try?","Who else did she tell?","What does highway vs. back road mean?"],
act:{n:"Quiet Mind Practice",m:"Paper, pens, calm music (optional)",s:["Brain dump: Give 3 minutes to write down everything on your mind. No order needed.","Fold the paper and set it aside: You can come back to these later.","Slow counting: Breathe in slowly and count 1, breathe out. In, count 2. Continue to 10, and repeat.","Focus point: Choose a calming word and repeat it slowly in your mind for 1 minute.","Ask how the mind feels now on a 1 to 10 scale of calm."]},
d:["How busy was your mind before and after?","Which tool helped most?","When will you use it?"],
disc:[["What makes the mind race?","Stress, caffeine, worry, some mental health conditions."],["How does a busy mind affect sleep?","Hard to fall or stay asleep."],["What should you do if racing thoughts last for days?","Tell the nurse."],["How can routines help calm the mind?","Predictability, less to remember."],["What brings your mind peace?","Let residents share."]],
take:"When your mind races, write it down, focus on one thing, count slowly, and tell the nurse if it keeps happening.",
tryit:"Do a brain dump before bed on three nights this week."}
]);
